window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"IM-1":{"unit":"IM-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-01-01","title":"Thymus Embryology and the Adult Thymus","subject":"Immune System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the thymus from its origin in the third pharyngeal pouch, through colonization by blood-borne T cell precursors and the formation of cortex and medulla, to its descent into the mediastinum and its slow involution in adult life. It then applies that development to 22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge) syndrome and to the anterior mediastinal masses. Board questions usually give a newborn with hypocalcemia, infections, and a heart defect, or an adult with a mediastinal mass, and ask you to name the failed structure or the likely tissue of origin. If you keep the three sources of the thymus separate (endoderm, neural crest, and marrow precursors), most of these questions follow directly.","prerequisites":["Germ layers and the pharyngeal apparatus: arches, clefts, and pouches","Neural crest cell migration and derivatives","Basic T cell biology: T cell receptor, CD4, CD8, and MHC class I and II","Parathyroid hormone actions on bone, kidney, and vitamin D activation","Divisions of the mediastinum and the great vessels of the superior mediastinum"],"connections":[{"to":"T cell development and selection (IM-2)","how":"The cortical and medullary epithelium described here is the stroma that performs positive and negative selection."},{"to":"Primary immunodeficiencies","how":"Thymic aplasia and severe combined immunodeficiency both lower T cell counts, one from missing stroma and the other from defective precursors."},{"to":"Parathyroid and calcium physiology (endocrine units)","how":"Parathyroid hypoplasia in DiGeorge syndrome produces the low PTH, low calcium, high phosphate pattern of hypoparathyroidism."},{"to":"Cardiac outflow tract development (cardiovascular embryology)","how":"Cardiac neural crest cells that septate the outflow tract depend on the same pharyngeal field, explaining conotruncal defects in 22q11.2 deletion."},{"to":"Neuromuscular junction disorders","how":"Thymoma and thymic follicular hyperplasia are linked to autoantibodies against the acetylcholine receptor in myasthenia gravis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pharyngeal pouch","def":"An endodermal outpocketing of the embryonic foregut between two pharyngeal arches that gives rise to glands and cavities of the head, neck, and mediastinum."},{"term":"Thymic epithelial cell","def":"An endoderm-derived stromal cell of the thymus, also called an epithelial reticular cell, that forms a meshwork and supplies the signals and self antigens that guide T cell maturation."},{"term":"Thymocyte","def":"A developing T lymphocyte within the thymus."},{"term":"FOXN1","def":"A transcription factor required for thymic epithelial cell differentiation; its loss causes athymia with hair and nail defects."},{"term":"Hassall corpuscle","def":"A concentric whorl of flattened, keratinizing medullary epithelial cells that identifies thymic medulla on histology."},{"term":"AIRE","def":"Autoimmune regulator, a protein in medullary thymic epithelial cells that switches on tissue-restricted self antigens so autoreactive thymocytes can be deleted."},{"term":"TBX1","def":"A transcription factor gene within the 22q11.2 region whose haploinsufficiency disrupts development of the pharyngeal apparatus and outflow tract."},{"term":"Conotruncal defect","def":"A congenital malformation of the cardiac outflow tract, such as truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot, or interrupted aortic arch."},{"term":"Thymic involution","def":"The progressive, age-related loss of the T cell producing epithelial space of the thymus with replacement by adipose tissue."},{"term":"Homeostatic proliferation","def":"Slow division of peripheral T cells driven by IL-7 or IL-15 and self peptide-MHC that maintains T cell numbers without antigen exposure."},{"term":"T cell receptor excision circle (TREC)","def":"A circular DNA fragment left after T cell receptor gene rearrangement that marks recently produced thymic emigrants because it is not copied when cells divide."},{"term":"Thymoma","def":"A neoplasm of thymic epithelial cells, usually in adults, associated with myasthenia gravis and other autoimmune disorders."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-01-01-C01","obj":"IM-1.1","title":"Origin of the thymus and its descent into the mediastinum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thymus is assembled from three sources. Endoderm of the third pharyngeal pouch forms the epithelial framework, neural crest derived mesenchyme forms the connective tissue, and blood-borne precursors later supply the lymphocytes (C02). Each source can fail on its own, so keeping them separate explains most embryology questions about this organ."},{"type":"p","text":"The pharyngeal apparatus consists of arches of mesoderm and neural crest mesenchyme, separated on the outside by ectodermal clefts and on the inside by endodermal pouches. Around the fifth to sixth week the third pouch forms a dorsal wing and a ventral wing. The dorsal wing becomes the inferior parathyroid gland, and the ventral wing becomes the thymus, so these two organs share an origin. The fourth pouch forms the superior parathyroids, and its ventral part, the ultimobranchial body, contributes the parafollicular (C) cells of the thyroid. Differentiation of the thymic epithelium depends on the transcription factor FOXN1. Loss of FOXN1 leaves a rudimentary thymus with no T cells, together with alopecia and nail dystrophy, which is the human counterpart of the nude mouse."},{"type":"table","title":"Pharyngeal pouch derivatives","cols":["Pouch","Main adult structures"],"rows":[["First","Middle ear cavity, pharyngotympanic (auditory) tube, mastoid antrum"],["Second","Epithelial lining of the palatine tonsil and its crypts"],["Third","Thymus (ventral wing) and inferior parathyroids (dorsal wing)"],["Fourth","Superior parathyroids; ultimobranchial body (parafollicular C cells of the thyroid)"]],"caption":"The third pouch is the only pouch that gives rise to both an immune organ and an endocrine gland, which is why a single developmental defect can cause both T cell deficiency and hypocalcemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Cranial neural crest cells migrate into the pharyngeal arches and surround the thymic primordium. They form the capsule, the septa that divide the gland into incompletely separated lobules, and the connective tissue and pericytes around vessels entering the gland. This mesenchyme is not inert packing: its signals support growth of the epithelium, so a neural crest defect can leave the thymus small even when the endoderm is normal."},{"type":"p","text":"By about the seventh week the thymic primordia lose their connection with the pharynx and migrate caudally and medially. The left and right primordia meet in the midline and join loosely by connective tissue, which is why the gland remains bilobed rather than truly fused. They come to rest in the superior and anterior mediastinum, in front of the great vessels and pericardium. The inferior parathyroids travel with the thymus until they detach near the lower poles of the thyroid, ending below the fourth pouch glands. The pouch numbers therefore run opposite to the final positions of the parathyroids."},{"type":"flow","title":"Formation and descent of the thymus","steps":["Third pouch endoderm forms dorsal and ventral wings","Ventral wing forms the thymic epithelial primordium","Neural crest mesenchyme surrounds it as capsule and septa","Primordia detach from the pharynx and migrate caudally","Left and right primordia join in the midline","Thymus settles in the superior and anterior mediastinum","Inferior parathyroids detach at the lower thyroid poles"],"caption":"Descent of the thymus drags the inferior parathyroids below the fourth pouch parathyroids."},{"type":"p","text":"Incomplete or excessive migration explains ectopic tissue. Thymic remnants can persist anywhere along the path from the angle of the mandible to the mediastinum, and cervical thymic cysts arise along the same line. An inferior parathyroid that fails to separate can be carried into the anterior mediastinum within the thymus, so a parathyroid adenoma causing hyperparathyroidism that is not found in the neck is often located along this third pouch route."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-01-01-C02","obj":"IM-1.2","title":"Colonization by T cell precursors and formation of cortex and medulla","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The endoderm builds a stromal framework but makes no lymphocytes. Thymic epithelial cells remain joined by desmosomes, as all epithelia are, yet they spread into a three-dimensional meshwork whose spaces fill with developing T cells (thymocytes). This stroma supplies what thymocytes cannot make for themselves: Notch ligands (delta-like 4) that commit precursors to the T lineage, interleukin 7 (IL-7) for survival and proliferation, chemokines that guide migration, and self peptides on MHC class I and II molecules for selection."},{"type":"p","text":"Hematopoietic progenitors begin entering the epithelial thymus at around the eighth to ninth week, attracted by chemokines such as CCL21 and CCL25. They come first from fetal liver and later from bone marrow. Because the lymphocytes are immigrants and the stroma is local, T cell deficiency can arise from either side: thymic aplasia removes the stroma, while severe combined immunodeficiency supplies precursors that cannot mature."},{"type":"p","text":"Once colonized, each lobule separates into a dense outer cortex and a paler inner medulla by about the end of the first trimester. Precursors enter through postcapillary venules near the corticomedullary junction, move outward to the subcapsular cortex while they proliferate, and then move inward as they mature. The cortex is crowded with small immature thymocytes, most CD4 and CD8 double positive, so it stains darkly. Cortical epithelial cells positively select thymocytes whose receptors can bind self peptide-MHC; most fail, die by apoptosis, and are cleared by macrophages. In the medulla, epithelial cells express AIRE, which switches on tissue-restricted antigens such as insulin, so strongly self-reactive thymocytes are deleted or diverted into regulatory T cells."},{"type":"table","title":"Cortex and medulla compared","cols":["Feature","Cortex","Medulla"],"rows":[["Staining","Dark, densely packed lymphocytes","Pale, fewer lymphocytes"],["Thymocytes","Immature, mostly CD4 and CD8 double positive","Mature CD4 or CD8 single positive"],["Main selection step","Positive selection by cortical epithelial cells","Negative selection by AIRE-expressing medullary epithelium and dendritic cells"],["Barrier","Blood-thymus barrier present","No comparable barrier"],["Distinctive structure","Macrophages clearing apoptotic thymocytes","Hassall corpuscles"]],"caption":"The thymus has lobules with cortex and medulla but, unlike a lymph node, no afferent lymphatics and no lymphoid follicles in the normal state."},{"type":"p","text":"Hassall corpuscles are concentric whorls of flattened, keratinizing medullary epithelial cells. They appear during fetal life, enlarge with age, and are the most reliable sign that tissue is thymus and the field is medulla. They secrete thymic stromal lymphopoietin, which conditions medullary dendritic cells to support regulatory T cell development. In the cortex, a blood-thymus barrier (continuous endothelium, thick basal lamina, an epithelial sheath, and perivascular macrophages) limits contact between circulating antigen and thymocytes during positive selection."},{"type":"flow","title":"Thymocyte traffic through a lobule","steps":["Marrow precursors enter at the corticomedullary junction","Notch signals commit them to the T lineage","Cells proliferate while moving out to the subcapsular cortex","Cortical epithelium positively selects self-MHC binders","Survivors move inward to the medulla","AIRE-driven antigens delete strongly self-reactive cells","Naive single positive T cells exit to the blood"],"caption":"Outward then inward migration places positive selection in the cortex and negative selection in the medulla."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A fetus has normal bone marrow but lacks functional FOXN1. Would a cortex and medulla form, and would B cells be present?","explain":"No cortex or medulla forms, because undifferentiated epithelium cannot attract precursors, supply Notch ligand and IL-7, or present self antigen. T cells are absent, while B cells, which mature in marrow, are present.","check":"Compare with the list of stromal signals in the first paragraph and the FOXN1 point in C01."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-01-01-C03","obj":"IM-1.3","title":"22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge) syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The 22q11.2 deletion syndrome is the most common human microdeletion syndrome, about 1 in 4,000 live births. A deletion of about 3 megabases on one chromosome 22 removes several dozen genes; most cases arise new, and transmission is autosomal dominant. DiGeorge and velocardiofacial syndrome are older names for the same deletion."},{"type":"p","text":"The key gene is TBX1, a transcription factor of pharyngeal endoderm and mesoderm. With one working copy, the pouches and arches segment poorly and fail to signal neural crest cells into the third and fourth arches and the outflow tract, so one deletion damages several neighboring structures that form at the same time."},{"type":"flow","title":"From deletion to clinical findings","steps":["22q11.2 microdeletion removes TBX1 and neighboring genes","Pharyngeal pouches and arches segment poorly","Third and fourth pouch derivatives are hypoplastic","Neural crest fails to septate the outflow tract","Low T cells, low PTH, and conotruncal heart defects"],"caption":"One field defect explains the immune, endocrine, and cardiac findings together."},{"type":"p","text":"Thymic hypoplasia is usual; true athymia (complete DiGeorge syndrome) occurs in under about 1% of cases. Few naive T cells are made, so CD3 positive counts are low while B cells and natural killer cells are normal. Infections follow the T cell pattern: viruses, Candida, and Pneumocystis jirovecii. Immunoglobulin levels are usually normal, though responses to protein antigens can be weak without T cell help. The broad anterior mediastinal shadow of the normal newborn thymus is absent on chest radiograph, but this sign is nonspecific, because severe combined immunodeficiency and stress involution in a sick neonate (C04) also shrink it."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypoplastic parathyroids secrete too little parathyroid hormone (PTH). Without PTH the distal tubule reabsorbs less calcium, the proximal tubule excretes less phosphate, bone releases less calcium, and renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase makes less calcitriol, reducing intestinal calcium absorption. The result is low calcium, high phosphate, and inappropriately low PTH. Low extracellular calcium lowers the threshold of nerve Na+ channels, so axons fire spontaneously: jitteriness, tetany, laryngospasm, and seizures in the first days of life. Adult total calcium is about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL (2.1 to 2.6 mmol/L), ionized about 1.1 to 1.3 mmol/L; neonatal values run slightly lower."},{"type":"table","title":"Failed structure and resulting finding","cols":["Structure","Origin","Finding"],"rows":[["Thymus","Third pouch endoderm with neural crest","Low T cells, absent thymic shadow, viral, fungal, and Pneumocystis infections"],["Parathyroids","Third and fourth pouch endoderm","Low PTH, hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, tetany, seizures"],["Outflow tract","Cardiac neural crest","Interrupted aortic arch type B, truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot, ventricular septal defect"],["Palate and face","Pharyngeal arch mesenchyme","Cleft palate or velopharyngeal insufficiency, characteristic facies"]],"caption":"Learning difficulties and later psychiatric illness, including schizophrenia, are also more common."},{"type":"p","text":"Cardiac neural crest cells form the aorticopulmonary septum. Too few cells leave septation absent or misaligned (truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot), and abnormal remodeling of the arch arteries gives interrupted aortic arch type B, the defect most specific for this deletion. Reduced thymic output also weakens central tolerance and regulatory T cell production, raising autoimmunity."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a seizing newborn","text":"A 3-day-old has a seizure. Total calcium is 6.4 mg/dL, phosphate is high, and PTH is low-normal. Echocardiography shows truncus arteriosus, and the chest radiograph shows no thymic shadow. Step 1: low calcium with high phosphate and an unraised PTH means parathyroid failure; vitamin D deficiency would raise PTH and lower phosphate. Step 2: a conotruncal defect points to failed cardiac neural crest. Step 3: an absent thymic shadow points to third pouch failure. One field defect links all three, so 22q11.2 deletion is the best explanation, and a low T cell count with normal B cells is the expected next finding."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-01-01-C04","obj":"IM-1.4","title":"Thymic involution and persistence of peripheral T cell immunity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thymus is largest relative to body weight at birth and largest in absolute size around puberty. Involution is the progressive loss of the true thymic epithelial space, the cortex and medulla where T cells are made. It begins in early childhood, well before puberty, and continues more slowly through adult life. Adipocytes accumulate between the lobules, so total gland size changes less than function: by late adulthood most of the thymus is fat surrounding small islands of cortex and medulla that still export a few naive T cells. The cortex thins first, the corticomedullary boundary blurs, and Hassall corpuscles remain prominent."},{"type":"p","text":"Several mechanisms contribute. Thymic epithelial cells express less FOXN1, sex steroids rising at puberty act on the epithelium and speed its loss, and aging marrow supplies fewer early progenitors. Acute involution is different and reversible. During severe illness, stress, or glucocorticoid exposure, double positive cortical thymocytes, which are highly sensitive to glucocorticoid-induced apoptosis, die in large numbers, and the gland can shrink within days. When the stress ends, for example after chemotherapy, the thymus can regrow beyond its earlier size, called thymic rebound, which can mimic a recurrent mediastinal mass on imaging."},{"type":"p","text":"Established T cell immunity survives involution because peripheral T cells are long-lived and maintain their own numbers. Naive T cells survive for years, sustained by IL-7 and by weak tonic T cell receptor signals from self peptide-MHC in lymph nodes. Memory T cells persist through slow turnover driven by IL-7 and IL-15. When numbers fall, each remaining cell receives more of these limited signals and divides, a process called homeostatic proliferation. In adults most new naive T cells come from this division of existing cells rather than from the thymus."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Homeostatic maintenance of the peripheral T cell pool","steps":["Peripheral T cell numbers fall","Less IL-7 consumed, so more IL-7 per cell","IL-7 plus self peptide-MHC drives slow division","Pool size recovers toward its set point","More cells consume IL-7, slowing division"],"caption":"Competition for IL-7 (and IL-15 for memory cells) holds T cell numbers steady with little thymic output."},{"type":"p","text":"For this reason thymectomy in an adult, for example to remove a thymoma, does not cause the clinical immunodeficiency seen in congenital athymia. The cost is subtle. The naive repertoire cannot be refilled with new receptor specificities, so after profound T cell loss from chemotherapy, HIV infection, or stem cell transplantation, adults recover slowly and mostly by expanding survivors, which narrows diversity. Recent thymic output can be estimated with T cell receptor excision circles (TRECs), DNA circles cut out during receptor gene rearrangement that are not copied when cells divide, so they dilute with homeostatic proliferation. Low TRECs on newborn screening flag severe combined immunodeficiency and athymia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a 50-year-old who has her thymus removed keep normal T cell immunity, while an infant born without a thymus does not?","explain":"The adult already has a large, diverse, self-renewing pool of naive and memory T cells maintained by IL-7, IL-15, and self peptide-MHC. The infant never built that pool, because the thymus is the only site where precursors become T cells.","check":"Compare with the homeostatic proliferation cycle and the paragraph on adult thymectomy."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-01-01-C05","obj":"IM-1.5","title":"The adult thymus in the mediastinum and anterior mediastinal masses","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In the anatomical scheme, a plane from the sternal angle to the T4 to T5 disc separates the superior from the inferior mediastinum, and the pericardium divides the inferior part into anterior, middle, and posterior compartments. The adult thymus lies mainly in the superior mediastinum behind the manubrium and extends into the anterior mediastinum between the sternum and pericardium, in front of the left brachiocephalic vein, superior vena cava, aortic arch, and upper pericardium. In children it is relatively larger and can reach toward the thyroid. Arteries come mainly from the internal thoracic arteries and veins drain mainly to the left brachiocephalic vein. Radiologists call this the prevascular compartment."},{"type":"p","text":"Masses here come from tissue that lives in or passes through the space: thymic epithelium, lymph nodes, germ cells, and thyroid extending from the neck. Age, associated findings, and contents on imaging sort them."},{"type":"table","title":"Classic anterior mediastinal masses","cols":["Mass","Tissue of origin","Typical patient","Clues explained by origin"],"rows":[["Thymoma","Thymic epithelial cells","Adult, usually over 40","Myasthenia gravis, pure red cell aplasia, hypogammaglobulinemia (Good syndrome)"],["Teratoma and other germ cell tumors","Germ cells outside the gonads","Adolescent or young adult","Fat, fluid, calcification, or teeth in teratoma; raised AFP or hCG in malignant germ cell tumors"],["Lymphoma","Lymphocytes of mediastinal nodes or thymus","Young adult (nodular sclerosis Hodgkin, primary mediastinal large B cell); adolescent (T lymphoblastic)","Fever, night sweats, weight loss; other enlarged nodes; rapid growth"],["Retrosternal thyroid","Thyroid extending from the neck","Older adult","Mass continuous with a cervical goiter, tracheal deviation"]],"caption":"Often remembered as the four Ts: thymoma, teratoma, lymphoma, and thyroid."},{"type":"p","text":"Thymoma is a neoplasm of thymic epithelial cells, usually mixed with nonneoplastic immature T cells, and the most common primary anterior mediastinal tumor in adults. Many are encapsulated and slow growing, and capsular invasion largely determines behavior; thymic carcinoma is a separate, aggressive tumor. About 30 to 50% of patients with thymoma have myasthenia gravis, and about 10 to 15% of patients with myasthenia have a thymoma. The neoplastic epithelium often lacks AIRE, so it selects T cells but fails to delete autoreactive ones, which help B cells make antibodies against the acetylcholine receptor; similar autoimmunity underlies pure red cell aplasia, while the cause of hypogammaglobulinemia with few B cells (Good syndrome) is unclear. Thymic follicular hyperplasia, germinal centers in the medulla of most younger patients with antibody positive myasthenia, is not a tumor."},{"type":"p","text":"Any large anterior mass can compress its neighbors: the superior vena cava (facial and arm swelling, distended neck veins), the trachea (stridor, worse lying flat), and the phrenic nerve (raised hemidiaphragm). Fast-growing tumors such as T lymphoblastic lymphoma produce these effects over days to weeks."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: sorting two anterior mediastinal masses","text":"Patient A, 55, has drooping eyelids and double vision that worsen through the day, and a smooth anterior mediastinal soft tissue mass without fat or calcium. Fatigable weakness indicates acetylcholine receptor antibodies, and an adult with this autoimmunity and a thymic mass most likely has a thymoma. Patient B, 20, has a mass containing fat and calcification with a normal AFP: age and contents indicate a mature teratoma. A high AFP would instead indicate a yolk sac component of a malignant nonseminomatous germ cell tumor, because thymic epithelium and lymphocytes do not make AFP."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 23-year-old woman has a large anterior mediastinal mass, night sweats, and an enlarged supraclavicular node. Which mass is most likely, and why is thymoma less likely?","explain":"Lymphoma, most likely nodular sclerosis Hodgkin: B symptoms and nodal spread reflect a lymphocyte tumor. Thymoma is an epithelial tumor of older adults that presents with autoimmunity or mass effect, not B symptoms.","check":"Compare with the table of anterior mediastinal masses."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and anatomy explain the pattern of disease. The third pharyngeal pouch makes both the thymus and the inferior parathyroids, and neural crest cells from the same pharyngeal region septate the cardiac outflow tract, so one field defect in 22q11.2 deletion produces T cell deficiency, hypocalcemia, and conotruncal heart disease together. The descent of the thymus explains ectopic parathyroid and thymic tissue in the neck and mediastinum, and its final position behind the manubrium, in front of the great veins, explains why its masses compress the superior vena cava and trachea."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology and pathology connect structure to function. The epithelium of the cortex and medulla selects T cells, so an absent thymus causes immunodeficiency, while a neoplastic thymus that selects badly causes autoimmunity such as myasthenia gravis. Once the peripheral pool is built, IL-7 and IL-15 driven homeostatic proliferation sustains it, so involution and adult thymectomy cost repertoire diversity rather than causing overt immunodeficiency. Limits remain: the chest radiograph shadow is nonspecific in sick neonates, and the residual function of the adult thymus varies widely between individuals."}],"summary":["The thymic epithelium comes from ventral third pouch endoderm, the capsule and septa from neural crest, and the lymphocytes from fetal liver and bone marrow precursors.","The third pouch also forms the inferior parathyroids, which the descending thymus pulls below the fourth pouch superior parathyroids.","Thymic epithelium supplies Notch ligand, IL-7, chemokines, and self peptide-MHC; FOXN1 loss leaves no functional thymus.","The dark cortex holds immature double positive thymocytes and performs positive selection; the pale medulla holds AIRE-expressing epithelium, Hassall corpuscles, and performs negative selection.","22q11.2 deletion (TBX1 haploinsufficiency) causes thymic hypoplasia with low T cells and normal B cells, hypoparathyroid hypocalcemia with high phosphate, and conotruncal defects.","Involution replaces the thymic epithelial space with fat from childhood onward; glucocorticoids cause acute, reversible loss of cortical thymocytes.","Peripheral T cells persist after involution or adult thymectomy through IL-7 and IL-15 driven homeostatic proliferation; TRECs measure recent thymic output.","The adult thymus lies in the superior and anterior mediastinum behind the manubrium, in front of the left brachiocephalic vein and great vessels.","Anterior mediastinal masses are thymoma, teratoma and other germ cell tumors, lymphoma, and retrosternal thyroid, sorted by age, contents, and associated findings.","Thymoma is an epithelial tumor of older adults associated with myasthenia gravis and pure red cell aplasia through defective tolerance, and with hypogammaglobulinemia."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck, pharyngeal pouches"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Immune System and Lymphoid Organs, thymus"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, superior and anterior mediastinum"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Lymphocyte development and congenital immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid hormone, calcium and phosphate metabolism, and vitamin D"}]},"IM-10":{"unit":"IM-10","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-10","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-10-01","title":"Immune Biochemistry and Nutrition","subject":"Immune System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the chemistry that immune cells depend on and the nutrients that keep them supplied. It traces how phagocytes turn oxygen into bleach and what happens when the first enzyme is missing, how a single purine salvage enzyme decides whether lymphocytes survive, which arachidonic acid products drive fever, pain, chemotaxis, and bronchoconstriction, and why granuloma macrophages can raise serum calcium. It ends with zinc, vitamin A, and protein-energy malnutrition, the most common cause of immunodeficiency worldwide. Board questions usually describe an infection pattern, a screening test, or a lab panel and ask you to name the broken step, so each section is built around the pathway that explains the finding.","prerequisites":["Pentose phosphate pathway and the role of NADPH","Purine salvage and ribonucleotide reductase in deoxynucleotide synthesis","Innate immune cells, phagocytosis, and the phagosome (IM-1, IM-2)","Vitamin D activation and calcium regulation by PTH and FGF23","Thymic T cell development and cell-mediated immunity"],"connections":[{"to":"Primary immunodeficiencies","how":"CGD and ADA deficiency are classic inherited defects of phagocyte killing and lymphocyte development, read against other SCID forms and phagocyte disorders."},{"to":"Acute inflammation and pathology","how":"Prostaglandins and leukotrienes are chemical mediators that explain the vascular and cellular events of acute inflammation."},{"to":"Endocrine calcium regulation","how":"Unregulated macrophage 1-alpha-hydroxylase produces PTH-independent hypercalcemia with suppressed PTH."},{"to":"Pulmonary system","how":"Cysteinyl leukotrienes drive bronchoconstriction in asthma, and sarcoidosis granulomas are most often pulmonary."},{"to":"Biochemistry of nutrition","how":"Zinc, vitamin A, and protein supply limit the rapidly dividing lymphoid tissue and epithelial barriers that defend against infection."}],"terms":[{"term":"Respiratory burst","def":"A rapid rise in oxygen consumption by activated phagocytes that is used to make reactive oxygen species, not ATP."},{"term":"NADPH oxidase (NOX2)","def":"A multi-subunit phagocyte enzyme that transfers electrons from NADPH to molecular oxygen to form superoxide."},{"term":"Myeloperoxidase","def":"A heme enzyme of neutrophil azurophilic granules that combines hydrogen peroxide with chloride to form hypochlorous acid."},{"term":"Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD)","def":"An inherited NADPH oxidase defect in which phagocytes ingest microbes normally but cannot generate an oxidative burst."},{"term":"Adenosine deaminase (ADA)","def":"A purine salvage enzyme that irreversibly deaminates adenosine and deoxyadenosine to inosine and deoxyinosine."},{"term":"Ribonucleotide reductase","def":"The enzyme that reduces ribonucleoside diphosphates to deoxyribonucleotides, supplying all four DNA precursors."},{"term":"Eicosanoids","def":"Signaling lipids made from 20-carbon arachidonic acid, including prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes."},{"term":"Cysteinyl leukotrienes","def":"LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4, glutathione-derived leukotrienes that constrict bronchi and increase venular permeability."},{"term":"Cathelicidin (LL-37)","def":"A cationic antimicrobial peptide whose gene is induced by calcitriol acting through the vitamin D receptor."},{"term":"Thymulin","def":"A thymic epithelial peptide hormone that supports T cell maturation and is biologically active only when bound to zinc."},{"term":"Squamous metaplasia","def":"Replacement of a specialized columnar epithelium by stratified squamous epithelium, which in vitamin A deficiency becomes keratinized."},{"term":"Kwashiorkor","def":"Protein-energy malnutrition in which protein lack outweighs calorie lack, producing hypoalbuminemic edema and fatty liver."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-10-01-C01","obj":"IM-10.1","title":"The respiratory burst and its failure in chronic granulomatous disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"When a neutrophil or macrophage engulfs a microbe, its oxygen consumption rises sharply within seconds. This respiratory burst is not mitochondrial respiration, makes no ATP, and continues when mitochondria are poisoned with cyanide. Its purpose is to convert molecular oxygen into reactive oxygen species inside the phagosome, through a fixed sequence in which each enzyme uses the product of the step before."},{"type":"flow","title":"Oxygen-dependent killing in the neutrophil phagosome","steps":["Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (pentose phosphate pathway) makes NADPH","NADPH oxidase moves electrons from NADPH to O2","Superoxide (O2 radical anion) forms in the phagosome","Superoxide dismutase converts superoxide to H2O2","Myeloperoxidase combines H2O2 with chloride","Hypochlorous acid (HOCl, bleach) oxidizes microbial proteins and lipids"],"caption":"Each step depends on the one before it. CGD breaks the second step, so nothing downstream forms unless the microbe supplies its own H2O2."},{"type":"p","text":"NADPH comes from the oxidative branch of the pentose phosphate pathway, in which glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) is the rate-limiting enzyme. NADPH oxidase (NOX2) is assembled only on activation. Its catalytic core, cytochrome b558, sits in the membranes of specific granules and the plasma membrane, which fuse to form the phagosome wall, and contains gp91phox (the NOX2 protein itself) and p22phox. On stimulation, the cytosolic subunits p47phox, p67phox, and p40phox move to the membrane with the small GTPase Rac, and the complex transfers one electron at a time to O2. Superoxide dismutase then makes hydrogen peroxide. Myeloperoxidase (MPO), the green heme enzyme of azurophilic granules that colors pus, is delivered into the phagosome by degranulation and uses H2O2 and chloride to make HOCl. In the presence of ferrous iron, H2O2 can also form the hydroxyl radical (Fenton reaction)."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD) is an inherited loss of NADPH oxidase activity. About two thirds of cases are X-linked, from mutations in CYBB, which encodes gp91phox. The rest are autosomal recessive, most often in NCF1 (p47phox), less often in CYBA (p22phox), NCF2 (p67phox), or NCF4 (p40phox). Phagocyte number, chemotaxis, ingestion, and MPO content are normal; only superoxide production fails. Without the burst, phagocytes kill poorly, and microbes persist inside them. Oxidants also normally help limit inflammation, so CGD tissues show exaggerated, persistent inflammation, and chronic stimulation of macrophages builds granulomas in lymph nodes, liver, lung, skin, bowel, and urinary tract, which can obstruct the gastric outlet or ureters."},{"type":"p","text":"Defects downstream are milder. Inherited MPO deficiency is the most common inherited phagocyte defect and is usually silent, because superoxide and H2O2 still form and H2O2 alone kills many organisms. Severe G6PD deficiency, with almost no residual enzyme in leukocytes, can starve NADPH oxidase of substrate and rarely produces a CGD-like picture; the common variants that cause hemolysis do not."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has normal neutrophil counts, normal ingestion of bacteria, and normal MPO staining, yet has recurrent liver abscesses. Which molecule is missing from the phagosome first?","explain":"Superoxide. NADPH oxidase is the first oxidative step, so its loss also removes the H2O2 and HOCl that depend on it, while ingestion and granule enzymes remain intact.","check":"The respiratory burst flow and the CGD paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-10-01-C02","obj":"IM-10.2","title":"Predicting CGD infections and reading the DHR and NBT tests","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A CGD phagocyte still holds MPO, so it can make HOCl if it receives H2O2 from another source. Many bacteria make H2O2 as a byproduct of aerobic metabolism. Catalase-negative organisms, such as streptococci, leave that H2O2 in the phagosome, where the phagocyte borrows it and kills the organism with its own MPO. Catalase-positive organisms break down their H2O2 to water and oxygen, removing the only substrate available, and survive. This explains the classic CGD list: Staphylococcus aureus, Aspergillus species, Burkholderia cepacia complex, Serratia marcescens, and Nocardia. Aspergillus pneumonia is the leading infectious cause of death, and S aureus commonly causes liver abscess, lymphadenitis, and skin infection."},{"type":"p","text":"The catalase rule is a useful teaching model rather than the whole explanation. Catalase-deficient strains of S aureus and Aspergillus still cause infection in CGD animal models, so other organism traits also matter. For the exam, an unusual infection with one of these catalase-positive organisms, especially with granulomas, points to CGD. Serum immunoglobulins are normal or high from chronic infection, which distinguishes CGD from antibody defects."},{"type":"h","text":"Screening tests read out superoxide production"},{"type":"p","text":"Both tests stimulate phagocytes and detect whether reactive oxygen species appear. In the dihydrorhodamine (DHR) test, cells are loaded with nonfluorescent dihydrorhodamine 123, stimulated (usually with phorbol myristate acetate), and analyzed by flow cytometry. Oxidants convert the dye to fluorescent rhodamine 123, so normal neutrophils shift strongly to higher fluorescence. In CGD the shift is absent or small. In the older nitroblue tetrazolium (NBT) slide test, superoxide reduces the pale yellow dye to dark blue formazan, so normal neutrophils turn blue and CGD neutrophils stay pale. DHR is now preferred because it is quantitative, counts individual cells, and shows inheritance patterns. Genetic testing then identifies the subunit."},{"type":"table","title":"Interpreting phagocyte oxidase tests","cols":["Condition","DHR after stimulation","NBT","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Normal","Strong shift to high fluorescence","Most cells blue","Intact NADPH oxidase"],["X-linked CGD (gp91phox)","No or almost no shift","Cells stay yellow","No functional cytochrome b558"],["Autosomal recessive CGD (p47phox)","Small residual shift","Few or faint blue cells","Some residual oxidase activity"],["X-linked CGD carrier mother","Two populations, one normal and one negative","Mixed blue and yellow cells","Random X inactivation (lyonization)"],["MPO deficiency","Can be falsely low","Normal","Superoxide forms; DHR oxidation partly depends on MPO"]],"caption":"An abnormal DHR result needs MPO deficiency excluded, because the DHR signal partly depends on MPO while the NBT result does not."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a DHR result in a family","text":"A 2-year-old boy has Serratia osteomyelitis and Aspergillus pneumonia. His neutrophils show no DHR shift after stimulation. His mother's neutrophils show two peaks: about 40% shift normally and about 60% do not. Step 1: the boy makes no oxidant, so NADPH oxidase is absent. Step 2: his mother has two neutrophil populations, which in a woman reflects random X inactivation, since each neutrophil expresses only one of her two X chromosomes. Step 3: she is therefore a carrier of an X-linked defect, so the gene is CYBB (gp91phox). Step 4: had his mother shown a single normal peak and the boy a small residual shift, an autosomal recessive form such as p47phox deficiency would be more likely. Carriers whose normal population is very small can have infections themselves."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-10-01-C03","obj":"IM-10.3","title":"Adenosine deaminase deficiency: a metabolic cause of SCID","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Adenosine deaminase (ADA) is a purine salvage enzyme present in all cells and most active in lymphoid tissue. It irreversibly deaminates adenosine to inosine and deoxyadenosine to deoxyinosine, which are then degraded through hypoxanthine to uric acid. Deoxyadenosine comes largely from DNA breakdown, which is heavy in the thymus, where most developing thymocytes die by apoptosis and are cleared. Loss of ADA, inherited as an autosomal recessive trait, causes about 10 to 15 percent of severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID)."},{"type":"flow","title":"How ADA deficiency kills lymphocytes","steps":["ADA absent: deoxyadenosine accumulates","Lymphocyte deoxycytidine kinase phosphorylates it to dAMP","Further phosphorylation traps it as dATP","dATP binds the activity site of ribonucleotide reductase","All four deoxyribonucleotides fall","DNA synthesis and repair fail; lymphocytes undergo apoptosis"],"caption":"Excess of one deoxynucleotide shuts down synthesis of all of them. Lymphocytes trap dATP most avidly, so they die first."},{"type":"p","text":"Ribonucleotide reductase converts ribonucleoside diphosphates to deoxyribonucleoside diphosphates and is the only de novo source of deoxynucleotides. Its activity site is a regulatory switch: ATP binding turns the enzyme on, and dATP binding turns it off for every substrate. This normally prevents overproduction, but when dATP accumulates to very high levels the enzyme stays off and dCTP, dGTP, and dTTP run out. Cells that must copy DNA, such as proliferating lymphoid precursors, cannot divide or repair breaks and die."},{"type":"p","text":"Lymphocytes are selectively vulnerable because they have high deoxycytidine kinase activity, which phosphorylates deoxyadenosine, and relatively low nucleotidase activity, which would remove the phosphates. Deoxyadenosine also irreversibly inactivates S-adenosylhomocysteine hydrolase, so S-adenosylhomocysteine accumulates and inhibits methylation reactions, adding further toxicity. Because the damage is metabolic and acts on every developing lymphocyte, T, B, and NK cells are all profoundly low (T minus, B minus, NK minus SCID). Infants present in the first months with persistent thrush, chronic diarrhea, failure to thrive, severe viral infections, and Pneumocystis pneumonia, with a small thymus, no palpable lymph nodes, and absent tonsils."},{"type":"p","text":"ADA is expressed throughout the body, so features beyond immunity occur: flaring and cupping of the costochondral junctions on chest radiograph, sensorineural deafness, and neurologic and hepatic abnormalities. Diagnosis rests on absent ADA activity and high deoxyadenosine nucleotides in red cells. Newborn screening detects all typical SCID forms by low T cell receptor excision circles (TRECs), DNA circles made during T cell receptor rearrangement that mark recent thymic emigrants."},{"type":"table","title":"ADA deficiency compared with related SCID forms","cols":["Feature","ADA deficiency","PNP deficiency","X-linked SCID"],"rows":[["Defect","Adenosine deaminase","Purine nucleoside phosphorylase","Common gamma chain (IL2RG)"],["Inheritance","Autosomal recessive","Autosomal recessive","X-linked"],["Toxic metabolite or lost signal","dATP","dGTP","IL-7 and IL-15 signaling lost"],["Lymphocyte profile","T, B, NK all low","Mainly T low; B often present","T and NK low; B present but nonfunctional"],["Other clues","Costochondral flaring, deafness","Neurologic disease, low uric acid","Most common SCID; boys"]],"caption":"Metabolic SCID removes lymphocytes by toxicity; X-linked SCID removes cytokine signals for T and NK development."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-10-01-C04","obj":"IM-10.4","title":"Arachidonic acid products in inflammation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Eicosanoids are not stored. Cells make them on demand from arachidonic acid, a 20-carbon omega-6 fatty acid held at the sn-2 position of membrane phospholipids. Stimuli that raise cytosolic calcium, such as receptor activation, mechanical injury, or complement fragments, activate cytosolic phospholipase A2, which releases arachidonic acid. Which product a cell then makes depends on the enzymes it expresses, which is why platelets, endothelium, mast cells, and neutrophils each produce a characteristic set."},{"type":"flow","title":"Two branches of arachidonic acid metabolism","steps":["Phospholipase A2 releases arachidonic acid from membrane phospholipid","COX branch: COX-1 or COX-2 makes PGG2, then PGH2","Cell-specific synthases make PGE2, PGI2, PGD2, or thromboxane A2","LOX branch: 5-lipoxygenase with FLAP makes 5-HPETE, then LTA4","LTA4 hydrolase makes LTB4","LTC4 synthase adds glutathione: LTC4, then LTD4 and LTE4"],"caption":"Glucocorticoids reduce both branches by inhibiting phospholipase A2 (through annexin A1) and suppressing COX-2 induction; NSAIDs block only the COX branch."},{"type":"p","text":"Cyclooxygenase has two activities in one enzyme: a cyclooxygenase step that makes PGG2 and a peroxidase step that reduces it to PGH2, the common precursor. COX-1 is constitutive and maintains housekeeping functions such as gastric mucosal protection and platelet thromboxane. COX-2 is induced by IL-1, TNF, and bacterial lipopolysaccharide at sites of inflammation, so it supplies most inflammatory prostaglandin."},{"type":"p","text":"PGE2 explains three cardinal features of inflammation. For fever, IL-1, IL-6, and TNF induce COX-2 in the endothelium of hypothalamic vessels, and the PGE2 they release acts on EP3 receptors of preoptic neurons, raising the temperature set point so heat is conserved and produced until core temperature rises, for example to about 39 °C (102.2 °F). For pain, PGE2 lowers the threshold of nociceptors to bradykinin, histamine, and mechanical stimuli (hyperalgesia). For redness and swelling, PGE2 and PGI2 dilate arterioles and add to the venular leak caused by histamine and bradykinin. PGD2, the main prostaglandin of mast cells, also dilates vessels and constricts bronchi."},{"type":"p","text":"Thromboxane A2 from platelets (via thromboxane synthase) acts through a Gq receptor that raises calcium, causing platelet aggregation and vasoconstriction; it lasts only seconds before hydrolysis to inactive thromboxane B2. Prostacyclin (PGI2) from endothelium raises platelet cAMP and does the opposite, so their balance sets local clotting and tone. Aspirin acetylates COX irreversibly, and anucleate platelets cannot replace it for their roughly 7 to 10 day life span, while endothelium makes new enzyme."},{"type":"p","text":"The lipoxygenase branch is most active in leukocytes and mast cells. LTB4, from neutrophils and macrophages, is a potent chemoattractant that also activates neutrophil adhesion and degranulation, so it amplifies recruitment. The cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4), mainly from mast cells, eosinophils, and basophils, act on CysLT1 receptors to constrict bronchial smooth muscle far more potently than histamine, increase venular permeability, and increase mucus secretion. They were once called slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis because their bronchoconstriction is slow and sustained. Lipoxins, made when neutrophils and platelets cooperate, inhibit further neutrophil recruitment and help end inflammation."},{"type":"table","title":"Major eicosanoids and their actions","cols":["Product","Enzyme and main source","Main actions"],"rows":[["PGE2","COX; macrophages, endothelium, many cells","Fever, nociceptor sensitization, vasodilation"],["PGI2 (prostacyclin)","COX; endothelium","Vasodilation, inhibits platelet aggregation"],["Thromboxane A2","COX; platelets","Platelet aggregation, vasoconstriction"],["PGD2","COX; mast cells","Vasodilation, bronchoconstriction"],["LTB4","5-lipoxygenase; neutrophils, macrophages","Neutrophil chemotaxis, adhesion, activation"],["LTC4, LTD4, LTE4","5-lipoxygenase; mast cells, eosinophils","Bronchoconstriction, increased venular permeability, mucus"],["Lipoxins","Lipoxygenases; neutrophils with platelets","Inhibit neutrophil recruitment; resolution"]],"caption":"COX products dominate fever, pain, vascular tone, and platelets; 5-lipoxygenase products dominate leukocyte recruitment and the airway."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why can blocking cyclooxygenase provoke bronchospasm in some people with asthma?","explain":"Arachidonic acid that cannot enter the COX branch is available to 5-lipoxygenase, and loss of PGE2, which normally restrains leukotriene release, removes a brake. Cysteinyl leukotrienes rise and constrict the airway.","check":"The two-branch flow and the cysteinyl leukotriene paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-10-01-C05","obj":"IM-10.5","title":"Macrophage vitamin D activation: sarcoidosis hypercalcemia and cathelicidin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin D3 from skin or diet is hydroxylated in the liver to 25-hydroxyvitamin D, the main circulating form, and then by 1-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) to calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D), the active hormone. In the proximal tubule this last step is tightly controlled. PTH and low phosphate induce it; calcitriol, FGF23, and high calcium suppress it; and calcitriol also induces 24-hydroxylase (CYP24A1), which inactivates it. Serum calcitriol therefore stays within a narrow range, about 20 to 60 pg/mL."},{"type":"p","text":"Activated macrophages express the same 1-alpha-hydroxylase, but they regulate it differently. Interferon-gamma from Th1 cells and Toll-like receptor signals induce it, PTH does not control it, calcitriol does not suppress it, and macrophages inactivate calcitriol poorly. In sarcoidosis, the large mass of activated macrophages and epithelioid cells in noncaseating granulomas converts circulating 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol without feedback."},{"type":"flow","title":"From granuloma to hypercalcemia","steps":["Interferon-gamma activates granuloma macrophages","Macrophage 1-alpha-hydroxylase converts 25(OH)D to calcitriol without feedback","Serum calcitriol rises","Gut calcium absorption and bone resorption increase","Hypercalciuria, then hypercalcemia","High calcium suppresses PTH"],"caption":"PTH-independent hypercalcemia: high calcitriol with low PTH. Other granulomatous diseases and some lymphomas can do the same."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypercalciuria is common in sarcoidosis, and hypercalcemia occurs in a smaller fraction of patients. It worsens with more sunlight or vitamin D intake, because more substrate reaches the macrophages. Consequences follow from calcium: nephrolithiasis, nephrocalcinosis, polyuria from impaired urinary concentration, and reduced kidney function. The same mechanism occurs in tuberculosis, fungal granulomas, berylliosis, and Hodgkin and some non-Hodgkin lymphomas. Total serum calcium normally runs about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL (2.1 to 2.6 mmol/L)."},{"type":"table","title":"Renal compared with macrophage 1-alpha-hydroxylase","cols":["Regulator","Proximal tubule enzyme","Macrophage enzyme"],"rows":[["PTH","Induces","No effect"],["Low phosphate","Induces","No important effect"],["Calcitriol","Suppresses and induces 24-hydroxylase","No effective suppression"],["Interferon-gamma and TLR signals","No role","Induce"],["Result in granulomatous disease","Suppressed by high calcium","Keeps producing calcitriol"]],"caption":"One enzyme, two control systems: endocrine feedback in the kidney, immune activation in the macrophage."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: separating causes of hypercalcemia","text":"A 38-year-old with bilateral hilar adenopathy has calcium 11.8 mg/dL, low PTH, high calcitriol, and low-normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D. Step 1: low PTH rules out primary hyperparathyroidism, where PTH is high or inappropriately normal. Step 2: high calcitriol with ordinary 25-hydroxyvitamin D means unregulated activation, not excess intake; vitamin D intoxication raises 25-hydroxyvitamin D markedly. Step 3: humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy from PTH-related peptide also suppresses PTH, but calcitriol is low or normal because PTH-related peptide raises it far less than PTH does. Step 4: high calcitriol, low PTH, and hilar granulomas fit macrophage 1-alpha-hydroxylase in sarcoidosis."},{"type":"p","text":"In host defense this pathway is useful. When TLR2/1 on human macrophages recognizes mycobacterial lipoproteins, the cell increases both the vitamin D receptor and 1-alpha-hydroxylase. Calcitriol made inside the cell binds the vitamin D receptor, which acts with RXR at a vitamin D response element in the cathelicidin gene promoter. The precursor protein is processed to LL-37, a cationic peptide that disrupts microbial membranes and promotes autophagy, aiding killing of Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Because the macrophage depends on circulating 25-hydroxyvitamin D as substrate, low serum levels limit cathelicidin induction, one proposed link between vitamin D deficiency and tuberculosis risk."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-10-01-C06","obj":"IM-10.6","title":"Zinc and vitamin A deficiency and infection risk","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Zinc is a cofactor for many enzymes, including alkaline phosphatase and DNA and RNA polymerases, and is the structural core of zinc finger transcription factors. Tissues with rapid cell turnover, such as lymphoid tissue, intestinal mucosa, and skin, are therefore the first to fail. Thymic epithelial cells secrete thymulin, which supports thymocyte maturation and is active only when bound to zinc. Zinc deficiency causes thymic atrophy and lymphopenia, reduces Th1 cytokines (IL-2 and interferon-gamma), weakens delayed hypersensitivity and NK cell activity, and impairs phagocyte function. Skin and gut barriers break down at the same time, so children with zinc deficiency have more diarrhea, pneumonia, and skin infection. Other effects include poor wound healing, loss of taste (dysgeusia), growth retardation, and hypogonadism."},{"type":"p","text":"Acrodermatitis enteropathica is the inherited form. It is an autosomal recessive defect in SLC39A4, which encodes ZIP4, the zinc importer on the apical membrane of duodenal and jejunal enterocytes. Breast milk contains zinc in a highly absorbable form, so symptoms typically start at weaning or earlier in formula-fed infants. The triad is dermatitis around the mouth, eyes, and anus and on the hands and feet (sharply bordered, crusted, sometimes vesiculobullous), alopecia, and diarrhea, often with irritability and secondary Candida and bacterial skin infection. Serum zinc is low, and alkaline phosphatase is often low because it is a zinc enzyme. Acquired deficiency with the same rash occurs with malabsorption, alcoholism, and parenteral nutrition lacking zinc."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin A, as retinoic acid acting through nuclear RAR and RXR receptors, keeps mucus-secreting columnar epithelium differentiated. Without it, epithelium of the conjunctiva, airways, and urinary tract undergoes squamous metaplasia with keratinization. Ciliated, mucus-producing linings that trap and clear microbes become dry keratin barriers that clear poorly, raising the risk of respiratory infection, and desquamated keratin in the urinary tract can seed stones and infection. In the eye this produces xerophthalmia, Bitot spots, and corneal softening (keratomalacia); night blindness reflects loss of rhodopsin. Retinoic acid made by intestinal dendritic cells also directs lymphocytes to home to the gut and supports IgA class switching, so mucosal antibody defense weakens."},{"type":"p","text":"Measles shows the interaction most clearly. Infection lowers serum retinol, because fever increases use, intake falls, and retinol-binding protein is a negative acute-phase protein whose synthesis drops during inflammation. In a child who is already deficient, measles causes more severe pneumonia, diarrhea, corneal ulceration leading to blindness, and higher mortality."},{"type":"table","title":"Zinc compared with vitamin A deficiency","cols":["Feature","Zinc deficiency","Vitamin A deficiency"],"rows":[["Core mechanism","Enzyme, zinc finger, and thymulin failure","Loss of retinoic acid-driven epithelial differentiation"],["Immune effect","Thymic atrophy, lymphopenia, weak T cell function","Barrier loss, weaker mucosal IgA and gut homing"],["Skin and mucosa","Periorificial and acral dermatitis, alopecia, diarrhea","Squamous metaplasia, dry keratinized skin and conjunctiva"],["Infection pattern","Diarrhea, pneumonia, Candida and skin infection","Respiratory infection, diarrhea, severe measles"],["Distinctive clue","Low alkaline phosphatase; inherited ZIP4 defect","Night blindness, Bitot spots, keratomalacia"]],"caption":"Both deficiencies impair epithelial barriers; zinc acts more on T cell development, vitamin A more on mucosal differentiation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-10-01-C07","obj":"IM-10.7","title":"Protein-energy malnutrition: the leading acquired immunodeficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Worldwide, the most common cause of acquired immunodeficiency is protein-energy malnutrition (PEM), not HIV. It affects large numbers of young children in low-income regions and also occurs in older adults, people with chronic illness or cancer, eating disorders, and severe malabsorption. Lymphocyte proliferation and clonal expansion demand amino acids, energy, and micronutrients, so the adaptive immune system is among the first systems to falter when supply falls."},{"type":"p","text":"The thymus is especially sensitive, and its shrinkage in malnutrition is sometimes called nutritional thymectomy. Thymocytes are lost through lack of substrate for proliferation and through apoptosis driven by the high cortisol of starvation. Falling leptin, which reflects fat loss, removes a signal that normally supports thymic output and Th1 responses. T cell areas of the lymph nodes (paracortex) and spleen (periarteriolar sheaths) are depleted, CD4 counts and the CD4 to CD8 ratio fall, and delayed-type hypersensitivity skin tests become anergic. Antibody responses to new antigens are weaker, secretory IgA and complement (especially C3) fall, and phagocyte killing is impaired, while total serum immunoglobulin is often normal or high because of repeated infection. The net effect is a predominantly cell-mediated defect, so tuberculosis, measles, Pneumocystis, and severe gastroenteritis are common and severe."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The malnutrition and infection cycle","steps":["Inadequate protein and energy intake","Thymic atrophy and weak cell-mediated immunity","More frequent, more severe infections","Fever, catabolism, anorexia, and diarrheal losses","Further depletion of protein and micronutrients"],"caption":"Each turn worsens the next: infection raises nutrient needs while reducing intake and absorption."},{"type":"p","text":"Marasmus and kwashiorkor are two patterns of PEM. Marasmus results from a total calorie deficit: the body consumes its fat and skeletal muscle (the somatic protein compartment) to supply energy and amino acids. Serum albumin, part of the visceral protein compartment, is relatively preserved, so there is no edema. Kwashiorkor occurs when protein lack is greater than calorie lack, classically in a child weaned onto a starchy, low-protein diet. The visceral compartment is depleted, low albumin lowers plasma oncotic pressure and contributes to edema, and the liver cannot make enough apolipoprotein to export triglyceride as VLDL, producing fatty liver. Edema can mask the weight loss."},{"type":"table","title":"Marasmus compared with kwashiorkor","cols":["Feature","Marasmus","Kwashiorkor"],"rows":[["Main deficit","Total calories","Protein more than calories"],["Compartment depleted","Somatic (muscle, fat)","Visceral (albumin, liver proteins)"],["Body weight","Severe wasting, often below about 60% of expected","Less wasting, about 60 to 80% of expected, masked by edema"],["Edema","Absent","Present"],["Serum albumin","Near normal","Low"],["Liver","Normal size","Enlarged, fatty"],["Skin and hair","Loose skin folds, wizened face","Flaky paint dermatosis, depigmented sparse hair"],["Immune effect","Thymic atrophy, impaired cell-mediated immunity","Same, often with more severe infection"]],"caption":"Weight thresholds follow traditional weight-for-age classifications; both forms share the immune defect, and mixed marasmic kwashiorkor occurs."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A severely malnourished child has a nonreactive tuberculin skin test despite active pulmonary tuberculosis. Why?","explain":"The skin test depends on memory Th1 cells and macrophage recruitment. Thymic atrophy, low CD4 counts, and weak Th1 cytokine production leave too few responding T cells, so the test is falsely negative (anergy).","check":"The thymus and T cell zone paragraph in this section."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each topic in this chapter is a biochemical pathway that becomes an immune phenotype. The pentose phosphate pathway supplies NADPH for the burst, so erythrocyte and phagocyte biochemistry meet in G6PD deficiency. Purine salvage links nucleotide metabolism to lymphocyte survival, and the pattern of lost cells (T, B, and NK in ADA deficiency, mainly T in PNP deficiency) separates metabolic SCID from cytokine receptor SCID. Arachidonic acid metabolism connects acute inflammation in pathology to fever physiology, platelet function, and asthma. Macrophage vitamin D activation ties granulomatous inflammation to calcium endocrinology and to innate killing of mycobacteria. In microbiology, the catalase test that sorts staphylococci from streptococci also predicts which organisms threaten a CGD patient."},{"type":"p","text":"These models have limits. Catalase alone does not determine virulence in CGD, and the DHR result must be read with MPO status. The edema of kwashiorkor is not fully explained by low albumin, and malnourished patients usually lack several micronutrients at once, so zinc, vitamin A, and protein effects overlap in real patients. Laboratory reference ranges differ between laboratories, and borderline values should be interpreted against the local range and the clinical picture."}],"summary":["The respiratory burst runs in order: G6PD makes NADPH, NADPH oxidase makes superoxide, superoxide dismutase makes H2O2, and myeloperoxidase makes HOCl.","CGD is loss of NADPH oxidase, about two thirds X-linked (gp91phox); ingestion and MPO are normal, but no oxidant forms and granulomas develop.","CGD phagocytes can borrow H2O2 from catalase-negative microbes, so infections come from catalase-positive organisms: S aureus, Aspergillus, B cepacia, Serratia, Nocardia.","DHR shows no fluorescence shift and NBT stays yellow in CGD; X-linked carriers show two populations, and MPO deficiency can falsely lower DHR.","ADA deficiency lets deoxyadenosine accumulate as dATP, which turns off ribonucleotide reductase, starving lymphocytes of deoxynucleotides and causing T, B, and NK negative SCID.","Phospholipase A2 releases arachidonic acid; COX makes PGE2 (fever, pain, vasodilation), PGI2, and thromboxane A2, and 5-lipoxygenase makes LTB4 (chemotaxis) and LTC4, LTD4, LTE4 (bronchoconstriction, permeability).","Granuloma macrophages activate vitamin D without PTH or calcitriol feedback, giving hypercalcemia with high calcitriol and low PTH.","Macrophage TLR2/1 signaling induces 1-alpha-hydroxylase and the vitamin D receptor, and intracellular calcitriol induces cathelicidin (LL-37) against mycobacteria.","Zinc deficiency causes thymic atrophy and weak T cell function; acrodermatitis enteropathica is inherited ZIP4 failure with periorificial and acral dermatitis, alopecia, and diarrhea.","Vitamin A deficiency causes mucosal squamous metaplasia and more lethal measles; protein-energy malnutrition causes thymic atrophy and anergy and is the leading acquired immunodeficiency worldwide."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Inflammation and Repair; Diseases of the Immune System; Environmental and Nutritional Diseases"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Congenital and Acquired Immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Pentose Phosphate Pathway and NADPH; Nucleotide Metabolism; Eicosanoids"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Body Temperature Regulation and Fever; Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Immune System; Disorders of the Parathyroids and Calcium Metabolism"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Staphylococci and the catalase test"}]},"IM-11":{"unit":"IM-11","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-11","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-11-01","title":"Lymph Biochemistry: Chylomicrons and Lymph Protein","subject":"Immune and Lymphatic System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows two things that lymph carries. Intestinal lymph carries dietary fat, which enterocytes package as chylomicrons that travel through lacteals and the thoracic duct into the blood without first passing through the liver. In the blood, lipoprotein lipase strips their triglyceride for muscle and adipose tissue, and the liver clears the remnant. Lymph also returns plasma protein that has leaked out of capillaries, and how much it carries depends on how leaky the local endothelium is. Board questions ask you to trace a chylomicron, name the protein acting at each step, predict what happens when one is missing, and explain why liver and gut lymph are rich in protein while muscle lymph is not.","prerequisites":["Luminal fat digestion by pancreatic lipase and micelle formation by bile salts (GI-6, GI-7)","General structure of lipoproteins: a neutral lipid core inside a phospholipid, free cholesterol, and apolipoprotein shell","Protein synthesis on the rough ER, ER to Golgi transport, and proteasomal degradation of misfolded proteins","Capillary structure and Starling forces (CV-11)","Lymph formation and the course of the thoracic duct (IM-4, IM-9)"],"connections":[{"to":"Products of digestion (GI-7)","how":"Micelles deliver the 2-monoacylglycerol and fatty acids that the enterocyte rebuilds into chylomicron triglyceride."},{"to":"Cardiovascular biochemistry (CV-13)","how":"The chylomicron pathway is the exogenous half of lipoprotein metabolism, and its enzyme and apolipoprotein defects are the type I and type III dyslipidemias."},{"to":"Lymphatic drainage and pleural disease (IM-4, PU-14-D)","how":"Thoracic duct injury spills chylomicron-rich lymph into the pleura as a chylothorax."},{"to":"Nervous system biochemistry and nutrition (NE-14)","how":"Vitamin E travels with chylomicrons and VLDL, so failure of lipoprotein assembly produces a spinocerebellar syndrome."},{"to":"Hepatic disease (GI-13)","how":"Leaky hepatic sinusoids explain why liver lymph is protein rich and why ascites protein differs between cardiac congestion and cirrhosis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Chylomicron","def":"The largest, least dense lipoprotein, made by enterocytes to carry dietary triglyceride, cholesterol, and fat-soluble vitamins, with apoB-48 as its structural protein."},{"term":"Chyle","def":"Milky intestinal lymph made opaque by the chylomicrons it carries after a fatty meal."},{"term":"Lacteal","def":"The blind-ended lymphatic capillary in the core of each intestinal villus."},{"term":"ApoB-48","def":"The intestinal form of apolipoprotein B, about 48% of the length of apoB-100, produced by editing of apoB mRNA."},{"term":"Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP)","def":"An ER luminal protein that transfers triglyceride, cholesteryl ester, and phospholipid onto newly made apoB, required to assemble chylomicrons and VLDL."},{"term":"Lipoprotein lipase (LPL)","def":"An enzyme anchored to the luminal surface of capillary endothelium that hydrolyzes triglyceride in chylomicrons and VLDL to fatty acids and glycerol."},{"term":"ApoC-II","def":"The apolipoprotein, donated by HDL, that is the required activator of lipoprotein lipase."},{"term":"ApoE","def":"The apolipoprotein on chylomicron remnants and IDL that binds hepatic LDL receptors and LRP1 to trigger remnant uptake."},{"term":"Chylomicron remnant","def":"The smaller, cholesterol-enriched particle left after lipoprotein lipase removes most of a chylomicron's triglyceride."},{"term":"Acanthocyte","def":"A red cell with a few irregular, unevenly spaced spiky projections caused by an abnormal membrane lipid composition."},{"term":"Reflection coefficient","def":"A value from 0 to 1 describing how well a capillary wall holds back a solute such as albumin; near 1 in continuous capillaries and near 0 in hepatic sinusoids."},{"term":"Space of Disse","def":"The narrow space between hepatic sinusoidal endothelium and hepatocytes, where interstitial fluid with almost plasma levels of protein forms hepatic lymph."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-11-01-C01","obj":"IM-11.1","title":"Chylomicron assembly in the enterocyte and the lymphatic route to the blood","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Triglyceride cannot cross a membrane intact. In the gut lumen, pancreatic lipase cuts it into 2-monoacylglycerol and free fatty acids, and bile salt micelles carry these to the brush border, where they enter the enterocyte. Inside the cell, fatty acid binding proteins carry long chain fatty acids to the smooth ER. There, acyl-CoA synthetase activates each fatty acid to fatty acyl-CoA, and two acyltransferases (monoacylglycerol acyltransferase, then diacylglycerol acyltransferase) rebuild triglyceride. Absorbed cholesterol is esterified by acyl-CoA:cholesterol acyltransferase (ACAT), and lysophospholipids are reacylated."},{"type":"p","text":"Neutral lipid must be wrapped in an amphipathic shell before it can travel in water. The structural protein of that shell is apoB-48. The APOB gene is transcribed in both liver and intestine, but in the intestine the editing enzyme APOBEC-1 deaminates a single cytidine in the mRNA to uridine, changing a glutamine codon (CAA) into a stop codon (UAA). Translation ends about halfway, giving apoB-48, which lacks the carboxy-terminal region that binds the LDL receptor. The liver does not edit the message and makes full-length apoB-100."},{"type":"p","text":"Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP) works in the ER lumen, loading triglyceride, cholesteryl ester, and phospholipid onto apoB-48 while it is still being translated. Unlipidated apoB misfolds and is degraded by the proteasome, so MTP decides whether any particle forms at all. The nascent particle, which also carries apoA-I and apoA-IV, moves to the Golgi in a specialized prechylomicron transport vesicle (a step that needs the GTPase SAR1B), is glycosylated, and leaves by exocytosis from the basolateral membrane into the lamina propria. A chylomicron is about 85 to 90% triglyceride by mass and roughly 100 to 1,000 nm across."},{"type":"flow","title":"Chylomicron assembly and transport to the blood","steps":["Micelles deliver 2-monoacylglycerol and fatty acids to the enterocyte","Fatty acids activated to acyl-CoA; smooth ER rebuilds triglyceride","MTP loads lipid onto apoB-48 as it is translated","Prechylomicron vesicle carries the particle to the Golgi","Basolateral exocytosis into the lamina propria","Entry into the lacteal, then mesenteric lymph and cisterna chyli","Thoracic duct empties into the left venous angle"],"caption":"Dietary long chain fat leaves the gut packaged as chylomicrons and reaches the systemic veins through lymph, not the portal vein."},{"type":"p","text":"Chylomicrons are too large to pass through the fenestrae and basement membrane of the villus blood capillaries. Lacteals have a discontinuous basement membrane and loose, overlapping endothelial junctions that act as flap valves, so the particles enter lymph instead. They travel through mesenteric lymph vessels and nodes to the cisterna chyli, up the thoracic duct, and into the blood at the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins. The first capillary beds dietary triglyceride meets are therefore in muscle, heart, and adipose tissue, not the liver."},{"type":"table","title":"Two routes for absorbed fat","cols":["Feature","Long chain fatty acids (more than 12 carbons)","Short and medium chain fatty acids (12 carbons or fewer)"],"rows":[["Re-esterified in enterocyte","Yes, to triglyceride","Mostly not"],["Packaging","Chylomicrons with apoB-48","None; travel bound to albumin"],["Route out of the gut","Lacteal and thoracic duct","Portal vein to the liver"],["Depends on MTP and lymph flow","Yes","No"]],"caption":"Shorter fatty acids are water soluble enough to diffuse into portal blood, so they are absorbed even when chylomicron formation or lymph flow fails."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If the thoracic duct were tied off just above the cisterna chyli, what would happen to absorption of a meal containing long chain fat and one containing only medium chain fat?","explain":"Long chain fat would still be packaged into chylomicrons but could not reach the blood by its normal route, so chyle would back up in the intestinal lymphatics and long chain fat absorption would be impaired. Medium chain fatty acids would be absorbed normally because they go straight to portal blood.","check":"The routes table and the paragraph on lacteals."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-11-01-C02","obj":"IM-11.2","title":"Chylomicron breakdown: lipoprotein lipase, apoC-II, and apoE","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A newly secreted (nascent) chylomicron cannot yet be unloaded. In the plasma it receives apoC-II and apoE from HDL and becomes a mature chylomicron. ApoC-II is the essential activator of lipoprotein lipase (LPL); apoC-III, also acquired from HDL, inhibits LPL and slows clearance."},{"type":"p","text":"LPL is made by adipocytes, cardiac and skeletal myocytes, and lactating mammary cells, secreted, and carried to the luminal surface of the neighboring capillary endothelium, where GPIHBP1 and heparan sulfate proteoglycans anchor it. Triglyceride is therefore hydrolyzed inside the capillary. The released fatty acids cross into the adjacent tissue: muscle and heart oxidize them, and adipocytes re-esterify them for storage. Glycerol travels to the liver, which phosphorylates it with glycerol kinase for gluconeogenesis or triglyceride synthesis. Adipocytes have little glycerol kinase, so they make the glycerol 3-phosphate backbone for storage from glucose, which is one reason fat storage depends on insulin-stimulated glucose uptake."},{"type":"p","text":"LPL activity is regulated by tissue and by feeding. Insulin increases LPL in adipose tissue after a meal, directing fat to storage. Cardiac LPL has a much lower Km for triglyceride than adipose LPL, so the heart keeps extracting fatty acids even when plasma triglyceride is low during fasting."},{"type":"flow","title":"Chylomicron breakdown and remnant clearance","steps":["HDL transfers apoC-II and apoE to the nascent chylomicron","ApoC-II activates LPL on muscle, heart, and adipose capillaries","LPL hydrolyzes core triglyceride to fatty acids and glycerol","Fatty acids enter tissue; glycerol goes to the liver","Shrinking particle returns apoC-II to HDL","Cholesterol-rich remnant keeps apoB-48 and apoE","ApoE binds hepatic LDL receptor and LRP1; remnant endocytosed"],"caption":"The particle is emptied of triglyceride in peripheral capillaries, and only the cholesterol-rich remnant reaches the liver."},{"type":"p","text":"As the core shrinks, surface phospholipid and apoC-II return to HDL, leaving a smaller, cholesterol-enriched remnant. Because apoB-48 lacks the LDL receptor binding domain, the liver recognizes the remnant only through apoE, which binds the LDL receptor and LDL receptor-related protein 1 (LRP1) after the particle is captured on heparan sulfate in the space of Disse; hepatic lipase trims it further. The liver thus receives dietary cholesterol and fat-soluble vitamins (vitamin A is stored in hepatic stellate cells), and the incoming cholesterol suppresses hepatic cholesterol synthesis. Chylomicrons are normally cleared within a few hours of a meal, so plasma after a 12 to 14 hour fast contains essentially none."},{"type":"table","title":"When a breakdown step fails","cols":["Defect","What accumulates","Plasma pattern","Key manifestations and mechanism"],"rows":[["LPL, apoC-II, or GPIHBP1 deficiency (type I)","Intact chylomicrons","Fasting triglyceride often above 1,000 mg/dL (reference under 150); creamy top layer on standing","Pancreatitis, eruptive xanthomas, lipemia retinalis, hepatosplenomegaly"],["ApoE2 homozygosity with a second factor (type III)","Chylomicron remnants and IDL","Cholesterol and triglyceride both raised","Palmar and tuberoeruptive xanthomas, premature atherosclerosis; apoE2 binds receptors poorly"]],"caption":"A block at LPL leaves whole chylomicrons in plasma; a block at apoE leaves remnants. Details of these dyslipidemias are in CV-13."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients have fasting plasma that is milky. In one, refrigerated plasma forms a creamy layer over a clear infranatant; in the other, the plasma stays uniformly turbid. Which fits LPL deficiency?","explain":"The first. Chylomicrons are large and light enough to float to the top on standing, leaving clear plasma below because VLDL is not greatly raised. Uniform turbidity means excess VLDL, which is too small to float.","check":"The breakdown failure table and the size of chylomicrons in C01."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-11-01-C03","obj":"IM-11.3","title":"Abetalipoproteinemia: predicting the findings of MTP deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Abetalipoproteinemia is an autosomal recessive loss of function of MTTP, the gene for microsomal triglyceride transfer protein. Every apoB-containing lipoprotein needs MTP to be lipidated, so the defect acts in two organs at once. In the enterocyte, apoB-48 is degraded and no chylomicrons are secreted; in the hepatocyte, apoB-100 is degraded and no VLDL is secreted. Because IDL and LDL are made from VLDL, they never form either."},{"type":"flow","title":"From MTP loss to the clinical picture","steps":["Biallelic MTTP mutations; MTP absent","ApoB-48 and apoB-100 not lipidated and degraded","No chylomicrons, VLDL, IDL, or LDL secreted","Dietary fat trapped in enterocytes; steatorrhea from infancy","Fat-soluble vitamins not delivered, vitamin E worst","Altered plasma lipids remodel red cell membranes into acanthocytes"],"caption":"A single missing transfer protein blocks lipoprotein secretion from both intestine and liver."},{"type":"p","text":"In the gut, fat is absorbed into the enterocyte but cannot leave. Biopsy shows normal villi whose enterocytes are filled with lipid droplets, so the cells look clear or foamy, most strikingly after a fatty meal. Infants present with steatorrhea, abdominal distension, and failure to thrive. Plasma triglyceride is nearly undetectable and total cholesterol very low (often under about 50 mg/dL), apoB is absent, and triglyceride does not rise after a fat load. The liver can also become fatty, because triglyceride it makes cannot be exported as VLDL."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin E deficiency is the most severe vitamin defect because vitamin E depends on lipoproteins at both stages: it enters the blood in chylomicrons, reaches the liver in remnants, and is then loaded by alpha-tocopherol transfer protein onto VLDL for delivery to tissues. Without vitamin E, polyunsaturated membrane lipids undergo uncontrolled peroxidation. Long axons with high metabolic demand are hit first: degeneration of the dorsal columns, spinocerebellar tracts, and peripheral nerves causes loss of proprioception and vibration sense, areflexia, and ataxia, usually emerging in childhood. Photoreceptor damage, with vitamin A deficiency contributing, produces a pigmentary retinopathy resembling retinitis pigmentosa. Low vitamin K can prolong the prothrombin time, and low vitamin D can impair bone mineralization."},{"type":"p","text":"Acanthocytes typically make up a large fraction of red cells. Red cells exchange lipid with plasma, and in this abnormal lipid environment the outer leaflet becomes enriched in sphingomyelin relative to phosphatidylcholine. The outer leaflet expands relative to the inner one, forcing the membrane into irregular spikes. Acanthocytes do not stack into rouleaux, so the erythrocyte sedimentation rate is very low."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing low and absent apoB lipoproteins from chylomicron excess","cols":["Disorder","Defect","Lipoproteins","Distinguishing point"],"rows":[["Abetalipoproteinemia","MTP (recessive)","No chylomicrons, VLDL, or LDL","Parents have normal lipid levels"],["Homozygous familial hypobetalipoproteinemia","Truncated apoB (codominant)","Same as abetalipoproteinemia","Heterozygous parents have low LDL"],["Chylomicron retention disease","SAR1B (recessive)","No chylomicrons; VLDL and LDL present but low","Fasting triglyceride often normal; acanthocytes uncommon"],["Type I hyperchylomicronemia","LPL or apoC-II","Chylomicrons greatly increased","Malabsorption absent; pancreatitis and eruptive xanthomas"]],"caption":"Abetalipoproteinemia and LPL deficiency produce opposite plasma pictures; the MTP and SAR1B defects differ in whether the liver can still export VLDL."},{"type":"example","title":"Reasoning from a lipid panel and smear to the missing protein","text":"A 10-month-old has bulky, greasy stools and poor weight gain. Fasting triglyceride is 5 mg/dL, total cholesterol 35 mg/dL, apoB undetectable, and the smear shows many spiky red cells. Step 1: steatorrhea with very low plasma triglyceride means fat is not leaving the gut, so the block is before the lymph, not at LPL (which would raise triglyceride). Step 2: absent apoB means the liver also fails to secrete VLDL, so the defect is shared by both organs, which points to MTP rather than SAR1B. Step 3: the parents' lipid levels separate recessive MTP deficiency (normal parental LDL) from homozygous hypobetalipoproteinemia (low parental LDL). Step 4: predict progressive ataxia and retinopathy from vitamin E deficiency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-11-01-C04","obj":"IM-11.4","title":"Why hepatic and intestinal lymph carry more protein than muscle lymph","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lymph is interstitial fluid that has entered a lymphatic capillary, so its protein concentration is set by how much plasma protein escapes the blood capillaries of the tissue it drains. Plasma total protein is about 6 to 8 g/dL, mostly albumin (about 69 kDa). How readily that protein crosses depends on endothelial structure, summarized by the reflection coefficient: near 1 when the wall holds protein back, near 0 when protein passes freely."},{"type":"table","title":"Capillary type and lymph protein","cols":["Tissue","Capillary type","Endothelial features","Lymph protein (about)"],"rows":[["Liver","Sinusoidal (discontinuous)","Clusters of open fenestrae without diaphragms, gaps, little or no basement membrane","6 g/dL"],["Intestine","Fenestrated","Diaphragm-covered fenestrae, continuous basement membrane","3 to 4 g/dL"],["Skeletal muscle, skin","Continuous","Tight intercellular clefts, continuous basement membrane, caveolar transcytosis","2 g/dL"],["Thoracic duct (mixed)","Not applicable","Dominated by liver and gut lymph","3 to 5 g/dL"]],"caption":"The leakier the endothelium, the closer lymph protein comes to plasma protein (about 7 g/dL)."},{"type":"p","text":"In the liver, sinusoidal endothelium has open fenestrae, intercellular gaps, and almost no basement membrane, so albumin and even larger proteins reach the space of Disse almost freely. This suits a hepatocyte that must take up protein-bound substances and secrete albumin and clotting factors directly into blood. The fluid in the space of Disse, and the hepatic lymph formed from it, therefore has nearly plasma levels of protein."},{"type":"p","text":"Intestinal mucosal capillaries are fenestrated. Their thin diaphragms and continuous basement membrane favor rapid exchange of water and small absorbed solutes, but they are still more permeable to protein than continuous capillaries, giving an intermediate lymph protein level. In skeletal muscle and skin, continuous capillaries let protein cross only slowly, through a small number of large pores and by vesicular transcytosis, so muscle lymph is protein poor. This low value is normal and is what sustains the oncotic gradient that limits filtration there."},{"type":"p","text":"Because liver and intestine produce about two thirds of all lymph, and that lymph is protein rich, they supply most of the protein carried in thoracic duct lymph. Lymph protein concentration also depends on flow: when filtration rises in a tissue with a high reflection coefficient, water outpaces protein, interstitial protein is washed out, and lymph protein falls, which is one of the safety factors against edema. Where the reflection coefficient is near zero, as in the liver, this oncotic buffer is absent, so a rise in sinusoidal pressure increases protein-rich hepatic lymph formation directly."},{"type":"example","title":"Ascites protein as a window on sinusoidal permeability","text":"Two patients have ascites from raised sinusoidal pressure with a serum-ascites albumin gradient of at least 1.1 g/dL. In right heart failure, back pressure congests normal, leaky sinusoids, so filtrate keeps near-plasma protein and ascites total protein is usually 2.5 g/dL or more. In cirrhosis, collagen is laid down in the space of Disse and the endothelium loses its fenestrae (capillarization), so the wall now holds protein back like a continuous capillary; together with low serum albumin, ascites protein is usually below 2.5 g/dL."},{"type":"stop","predict":"When plasma albumin falls, why does filtration rise in skin and muscle capillaries while filtration in hepatic sinusoids is barely changed by the oncotic drop?","explain":"Continuous capillaries hold albumin back, so the plasma oncotic gradient opposes filtration there and lowering it increases filtration, most visibly in dependent tissues where hydrostatic pressure is highest. Hepatic sinusoids have a reflection coefficient near zero, so oncotic pressure has little influence on hepatic filtration in the first place.","check":"The reflection coefficient and the paragraph on flow and safety factors."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and histology set the route: lacteals, mesenteric nodes, cisterna chyli, and thoracic duct deliver dietary fat to the left venous angle, so muscle, heart, and adipose tissue draw on it before the liver, which receives only the remnant. Endothelial structure, sinusoidal in the liver, fenestrated in the gut, and continuous in muscle, sets how much plasma protein each tissue's lymph returns. The same lymph that carries fat also carries lymphocytes, which is why chyle leaking from the gut wall in intestinal lymphangiectasia or from a torn thoracic duct removes fat, protein, and lymphocytes together and, if the loss persists, causes hypoalbuminemia and lymphopenia."},{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry and pathology explain the defects. One gene gives two proteins through mRNA editing, one transfer protein (MTP) serves both intestine and liver, and one activator (apoC-II) and one ligand (apoE) decide where the particle unloads and where it is cleared. A missing step either prevents chylomicrons from forming (abetalipoproteinemia, with malabsorption, acanthocytes, and vitamin E neuropathy) or prevents their clearance (LPL or apoC-II deficiency, with pancreatitis and eruptive xanthomas). Lymph protein values are approximate, vary with species and measurement, and fall as lymph flow rises, so they describe a ranking of tissues more reliably than exact concentrations."}],"summary":["Enterocytes rebuild absorbed 2-monoacylglycerol and fatty acids into triglyceride in the smooth ER and package it with apoB-48 into chylomicrons.","ApoB-48 comes from the same gene as apoB-100; intestinal APOBEC-1 editing creates a stop codon, removing the LDL receptor binding region.","MTP lipidates apoB as it is made; without it apoB is degraded and neither chylomicrons nor VLDL form.","Chylomicrons are too large for villus blood capillaries, enter lacteals, and reach the blood through the thoracic duct, bypassing the liver; medium chain fatty acids go directly to portal blood.","HDL donates apoC-II and apoE; apoC-II activates endothelial LPL in muscle, heart, and adipose tissue, and insulin raises adipose LPL after meals.","The cholesterol-rich remnant is cleared by the liver through apoE binding to the LDL receptor and LRP1; LPL or apoC-II deficiency leaves intact chylomicrons, and apoE2 leaves remnants.","Abetalipoproteinemia removes all apoB lipoproteins: steatorrhea with lipid-filled enterocytes, very low triglyceride and cholesterol, acanthocytes, and vitamin E deficiency with spinocerebellar ataxia and retinopathy.","Lymph protein mirrors capillary leakiness: liver (sinusoidal, about 6 g/dL) above intestine (fenestrated, about 3 to 4 g/dL) above muscle (continuous, about 2 g/dL).","Liver and gut produce most lymph and most of the protein in thoracic duct lymph; in tissues that hold protein back, rising filtration lowers lymph protein and protects against edema."],"sources":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Dietary Lipid Metabolism"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Nutrient digestion and absorption, section on lipid absorption and chylomicron formation"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Circulatory System, section on types of capillaries"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract, section on malabsorption and abetalipoproteinemia"}]},"IM-12":{"unit":"IM-12","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-12-01","title":"Immunogenetics: Receptor Assembly, Immunodeficiency, and Tolerance Genes","subject":"Immunology (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows single genes through the life of a lymphocyte: the enzymes that assemble antigen receptors, the cytokine and metabolic genes whose loss causes severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID), the cytoskeletal and costimulatory genes behind Wiskott-Aldrich and hyper-IgM syndromes, and the genes that enforce tolerance (AIRE, FOXP3, FAS). Board questions give a lymphocyte profile, an immunoglobulin pattern, a newborn screen, or a pedigree and ask which molecule is broken. Each disorder is predictable once you know what the normal protein does and which cells depend on it, so this chapter teaches the normal step first and then the failure that follows from it.","prerequisites":["Structure of immunoglobulins and the T cell receptor, including variable and constant regions","B and T cell development in bone marrow and thymus, including positive and negative selection (IM-1, IM-5)","Cytokine receptor signaling through JAK and STAT proteins","Purine metabolism and the role of ribonucleotide reductase in DNA synthesis","X-linked recessive and autosomal recessive inheritance patterns"],"connections":[{"to":"Thymus embryology and lymphoid histology (IM-1, IM-2)","how":"An absent thymic shadow and empty lymphoid follicles are the anatomical result of failed lymphocyte development in SCID."},{"to":"Humoral and cell-mediated immunity (IM-5)","how":"CD40 ligand and the immune synapse are the contact points between T cell help and B cell or macrophage responses that fail in hyper-IgM and Wiskott-Aldrich syndromes."},{"to":"Immune biochemistry and nutrition (IM-10)","how":"ADA deficiency is a purine salvage defect in which a toxic nucleotide, dATP, starves lymphocytes of DNA precursors."},{"to":"Congenital immunodeficiency diseases (IM-15-A)","how":"This chapter supplies the genetic and molecular basis that the disease chapter applies to clinical presentation."},{"to":"Endocrine system","how":"Hypoparathyroidism, adrenal insufficiency, and neonatal type 1 diabetes in AIRE and FOXP3 disorders show endocrine organs as targets of failed tolerance."}],"terms":[{"term":"V(D)J recombination","def":"Somatic DNA rearrangement that joins one variable (V), one diversity (D, in some loci), and one joining (J) gene segment to build the variable exon of an antigen receptor."},{"term":"Recombination signal sequence (RSS)","def":"A conserved heptamer and nonamer separated by a 12 or 23 base pair spacer that flanks each V, D, and J segment and directs RAG cutting."},{"term":"Junctional diversity","def":"Variation created at segment joints by nucleotide loss, palindromic (P) nucleotides, and untemplated (N) nucleotides added by TdT."},{"term":"T cell receptor excision circle (TREC)","def":"A circle of DNA cut out during T cell receptor gene rearrangement that does not replicate and therefore marks recent thymic output."},{"term":"Common gamma chain (gamma c)","def":"The IL2RG-encoded receptor subunit shared by the IL-2, IL-4, IL-7, IL-9, IL-15, and IL-21 receptors, which signals through JAK3."},{"term":"Class switch recombination","def":"Deletional recombination between switch regions of the heavy chain locus that replaces the mu constant region with gamma, alpha, or epsilon while keeping antigen specificity."},{"term":"Activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID)","def":"A B cell enzyme that converts cytosine to uracil in DNA, initiating class switch recombination and somatic hypermutation."},{"term":"Central tolerance","def":"Deletion or diversion of self-reactive lymphocytes during development in the thymus or bone marrow."},{"term":"Peripheral tolerance","def":"Control of mature self-reactive lymphocytes outside the primary organs by anergy, regulatory T cells, and apoptosis."},{"term":"Regulatory T cell (Treg)","def":"A CD4+ CD25high FOXP3+ T cell that suppresses activation of other lymphocytes."},{"term":"Death-inducing signaling complex (DISC)","def":"The complex of trimerized Fas, the adaptor FADD, and procaspase 8 that starts extrinsic apoptosis."},{"term":"Double negative T cell","def":"A mature alpha-beta T cell expressing neither CD4 nor CD8, increased in blood in autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-12-01-C01","obj":"IM-12.1","title":"V(D)J recombination, junctional diversity, and RAG deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The genome cannot encode a separate gene for every antigen receptor, so each developing lymphocyte assembles its own by cutting and joining gene segments. The immunoglobulin heavy chain and the T cell receptor (TCR) beta and delta chains are built from variable (V), diversity (D), and joining (J) segments, while immunoglobulin kappa and lambda light chains and TCR alpha and gamma chains use only V and J. The joined unit becomes the variable domain exon, and its joints form the third complementarity determining region (CDR3), the most variable antigen contact."},{"type":"p","text":"Each segment is flanked by a recombination signal sequence (RSS), a heptamer and nonamer separated by a spacer of 12 or 23 base pairs. Recombination occurs only between a 12 spacer and a 23 spacer signal (the 12/23 rule), which prevents useless joins such as V to V. The lymphoid-specific recombinase, RAG1 with RAG2, binds a pair of signals, nicks the DNA, and seals each coding end into a hairpin. Nonhomologous end joining (NHEJ), present in all cells, completes the join: Ku70 and Ku80 bind the ends, DNA-PKcs activates the nuclease Artemis to open the hairpins, and XRCC4 with DNA ligase IV ligates them."},{"type":"flow","title":"Assembling a variable region exon","steps":["RAG1 and RAG2 bind a 12 and a 23 spacer RSS","DNA is cut; coding ends form hairpins","Artemis opens hairpins, often off center (P nucleotides)","Exonuclease trims; TdT adds random N nucleotides","Ku, XRCC4, and DNA ligase IV join coding ends","Excised signal ends close into a circle (TREC in T cells)"],"caption":"RAG starts the reaction and general DNA repair completes it; a defect in either blocks receptor assembly."},{"type":"p","text":"Diversity comes from three sources. Combinatorial diversity is the choice of one V, D, and J from many. Pairing two different chains multiplies it. Junctional diversity is the largest contributor: Artemis opens hairpins off center, creating short palindromic (P) nucleotides, exonucleases remove bases, and terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase (TdT), a template-independent DNA polymerase expressed in early lymphoid precursors, adds untemplated N nucleotides. Many joins shift the reading frame, so the cell often must try its second allele."},{"type":"p","text":"A completed heavy chain or TCR beta chain is tested by pairing it with a surrogate partner to form the pre-B cell receptor or pre-TCR. Signaling from this receptor gives survival, proliferation, and allelic exclusion. A cell that never completes rearrangement never receives the signal and dies. Loss of RAG1 or RAG2 therefore removes every T and B cell, while NK cells, which recognize targets with germline-encoded receptors (killer cell immunoglobulin-like receptors, NKG2D) and depend on IL-15 rather than rearrangement, develop normally. This is autosomal recessive T negative, B negative, NK positive SCID. Artemis or DNA-PKcs deficiency produces the same lymphocyte profile with added sensitivity to ionizing radiation, because NHEJ also repairs radiation-induced breaks. Partial RAG activity yields a few oligoclonal T cells and Omenn syndrome (erythroderma, eosinophilia, high IgE, low or absent B cells)."},{"type":"table","title":"Molecules of V(D)J recombination and the effect of their loss","cols":["Molecule","Normal job","Expression","Result of loss"],"rows":[["RAG1, RAG2","Recognize RSS, cut DNA, form hairpins","Developing B and T cells only","T-B-NK+ SCID; Omenn syndrome if partial"],["Artemis (DCLRE1C)","Opens coding hairpins","All cells","T-B-NK+ SCID with radiosensitivity"],["DNA-PKcs, DNA ligase IV","Activate Artemis; ligate ends (NHEJ)","All cells","Radiosensitive SCID or combined immunodeficiency; ligase IV often with microcephaly"],["TdT","Adds N nucleotides at joints","Early B and T precursors","Less junctional diversity; development proceeds"]],"caption":"Only RAG and TdT are lymphoid specific."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An infant has no T or B cells and normal NK cells. Cultured fibroblasts die after a low dose of radiation. Is the defect in RAG2 or Artemis?","explain":"Artemis. RAG2 exists only in developing lymphocytes, so fibroblasts lacking it repair radiation breaks normally; Artemis belongs to the NHEJ pathway in all cells.","check":"The table of recombination molecules and where each is expressed."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-12-01-C02","obj":"IM-12.2","title":"Classifying SCID by lymphocyte profile and TRECs","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) is a group of genetic disorders in which T cell development fails and B cell function is lost, directly or through absent T cell help. All forms share early-onset persistent thrush, chronic diarrhea with failure to thrive, Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, severe viral infections, disease from live vaccines such as rotavirus and BCG, an absent thymic shadow on chest radiograph, and marked lymphopenia for age. Maternal IgG delays serious bacterial infection for a few months. Which lymphocyte populations are missing predicts the genotype."},{"type":"p","text":"X-linked SCID, from mutation of IL2RG, removes the common gamma chain shared by the receptors for IL-2, IL-4, IL-7, IL-9, IL-15, and IL-21. IL-7 drives proliferation and survival of early thymocytes, so T cells are absent, and IL-15 is required for NK cell development, so NK cells are absent. B cells are present in normal or increased numbers but do not make useful antibody, because they lack T cell help and cannot respond to IL-21. JAK3 is the kinase that carries the gamma chain signal, so autosomal recessive JAK3 deficiency copies this T-B+NK- profile in both sexes. IL-7 receptor alpha deficiency removes only T cells (T-B+NK+), confirming that NK loss in X-linked SCID is due to IL-15."},{"type":"p","text":"Adenosine deaminase (ADA) converts adenosine and deoxyadenosine to inosine and deoxyinosine in purine salvage. Without it, deoxyadenosine accumulates and is phosphorylated to dATP, especially in lymphocytes, which have high kinase and low nucleotidase activity. dATP inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, depleting the other deoxynucleotides needed for DNA synthesis, and deoxyadenosine also inhibits S-adenosylhomocysteine hydrolase. The toxin poisons every lymphocyte lineage, giving the most profound form, T-B-NK-. Because ADA works in all tissues, skeletal changes (flared costochondral junctions), sensorineural hearing loss, and neurologic findings can accompany it."},{"type":"table","title":"SCID genotypes by lymphocyte profile","cols":["Gene","Inheritance","T","B","NK","Mechanism"],"rows":[["IL2RG (common gamma chain)","X-linked recessive","Absent","Present","Absent","No IL-7 or IL-15 signal"],["JAK3","Autosomal recessive","Absent","Present","Absent","Kinase for the common gamma chain"],["IL7R alpha","Autosomal recessive","Absent","Present","Present","No IL-7 signal; IL-15 intact"],["ADA","Autosomal recessive","Absent","Absent","Absent","dATP inhibits ribonucleotide reductase"],["RAG1, RAG2, Artemis","Autosomal recessive","Absent","Absent","Present","No V(D)J recombination"]],"caption":"B cells listed as present are nonfunctional."},{"type":"p","text":"T cell receptor excision circles (TRECs) form when the TCR delta locus is deleted during TCR alpha rearrangement in the thymus. The circle is stable but is not copied when the cell divides, so its concentration in blood reflects how many new naive T cells the thymus is releasing. Newborn screening measures TRECs by PCR on the dried blood spot. Every typical SCID genotype gives very low or absent TRECs, because the shared defect is failure of thymic output; in RAG deficiency the circle cannot form at all, because no TCR gene is ever cut. Thymic aplasia (complete DiGeorge syndrome), prematurity, and secondary T cell loss also lower TRECs, so flow cytometry follows."},{"type":"flow","title":"From newborn screen to genotype","steps":["Low or absent TRECs on newborn screen","Flow cytometry confirms very low naive T cells","B cells present: gamma c, JAK3, or IL7R alpha","Boy with B+ and NK absent: IL2RG first","B absent, NK present: RAG1, RAG2, or Artemis","T, B, and NK all absent: ADA deficiency"],"caption":"The lymphocyte profile narrows the gene list."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading a SCID panel","text":"A 4-month-old boy with thrush and Pneumocystis pneumonia had undetectable TRECs at birth. CD3+ T cells are nearly absent, CD19+ B cells are increased, and NK cells are absent. Step 1: B cells present excludes RAG and ADA defects, which remove B cells too. Step 2: absent NK cells means IL-15 signaling is also lost, so the defect lies in the common gamma chain or JAK3, not IL7R alpha. Step 3: in a boy, IL2RG is most likely, with JAK3 the autosomal alternative. Any IgG he has now is maternal."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-12-01-C03","obj":"IM-12.3","title":"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome and X-linked risk","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome (WAS) is an X-linked recessive disorder caused by mutation of the WAS gene, which encodes the Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome protein (WASp). WASp is expressed only in hematopoietic cells. When a receptor is engaged, the GTPase Cdc42 opens WASp so it can activate the Arp2/3 complex, which nucleates branched actin. Blood cells need this actin to change shape, move, and form contacts with other cells, so WASp loss damages several lineages at once."},{"type":"p","text":"Platelets are produced in reduced numbers, are small (low mean platelet volume), and are removed early in the spleen, giving microthrombocytopenia with petechiae, bloody diarrhea, and bleeding after circumcision in infancy. Platelets in immune thrombocytopenia are normal sized or large. T cells form an unstable immune synapse with antigen-presenting cells and proliferate poorly, and their numbers often fall with age. Impaired leukocyte migration, NK cytotoxicity, and regulatory T cell function explain the eczema, the autoimmunity (especially autoimmune hemolytic anemia and vasculitis), and the increased risk of B cell lymphoma, often associated with Epstein-Barr virus."},{"type":"p","text":"Antibody production is most defective against polysaccharide antigens, the capsules of Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, and Neisseria meningitidis. Serum IgM and isohemagglutinins (IgM antibodies to blood group carbohydrates) are typically low, and patients have recurrent otitis, sinusitis, and pneumonia with encapsulated bacteria. IgG is usually normal, while IgA and IgE are raised, reflecting dysregulated B cell activation and a shift toward Th2 (IL-4 driven) responses. Milder mutations that preserve some WASp cause X-linked thrombocytopenia, with small platelets but little or no immune disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Wiskott-Aldrich findings explained by WASp loss","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Thrombocytopenia, small platelets","Abnormal platelet formation and early splenic clearance"],["Eczema","Immune dysregulation: impaired regulatory T cells and a shift toward Th2 responses"],["Infections with encapsulated bacteria","Poor antibody response to polysaccharide antigens; low IgM"],["High IgA and IgE, normal IgG","Dysregulated B cell activation with Th2 skewing"],["Autoimmunity, lymphoma","Defective regulatory T cells and impaired immune surveillance"]],"caption":"One cytoskeletal regulator, many cell types, many findings."},{"type":"p","text":"Risk calculation follows X-linked recessive rules. A carrier mother has one mutant and one normal X. Each son receives one of her two X chromosomes and the father's Y, so each son has a 50% chance of being affected. Each daughter receives one maternal X and the father's normal X, so she has a 50% chance of being a carrier and is not affected. For a pregnancy of unknown sex, the risk of an affected child is 25%. Carrier women are healthy because their blood cells that express normal WASp have a survival advantage, so their hematopoietic cells show nonrandom (skewed) X inactivation, which can be used to detect carriers."},{"type":"table","title":"Offspring risk in X-linked recessive inheritance","cols":["Parents","Sons","Daughters"],"rows":[["Carrier mother, normal father","50% affected, 50% unaffected","50% carriers, none affected"],["Normal mother, affected father","None affected (receive the Y)","All carriers"],["Woman whose mother is a carrier (her own status unknown)","25% affected (1/2 carrier x 1/2 transmission)","25% carriers"]],"caption":"Sons receive their only X from the mother."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a sister's risk","text":"A woman's brother has Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome inherited from their healthy carrier mother. What is the risk that her son will be affected? Step 1: her mother is a carrier, so the chance that she inherited the mutant X is 1/2. Step 2: if she is a carrier, each son has a 1/2 chance of receiving that X. Step 3: multiply, 1/2 x 1/2 = 1/4, so the risk for a son is 25%. Step 4: for a pregnancy of unknown sex, multiply again by 1/2 for a male child, giving 1/8. Carrier testing changes her risk to either 50% or 0% for each son."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-12-01-C04","obj":"IM-12.4","title":"CD40 ligand deficiency and hyper-IgM syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Every naive B cell first makes IgM (and IgD), because the mu constant region lies closest to the rearranged VDJ exon. Switching to IgG, IgA, or IgE requires class switch recombination, which deletes the DNA between two switch regions and moves a new constant region next to the same VDJ, so specificity is kept while effector function changes. The enzyme that starts it is activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID), which converts cytosine to uracil in switch region DNA. Uracil-DNA glycosylase (UNG) removes the uracils, the abasic sites are cut into double-strand breaks, NHEJ joins the two switch regions, and the intervening DNA is lost. AID also drives somatic hypermutation and affinity maturation in germinal centers."},{"type":"p","text":"B cells express AID only after receiving a contact signal from a helper T cell. When a CD4 T cell recognizes peptide on a B cell's MHC class II, it upregulates CD40 ligand (CD40L, CD154), which binds CD40 on the B cell. CD40 activates NF-kappa B and, together with cytokines that choose the isotype (IL-4 for IgE, TGF-beta for IgA), induces AID, B cell proliferation, and germinal center formation."},{"type":"flow","title":"T cell help for class switching","steps":["B cell presents peptide on MHC class II","CD4 T cell activates and expresses CD40L","CD40L binds CD40 on the B cell","NF-kappa B and cytokines induce AID","AID deaminates cytosine in switch regions","Switch recombination: IgG, IgA, or IgE"],"caption":"X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome breaks the third step, so B cells remain at IgM."},{"type":"p","text":"In X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome, mutation of CD40LG means activated T cells cannot deliver this signal. B cells are normal in number and make IgM, but they cannot switch isotype, form germinal centers, or produce switched memory B cells. Serum IgM is normal or high, while IgG, IgA, and IgE are very low. Lymph nodes are small and lack germinal centers. Recurrent sinopulmonary infections with encapsulated bacteria follow from the lack of opsonizing IgG and mucosal IgA."},{"type":"p","text":"CD40 is also present on macrophages and dendritic cells. CD40L ligation drives dendritic cells to make IL-12, which promotes IFN-gamma production by T helper 1 cells, and it activates macrophages to kill intracellular organisms. Losing this signal makes X-linked hyper-IgM a combined immunodeficiency, which explains Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia in infancy and chronic Cryptosporidium infection, which causes protracted diarrhea and can ascend into the bile ducts to cause sclerosing cholangitis and liver disease. Neutropenia is also common. Autosomal recessive forms from CD40, AID (AICDA), or UNG mutation share the immunoglobulin pattern, but AID and UNG defects are confined to B cells: germinal centers enlarge into lymphoid hyperplasia and opportunistic infections do not occur."},{"type":"table","title":"Forms of hyper-IgM syndrome","cols":["Gene","Inheritance","Cell affected","Germinal centers","Opportunistic infections"],"rows":[["CD40LG","X-linked recessive","Activated CD4 T cell","Absent","Yes (Pneumocystis, Cryptosporidium)"],["CD40","Autosomal recessive","B cell, macrophage, dendritic cell","Absent","Yes"],["AICDA (AID)","Autosomal recessive","B cell","Enlarged (lymphoid hyperplasia)","No"],["UNG","Autosomal recessive","B cell","Enlarged","No"]],"caption":"Defects in the CD40 signal impair both B cells and macrophages; defects inside the switching machinery impair only B cells."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two boys both have high IgM and undetectable IgG. One has Pneumocystis pneumonia and small lymph nodes; the other has large lymph nodes and only bacterial sinusitis. Which gene is likely in each?","explain":"The first has CD40L deficiency: CD40 signaling is lost in macrophages and dendritic cells, permitting opportunistic infection, and no germinal centers form. The second has an AID defect: the B cell receives T cell help but cannot complete switching, so germinal centers enlarge, and macrophage activation is intact.","check":"The hyper-IgM forms table and the role of CD40 on macrophages."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-12-01-C05","obj":"IM-12.5","title":"AIRE, FOXP3, and the two layers of T cell tolerance","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Random receptor assembly inevitably produces T cells that recognize self. Tolerance removes or restrains them at two levels. Central tolerance acts in the thymus, where thymocytes that bind self peptide on MHC too strongly are deleted by apoptosis or diverted into regulatory T cells. Peripheral tolerance controls the self-reactive cells that escape, and regulatory T cells are its main enforcers. AIRE and FOXP3 are the key genes at each level, and their loss produces two distinct autoimmune syndromes."},{"type":"p","text":"Negative selection requires that thymocytes see self antigens from every tissue, including proteins normally made only in the parathyroid, adrenal cortex, pancreatic islets, or skin. The autoimmune regulator (AIRE), a transcriptional regulator in medullary thymic epithelial cells, switches on low-level expression of thousands of these tissue-restricted genes so their peptides can be presented. With AIRE lost, T cells specific for those antigens are neither deleted nor converted to regulatory cells, and they leave the thymus."},{"type":"flow","title":"How AIRE loss leads to endocrine autoimmunity","steps":["AIRE mutation in medullary thymic epithelium","Tissue-restricted self antigens not expressed in thymus","Self-reactive thymocytes escape deletion","T cells attack parathyroid, adrenal, and other tissues","Autoantibodies to type I interferons and IL-17, IL-22"],"caption":"Organ damage is mainly T cell mediated; the cytokine autoantibodies explain the candidiasis."},{"type":"p","text":"The result is autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1 (APS-1), also called APECED (autoimmune polyendocrinopathy, candidiasis, ectodermal dystrophy), inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern and beginning in childhood. The classic triad is chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, and primary adrenal insufficiency, usually appearing in that order; two of the three establish the diagnosis. Candidiasis arises because patients make neutralizing autoantibodies against IL-17A, IL-17F, and IL-22, the cytokines that induce antimicrobial peptides and neutrophil recruitment at skin and mucosal surfaces. Autoantibodies against type I interferons (especially IFN-omega) are found in nearly all patients and are a useful diagnostic marker. Hypoparathyroidism produces hypocalcemia with high phosphate and low PTH, causing tetany and seizures, and adrenal destruction produces low cortisol and aldosterone with hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypotension, and hyperpigmentation from raised ACTH. Enamel hypoplasia, nail dystrophy, type 1 diabetes, primary ovarian insufficiency, vitiligo, and autoimmune hepatitis may follow. APS-2, by contrast, is an adult, polygenic, HLA-associated syndrome of adrenal insufficiency with thyroid disease or type 1 diabetes, without candidiasis."},{"type":"p","text":"FOXP3 is the transcription factor that programs regulatory T cells (Tregs). It drives high expression of CD25, the IL-2 receptor alpha chain, and of CTLA-4. Tregs suppress neighboring T cells by consuming IL-2, by using CTLA-4 to strip CD80 and CD86 from antigen-presenting cells, and by secreting IL-10 and TGF-beta. In IPEX syndrome (immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X-linked), FOXP3 mutation leaves boys without functional Tregs. Autoimmunity begins in the first months of life: severe watery diarrhea from autoimmune enteropathy with villous atrophy, neonatal type 1 diabetes, eczematous dermatitis, thyroiditis, and autoimmune cytopenias, often with high IgE and eosinophilia."},{"type":"table","title":"AIRE versus FOXP3 disorders","cols":["Feature","APS-1 (AIRE)","IPEX (FOXP3)"],"rows":[["Tolerance layer lost","Central (thymic negative selection)","Peripheral (regulatory T cells)"],["Inheritance","Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive"],["Onset","Childhood, organs added over years","First months of life"],["Hallmarks","Candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, adrenal insufficiency","Enteropathy, neonatal type 1 diabetes, dermatitis"],["Laboratory clue","Autoantibodies to interferons and IL-17, IL-22","Absent or nonfunctional FOXP3+ Tregs; high IgE"]],"caption":"Both cause multiorgan endocrine autoimmunity, but timing, inheritance, and candidiasis separate them."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-12-01-C06","obj":"IM-12.6","title":"Fas-mediated apoptosis and autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An immune response must end. After an infection, an antigen-specific clone may have expanded many thousandfold, and most of these cells must die, leaving lymphoid tissue at normal size with only memory cells. Lymphocytes die by two apoptotic routes. The intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway is triggered when antigen and growth factors such as IL-2 are withdrawn, and it accounts for much of the contraction after an infection clears. The extrinsic pathway is triggered by death receptors, of which Fas (CD95) is the key one in lymphocytes."},{"type":"p","text":"Repeated or persistent stimulation through the antigen receptor raises Fas and Fas ligand (FasL, CD178) on activated T cells. FasL, a trimer, binds and clusters Fas on the same or a neighboring cell. The clustered death domains recruit the adaptor FADD, which recruits procaspase 8, forming the death-inducing signaling complex (DISC). Procaspase 8 molecules activate one another, and caspase 8 then activates the executioner caspases 3 and 7 and cleaves BID, which amplifies the signal through the mitochondria. This activation-induced cell death removes chronically stimulated T cells. The cells most often restimulated are those that recognize self antigens, which are always present, so Fas signaling is a main mechanism for deleting self-reactive T cells in the periphery. FasL on helper T cells can also kill B cells that bind self antigen but do not receive the signals needed to survive."},{"type":"flow","title":"Fas-mediated (extrinsic) apoptosis","steps":["Repeated antigen stimulation raises Fas and FasL","FasL trimer clusters Fas","Fas death domains recruit FADD","FADD recruits procaspase 8 (DISC)","Caspase 8 activates caspases 3 and 7; cleaves BID","Lymphocyte dies; clone and self-reactive cells removed"],"caption":"Loss of Fas blocks this pathway at the receptor, so cells that should be deleted accumulate."},{"type":"p","text":"Autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome (ALPS) is most often caused by a heterozygous germline FAS mutation inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with variable penetrance. Because Fas acts as a trimer, one mutant subunit can disable a complex built mostly of normal subunits (a dominant negative effect). Somatic FAS mutations limited to lymphocytes, and mutations in FASLG or caspase 10, cause similar disease. Activated lymphocytes that should have died accumulate, giving chronic nonmalignant lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly beginning in childhood. Self-reactive B cell clones escape deletion and produce autoantibodies against blood cells, causing autoimmune hemolytic anemia, immune thrombocytopenia, and neutropenia, worsened by splenic sequestration."},{"type":"p","text":"A distinctive population, alpha-beta T cells that are CD4 negative and CD8 negative (double negative T cells), expands in blood and lymphoid tissue. These are thought to be chronically activated T cells that lost their coreceptors and would normally have been removed by Fas, and their increase is the main laboratory criterion. Supporting markers include raised soluble FasL, IL-10, and vitamin B12, polyclonal hypergammaglobulinemia, and defective Fas-induced apoptosis of lymphocytes in vitro. Patients have an increased risk of Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma, because cells that escape apoptosis survive long enough to accumulate further mutations."},{"type":"table","title":"ALPS findings and their mechanism","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly","Activated lymphocytes not deleted after stimulation"],["Autoimmune cytopenias","Self-reactive B and T cells escape peripheral deletion"],["Increased double negative alpha-beta T cells","Chronically activated T cells normally removed by Fas"],["Lymphoma","Long-lived lymphocytes accumulate oncogenic mutations"]],"caption":"Every feature follows from lymphocytes that live too long."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Would a defect in the intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway produce the same disease as loss of Fas?","explain":"Not the same. Both allow lymphocytes to accumulate, but the intrinsic pathway mainly removes cells after antigen and IL-2 are withdrawn, while Fas deletes chronically stimulated mature cells, including self-reactive ones. Only loss of Fas signaling gives the ALPS pattern with expanded double negative T cells.","check":"The paragraphs on the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways and on double negative T cells."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"These disorders can be organized along the life of a lymphocyte. Receptor assembly needs a lymphoid recombinase (RAG) and general DNA repair (Artemis, ligase IV), linking immunology to molecular biology and explaining radiosensitivity. Survival and expansion need cytokine signals (common gamma chain, JAK3) and an intact nucleotide supply (ADA), linking immunology to purine biochemistry. Effective help needs physical contact and a working cytoskeleton (CD40L, WASp), and the end of a response needs tolerance and death signals (AIRE, FOXP3, Fas). Anatomy follows function: the thymus and nodes are tiny where cells are never made and enlarged where cells fail to die, as in ALPS and AID deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and genetics apply the same logic. X-linked inheritance explains the male predominance of IL2RG SCID, WAS, CD40L deficiency, and IPEX, while autosomal genes affect both sexes and can mimic them (JAK3 copies X-linked SCID; CD40 copies CD40L deficiency). Lymphocyte profiles and immunoglobulin patterns point to a gene but rarely prove it; hypomorphic mutations give milder or atypical phenotypes, maternal IgG and engrafted maternal T cells can mask a defect in infancy, and confirmation requires molecular testing."}],"summary":["RAG1 and RAG2 cut at recombination signal sequences, NHEJ (Artemis, ligase IV) joins the ends, and TdT adds N nucleotides that diversify CDR3.","RAG deficiency causes T-B-NK+ SCID because only T and B cells need a rearranged receptor; Artemis deficiency adds radiosensitivity.","IL2RG (X-linked) and JAK3 (autosomal recessive) loss gives T-B+NK- SCID: no IL-7 signal for thymocytes and no IL-15 signal for NK cells.","ADA deficiency causes dATP accumulation that inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, removing T, B, and NK cells.","TRECs mark new thymic output and are low in all typical SCID, which is the basis of newborn screening.","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome (X-linked WAS) gives small platelets, eczema, and infections with low IgM, high IgA and IgE; each son of a carrier has a 50% risk.","CD40L deficiency blocks AID induction and class switching, giving normal or high IgM with low IgG, IgA, and IgE, plus Pneumocystis and Cryptosporidium from impaired macrophage activation.","AIRE loss breaks central tolerance (APS-1: candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, adrenal insufficiency); FOXP3 loss removes regulatory T cells (IPEX).","Fas signaling through FADD and caspase 8 deletes chronically stimulated and self-reactive lymphocytes; FAS mutation causes ALPS with lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, autoimmune cytopenias, and double negative T cells."],"sources":[{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Lymphocyte development and antigen receptor gene rearrangement"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunologic tolerance and autoimmunity"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Congenital and acquired immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System, primary immune deficiencies and autoimmunity"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Nucleotide metabolism, degradation of purine nucleotides"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Immune System"}]},"IM-13":{"unit":"IM-13","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-13-01","title":"Innate and Adaptive Immune Activation","subject":"Immune System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the immune system decides to respond, how a response is started and stopped, and what happens when that control fails. It moves from innate recognition and two-signal T cell activation to tumor immunity and tumor escape, then to vaccines, autoimmunity, and the four hypersensitivity types. Board questions in this area usually describe a patient, a lab finding, or a blocked molecule and ask you to name the effector or predict the result. If you can say which signal is present, which cell sees which MHC class, and whether antibody or T cells cause the injury, most of these questions become straightforward.","prerequisites":["Cells of the immune system and primary and secondary lymphoid organs","MHC class I and class II structure and antigen processing pathways","Antibody isotypes and their functions, including complement activation","T and B cell development, V(D)J recombination, and thymic selection","Cytokine families and helper T cell subsets (Th1, Th2, Th17, Treg)"],"connections":[{"to":"Microbiology","how":"Microbial molecules such as LPS, flagellin, and viral nucleic acids are the patterns innate receptors detect, and some microbes trigger autoimmunity by mimicry."},{"to":"Oncology and pathology","how":"Tumor growth reflects selection of cells that escape CD8 T cell and NK cell killing, the basis of checkpoint biology."},{"to":"Renal pathology","how":"Glomerulonephritis is classified by whether antibody binds the basement membrane (type II) or immune complexes deposit (type III)."},{"to":"Neurology and cardiology","how":"Guillain-Barre syndrome and rheumatic heart disease are the classic examples of molecular mimicry after infection."},{"to":"Hematology","how":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia, immune thrombocytopenia, and transfusion reactions are type II reactions against blood cells."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pattern recognition receptor (PRR)","def":"A germline-encoded receptor that detects conserved microbial structures or molecules released by damaged cells."},{"term":"PAMP and DAMP","def":"Pathogen-associated and damage-associated molecular patterns, the microbial and host danger molecules that PRRs detect."},{"term":"Clonal expansion","def":"Proliferation of the few lymphocytes whose receptors bind an antigen, producing many effector cells with the same specificity."},{"term":"Costimulation","def":"A second activating signal, mainly CD28 binding B7 (CD80 or CD86), required with antigen recognition to activate a naive T cell."},{"term":"Anergy","def":"A state of functional unresponsiveness in a lymphocyte that has recognized antigen without adequate costimulation."},{"term":"Immune checkpoint","def":"An inhibitory receptor pathway, such as CTLA-4 or PD-1, that limits T cell activation and protects tissues from immune damage."},{"term":"Cross-presentation","def":"Presentation of peptides from ingested exogenous antigen on MHC class I by dendritic cells, allowing priming of CD8 T cells."},{"term":"Missing self","def":"NK cell recognition of a cell that has lost MHC class I, which removes the inhibitory signal that normally prevents killing."},{"term":"Toxoid","def":"A bacterial exotoxin chemically inactivated so it is no longer toxic but still induces neutralizing antibody."},{"term":"Molecular mimicry","def":"Cross-reaction of an immune response raised against a microbial epitope with a structurally similar self antigen."},{"term":"Immune complex","def":"Antigen bound to antibody, which can activate complement and deposit in vessel walls, glomeruli, and joints."},{"term":"Hypersensitivity","def":"An immune response, against foreign or self antigen, that is excessive or misdirected and injures the host."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-13-01-C01","obj":"IM-13.1","title":"Innate and adaptive immunity compared","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Innate immunity is the defense present before any infection. Its receptors are encoded in the germline, so every macrophage or neutrophil carries the same set, aimed at structures microbes need and cannot easily change, such as lipopolysaccharide (LPS) of gram-negative walls, flagellin, and viral double-stranded RNA. Because no selection or expansion is needed, innate cells respond within minutes to hours. The same receptors also detect damage-associated molecules from dead host cells, which is why sterile injury causes inflammation."},{"type":"table","title":"Major pattern recognition receptors","cols":["Receptor","Location","Ligand","Main output"],"rows":[["TLR4 (with MD-2 and CD14)","Cell surface","LPS (lipid A)","NF-kB: TNF, IL-1, IL-6"],["TLR2 (with TLR1 or TLR6)","Cell surface","Lipoteichoic acid, lipoproteins","NF-kB cytokines"],["TLR5","Cell surface","Flagellin","NF-kB cytokines"],["TLR3, TLR7, TLR8, TLR9","Endosome","dsRNA, ssRNA, unmethylated CpG DNA","Type I interferon and cytokines"],["NOD1, NOD2","Cytosol","Peptidoglycan fragments","NF-kB cytokines"],["NLRP3 inflammasome","Cytosol","Crystals, ATP, K+ efflux","Caspase-1 cleaves pro-IL-1 beta and pro-IL-18"]],"caption":"Germline receptors grouped by location; nucleic acid sensors sit in endosomes, where ingested microbes are degraded."},{"type":"p","text":"The innate effectors are neutrophils, which arrive first and phagocytose; macrophages, which phagocytose, kill, and release TNF, IL-1, and IL-12; natural killer (NK) cells, which kill infected or stressed cells without prior sensitization; dendritic cells; and complement. Complement can be activated by antibody (classical pathway), by mannose-binding lectin on microbial sugars (lectin pathway), or spontaneously on surfaces that lack host regulatory proteins (alternative pathway). All three converge on C3: C3b opsonizes, C5a recruits neutrophils, and C5b to C9 form the membrane attack complex."},{"type":"p","text":"Adaptive immunity uses T and B cells whose receptors are assembled by V(D)J recombination (RAG1 and RAG2) with random nucleotide addition (TdT). This creates an enormous repertoire, but each specificity is rare, about 1 naive T cell in 10^5 to 10^6 for a given antigen. A first response therefore needs days of clonal expansion. Some expanded cells persist as memory cells, so a second exposure produces a faster, larger response, with antibody that has class switched and undergone affinity maturation."},{"type":"table","title":"Innate versus adaptive immunity","cols":["Feature","Innate","Adaptive"],"rows":[["Receptors","Germline PRRs, identical within a cell type","Rearranged TCR and BCR, unique to each clone"],["Target","Shared microbial and danger patterns","A single epitope"],["Speed","Minutes to hours","Days on first exposure"],["Memory","None in the classic sense","Yes; faster, stronger secondary response"],["Self discrimination","Built in by receptor choice","Learned by selection and tolerance"],["Main components","Neutrophils, macrophages, NK cells, dendritic cells, complement","T cells, B cells, antibody"]],"caption":"The two systems differ in how receptors are generated, which sets their speed, breadth, and memory."},{"type":"p","text":"The two systems are linked rather than separate. When a dendritic cell's TLRs are engaged, it raises B7 and secretes cytokines, supplying the costimulatory and polarizing signals naive T cells need (next section); IL-12, for example, favors Th1. In the other direction, antibodies activate complement and coat microbes for phagocytes, and Th1 cytokines such as IFN-gamma activate macrophages. Innate-like lymphocytes (gamma-delta T cells, NKT cells, and B-1 cells) have limited receptor diversity and sit between the two arms."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-13-01-C02","obj":"IM-13.2","title":"Two-signal T cell activation, anergy, and inhibitory checkpoints","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A naive T cell needs two signals from an antigen-presenting cell (APC) to become activated. Signal 1 is the T cell receptor (TCR) binding its peptide on MHC, with CD4 binding MHC class II or CD8 binding MHC class I; it supplies specificity. Signal 2 is CD28 on the T cell binding B7-1 (CD80) or B7-2 (CD86) on the APC; it confirms danger, because APCs express high B7 mainly after microbial products or cytokines have stimulated them. Cytokines from the APC, sometimes called signal 3, then steer the cell toward a Th1, Th2, Th17, or other fate."},{"type":"flow","title":"Activation of a naive T cell","steps":["Dendritic cell senses microbe through TLRs","Dendritic cell raises B7 and migrates to lymph node","TCR binds peptide on MHC (signal 1)","CD28 binds B7 (signal 2)","IL-2 and IL-2 receptor alpha (CD25) induced","Survival proteins rise; clonal expansion","Effector and memory cells form"],"caption":"Both signals together drive IL-2 production, survival, and proliferation."},{"type":"p","text":"CD28 signaling amplifies TCR signals, raises IL-2 synthesis, induces anti-apoptotic proteins such as Bcl-xL, and increases glucose uptake to fuel division. When a T cell receives signal 1 without signal 2, as when it meets a self peptide on a resting APC or a tissue cell that lacks B7, it makes little IL-2 and enters anergy, a lasting unresponsive state. Anergy is a major mechanism of peripheral tolerance and explains why most self-reactive T cells that escape the thymus cause no disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Activation also switches on brakes. CTLA-4 is induced on activated T cells and expressed constitutively on regulatory T cells (Tregs). It binds B7 with much higher affinity than CD28 does, so it outcompetes CD28 and can strip B7 from APCs, limiting priming mainly in lymphoid organs. PD-1 is expressed on activated and chronically stimulated T cells; when it binds PD-L1 (inducible on many tissue and tumor cells, often by IFN-gamma) or PD-L2, it recruits the phosphatase SHP-2, which dephosphorylates TCR and CD28 signaling molecules. PD-1 therefore acts mainly in peripheral tissues, damping effector cells already at work."},{"type":"table","title":"Costimulatory and inhibitory receptors on T cells","cols":["Receptor","Ligand","Effect","Result of loss or blockade"],"rows":[["CD28","B7-1, B7-2 on APC","Activates; IL-2 and survival","No signal 2: anergy, weak responses"],["CTLA-4","B7-1, B7-2 (higher affinity)","Inhibits priming; Treg function","Early, severe multiorgan autoimmunity"],["PD-1","PD-L1, PD-L2 on tissue and tumor cells","Inhibits effector T cells in tissues","Organ-specific autoimmunity; restored antitumor killing"]],"caption":"CD28 and CTLA-4 share B7; PD-1 uses a separate ligand pair."},{"type":"p","text":"Antibodies that block CTLA-4 or PD-1 are used against cancer, and their effects confirm the physiology: tumor-specific T cells regain function, while self-reactive T cells also escape control, producing immune-related colitis, hepatitis, pneumonitis, thyroiditis, and hypophysitis. Conversely, a soluble CTLA-4 fusion protein binds B7 and denies CD28 its ligand, which suppresses T cell activation."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A CD8 T cell recognizes a self peptide on MHC class I of a thyroid follicular cell, which has no B7. What happens to that T cell, and what would change if the gland were inflamed by a viral infection?","explain":"Signal 1 without signal 2 produces anergy. Infection activates dendritic cells that carry thyroid antigen, so they raise B7 and present the same self peptide with both signals, activating cells that would otherwise become anergic.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on anergy and the activation flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-13-01-C03","obj":"IM-13.3","title":"Antitumor immunity and tumor escape","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Tumors are recognized because they display antigens normal cells do not. These include neoantigens from mutated proteins, viral oncoproteins such as HPV E6 and E7 or EBV proteins, and normal proteins that are overexpressed or reexpressed, such as cancer-testis antigens. Tumor cells present their own cytosolic proteins on MHC class I, which is what CD8 cytotoxic T cells read."},{"type":"p","text":"Most tumor cells lack B7 and do not travel to lymph nodes, so they cannot prime naive CD8 T cells themselves. Dendritic cells ingest dying tumor cells and cross-present tumor peptides on MHC class I, while presenting others on MHC class II to CD4 T cells that provide help. Primed CD8 T cells enter the tumor, recognize the same peptide on MHC class I, and kill by releasing perforin and granzymes or by Fas ligand binding Fas, both of which trigger apoptosis. IFN-gamma from T cells raises MHC expression on the tumor and activates macrophages."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Antitumor immune cycle","steps":["Tumor cells die and release antigen","Dendritic cells cross-present antigen in lymph node","Naive CD8 T cells primed with CD4 help","Effector CD8 T cells traffic into tumor","T cells recognize peptide on MHC class I","Perforin, granzymes, and Fas ligand kill tumor cells","More tumor antigen released"],"caption":"Each round of killing releases more antigen and can broaden the response."},{"type":"p","text":"NK cells provide a second, independent check. Their inhibitory receptors (killer cell immunoglobulin-like receptors in humans) bind self MHC class I and block killing. Activating receptors such as NKG2D bind stress ligands that transformed or infected cells display. A cell that loses MHC class I or gains stress ligands tips the balance toward killing, the missing-self principle. NK cells also kill antibody-coated cells through the Fc receptor CD16 (antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity) and are activated by IL-12, IL-15, and type I interferon."},{"type":"table","title":"CD8 T cells and NK cells in tumor killing","cols":["Feature","CD8 T cell","NK cell"],"rows":[["Recognition","Specific peptide on MHC class I","Loss of MHC class I, stress ligands, IgG via CD16"],["Prior priming","Required (dendritic cell cross-presentation)","Not required"],["Effect of MHC class I loss","Tumor becomes invisible","Inhibition removed; killing increases"],["Killing mechanism","Perforin, granzymes, Fas ligand","Perforin, granzymes, Fas ligand"]],"caption":"The two killers are complementary: losing MHC class I escapes one and exposes the tumor to the other."},{"type":"p","text":"Tumors that grow to clinical size have been selected for escape. One route is loss of antigen presentation: downregulation of MHC class I, mutation of beta-2 microglobulin, or loss of the peptide transporter TAP. Complete loss should invite NK attack, so successful tumors often also shed NKG2D ligands or keep partial class I. A second route is active suppression: PD-L1 expression, often induced by IFN-gamma from the very T cells attacking the tumor, recruitment of Tregs and myeloid suppressor cells, and secretion of TGF-beta and IL-10."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: why PD-1 blockade can fail","text":"A melanoma responds to PD-1 blockade, then regrows. The regrowing cells carry an inactivating beta-2 microglobulin mutation. Step 1: PD-1 blockade works only if CD8 T cells can see tumor peptide on MHC class I. Step 2: MHC class I heavy chains need beta-2 microglobulin to fold and reach the surface, so these cells display no class I. Step 3: CD8 T cells, even with the PD-1 brake removed, now have no signal 1 on the tumor, so blockade loses its effect. Step 4: NK cells are no longer inhibited by class I, so NK killing becomes the remaining cytotoxic route, unless the tumor has also suppressed NK activating signals."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-13-01-C04","obj":"IM-13.4","title":"Vaccine types and the immunity each induces","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A vaccine works by producing memory cells and, for many pathogens, long-lived plasma cells that keep protective antibody in the blood. Which arm of adaptive immunity it engages depends on where the antigen ends up inside the host. Antigen made inside a host cell enters the cytosolic pathway and is shown on MHC class I to CD8 T cells; antigen taken up from outside is shown mainly on MHC class II to CD4 T cells, which help B cells make antibody."},{"type":"p","text":"Live attenuated vaccines (measles, mumps, rubella, varicella, yellow fever; also intranasal influenza, rotavirus, oral polio, and BCG) contain organisms weakened so they replicate briefly without causing disease. Because they infect host cells, their proteins reach MHC class I, so these vaccines induce CD8 T cells as well as antibody, with long-lasting protection. The same replication is the hazard: in a person with severe T cell deficiency, the organism is not contained and can cause disseminated infection, which is why live vaccines are avoided in severe immunodeficiency and, because of theoretical fetal risk, in pregnancy."},{"type":"table","title":"Vaccine types","cols":["Type","Examples","Main response","Key point"],"rows":[["Live attenuated","MMR, varicella, yellow fever","CD8 and CD4 T cells plus antibody","Strongest, durable; avoided in severe immunodeficiency and pregnancy"],["Inactivated (killed)","Rabies, hepatitis A, injected polio, injected influenza","Mainly antibody","Cannot replicate; boosters needed"],["Subunit or recombinant","Hepatitis B surface antigen, HPV, acellular pertussis","Antibody","Selected proteins; safe in immunodeficiency, though responses may be weaker"],["Toxoid","Tetanus, diphtheria","Neutralizing antitoxin IgG","Blocks toxin, not colonization"],["Polysaccharide-protein conjugate","Hib, pneumococcal, meningococcal","T-dependent antibody with memory","Effective in infants under 2 years"]],"caption":"Nonliving vaccines are presented as exogenous antigen on MHC class II and rely on antibody and boosters; adjuvants are added to supply innate signals."},{"type":"p","text":"Inactivated, subunit, and toxoid vaccines cannot replicate, so they are safe when T cell function is poor, but they deliver a fixed dose of exogenous antigen. Responses are mostly antibody, need adjuvants such as aluminum salts to provide innate signals, and need boosters. A toxoid raises IgG that neutralizes the exotoxin, so it prevents disease without necessarily preventing carriage of the organism."},{"type":"p","text":"Encapsulated bacteria pose a special problem. Pure polysaccharide cannot be loaded onto MHC, so it activates B cells only by cross-linking their receptors, a T-independent response that yields mostly IgM, little class switching, and weak memory. Infants under about 2 years respond poorly to such antigens because their marginal zone B cells are immature. Linking the polysaccharide to a carrier protein solves this."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a conjugate vaccine creates T cell help","steps":["B cell receptor binds the capsular polysaccharide","B cell internalizes the whole polysaccharide-protein conjugate","Carrier peptides loaded on MHC class II","Carrier-specific helper T cell binds and gives CD40L and cytokines","Class switching to IgG and affinity maturation","Memory B cells and plasma cells against the polysaccharide"],"caption":"The antibody is against the sugar, but the help comes from T cells that recognize the protein."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An 8-month-old receives a plain pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine and, separately, a conjugate pneumococcal vaccine. Which produces protective IgG and memory, and why?","explain":"Only the conjugate. Plain polysaccharide gives a T-independent response that infants handle poorly, while the conjugate lets polysaccharide-specific B cells present carrier peptides to helper T cells and receive help for class switching and memory.","check":"Compare with the conjugate flow and the paragraph before it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-13-01-C05","obj":"IM-13.5","title":"Mechanisms of autoimmunity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Self-tolerance is built in layers. Central tolerance occurs in the thymus and bone marrow: developing T cells that bind self peptide on MHC strongly are deleted (negative selection) or become Tregs, and self-reactive B cells edit their receptors or die. The transcription factor AIRE lets thymic epithelial cells express tissue-restricted proteins such as insulin, so T cells against them can be removed. Peripheral tolerance controls the cells that escape: anergy from signal 1 without costimulation, suppression by FOXP3-positive Tregs through CTLA-4, IL-10, and TGF-beta, and deletion by Fas-Fas ligand apoptosis after repeated stimulation."},{"type":"table","title":"Single-gene failures of tolerance","cols":["Defect","Tolerance step lost","Result"],"rows":[["AIRE","Central deletion of tissue-specific T cells","APS-1: chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, adrenal insufficiency"],["FOXP3","Treg development","IPEX: enteropathy, early type 1 diabetes, eczema"],["Fas or Fas ligand","Deletion of activated lymphocytes","ALPS: lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, autoimmune cytopenias"]],"caption":"Rare monogenic diseases show what each tolerance mechanism normally prevents."},{"type":"p","text":"Common autoimmune diseases are not single-gene disorders. They usually require inherited susceptibility plus an environmental trigger such as infection or tissue injury. The strongest genetic links are to HLA (human MHC) alleles, because the shape of the peptide-binding groove decides which self peptides are presented and how well. HLA-DQ2 and DQ8, for example, bind gliadin peptides that tissue transglutaminase has deamidated, presenting them strongly to CD4 T cells in celiac disease. An HLA allele raises risk but rarely causes disease alone; most carriers stay healthy."},{"type":"table","title":"Classic HLA associations","cols":["HLA allele","Disease"],"rows":[["B27","Ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis, psoriatic arthritis"],["DQ2, DQ8","Celiac disease"],["DR3, DR4","Type 1 diabetes"],["DR4","Rheumatoid arthritis"],["DR2 (DR15)","Multiple sclerosis, Goodpasture syndrome"]],"caption":"Association raises relative risk; it is neither necessary nor sufficient for disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Infection can break tolerance in two ways. Its inflammation activates APCs and raises B7, so self-reactive cells that were ignored now receive costimulation (bystander activation). In molecular mimicry, lymphocytes made against a microbial epitope cross-react with a similar self structure. After group A streptococcal pharyngitis, antibodies and T cells against the M protein cross-react with cardiac myosin and valve tissue, producing acute rheumatic fever about 2 to 4 weeks later, with carditis that most often scars the mitral valve. After Campylobacter jejuni enteritis, antibodies against its lipooligosaccharide bind gangliosides such as GM1 on peripheral nerve, fix complement, injure the nerve, and produce Guillain-Barre syndrome: ascending symmetric weakness with lost reflexes and high CSF protein with a normal cell count. Other mechanisms include release of antigens normally hidden from the immune system, as in sympathetic ophthalmia after eye injury, and epitope spreading, in which tissue damage exposes new self antigens."},{"type":"flow","title":"Molecular mimicry after infection","steps":["Infection supplies microbial antigen and inflammation","B and T cells respond to a microbial epitope","Epitope resembles a self structure","Antibody or T cells bind the self tissue","Complement and inflammation injure the tissue"],"caption":"The delay of 1 to several weeks after infection reflects the time to build the adaptive response."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-13-01-C06","obj":"IM-13.6","title":"The four types of hypersensitivity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypersensitivity reactions are classified by the effector that causes injury, not by the antigen or organ. The same organism, group A streptococcus, can cause type II disease (rheumatic fever) or type III disease (poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis)."},{"type":"p","text":"Type I (immediate) hypersensitivity needs prior sensitization. Th2 cells release IL-4 and IL-13, which switch B cells to IgE, and IgE binds the high-affinity receptor FcepsilonRI on mast cells and basophils. On re-exposure, antigen cross-links bound IgE and the cells degranulate within minutes, releasing preformed histamine and tryptase, then newly made leukotrienes and prostaglandin D2. These cause vasodilation, leaky vessels, bronchoconstriction, and mucus. A late phase about 2 to 24 hours later brings eosinophils and further inflammation. Anaphylaxis, allergic asthma, urticaria, and allergic rhinitis are examples, and a raised serum tryptase marks mast cell activation."},{"type":"flow","title":"Type I hypersensitivity","steps":["First exposure: Th2 cells drive IgE class switch","IgE binds FcepsilonRI on mast cells","Re-exposure: antigen cross-links bound IgE","Degranulation: histamine and tryptase within minutes","Leukotrienes and prostaglandins synthesized","Late phase with eosinophils hours later"],"caption":"Sensitization is silent; symptoms appear only after re-exposure."},{"type":"p","text":"Type II reactions use IgG or IgM bound to antigens fixed on cells or matrix. The antibody can mark cells for phagocytosis or complement lysis (autoimmune hemolytic anemia, ABO transfusion reaction, immune thrombocytopenia), recruit neutrophils that damage matrix (Goodpasture syndrome against type IV collagen, with linear immunofluorescence), or alter receptor function without inflammation (stimulating TSH receptor antibodies in Graves disease, blocking acetylcholine receptor antibodies in myasthenia gravis). The direct Coombs test detects antibody on red cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Type III reactions are caused by circulating antigen-antibody complexes that deposit where blood is filtered at pressure, in glomeruli, small vessels, and synovium. Complement is activated, so serum C3 falls, and C5a draws neutrophils that cause vasculitis, glomerulonephritis, and arthritis with granular deposits. Serum sickness appears about 1 to 2 weeks after a foreign protein, when antibody has formed, while the local Arthus reaction appears within hours in a previously sensitized person."},{"type":"p","text":"Type IV reactions involve no antibody. Sensitized CD4 Th1 cells release IFN-gamma that activates macrophages, Th17 cells recruit neutrophils, and CD8 T cells kill directly. Recruiting and activating these cells takes time, so reactions peak at 48 to 72 hours, as in the tuberculin skin test, contact dermatitis, granulomas, and acute cellular rejection."},{"type":"table","title":"Classifying hypersensitivity","cols":["Type","Effector","Timing","Examples"],"rows":[["I","IgE, mast cells, basophils","Minutes; late phase hours","Anaphylaxis, allergic asthma, urticaria, latex allergy"],["II","IgG or IgM against fixed cell or matrix antigen","Minutes to days","Hemolytic anemia, Goodpasture, rheumatic fever, Graves, myasthenia gravis"],["III","Immune complexes, complement, neutrophils","About 1 to 2 weeks; Arthus hours","SLE, poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, serum sickness, polyarteritis nodosa"],["IV","CD4 Th1 or CD8 T cells, macrophages","48 to 72 hours","PPD test, poison ivy or nickel dermatitis, granulomas"]],"caption":"Ask whether antibody is involved, which isotype, and whether the antigen is fixed in tissue or circulating."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: classifying a reaction","text":"Ten days after receiving horse-derived antivenom, a man develops fever, rash, joint pain, and proteinuria, with low serum C3. Step 1: the delay rules out type I, which occurs in minutes. Step 2: low C3 means complement consumption, which requires antibody, so this is not type IV. Step 3: the antigen, horse protein, circulates rather than sitting fixed in one tissue, and injury appears at several filtering sites at once. Step 4: complexes of antibody with soluble antigen that deposit are type III, here serum sickness."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients have glomerulonephritis. One has linear IgG along the basement membrane; the other has granular, lumpy deposits and low C3 after a skin infection. Which type is each?","explain":"Linear staining means antibody binds a fixed basement membrane antigen (type II, Goodpasture). Granular deposits with complement consumption mean immune complexes deposited from the blood (type III, poststreptococcal).","check":"Compare with the type II and type III paragraphs."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Activation rules link the whole chapter. Innate recognition by PRRs decides whether dendritic cells raise B7, and B7 decides whether a T cell is activated or made anergic. The same logic explains why live vaccines and adjuvants work, why infection can break tolerance through bystander activation or mimicry, and why removing CTLA-4 or PD-1 helps tumor immunity at the price of autoimmunity. Microbiology supplies the triggers, from LPS to streptococcal M protein and Campylobacter lipooligosaccharide, and pathology shows the results as granulomas, vasculitis, and glomerular deposits."},{"type":"p","text":"The Gell and Coombs types are a teaching scheme with limits. Many diseases mix mechanisms: SLE has type II cytopenias and type III nephritis, and type 1 diabetes involves autoantibodies even though T cells cause the beta cell loss. Classify by the effector that causes the injury in the scenario given, and use clues such as timing, complement levels, and immunofluorescence pattern to decide."}],"summary":["Innate immunity uses germline PRRs such as TLR4 (LPS) and acts in minutes without memory; adaptive immunity uses rearranged TCRs and BCRs, needs days of clonal expansion, and forms memory.","Innate signals start adaptive immunity: TLR engagement makes dendritic cells raise B7 and secrete polarizing cytokines.","Naive T cells need TCR binding peptide on MHC (signal 1) and CD28 binding B7 (signal 2); signal 1 alone produces anergy.","CTLA-4 outcompetes CD28 for B7 during priming, and PD-1 binding PD-L1 recruits SHP-2 to damp effector T cells in tissues; blocking either boosts antitumor immunity and causes autoimmunity.","CD8 T cells kill tumor cells presenting peptide on MHC class I after dendritic cell cross-presentation; NK cells kill cells that lose class I or display stress ligands.","Tumors escape by losing MHC class I, beta-2 microglobulin, or TAP, by expressing PD-L1, and by building a suppressive environment with Tregs and TGF-beta.","Live attenuated vaccines enter the MHC class I pathway and give cellular and humoral immunity but are avoided in severe immunodeficiency and pregnancy; nonliving vaccines give mainly antibody and need boosters.","Conjugating polysaccharide to protein lets B cells obtain T cell help, so infants under 2 years make class-switched antibody and memory.","Autoimmunity follows failed central (AIRE) or peripheral (anergy, FOXP3 Tregs, Fas) tolerance, usually with HLA susceptibility plus a trigger such as M protein or C. jejuni mimicry.","Type I is IgE and mast cells in minutes, type II is antibody against fixed antigen, type III is immune complexes with low C3, and type IV is T cells peaking at 48 to 72 hours."],"sources":[{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Innate immunity; activation of T lymphocytes"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunologic tolerance and autoimmunity; immunity to tumors"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity disorders"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System, sections on hypersensitivity and autoimmunity"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Immunology"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the immune system"}]},"IM-14":{"unit":"IM-14","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-14","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-14-01","title":"Systemic Pathogens: Structure, Tropism, and Spread","subject":"Immunology and Microbiology (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers pathogens that spread from a mucosal entry point to cause systemic disease: parvovirus B19, Haemophilus influenzae type b, influenza, measles, mumps, rubella, the herpesviruses of mononucleosis and roseola, Streptococcus pyogenes, and Toxoplasma gondii. For each organism the chapter links structure, receptor, route of spread, and virulence factors to the disease they produce. Board questions rarely ask for a bare fact; they describe a patient or a laboratory finding and expect you to name the receptor, the genome type, or the toxin that explains it. If you can say why each organism reaches the tissue it damages and how that damage happens, you can reason through most of these questions.","prerequisites":["Viral classification by genome (DNA or RNA, single or double stranded, positive or negative sense) and envelope","Gram stain, cell wall structure, and basic bacterial identification tests","Complement activation, opsonization, and phagocytosis","Antigen presentation on MHC class I and II and T cell receptor recognition","Erythropoiesis, red cell life span, and the reticulocyte count"],"connections":[{"to":"Hematology","how":"Parvovirus B19 halts erythropoiesis, so its effect depends on red cell survival, linking it to hemolytic anemias and fetal hydrops."},{"to":"Hypersensitivity and autoimmunity","how":"M protein mimicry explains acute rheumatic fever, and streptococcal superantigens show how polyclonal T cell activation causes shock."},{"to":"Embryology and congenital infection","how":"Rubella, parvovirus B19, and Toxoplasma cross the placenta, and the timing of infection predicts which developing tissues are damaged."},{"to":"Immunodeficiency and HIV","how":"Loss of T cell control lets latent EBV drive lymphoproliferation and lets Toxoplasma cysts reactivate in the brain."},{"to":"Respiratory and nervous system pathology","how":"Influenza and measles damage airway defenses that set up bacterial pneumonia, and Hib, mumps, and measles reach the meninges and brain."}],"terms":[{"term":"Tropism","def":"The restriction of a pathogen to particular cells or tissues, set mainly by receptor distribution and the cell's ability to support replication."},{"term":"Negative-sense RNA","def":"A viral RNA genome complementary to mRNA, which must be transcribed by a polymerase carried in the virion before proteins can be made."},{"term":"Positive-sense RNA","def":"A viral RNA genome with the same sense as mRNA, which ribosomes can translate directly on entry."},{"term":"Syncytium","def":"A multinucleated cell formed when viral fusion proteins on an infected cell fuse it with neighboring cells."},{"term":"Antigenic drift","def":"Gradual change in influenza surface antigens from point mutations, producing strains that partly escape existing immunity."},{"term":"Antigenic shift","def":"Abrupt replacement of an influenza A hemagglutinin or neuraminidase subtype by reassortment of genome segments between strains."},{"term":"Latency","def":"A persistent infection in which the viral genome remains in the cell with few or no viral proteins made and no new virions released."},{"term":"Heterophile antibody","def":"An IgM antibody produced during EBV infection that agglutinates sheep or horse red cells although it was not raised against them."},{"term":"Superantigen","def":"A microbial protein that binds MHC class II and the T cell receptor beta chain outside the peptide groove, activating a large fraction of all T cells."},{"term":"Molecular mimicry","def":"Cross-reaction of antibodies or T cells raised against a microbial antigen with a structurally similar host antigen."},{"term":"Definitive host","def":"The host in which a parasite completes its sexual reproduction; for Toxoplasma gondii, members of the cat family."},{"term":"Tachyzoite and bradyzoite","def":"The rapidly dividing invasive stage of Toxoplasma during acute infection and the slowly dividing stage that persists inside tissue cysts."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-14-01-C01","obj":"IM-14.1","title":"Parvovirus B19: erythroid tropism and arrest of red cell production","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Parvovirus B19 is one of the smallest DNA viruses that infect humans: a nonenveloped icosahedral capsid about 22 to 24 nm across that holds a linear single-stranded DNA genome of about 5.6 kb. The genome encodes no DNA polymerase, so the virus can replicate only in cells that are in S phase and supply the host replication machinery. Because it has no envelope, it resists drying and the solvent and detergent treatment that inactivates enveloped viruses in plasma products."},{"type":"p","text":"The receptor is P antigen (globoside), a glycosphingolipid on erythroid progenitors from the burst-forming and colony-forming units through the pronormoblast. Entry also needs coreceptors (alpha5beta1 integrin and Ku80), and productive infection needs a dividing cell, so these requirements together confine replication mainly to erythroid precursors. People who lack P antigen (the rare p blood group phenotype) are naturally resistant. P antigen is also present on placental trophoblast, endothelium, and fetal myocardium, which helps explain transplacental infection and fetal myocarditis."},{"type":"p","text":"Inside an infected precursor, the nonstructural protein NS1 is cytotoxic and triggers apoptosis, so the cells that should mature into red cells die. The marrow shows absent late erythroid forms and occasional giant pronormoblasts with glassy intranuclear inclusions. Viremia peaks about 1 week after exposure, and reticulocytes fall to near zero for about 7 to 10 days until neutralizing IgM and then IgG clear the virus. In a person who cannot make neutralizing antibody (advanced HIV, transplant immunosuppression, congenital immunodeficiency), viremia persists and causes chronic pure red cell aplasia."},{"type":"p","text":"B19 spreads by respiratory droplets, by blood products, and across the placenta. A person is most infectious during viremia, before any rash. The slapped-cheek rash of erythema infectiosum (fifth disease) in children and the symmetric small-joint arthropathy of adults, especially women, are immune complex effects that appear with antibody, when the person is no longer infectious. The fetus has a rapidly expanding red cell mass and red cells with a shorter life span, so arrest of erythropoiesis causes severe anemia, high-output heart failure, and nonimmune hydrops fetalis; the risk is greatest when maternal infection occurs in the first half of pregnancy."},{"type":"flow","title":"Parvovirus B19 and transient aplastic crisis","steps":["Respiratory droplets carry B19 to the bloodstream","Virus binds P antigen on dividing erythroid progenitors","NS1 triggers apoptosis of infected progenitors","Reticulocytes fall to near zero for about 7 to 10 days","Short red cell survival turns the pause into severe anemia","Neutralizing antibody clears virus; reticulocytes rebound"],"caption":"The same marrow pause is trivial with normal red cell survival and dangerous when survival is short."},{"type":"example","title":"Why the same infection is mild in one child and severe in another","text":"Normal red cells live about 120 days, so about 0.8 percent are removed each day. A 7-day production pause lowers hemoglobin by only about 5 to 6 percent, for example from 13 to about 12.3 g/dL, which goes unnoticed. In sickle cell disease red cells survive only about 10 to 20 days, so 5 to 10 percent are removed daily and the marrow normally compensates with a reticulocyte count often above 10 percent. Stop production for 7 days and roughly 40 percent of the red cell mass is lost: hemoglobin falls from a baseline of 8 to about 5 g/dL, and the reticulocyte count falls below 1 percent. A low reticulocyte count with falling hemoglobin points to an aplastic crisis, not a hemolytic crisis, in which reticulocytes would rise."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-14-01-C02","obj":"IM-14.2","title":"Haemophilus influenzae type b: capsule, IgA protease, and growth factors","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Haemophilus influenzae is a small, pleomorphic, gram-negative coccobacillus and facultative anaerobe whose only natural host is humans. Despite its name, it is a bacterium unrelated to the influenza virus. Strains are either encapsulated, with six capsular serotypes (a to f), or unencapsulated (nontypeable). Nontypeable strains cause mucosal infections such as otitis media, sinusitis, and bronchitis and rarely invade. Type b caused most invasive disease in young children before conjugate vaccination: meningitis, epiglottitis, bacteremia, pneumonia, septic arthritis, and cellulitis."},{"type":"p","text":"The type b capsule is polyribosylribitol phosphate (PRP), a polymer of ribose, ribitol, and phosphate. This hydrophilic, negatively charged coat covers the surface structures that would otherwise activate the alternative complement pathway, so little C3b is deposited and phagocytes cannot bind the organism. Clearance from blood therefore depends on anti-PRP antibody, which fixes complement and opsonizes, and on the spleen, which removes poorly opsonized bacteria. PRP is a T cell independent antigen, and children under about 2 years respond poorly to polysaccharides. Maternal IgG protects for the first few months, which is why invasive Hib disease peaked between about 6 months and 2 years of age. Linking PRP to a protein carrier recruits T cell help and produces a protective, class-switched response in infants, the principle of the conjugate vaccine."},{"type":"p","text":"Other virulence factors help the organism colonize and inflame. IgA protease cleaves the hinge region of IgA1, separating the antigen-binding Fab fragments from Fc, so secretory antibody can no longer block adherence or agglutinate bacteria. Pili and other adhesins attach to nasopharyngeal epithelium, and lipooligosaccharide, a shorter form of endotoxin, drives inflammation in the meninges and airway. Spread is by respiratory droplets and contact with secretions; organisms colonize the nasopharynx, invade the bloodstream, and seed the meninges, epiglottis, joints, and lungs."},{"type":"p","text":"H influenzae cannot make heme or NAD+, so it needs both from its surroundings, as the table shows. Sheep blood agar contains both factors locked inside intact red cells and also contains NADases that destroy free NAD+. Heating blood agar to about 80 degrees C (176 degrees F) lyses the red cells, releases hemin and NAD+, and inactivates the NADases; the medium turns brown and is called chocolate agar."},{"type":"table","title":"Growth factors required by H influenzae","cols":["Factor","Substance","Why the organism needs it"],"rows":[["X factor","Hemin (protoporphyrin with iron)","Heme for cytochromes and catalase in aerobic respiration"],["V factor","NAD+ or NADP+","Electron carrier for oxidation and reduction reactions"]],"caption":"Chocolate agar supplies both; unheated sheep blood agar supplies neither in usable form."},{"type":"stop","predict":"On sheep blood agar, H influenzae grows only as tiny colonies close to a streak of Staphylococcus aureus. Why?","explain":"S aureus lyses nearby red cells with its hemolysins, releasing hemin (X factor), and it secretes NAD+ (V factor). Only the area around the streak supplies both factors, so H influenzae grows there, a pattern called satellite growth.","check":"The paragraph on X and V factors and the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-14-01-C03","obj":"IM-14.3","title":"Influenza virus: hemagglutinin, neuraminidase, drift, and shift","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Influenza viruses are orthomyxoviruses: enveloped viruses with helical nucleocapsids and a segmented, negative-sense, single-stranded RNA genome, with eight segments in influenza A and B. Because the genome is complementary to mRNA, the virion carries its own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase. Unusually for an RNA virus, influenza transcribes and replicates in the nucleus, taking 5 prime caps from host pre-mRNA to prime its own transcripts. Types A, B, and C are defined by internal proteins; influenza A is further subtyped by its hemagglutinin (HA) and neuraminidase (NA), as in H1N1 or H3N2."},{"type":"p","text":"Hemagglutinin is the attachment and fusion protein. It binds sialic acid on glycoproteins and glycolipids of respiratory epithelium; human strains prefer sialic acid in alpha2,6 linkage, abundant in the upper airway, whereas avian strains prefer alpha2,3 linkage. HA is made as HA0 and must be cleaved into HA1 and HA2 by trypsin-like proteases of the airway, which helps confine human infection to the respiratory tract. After endocytosis, the acid pH of the endosome changes HA shape and exposes the HA2 fusion peptide, which fuses the envelope with the endosomal membrane. The M2 proton channel acidifies the virion interior so the ribonucleoproteins separate and enter the nucleus."},{"type":"p","text":"Neuraminidase acts at the end of the cycle. New virions bud from the apical surface, and their HA would bind sialic acid on the cell and on each other; NA cleaves sialic acid so virions are released and do not clump. NA also cuts sialic acid in mucus, helping virus reach the epithelium. Blocking NA leaves new virions stuck to the cell surface, which shows its role. Antibody against HA neutralizes infection, while antibody against NA limits spread."},{"type":"flow","title":"Influenza entry, replication, and release","steps":["HA binds sialic acid on airway epithelium","Virus enters by endocytosis","Endosomal acid triggers HA fusion; M2 acidifies virion","Segments enter nucleus for transcription and replication","Virions bud from the apical membrane","NA cleaves sialic acid and releases virions"],"caption":"HA works at entry and NA at exit; both are the main targets of protective antibody."},{"type":"p","text":"The virus kills ciliated columnar cells, so mucociliary clearance fails and the basement membrane is exposed; fever and myalgia come from cytokines such as interferons, IL-6, and TNF rather than from viremia. This damage sets up secondary bacterial pneumonia with S aureus, S pneumoniae, or H influenzae, often after initial improvement."},{"type":"p","text":"The viral polymerase lacks proofreading, so point mutations accumulate. Mutations in HA and NA antibody-binding sites are selected by population immunity, producing antigenic drift and yearly epidemics in influenza A and B. Antigenic shift requires two influenza A strains, often human and avian, to infect the same cell; segments are packaged in new combinations (reassortment), creating a virus with an HA or NA subtype few people have met. Only influenza A has animal reservoirs carrying many subtypes, so only A shifts. Pigs, whose airway carries both sialic acid linkages, can host both. The 1957 H2N2 and 1968 H3N2 pandemic viruses arose by reassortment of human and avian segments."},{"type":"table","title":"Antigenic drift versus antigenic shift","cols":["Feature","Drift","Shift"],"rows":[["Genetic change","Point mutations in HA and NA genes","Reassortment of whole segments"],["Types affected","Influenza A and B","Influenza A only"],["Population immunity","Partly escaped","Largely absent"],["Result","Seasonal epidemics","Pandemics"]],"caption":"Drift is gradual and continuous; shift is abrupt and needs a segmented genome plus coinfection."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Could measles virus undergo antigenic shift?","explain":"No. Shift needs a segmented genome so whole genes can be swapped between strains. Measles has a single nonsegmented RNA, has no animal reservoir, and is antigenically stable with one serotype.","check":"The drift and shift paragraph and the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-14-01-C04","obj":"IM-14.4","title":"Measles, mumps, and rubella viruses","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Measles virus (genus Morbillivirus) and mumps virus (genus Orthorubulavirus, formerly Rubulavirus) are paramyxoviruses: enveloped viruses with a helical nucleocapsid and a single nonsegmented, negative-sense, single-stranded RNA genome that replicate in the cytoplasm. Each has an attachment protein (H in measles; HN, with hemagglutinin and neuraminidase activity, in mumps) and a fusion (F) protein. With no segments to reassort, each is antigenically stable with one serotype, so infection or vaccination gives lasting immunity."},{"type":"p","text":"F is made as F0 and cleaved by the host protease furin, exposing a hydrophobic fusion peptide. Unlike influenza HA, F works at neutral pH, so after the attachment protein binds, the envelope fuses directly with the plasma membrane. F on the surface of an infected cell fuses it with its neighbors, forming multinucleated syncytia that let virus spread cell to cell, out of reach of antibody. In measles, Warthin-Finkeldey cells are syncytial giant cells with many nuclei found in tonsils, lymph nodes, appendix, and Peyer patches during the prodrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Measles H binds SLAM (CD150) on activated lymphocytes, dendritic cells, and alveolar macrophages, which carry virus to lymph nodes and blood; it later binds nectin-4 on the basolateral surface of airway epithelium, from which virus is shed. Measles is airborne and among the most contagious human infections. The rash is produced by T cells attacking infected endothelium. Infection and loss of memory lymphocytes suppress immunity for months or longer, so pneumonia (the main cause of measles death) and otitis follow. Rarer outcomes are acute encephalitis and, years later, subacute sclerosing panencephalitis from persistent defective virus."},{"type":"p","text":"Mumps HN binds sialic acid. The virus replicates in upper airway epithelium and regional nodes, and viremia seeds glands and the nervous system. Inflammation inside tight capsules, such as the parotid fascia and the tunica albuginea of the testis, causes pain and, in the testis, pressure atrophy."},{"type":"p","text":"Rubella virus (genus Rubivirus) was classed as a togavirus and is now placed in its own family, Matonaviridae. It is enveloped with an icosahedral capsid and positive-sense single-stranded RNA that ribosomes translate directly, so no polymerase is packaged. Postnatal rubella is mild. Transplacental infection, most damaging in the first 12 weeks, inhibits cell division and injures vascular endothelium in developing organs, causing congenital rubella syndrome: cataracts, sensorineural deafness, and patent ductus arteriosus or pulmonary artery stenosis, with blueberry muffin skin lesions from dermal extramedullary hematopoiesis."},{"type":"table","title":"Measles, mumps, and rubella compared","cols":["Feature","Measles","Mumps","Rubella"],"rows":[["Family","Paramyxoviridae","Paramyxoviridae","Matonaviridae (formerly Togaviridae)"],["Genome","Negative-sense ssRNA, nonsegmented","Negative-sense ssRNA, nonsegmented","Positive-sense ssRNA"],["Attachment protein and receptor","H; SLAM (CD150), nectin-4","HN; sialic acid","E1 and E2 glycoproteins"],["Spread","Airborne and droplets","Droplets and saliva","Droplets; transplacental"],["Key features","Cough, coryza, conjunctivitis; Koplik spots; rash from hairline down","Parotitis, orchitis after puberty, aseptic meningitis, pancreatitis","Mild rash spreading from face, fading in about 3 days; postauricular and suboccipital nodes; arthralgia in women"]],"caption":"Measles and mumps share structure and F-mediated fusion; rubella differs in genome sense and family."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-14-01-C05","obj":"IM-14.5","title":"EBV, HHV-6, and HHV-7: latency, lymphocyte tropism, and salivary spread","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Herpesviruses are large enveloped viruses with an icosahedral capsid, a protein tegument, and linear double-stranded DNA; they replicate and assemble in the nucleus. After primary infection the genome circularizes into an episome and expresses few or no viral proteins, so infected cells are nearly invisible to CD8 T cells. This latency lasts for life, and the virus reactivates when T cell immunity weakens. Healthy carriers shed EBV, HHV-6, and HHV-7 intermittently in saliva, so most people acquire them in childhood from family members, and EBV acquired in adolescence spreads by kissing."},{"type":"p","text":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV, HHV-4, a gammaherpesvirus) replicates in oropharyngeal epithelium and tonsillar B cells. Its envelope glycoprotein gp350 binds CD21 (complement receptor 2, the receptor for the C3d fragment), and gp42 binds MHC class II as a coreceptor to trigger fusion; mature B cells carry both. Latent proteins drive the infected B cell: EBNA-1 keeps the episome in dividing cells, LMP-1 mimics a constantly active CD40 and switches on NF-kappaB, and LMP-2A mimics B cell receptor signaling. Infected B cells proliferate and then persist in memory B cells for life."},{"type":"p","text":"The findings of infectious mononucleosis come mostly from the immune response. CD8 T cells expand massively against infected B cells and appear in blood as atypical lymphocytes (large cells with abundant cytoplasm indented by neighboring red cells); EBV infects the B cells, but the atypical cells are T cells. Lymphoid expansion produces exudative pharyngitis, posterior cervical lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly with a stretched capsule that can rupture, and mild hepatitis. Polyclonal B cell activation yields heterophile IgM that agglutinates sheep or horse red cells, the basis of the Monospot test; it is often negative early in illness and in children under about 4 years. When T cell control fails, EBV-driven B cells can become lymphoma, and EBV is linked to Burkitt lymphoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, and post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder."},{"type":"p","text":"HHV-6 and HHV-7 are betaherpesviruses (genus Roseolovirus) that infect CD4 T cells. HHV-7 uses CD4 itself as its receptor, HHV-6B uses CD134 (OX40), and HHV-6A uses CD46; HHV-6 latency is mainly in monocytes and macrophages, and HHV-7 latency in CD4 T cells. Nearly all children are infected by about 2 to 3 years of age. Roseola infantum (exanthem subitum, sixth disease), caused mainly by HHV-6B, affects children about 6 months to 2 years old: 3 to 5 days of high fever, often 39 to 40 degrees C (102 to 104 degrees F), which can provoke febrile seizures, then a rosy, blanching maculopapular rash on the trunk as the fever falls and the immune response takes hold."},{"type":"table","title":"Herpesviruses of mononucleosis and roseola","cols":["Feature","EBV (HHV-4)","HHV-6 and HHV-7"],"rows":[["Subfamily","Gammaherpesvirus","Betaherpesvirus (Roseolovirus)"],["Receptor and target cell","gp350 binds CD21; gp42 binds MHC class II; B cells and oropharyngeal epithelium","CD4 T cells; HHV-7 binds CD4, HHV-6B binds CD134"],["Site of latency","Memory B cells","HHV-6: monocytes and macrophages; HHV-7: CD4 T cells"],["Main primary disease","Infectious mononucleosis in adolescents and young adults","Roseola infantum in infants and toddlers"],["Key laboratory finding","Atypical CD8 T cells; heterophile antibody","Heterophile negative; diagnosis is mostly clinical"]],"caption":"All are enveloped dsDNA viruses spread by saliva with lifelong latency; they differ in which lymphocyte they enter."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-14-01-C06","obj":"IM-14.6","title":"Streptococcus pyogenes: identification, M protein, and superantigens","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes (group A Streptococcus) is a gram-positive coccus that grows in chains and colonizes the pharynx and skin. It spreads by respiratory droplets and direct contact. Its laboratory identity rests on a sequence of tests that separates it from staphylococci and from other streptococci, summarized below. Complete (beta) hemolysis comes from streptolysin S, which is oxygen stable, and streptolysin O, which is oxygen labile and immunogenic; antibody to streptolysin O (ASO) is the classic marker of recent infection."},{"type":"table","title":"Identifying S pyogenes","cols":["Test","Result","What it separates"],"rows":[["Catalase","Negative","Streptococci from staphylococci (catalase positive)"],["Hemolysis on blood agar","Beta (complete, clear zone)","From alpha-hemolytic S pneumoniae and viridans streptococci"],["Bacitracin disk","Sensitive","From group B Streptococcus (resistant)"],["PYR test","Positive","Supports group A; group B is negative"],["Lancefield antigen","Group A cell wall carbohydrate","Defines the group serologically"]],"caption":"Catalase first, then hemolysis, then bacitracin is the standard decision path."},{"type":"p","text":"M protein is a coiled-coil fibrillar protein anchored in the cell wall that projects from the surface. It binds factor H and C4b-binding protein, host regulators that inactivate C3b, and it binds fibrinogen, which coats the bacterium. Little opsonin remains on the surface, so neutrophils cannot ingest the organism unless antibody to that particular M protein is present. The N-terminal end is hypervariable, giving more than 200 M types, so immunity is type specific and repeated infections occur. A hyaluronic acid capsule, chemically identical to host hyaluronan and therefore nonimmunogenic, and C5a peptidase, which limits neutrophil recruitment, add to this defense."},{"type":"p","text":"The conserved regions of M protein share epitopes with cardiac myosin, tropomyosin, and other host proteins. Antibodies and T cells raised against pharyngeal infection cross-react with heart, joints, and brain, causing acute rheumatic fever about 2 to 4 weeks later, a type II hypersensitivity reaction; carditis with valvulitis, especially of the mitral valve, is the lasting damage. Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is a different mechanism, immune complex (type III) injury after pharyngeal or skin infection with nephritogenic strains."},{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins A and C are superantigens encoded by temperate bacteriophages, so only lysogenized strains make them. A conventional peptide antigen activates about 0.001 to 0.01 percent of T cells; a superantigen activates every T cell that uses a matching V beta region, up to about 20 percent. The cytokine surge produces fever and dilation of dermal capillaries. Scarlet fever follows pharyngitis, usually in school-age children: a diffuse, fine, sandpaper-like erythematous rash that starts on the neck and upper trunk, deepens in skin folds (Pastia lines), spares the area around the mouth (circumoral pallor), with a strawberry tongue and later peeling of the hands and feet. At its extreme the same mechanism causes streptococcal toxic shock syndrome with capillary leak and multiorgan failure."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a streptococcal superantigen causes scarlet fever and shock","steps":["Toxin binds MHC class II outside the peptide groove","Same toxin binds the TCR beta chain variable region","Up to about 20 percent of T cells activate","Massive IL-2, interferon-gamma, TNF, and IL-1 release","Fever, dermal vasodilation, and diffuse erythematous rash","Severe cases: capillary leak, hypotension, organ failure"],"caption":"No antigen processing or peptide specificity is needed, which is why so many T cells respond at once."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-14-01-C07","obj":"IM-14.7","title":"Toxoplasma gondii: life cycle and routes of human infection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Toxoplasma gondii is an obligate intracellular protozoan of the phylum Apicomplexa, the group that includes Plasmodium. It has three infectious stages: the sporozoite inside an oocyst, the rapidly dividing tachyzoite of acute infection, and the slowly dividing bradyzoite inside a tissue cyst. Members of the cat family are the definitive hosts because only they support the sexual cycle. Almost any warm-blooded animal, including humans, livestock, and rodents, can be an intermediate host, carrying tissue cysts but never producing oocysts."},{"type":"p","text":"A cat is infected by eating prey with tissue cysts or by swallowing oocysts. Bradyzoites invade the cat's intestinal epithelium, multiply asexually, and then form male and female gametes; fertilization produces oocysts. After a first infection the cat sheds very large numbers of unsporulated oocysts in feces for about 1 to 2 weeks. Oocysts need 1 to 5 days in the environment to sporulate and become infectious, and then survive in moist soil and water for many months. Fresh feces are therefore not infectious, while soil, garden produce, and unchanged litter can be."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Toxoplasma gondii life cycle","steps":["Sexual cycle in cat intestinal epithelium","Unsporulated oocysts shed in cat feces","Oocysts sporulate in soil in 1 to 5 days","Intermediate host ingests sporulated oocysts","Tachyzoites spread; bradyzoite cysts form in muscle and brain","Cat eats prey with tissue cysts"],"caption":"Humans enter as intermediate hosts by swallowing sporulated oocysts or tissue cysts in undercooked meat; the cycle continues only through cats."},{"type":"p","text":"Humans are infected by swallowing sporulated oocysts in soil, water, unwashed produce, or litter; by eating tissue cysts in undercooked meat such as pork, lamb, or venison; across the placenta; and rarely through organ transplant or transfusion. Digestion releases sporozoites or bradyzoites, which invade the intestinal epithelium and become tachyzoites. Tachyzoites enter cells actively using secretory organelles of the apical complex and live in a parasitophorous vacuole that does not fuse with lysosomes. They divide until the cell bursts and spread through blood and lymph, often inside monocytes, to almost any nucleated cell."},{"type":"p","text":"Dendritic cell IL-12 drives NK cells and T cells to make interferon-gamma, which activates macrophages and pushes the parasite into the bradyzoite stage inside tissue cysts in brain, retina, and skeletal and cardiac muscle, where it persists for life. Primary infection in an immunocompetent person is usually silent or a mononucleosis-like illness with cervical lymphadenopathy and a negative heterophile test. If CD4 counts fall below about 100 cells per microliter, cysts rupture and tachyzoites cause encephalitis with multiple ring-enhancing brain lesions."},{"type":"p","text":"Congenital infection needs tachyzoites in maternal blood, so it follows primary infection during pregnancy, or reactivation in a severely immunosuppressed mother; an immunocompetent woman infected well before conception almost never transmits. As the placenta grows, the chance of transmission rises across pregnancy while fetal damage becomes less severe, so early infection causes the worst disease and late infection is often silent at birth, with chorioretinitis appearing later. The classic findings are chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, and intracranial calcifications scattered through the brain, unlike the periventricular calcifications of congenital cytomegalovirus."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A pregnant woman with long-standing antibody to Toxoplasma and normal immunity eats undercooked lamb. Is her fetus at risk of congenital toxoplasmosis?","explain":"Very little. Her existing immunity controls new parasites before tachyzoites circulate in large numbers, and congenital disease requires tachyzoites in maternal blood, which is mainly a feature of primary infection during pregnancy.","check":"The paragraph on congenital infection and the role of interferon-gamma."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each organism in this chapter is explained by where it enters, which receptor it uses, and how the host responds. Receptors set tropism: P antigen sends parvovirus B19 to erythroid precursors, CD21 sends EBV to B cells, CD4 and CD134 send HHV-6 and HHV-7 to helper T cells, and sialic acid linkage restricts influenza to the airway. Genome structure sets variability: only the segmented influenza A genome can shift, while nonsegmented measles and mumps remain single serotypes. Many of the clinical findings are immune effects rather than direct viral damage, including the rash of fifth disease, the atypical lymphocytes of mononucleosis, the measles rash, rheumatic fever, and superantigen shock."},{"type":"p","text":"Three organisms cross the placenta, and each injures the fetus in a way that follows from its biology: parvovirus stops red cell production and causes hydrops, rubella blocks cell division during organogenesis, and Toxoplasma tachyzoites destroy brain and retina. Immune status changes the picture. Asplenia and absent antibody leave encapsulated Hib unchecked, absent neutralizing antibody turns parvovirus into chronic red cell aplasia, and T cell failure lets latent EBV and Toxoplasma escape control. These are generalizations, and individual strains, host genetics, and the timing of exposure all modify the outcome."}],"summary":["Parvovirus B19 is a nonenveloped ssDNA virus that binds P antigen and kills dividing erythroid progenitors, halting red cell production for about a week; this is serious only when red cell survival is short, as in hemolysis or the fetus.","Hib is a gram-negative coccobacillus whose PRP capsule blocks complement and phagocytosis; IgA protease aids colonization, and growth needs X (hemin) and V (NAD+) factors supplied by chocolate agar.","Influenza hemagglutinin binds sialic acid and fuses in the acid endosome; neuraminidase releases new virions.","Drift is point mutation in HA and NA causing epidemics; shift is reassortment of segments in influenza A causing pandemics.","Measles and mumps are nonsegmented negative-sense paramyxoviruses whose F protein fuses cells at neutral pH, forming syncytia such as Warthin-Finkeldey cells; rubella is a positive-sense virus that damages the first-trimester fetus.","EBV enters B cells through CD21 and persists in memory B cells; the atypical lymphocytes of mononucleosis are reactive CD8 T cells.","HHV-6 and HHV-7 infect CD4 T cells, spread by saliva, and cause roseola with high fever followed by rash.","S pyogenes is catalase negative, beta-hemolytic, and bacitracin sensitive; M protein blocks opsonization and mimics cardiac proteins.","Streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins are superantigens that bind MHC class II and TCR V beta outside the groove, causing scarlet fever and toxic shock.","Toxoplasma reproduces sexually only in cats; humans ingest sporulated oocysts or tissue cysts, and tachyzoites cross the placenta after primary maternal infection."],"sources":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Parvoviruses; Orthomyxoviruses (Influenza Viruses)"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Paramyxoviruses and Rubella Virus; Herpesviruses"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Streptococci; Haemophilus species; Medical Parasitology section on Toxoplasma"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to Microbes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of Infancy and Childhood, section on congenital infections"}]},"IM-15-A":{"unit":"IM-15-A","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-01","title":"Congenital Immunodeficiency Diseases","subject":"Immune System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers five classic inborn immune defects: common variable immunodeficiency, DiGeorge syndrome, selective IgA deficiency, severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID), and X-linked agammaglobulinemia. Each disease is explained from the developmental step that fails, so its infections, laboratory pattern, and associated findings follow from mechanism. The final section turns this into a method: read the infection pattern and the age of onset, name the defective arm of immunity (B cell, T cell, phagocyte, or complement), and choose the first test. Board questions usually present a child or adult with recurrent infections and expect exactly that chain of reasoning.","prerequisites":["B cell development in the marrow, V(D)J recombination, and the pre-B cell receptor (IM-12)","T cell development and selection in the thymus","T cell help for B cells: CD40 ligand, cytokines, germinal centers, and class switching","Immunoglobulin classes, secretory IgA, and placental transfer of IgG","Complement pathways, opsonization, and the membrane attack complex"],"connections":[{"to":"Thymus embryology (IM-1)","how":"Failed third and fourth pharyngeal pouch development explains the thymic and parathyroid defects of DiGeorge syndrome."},{"to":"Immunogenetics (IM-12)","how":"RAG, IL2RG, JAK3, and ADA defects define the SCID genotypes and their lymphocyte profiles."},{"to":"Immune biochemistry (IM-10)","how":"NADPH oxidase and purine salvage explain chronic granulomatous disease and ADA-deficient SCID."},{"to":"Congenital heart disease and hypoparathyroidism (CV-14, EN-12-B)","how":"Neural crest and pharyngeal pouch defects link conotruncal anomalies and neonatal hypocalcemia to DiGeorge syndrome."},{"to":"Celiac disease and transfusion reactions (GI-11, HE-7)","how":"IgA deficiency causes falsely negative IgA celiac serology and anaphylactic transfusion reactions."}],"terms":[{"term":"Primary immunodeficiency","def":"An inherited or developmental defect of the immune system, as opposed to one acquired from infection, drugs, or protein loss."},{"term":"Plasma cell","def":"A terminally differentiated B cell that secretes large amounts of a single antibody."},{"term":"Class switch recombination","def":"DNA recombination in activated B cells that replaces the IgM constant region with that of IgG, IgA, or IgE, driven by CD40 ligand and cytokines from helper T cells."},{"term":"Pre-B cell receptor","def":"A mu heavy chain paired with a surrogate light chain whose signal, relayed through Bruton tyrosine kinase, lets a pre-B cell proliferate and mature."},{"term":"Common gamma chain","def":"The receptor subunit, encoded by IL2RG, shared by the receptors for IL-2, IL-4, IL-7, IL-9, IL-15, and IL-21."},{"term":"TREC","def":"T cell receptor excision circle, a piece of DNA cut out during T cell receptor gene rearrangement in the thymus, used as a marker of new T cell production."},{"term":"Thymic shadow","def":"The soft tissue density of the thymus in the anterior mediastinum on an infant chest radiograph."},{"term":"Secretory IgA","def":"Dimeric IgA joined by J chain and transported across mucosal epithelium by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor."},{"term":"Encapsulated bacteria","def":"Bacteria with a polysaccharide capsule that resists phagocytosis until the organism is opsonized by antibody and complement."},{"term":"Dihydrorhodamine (DHR) test","def":"A flow cytometry test of neutrophil oxidative burst in which reactive oxygen species convert dihydrorhodamine to fluorescent rhodamine."},{"term":"CH50","def":"A functional assay of the whole classical complement pathway, C1 through C9, measured by lysis of antibody-coated sheep red cells."},{"term":"Transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease","def":"Attack on host tissues by viable donor T cells from a blood product that the recipient cannot reject."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-01-C01","obj":"IM-15-A.1","title":"Common variable immunodeficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Common variable immunodeficiency (CVID) is a group of disorders in which B cells are present, usually in normal numbers, but fail to complete the last stage of their development: activation in germinal centers, class switching, and differentiation into antibody-secreting plasma cells. The result is low IgG together with low IgA, low IgM, or both, and a poor antibody response to vaccines. It is the most frequent clinically significant primary antibody deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"Marrow development is intact, so naive B cells reach the lymphoid follicles, but they respond poorly to antigen and T cell help. Few switched memory B cells and plasma cells form, and gut or marrow biopsies show scarce plasma cells. Variants in genes for B cell activation and survival (for example TACI, ICOS, CD19, and NFKB2) and in immune regulators (CTLA4, LRBA) explain only a minority of cases; most are sporadic with complex inheritance. Some patients also have T cell dysfunction."},{"type":"p","text":"With no single developmental block, the defect often appears late. Onset peaks in the second to fourth decades, although children are also affected, and men and women are affected equally. The classic patient is an adult with a normal early history who develops recurrent sinusitis, otitis, and pneumonia with encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae. Repeated lower respiratory infection destroys bronchial walls and leaves bronchiectasis. Without mucosal IgA, Giardia persists in the small intestine and causes chronic diarrhea and malabsorption."},{"type":"p","text":"CVID is a disorder of immune regulation as well as deficiency. About a fifth or more of patients develop autoimmunity, most often immune thrombocytopenia and autoimmune hemolytic anemia; total IgG is low, but defective B cell selection and regulatory T cell function still allow a few self-reactive clones to make pathogenic autoantibody. Chronic immune activation causes lymphoid hyperplasia (splenomegaly, nodular lymphoid hyperplasia of the gut) and noncaseating granulomas in lung, liver, and lymph nodes that resemble sarcoidosis. The risk of B cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma is greatly increased, and gastric adenocarcinoma is also more common."},{"type":"table","title":"Laboratory pattern in CVID","cols":["Test","Typical finding","Reason"],"rows":[["Serum IgG","Low (well below the age-specific range)","Few plasma cells form"],["Serum IgA and IgM","IgA low; IgM low in about half","Failed class switching and plasma cell differentiation"],["B cells (CD19) by flow cytometry","Usually normal, sometimes mildly low","The block is after marrow development"],["Switched memory B cells","Reduced","Germinal center reactions fail"],["Vaccine responses (tetanus, pneumococcal)","Poor","Specific antibody cannot be produced"],["T cells","Usually normal number, variable function","A subset has combined dysfunction"]],"caption":"Diagnosis rests on low IgG with low IgA or IgM, poor vaccine responses, and exclusion of secondary causes such as protein loss or drugs."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 32-year-old woman with CVID has a negative IgG antibody test for an infection she clearly had last year. Is the infection excluded?","explain":"No. She cannot reliably make specific antibody, so serology is falsely negative; infection must be shown directly by culture, antigen, or nucleic acid testing.","check":"Compare with the vaccine response row of the laboratory table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-01-C02","obj":"IM-15-A.2","title":"DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion)","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"DiGeorge syndrome is the immunologic part of the 22q11.2 deletion syndrome, the most common microdeletion syndrome in humans at about 1 in 4,000 live births. A segment of about 3 million base pairs is lost from one copy of chromosome 22, usually de novo, and passed on as an autosomal dominant trait when a parent carries it. The key gene for the pharyngeal features is TBX1, a T-box transcription factor; losing one copy disturbs the pharyngeal pouches and arches and the neural crest-derived tissue that fills them."},{"type":"flow","title":"From 22q11.2 deletion to the clinical triad","steps":["22q11.2 deletion removes one copy of TBX1","Pharyngeal pouches, arches, and neural crest mesenchyme develop abnormally","Third pouch: thymus and inferior parathyroids hypoplastic","Fourth pouch: superior parathyroids hypoplastic","Neural crest: faulty aorticopulmonary septum and outflow tract","T cell lymphopenia, hypocalcemia, conotruncal heart defects"],"caption":"One embryologic defect explains the immune, endocrine, and cardiac findings."},{"type":"p","text":"The thymus forms from third pouch endoderm, so a small thymus limits production of mature T cells. Most patients have partial DiGeorge syndrome, with low to near-normal T cell counts that often improve with age; less than about 1% have complete athymia, with almost no naive T cells and a course like SCID. When T cells are deficient, infections are those that T cells control: viruses, Candida and other fungi, and Pneumocystis. B cells are present and immunoglobulins are usually normal, although antibody responses can be weak without T cell help. The thymic shadow is small or absent on chest radiograph."},{"type":"p","text":"Absent or small parathyroids cause hypoparathyroidism. Low PTH reduces bone resorption, renal calcium reabsorption, phosphate excretion, and calcitriol synthesis, giving low calcium with high phosphate. Low ionized calcium makes nerve Na+ channels open more easily, so neuromuscular excitability rises; newborns present in the first days with tetany or seizures, and the QT interval lengthens."},{"type":"p","text":"Cardiac neural crest cells build the aorticopulmonary septum that divides the truncus arteriosus into aorta and pulmonary trunk. Faulty migration causes conotruncal defects: interrupted aortic arch type B, persistent truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot, and ventricular septal defects. Abnormal arch development produces the facies (hooded eyelids, low-set ears, short philtrum, small mandible) and cleft palate or velopharyngeal insufficiency. The CATCH-22 mnemonic (cardiac defects, abnormal facies, thymic hypoplasia, cleft palate, hypocalcemia, chromosome 22) collects these features. Learning difficulty and later psychiatric illness, including schizophrenia, are also more common. The deletion is confirmed by fluorescence in situ hybridization or chromosomal microarray."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-01-C03","obj":"IM-15-A.3","title":"Selective IgA deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Selective IgA deficiency is defined as serum IgA below about 7 mg/dL with normal IgG and IgM in a person older than 4 years, once other causes are excluded; the age limit exists because IgA normally rises slowly through childhood. It is the most common primary immunodeficiency, affecting about 1 in 300 to 700 people of European descent and fewer in East Asian populations. IgA-bearing B cells are present but fail to mature into IgA-secreting plasma cells. The cause is usually unknown, and it clusters in families with CVID, with which it shares susceptibility genes."},{"type":"p","text":"Most affected people are healthy because mucosal defense has a backup. Secretory IgA is a J chain-linked dimer carried across epithelium by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor; pentameric IgM also contains J chain and uses the same receptor, so secretory IgM partly takes its place. Symptomatic patients, often those with an accompanying IgG2 subclass deficiency or poor polysaccharide responses, have recurrent sinusitis, otitis, and bronchitis, and Giardia infection of the small bowel: the surfaces where IgA normally neutralizes microbes before they attach."},{"type":"p","text":"IgA deficiency is associated with atopy and with autoimmune disease, including systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, immune thrombocytopenia, and type 1 diabetes. The strongest link is celiac disease, in which IgA deficiency is about 10 to 15 times more common than in the general population. Reduced mucosal exclusion of food and microbial antigens is one proposed explanation, but the mechanism is not settled."},{"type":"flow","title":"Anaphylactic transfusion reaction in IgA deficiency","steps":["IgA never made, so no tolerance to IgA","Some patients form anti-IgA antibodies, mostly IgG","Transfused plasma or whole blood delivers donor IgA","Anti-IgA binds donor IgA; mast cell and complement mediators released","Bronchospasm, hypotension, and urticaria within minutes"],"caption":"Anaphylaxis can occur during a first transfusion because sensitization needs no prior transfusion. Severe reactions are uncommon even among patients with anti-IgA."},{"type":"p","text":"Any serologic test that detects a patient's IgA antibody cannot be positive if no IgA is made, so the usual celiac screen, IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase, is falsely negative. Total IgA is therefore measured alongside it, and in IgA deficiency the IgG-based tests (IgG anti-deamidated gliadin peptide or IgG anti-tissue transglutaminase) are informative instead. IgA deficiency can also progress to CVID, so a falling IgG in a known patient is significant."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-01-C04","obj":"IM-15-A.4","title":"Severe combined immunodeficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) is a group of genetic disorders in which T cell development fails. B cells need T cell help for class switching and for most antibody responses, so both arms of adaptive immunity are lost even when B cells are present. Incidence is about 1 in 50,000 to 60,000 births. Maternal IgG gives partial protection against bacteria for a few months, but nothing controls viruses, fungi, or Pneumocystis, so infants become ill within the first months of life."},{"type":"table","title":"Major SCID genotypes","cols":["Defect","Inheritance","T / B / NK cells","Mechanism"],"rows":[["IL2RG (common gamma chain)","X-linked","Absent / present / absent","No IL-7 signal for thymocytes and no IL-15 signal for NK cells"],["JAK3","Autosomal recessive","Absent / present / absent","Kinase that carries the common gamma chain signal"],["IL-7 receptor alpha","Autosomal recessive","Absent / present / present","Loss of the IL-7 signal alone"],["RAG1, RAG2, or Artemis","Autosomal recessive","Absent / absent / present","No V(D)J recombination, so no T or B cell receptors"],["Adenosine deaminase (ADA)","Autosomal recessive","Absent / absent / absent","Toxic purine metabolites kill all lymphocytes"]],"caption":"X-linked IL2RG deficiency is historically the most common form. B cells present in IL2RG and JAK3 SCID still cannot make useful antibody."},{"type":"flow","title":"How ADA deficiency kills lymphocytes","steps":["ADA deficiency: deoxyadenosine and adenosine accumulate","Lymphocytes phosphorylate deoxyadenosine to dATP","dATP inhibits ribonucleotide reductase","Deoxynucleotide pools fall; DNA synthesis and repair fail","Developing T, B, and NK cells die"],"caption":"Lymphocytes are vulnerable because they have high kinase activity that traps deoxyadenosine as dATP."},{"type":"p","text":"The typical infant has failure to thrive, chronic diarrhea, persistent oral thrush, Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, and severe or prolonged respiratory viral infections. The chest radiograph lacks a thymic shadow, tonsils and lymph nodes are absent, and the absolute lymphocyte count is usually low. Maternal T cells that crossed the placenta can engraft and cause a rash resembling graft-versus-host disease. Newborn screening detects SCID before infection by measuring TRECs; few TRECs means few newly made T cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Live attenuated vaccines depend on the host to contain a weakened organism. In SCID, rotavirus vaccine causes chronic diarrhea, BCG causes disseminated mycobacterial infection, and oral polio, measles, and varicella vaccine strains can cause fatal disease. Cellular blood products contain viable donor T lymphocytes. A normal recipient rejects them, but an infant with SCID cannot, so donor T cells engraft, proliferate, and attack host skin, gut, liver, and marrow. This transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease causes rash, diarrhea, hepatitis, and pancytopenia and is usually fatal. Gamma irradiation damages lymphocyte DNA so donor T cells cannot proliferate, which is why irradiated products prevent it."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: two infants without a thymic shadow","text":"Infant A, a 4-month-old boy, has thrush, Pneumocystis pneumonia, and diarrhea. CD3 T cells are nearly absent, B cells are present, NK cells are absent, and calcium is normal. Infant B, 5 days old, has a seizure; calcium is low, phosphate is high, an echocardiogram shows truncus arteriosus, and CD3 T cells are low but present. Both lack a thymic shadow, so the radiograph alone cannot separate them. Infant A has the T absent, B present, NK absent pattern of IL2RG or JAK3 SCID; in a boy, X-linked IL2RG is likeliest. Infant B has a pharyngeal pouch defect: thymus, parathyroids, and outflow tract fail together, which points to 22q11.2 deletion. Calcium, the heart, and the face distinguish DiGeorge syndrome from SCID."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Will a 2-month-old boy with IL2RG SCID have normal serum IgG and a normal B cell count?","explain":"Often yes for both. B cells develop without the common gamma chain, and IgG at 2 months is mostly maternal. IgA and IgM are low, and he cannot make specific antibody, because T cell help and IL-21 signaling are missing.","check":"Compare with the IL2RG row of the genotype table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-01-C05","obj":"IM-15-A.5","title":"X-linked (Bruton) agammaglobulinemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"X-linked agammaglobulinemia (XLA) results from mutations in BTK on the long arm of the X chromosome, which encodes Bruton tyrosine kinase. In the marrow, pro-B cells rearrange heavy chain genes and become pre-B cells, which display the mu heavy chain with a surrogate light chain as the pre-B cell receptor. Signaling from this receptor, relayed through BTK, drives proliferation and progression to light chain rearrangement. Without BTK the signal is lost and development stops at the pre-B stage."},{"type":"flow","title":"B cell development and where antibody deficiencies block it","steps":["Stem cell to pro-B cell: heavy chain V(D)J rearrangement","Pre-B cell: pre-B cell receptor signals through BTK (XLA blocks here)","Immature, then mature naive B cell with surface IgM and IgD","Germinal center: T cell help drives class switching","Plasma cell secretes antibody (CVID and IgA deficiency block here)"],"caption":"An early block removes B cells; a late block leaves B cells but no plasma cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Mature B cells are nearly absent from blood (CD19-positive cells usually under 2% of lymphocytes) and from lymphoid tissue, so there are no germinal centers and no plasma cells. All immunoglobulin classes are very low. Tonsils, adenoids, and lymph nodes are small or impalpable because B cell follicles make up much of their bulk. T cell number and function are normal. The disease is X-linked recessive, so patients are almost always boys, sometimes with affected maternal male relatives."},{"type":"p","text":"Maternal IgG, with a half-life of about 3 weeks, protects the infant for the first months, so infections begin after about 6 months as it is catabolized. The organisms are those that antibody controls. Encapsulated bacteria such as S pneumoniae and H influenzae type b resist phagocytosis until IgG and complement opsonize them, causing otitis, sinusitis, pneumonia, and bacteremia. Enteroviruses reach the central nervous system through the blood and are cleared mainly by neutralizing antibody, so echovirus can cause chronic meningoencephalitis and live oral polio vaccine can cause paralytic polio. Giardia persists without mucosal IgA, and Mycoplasma infections of joints and airways also occur."},{"type":"table","title":"Primary antibody deficiencies compared","cols":["Feature","XLA","CVID","Selective IgA deficiency"],"rows":[["Block","Pre-B to B cell (BTK)","B cell to plasma cell","IgA B cell to IgA plasma cell"],["Sex and onset","Boys, after about 6 months","Either sex, usually adults","Either sex, any age; often silent"],["Blood B cells","Absent","Usually normal","Normal"],["IgG / IgA / IgM","All very low","IgG low with low IgA and/or IgM","IgA very low; IgG and IgM normal"],["Tonsils and nodes","Small or absent","Normal or enlarged (lymphoid hyperplasia)","Normal"],["Distinctive risks","Enteroviral meningoencephalitis, vaccine polio","Autoimmune cytopenias, granulomas, lymphoma","Celiac disease, anaphylaxis to IgA in blood products"]],"caption":"Low IgG with absent B cells means XLA; low IgG with B cells present means CVID."},{"type":"p","text":"A girl with the same picture suggests autosomal recessive agammaglobulinemia, for example from mu heavy chain defects. Transient hypogammaglobulinemia of infancy, a delay in the infant's own IgG production, differs by normal B cell numbers and spontaneous recovery."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-01-C06","obj":"IM-15-A.6","title":"Reading the infection pattern and choosing the first test","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each arm of immunity is essential against particular organisms, so the infection pattern identifies the arm that has failed. Antibody with complement opsonizes encapsulated bacteria, whose polysaccharide capsules otherwise block phagocytosis, and neutralizing antibody stops enteroviruses during their viremic spread. T cells kill virus-infected cells and activate macrophages through interferon gamma, controlling viruses, fungi such as Candida, Pneumocystis, and intracellular bacteria such as mycobacteria. Neutrophils kill bacteria and fungi with oxidants derived from superoxide made by NADPH oxidase. When the oxidase is absent, as in chronic granulomatous disease (CGD), a characteristic set of catalase-positive bacteria and fungi survive; the classic explanation is that catalase-negative bacteria supply their own peroxide while catalase-positive organisms destroy it. The membrane attack complex (C5b to C9) is uniquely important for lysing Neisseria."},{"type":"p","text":"Age at onset is the second clue. Antibody defects stay hidden until maternal IgG falls after about 6 months, and CVID often appears in adults. T cell and combined defects present in the first weeks to months, as do severe phagocyte defects. Delayed separation of the umbilical cord, infections without pus, and a very high neutrophil count point to leukocyte adhesion deficiency, a defect of neutrophil migration, rather than CGD."},{"type":"table","title":"Infection pattern, defective arm, and first test","cols":["Infection pattern","Defective arm","Examples","First test","Abnormal result"],"rows":[["Encapsulated bacteria, enteroviruses, Giardia; sinopulmonary infections","B cell or antibody","XLA, CVID, IgA deficiency","Quantitative IgG, IgA, IgM","Low for age; then vaccine titers and B cell count"],["Viruses, Candida, Pneumocystis, mycobacteria","T cell","DiGeorge syndrome","Lymphocyte count and flow cytometry (CD3, CD4, CD8)","Low T cells"],["Both groups in an infant with failure to thrive","Combined T and B","SCID","Flow cytometry for T, B, and NK cells","T cells nearly absent; B and NK depend on gene"],["S aureus, Serratia, Burkholderia cepacia, Nocardia, Aspergillus","Phagocyte oxidative burst","CGD","Dihydrorhodamine (DHR) flow cytometry","No rise in fluorescence after stimulation"],["Recurrent or disseminated Neisseria","Terminal complement C5 to C9","C5 to C9 deficiency","CH50","Very low or absent"]],"caption":"The first test screens the suspected arm; specific gene, protein, or functional tests follow."},{"type":"p","text":"Each test measures function or cells directly. Quantitative immunoglobulins are compared with age-specific ranges, since normal infant values are far lower than adult ones. Flow cytometry counts cells by surface markers: CD3 for T cells, CD4 and CD8 subsets, CD19 or CD20 for B cells, and CD16 and CD56 for NK cells. In the DHR test, stimulated neutrophils oxidize dihydrorhodamine 123 to fluorescent rhodamine, so absent fluorescence means absent NADPH oxidase activity; it has replaced the nitroblue tetrazolium test. CH50 measures the serum dilution that lyses 50% of antibody-coated sheep red cells, which requires every classical component from C1 through C9, so loss of any one gives a very low or zero value."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: an 8-month-old boy with pneumonia","text":"An 8-month-old boy has his third episode of otitis and now pneumococcal pneumonia. His tonsils cannot be seen, and a maternal uncle died of meningitis in childhood. Encapsulated bacteria point to the antibody arm, and onset after 6 months fits loss of maternal IgG, so the first test is quantitative immunoglobulins: IgG, IgA, and IgM are all very low. The next question is whether B cells exist. Flow cytometry shows CD19 B cells under 1% with normal CD3 T cells. Absent B cells, absent tonsils, a boy, and a maternal family history indicate XLA. Had B cells been normal in an adult, the diagnosis would be CVID."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 19-year-old has a second episode of meningococcal meningitis. Which arm is defective, which test comes first, and would a normal IgG level rule out an immunodeficiency?","explain":"Terminal complement (C5 to C9), screened with CH50. A normal IgG does not rule it out, because the defect is the membrane attack complex that lyses Neisseria, not antibody production.","check":"Compare with the first paragraph and the last row of the table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"These diseases connect embryology, genetics, biochemistry, and immunology. DiGeorge syndrome is a pharyngeal pouch and neural crest defect that happens to remove the thymus, so its immune, endocrine, and cardiac findings share one origin. SCID genotypes map onto steps of lymphocyte development: cytokine signaling (IL2RG, JAK3), V(D)J recombination (RAG), and purine salvage (ADA). The antibody deficiencies sit along the B cell pathway, with XLA blocking early and CVID and IgA deficiency blocking late."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology adds the paradox of immune dysregulation: CVID and IgA deficiency carry autoimmunity, and CVID carries lymphoma, because the same faults that reduce protective antibody also impair tolerance and lymphoid control. The infection-pattern method is a screening tool rather than a rule; organisms overlap, partial defects blur the categories, and secondary causes such as HIV infection, protein-losing states, and immunosuppressive drugs must be excluded before a defect is called primary."}],"summary":["Antibody defects cause encapsulated bacterial, enteroviral, and Giardia infections and appear after about 6 months, when maternal IgG is gone.","XLA is a BTK defect that stops B cell development at the pre-B stage: boys with absent B cells, very low immunoglobulins, and scant tonsils.","CVID has B cells that fail to become plasma cells: low IgG with low IgA or IgM, usually adult onset, plus autoimmunity, granulomas, and lymphoma risk.","Selective IgA deficiency is the most common primary immunodeficiency; it is usually silent because secretory IgM compensates.","IgA deficiency causes falsely negative IgA celiac serology and, in patients with anti-IgA antibodies, anaphylaxis to IgA in blood products.","DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2, TBX1) disrupts the third and fourth pouches and neural crest: T cell lymphopenia, hypocalcemic tetany, conotruncal defects, abnormal facies, and cleft palate.","SCID removes T cell function and therefore antibody responses, causing thrush, Pneumocystis, viral infection, diarrhea, and failure to thrive in early infancy with no thymic shadow and low TRECs.","In SCID, live vaccines cause disseminated infection and nonirradiated cellular blood products cause transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease.","Infection pattern to first test: antibody, quantitative immunoglobulins; T cell, flow cytometry; phagocyte, DHR; terminal complement, CH50."],"sources":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Congenital and Acquired Immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System, primary immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck, pharyngeal pouches and their derivatives"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Nucleotide metabolism, purine degradation and adenosine deaminase deficiency"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Immune System, primary immunodeficiency"}]},"IM-15-B":{"unit":"IM-15-B","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-02","title":"Acquired Immunodeficiency Diseases","subject":"Immune System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers immune deficiency acquired after birth, first from immunosuppressive drugs and then from HIV infection. Both follow one rule: when a drug or a virus removes a specific arm of immunity, the infections and cancers that follow are the ones that arm normally contains. Board questions give a drug, a CD4 count, a test result, or a lesion and ask you to name the organism, the mechanism, or the meaning of a test result. If you can explain what each drug removes, how HIV enters and depletes CD4 T cells, how each HIV test works, and why each opportunistic disease appears at its threshold, you can reason through most questions here.","prerequisites":["CD4 helper T cell subsets (Th1, Th2, Th17) and their cytokines (IM-5)","Macrophage activation by IFN-gamma and the structure of granulomas","B cell development, CD20 expression, and plasma cell differentiation","Retrovirus replication: reverse transcription and integration","Placental transfer of IgG and the immunology of the newborn"],"connections":[{"to":"Congenital immunodeficiencies (IM-15-A)","how":"The infection pattern of each acquired defect mirrors the matching inborn defect, so the same organism-to-defect reasoning applies."},{"to":"Microbiology","how":"Pneumocystis, Toxoplasma, Cryptococcus, CMV, EBV, HHV-8, and JC virus are the organisms that define the late stages of HIV disease."},{"to":"Pulmonary infections","how":"Reactivated tuberculosis after TNF-alpha blockade and Pneumocystis pneumonia after T cell suppression are common lung presentations of acquired immunodeficiency."},{"to":"Renal pathology","how":"HIV-associated nephropathy is a collapsing form of focal segmental glomerulosclerosis caused by direct infection of kidney epithelium."},{"to":"Oncology and viral oncogenesis","how":"Loss of T cell surveillance lets latent oncogenic herpesviruses drive Kaposi sarcoma and EBV-positive lymphomas."}],"terms":[{"term":"Calcineurin","def":"A calcium-dependent phosphatase in T cells that removes phosphate from NFAT so it can enter the nucleus and switch on the IL-2 gene."},{"term":"Absolute neutrophil count (ANC)","def":"The white cell count multiplied by the fraction of segmented and band neutrophils; below 500 cells per mm3 is severe neutropenia."},{"term":"Retrovirus","def":"An RNA virus that copies its genome into DNA with reverse transcriptase and integrates that DNA into the host genome."},{"term":"Provirus","def":"Viral DNA integrated into a host chromosome, which persists for the life of the cell and is copied with it."},{"term":"Coreceptor tropism","def":"Whether an HIV strain uses CCR5 (R5 strains) or CXCR4 (X4 strains), or both, together with CD4 to enter cells."},{"term":"Viral set point","def":"The relatively stable plasma viral load reached about 6 months after infection, once early immune responses have lowered peak viremia."},{"term":"Window period","def":"The interval after infection during which a given test cannot yet detect it."},{"term":"AIDS-defining illness","def":"An opportunistic infection or cancer that, in a person with HIV, establishes the diagnosis of AIDS regardless of CD4 count."},{"term":"Opportunistic infection","def":"An infection by an organism that rarely causes disease in a person with intact immunity."},{"term":"Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS)","def":"Clinical worsening caused by a recovering immune response against an infection or antigen already present, after immunity is restored."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-02-C01","obj":"IM-15-B.1","title":"Drug-induced immunodeficiency and the infections it unmasks","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Immunosuppressive drugs and cancer chemotherapy are a leading cause of acquired immunodeficiency. Each removes a particular defense, and the infection that follows is the one that defense normally holds back."},{"type":"h","text":"T cell suppression: glucocorticoids and calcineurin inhibitors"},{"type":"p","text":"Glucocorticoids bind a cytoplasmic receptor that moves to the nucleus and inhibits the transcription factors NF-kappaB and AP-1, in part by inducing the inhibitor IkappaB. Production of IL-1, IL-2, TNF-alpha, IFN-gamma, and many chemokines falls. Lymphocytes, monocytes, and eosinophils leave the blood (lymphopenia, monocytopenia, eosinopenia), while neutrophils rise because they detach from vessel walls, yet they migrate poorly into tissue. The net loss of T cell help and macrophage activation explains the classic risks: Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, reactivated tuberculosis, invasive fungi, and Strongyloides hyperinfection."},{"type":"p","text":"Cyclosporine binds cyclophilin and tacrolimus binds FKBP12; each complex inhibits calcineurin. NFAT stays phosphorylated and trapped in the cytoplasm, so activated T cells do not transcribe IL-2 and cannot expand clonally. Because cytotoxic T cells control latent herpesviruses, recipients develop CMV disease, EBV-driven post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder, BK polyomavirus nephropathy, Pneumocystis, and fungal infection."},{"type":"flow","title":"How calcineurin inhibitors stop T cell activation","steps":["T cell receptor signaling raises cytoplasmic Ca2+","Ca2+ and calmodulin activate calcineurin","Cyclosporine-cyclophilin or tacrolimus-FKBP12 blocks calcineurin","NFAT stays phosphorylated in the cytoplasm","No IL-2 transcription, so no clonal T cell expansion"],"caption":"Blocking one phosphatase removes the IL-2 signal that drives T cell proliferation."},{"type":"h","text":"TNF-alpha blockade, B cell depletion, and neutropenia"},{"type":"p","text":"TNF-alpha, made by macrophages and T cells, keeps macrophages activated and holds the granuloma together. Infliximab and adalimumab (monoclonal antibodies) and etanercept (a soluble receptor fusion protein) neutralize it, so granulomas that wall off latent Mycobacterium tuberculosis break down. Reactivation is often extrapulmonary or disseminated, and endemic granulomatous fungi (Histoplasma, Coccidioides) and Listeria also emerge."},{"type":"p","text":"Rituximab is a chimeric anti-CD20 antibody. CD20 is present from the late pre-B to the mature B cell stage but absent from most plasma cells, so circulating B cells disappear while serum IgG often falls only slowly. Hepatitis B virus persists as covalently closed circular DNA in hepatocytes, and B cells help keep it suppressed by making neutralizing antibody and presenting antigen; when they are removed the virus reactivates, even in people who are HBsAg-negative but anti-HBc-positive. Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy from JC virus is a rare but classic complication."},{"type":"p","text":"Cytotoxic chemotherapy kills dividing marrow precursors and mucosal epithelium. Neutropenia is an ANC below 1,500 cells per mm3 and is severe below 500. Without neutrophils there is little pus, so fever may be the only sign: neutropenic fever is a single temperature of at least 38.3 °C (100.9 °F) or at least 38.0 °C (100.4 °F) sustained over an hour. Gut and skin flora enter through mucositis and catheters, causing gram-negative bacteremia (Escherichia coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas aeruginosa) and gram-positive infection; when neutropenia lasts more than about 7 days, Candida and Aspergillus follow."},{"type":"table","title":"Immunosuppressive drugs and the infections they unmask","cols":["Drug","Defense removed","Characteristic infections"],"rows":[["Glucocorticoids","Cytokine transcription, lymphocyte and monocyte numbers, macrophage activation","Pneumocystis, tuberculosis, fungi, Strongyloides hyperinfection"],["Cyclosporine, tacrolimus","IL-2 production and T cell proliferation","CMV, EBV lymphoproliferation, BK virus, Pneumocystis, fungi"],["Infliximab, adalimumab, etanercept","TNF-alpha maintenance of granulomas","Reactivated tuberculosis, histoplasmosis, coccidioidomycosis, Listeria"],["Rituximab (anti-CD20)","B cells and new antibody responses","Hepatitis B reactivation, JC virus (PML)"],["Cytotoxic chemotherapy","Neutrophils and mucosal barriers","Gram-negative rods including Pseudomonas, gram-positive cocci, Candida, Aspergillus"]],"caption":"Each drug class removes one arm of immunity, and the expected organisms are those that arm normally contains."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-02-C02","obj":"IM-15-B.2","title":"HIV structure, genes, and entry","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"HIV is an enveloped retrovirus of the lentivirus genus. Each virion carries two copies of positive-sense single-stranded RNA inside a cone-shaped capsid, together with the enzymes needed to establish infection. HIV-1 causes most disease worldwide; HIV-2, concentrated in West Africa, is less transmissible and progresses more slowly."},{"type":"p","text":"Three structural genes are shared by all retroviruses. gag encodes the core proteins, including p24 (the capsid protein detected by fourth generation tests) and p17 (matrix). pol encodes reverse transcriptase, integrase, and protease. env encodes gp160, which a host protease cleaves into gp120, the surface protein that binds receptors, and gp41, the transmembrane protein that fuses membranes. HIV also carries regulatory and accessory genes; tat enhances transcription of the provirus, rev exports viral RNA from the nucleus, nef downregulates CD4 and MHC class I so infected cells escape cytotoxic T cells, and vif counteracts the host antiviral enzyme APOBEC3G."},{"type":"table","title":"HIV genes and their products","cols":["Gene","Product","Function"],"rows":[["gag","p24 capsid, p17 matrix","Structural core; p24 antigen appears in blood before antibody"],["pol","Reverse transcriptase","RNA-dependent and DNA-dependent DNA polymerase with RNase H activity; no proofreading"],["pol","Integrase","Inserts viral DNA into host DNA as a provirus"],["pol","Protease","Cleaves gag and gag-pol polyproteins so virions mature"],["env","gp120","Binds CD4, then CCR5 or CXCR4"],["env","gp41","Inserts into the host membrane and fuses it with the envelope"]],"caption":"The three structural genes of HIV, each a target of laboratory tests or of drugs that act on that step."},{"type":"p","text":"Entry is a two-step process. gp120 first binds CD4, which changes its shape and exposes a site that binds a chemokine receptor, CCR5 or CXCR4. Coreceptor binding releases gp41, whose fusion peptide inserts into the host membrane; gp41 then folds back on itself and pulls the two membranes together until they fuse. CD4 is found on helper T cells, macrophages, and dendritic cells, which explains the cells HIV infects. Dendritic cells in the mucosa also carry virions to lymph nodes, where CD4 T cells are concentrated."},{"type":"flow","title":"HIV life cycle","steps":["gp120 binds CD4, then CCR5 or CXCR4","gp41 fuses the viral envelope with the cell membrane","Reverse transcriptase copies viral RNA into double-stranded DNA","Integrase inserts the DNA into host chromosomes","Host RNA polymerase II transcribes viral RNA; polyproteins are made","Virions bud; protease cleaves polyproteins into mature infectious virus"],"caption":"Integration makes infection lifelong; maturation by protease occurs after budding."},{"type":"p","text":"Reverse transcriptase lacks proofreading and is highly error-prone, so each patient carries a swarm of related variants that can escape antibodies and T cells. Strains that use CCR5 (R5 strains) infect macrophages and memory T cells and dominate transmission and early infection. In many patients, strains that use CXCR4 (X4 strains) appear later and are associated with faster CD4 decline."},{"type":"p","text":"The CCR5 delta32 allele has a 32 base pair deletion that shifts the reading frame, producing a truncated protein that never reaches the cell surface. Homozygotes, about 1% of people of northern European descent, lack surface CCR5 and strongly resist infection by R5 strains. Protection is not absolute, since rare infections with X4 strains occur, and heterozygotes, with less surface CCR5, tend to progress more slowly."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man homozygous for CCR5 delta32 is exposed to a partner whose virus uses only CXCR4. Will he be protected?","explain":"No. The deletion removes CCR5 from the cell surface, which blocks R5 strains, but CXCR4 is still present, so gp120 of an X4 strain can bind CD4 and CXCR4 and trigger gp41 fusion.","check":"Compare with the entry steps and the paragraph on the delta32 allele."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-02-C03","obj":"IM-15-B.3","title":"Natural history of HIV infection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"HIV spreads through sexual contact, blood, and from mother to child. Receptive anal intercourse carries the highest sexual risk per act, and genital ulcers and other sexually transmitted infections raise risk by breaching mucosa and recruiting target cells. Parenteral spread now comes mainly from shared needles, since donated blood is screened. Perinatal transmission occurs in utero, during delivery (the largest share where infants are not breastfed), and through breastfeeding. Across all routes, the viral load of the source is the strongest determinant of risk."},{"type":"h","text":"Acute infection"},{"type":"p","text":"After mucosal entry, R5 virus infects memory CD4 T cells, which are abundant in gut-associated lymphoid tissue. Within weeks a large fraction of mucosal CD4 T cells is destroyed, and plasma viral load often exceeds a million copies per mL, making this the most infectious stage. About 2 to 6 weeks after exposure, many patients develop acute retroviral syndrome, a mononucleosis-like illness of fever, pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy, maculopapular rash, mucocutaneous ulcers, and myalgia, sometimes with aseptic meningitis. The CD4 count dips transiently, and antibody tests may still be negative."},{"type":"p","text":"Cytotoxic CD8 T cells, followed by antibodies, then lower viremia to a set point reached about 6 months after infection. A higher set point predicts faster progression."},{"type":"h","text":"Clinical latency and progression"},{"type":"p","text":"Clinical latency is not viral latency. For a median of about 8 to 10 years without treatment, the patient may be well or have persistent generalized lymphadenopathy, but billions of virions are produced each day, mostly in lymphoid tissue, where follicular dendritic cells trap virus on their surface. Integrated provirus in resting memory T cells forms a long-lived reservoir."},{"type":"p","text":"CD4 T cells are lost by several mechanisms: direct cytopathic killing, pyroptosis of abortively infected bystander cells, killing of infected cells by CD8 T cells, and chronic immune activation that drives cells to divide and die. Bacterial products leak through the damaged gut mucosa into the blood and sustain that activation, and fibrosis of lymphoid tissue limits replacement. B cells are activated polyclonally, so total immunoglobulin rises while specific antibody responses to new antigens are poor."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Immune activation drives HIV progression","steps":["HIV replicates in lymphoid tissue","Gut CD4 loss lets bacterial products enter blood","Chronic immune activation recruits and activates CD4 T cells","Activated CD4 T cells are the best targets for HIV"],"caption":"Activation creates new target cells, which sustains replication and progressive CD4 depletion."},{"type":"p","text":"A normal adult CD4 count is roughly 500 to 1,500 cells per mm3. AIDS is defined by a CD4 count below 200 cells per mm3 (or a CD4 percentage below 14%) or by any AIDS-defining illness, whatever the count. Below 200, Th1 help for macrophages and CD8 T cells is too weak to contain organisms that live within cells or remain latent."},{"type":"flow","title":"From HIV exposure to AIDS","steps":["Mucosal or blood exposure; R5 virus infects CD4 cells","Acute infection: peak viremia and gut CD4 depletion","CD8 T cells and antibodies lower viremia to a set point","Years of lymphoid replication with steady CD4 decline","CD4 below 200 per mm3 or AIDS-defining illness: AIDS"],"caption":"Untreated progression is set mainly by the viral set point."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-02-C04","obj":"IM-15-B.4","title":"Interpreting HIV tests","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each HIV test detects a different marker, and each marker appears at a different time after infection. Viral RNA appears first, about 10 days after exposure, then p24 antigen a few days later, and finally antibody, IgM before IgG, after roughly 3 weeks or more. A test is negative during its window period even though the person is infected and highly infectious."},{"type":"p","text":"Laboratory screening uses a fourth generation antigen and antibody immunoassay, which detects p24 antigen together with antibodies to HIV-1 and HIV-2. Because p24 appears before antibody, it narrows the window period compared with antibody-only tests, to roughly 2 to 6 weeks. A reactive screen is followed by an HIV-1 and HIV-2 antibody differentiation assay, which confirms antibody and identifies the virus type. If the differentiation assay is negative or indeterminate, an HIV-1 nucleic acid test (NAT) decides the case: RNA present means acute HIV-1 infection before antibody has formed, and RNA absent means the screen was falsely reactive. This sequence replaced the older Western blot, which missed early infections."},{"type":"flow","title":"Laboratory HIV diagnostic sequence","steps":["Fourth generation antigen and antibody immunoassay","Nonreactive: no infection unless exposure was recent","Reactive: HIV-1 and HIV-2 antibody differentiation assay","Antibody positive: HIV-1, HIV-2, or both confirmed","Negative or indeterminate: HIV-1 nucleic acid test","RNA positive: acute HIV-1; RNA negative: false-positive screen"],"caption":"Each step answers the question the previous one could not."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a reactive screen with no antibody","text":"A woman has fever, sore throat, rash, and cervical lymphadenopathy 3 weeks after condomless sex with a new partner. Her fourth generation immunoassay is reactive, but the differentiation assay is negative. The screen detected something, yet she has no antibody to HIV-1 or HIV-2. At 3 weeks, p24 antigen and RNA are present while antibody may not yet be. The HIV-1 RNA test is strongly positive, confirming acute HIV-1 infection. Had the RNA been negative, the reactive screen would have been a false positive. Repeating antibody testing weeks later would show seroconversion."},{"type":"h","text":"Monitoring tests"},{"type":"p","text":"Once infection is confirmed, two measurements describe it. The CD4 count, measured by flow cytometry, stages immune damage and predicts which opportunistic infections are likely now. The plasma viral load, HIV-1 RNA copies per mL by quantitative nucleic acid amplification, measures how fast the virus is replicating and therefore how fast the CD4 count will fall; it is also the main determinant of transmission risk. The two answer different questions and cannot substitute for each other. Standard HIV-1 viral load assays do not reliably quantify HIV-2."},{"type":"h","text":"Infants of infected mothers"},{"type":"p","text":"Maternal IgG crosses the placenta through neonatal Fc receptors and can persist in the infant for up to about 18 months. Every infant of an infected mother is therefore antibody-positive at birth, whether or not the infant is infected, and a positive antibody or fourth generation result proves only maternal infection. Diagnosis in infants under 18 months relies on HIV NAT (RNA or proviral DNA) repeated at intervals in the first months of life, because only direct detection of the virus shows infant infection. Loss of maternal antibody later confirms an uninfected child."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 4-month-old born to a mother with HIV has a reactive fourth generation test. Is the infant infected?","explain":"Not necessarily. The antibody component detects transplacental maternal IgG, which persists for many months, so the result cannot separate maternal antibody from infant infection. Only an HIV nucleic acid test shows whether the virus is present in the infant.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on infants of infected mothers."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-02-C05","obj":"IM-15-B.5","title":"Opportunistic infections by CD4 count","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"CD4 T cells defend against intracellular and latent organisms in two ways. Th1 cells secrete IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages to kill organisms they have ingested, such as mycobacteria, Toxoplasma, Pneumocystis, and fungi. CD4 help also sustains the CD8 T cells that keep latent herpesviruses (CMV, EBV) and JC virus under control. Mucosal Th17 cells recruit neutrophils and induce antimicrobial peptides that contain Candida. As the count falls, these defenses fail in a predictable order, so the CD4 count tells you which organisms are likely. The thresholds are approximate: each infection becomes common below its threshold, not impossible above it."},{"type":"h","text":"Above 200: mucosal and reactivation diseases"},{"type":"p","text":"Oral candidiasis (thrush) produces white plaques that scrape off to leave a red base. Oral hairy leukoplakia is an EBV-driven hyperplasia of epithelium on the lateral tongue, with corrugated white plaques that do not scrape off. Herpes zoster, bacterial pneumonia from Streptococcus pneumoniae, and tuberculosis increase at any count, because they need only partial loss of immunity; in advanced disease tuberculosis is more often extrapulmonary and forms poor granulomas."},{"type":"h","text":"Below 200, 100, and 50"},{"type":"p","text":"Pneumocystis jirovecii, a fungus, causes subacute dry cough, fever, and exertional hypoxemia, with bilateral interstitial or ground-glass infiltrates and elevated lactate dehydrogenase; cysts are seen with silver stain. Below 100, Toxoplasma gondii reactivates from tissue cysts in seropositive patients to cause encephalitis with multiple ring-enhancing lesions, often in the basal ganglia and at the gray-white junction. Cryptococcus neoformans meningitis, chronic Cryptosporidium diarrhea, and Candida esophagitis also cluster here. Below 50, CMV retinitis causes painless vision loss with retinal necrosis and hemorrhage, and disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) causes fever, night sweats, weight loss, diarrhea, anemia, hepatosplenomegaly, and a raised alkaline phosphatase."},{"type":"table","title":"Opportunistic infections by CD4 count (cells per mm3)","cols":["CD4 count","Infection","Key finding and mechanism"],"rows":[["Below 500","Oral candidiasis","White plaques that scrape off; Th17 loss at mucosa"],["Below 500","Oral hairy leukoplakia (EBV)","Lateral tongue plaques that do not scrape off"],["Any count, rising as CD4 falls","Tuberculosis, zoster, pneumococcal pneumonia","Reactivation and encapsulated bacteria; atypical TB when CD4 is low"],["Below 200","Pneumocystis pneumonia","Dry cough, hypoxemia, bilateral ground-glass infiltrates"],["Below 200","Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (JC virus)","Nonenhancing white matter lesions; oligodendrocyte infection"],["Below 100","Toxoplasma encephalitis","Multiple ring-enhancing lesions; IgG seropositive"],["Below 100","Cryptococcal meningitis","Headache, raised opening pressure, capsular antigen in CSF"],["Below 100","Candida esophagitis, Cryptosporidium","Painful swallowing; chronic watery diarrhea"],["Below 50","CMV retinitis, colitis","Retinal necrosis with hemorrhage; owl's eye inclusions"],["Below 50","Disseminated MAC","Fever, weight loss, diarrhea, anemia, raised alkaline phosphatase"]],"caption":"Organisms appear in order as successive layers of CD4-dependent defense fail."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does Pneumocystis appear at a higher CD4 count than CMV retinitis?","explain":"Pneumocystis is contained mainly by alveolar macrophages activated through CD4 Th1 help, so moderate loss of CD4 cells allows it to grow. CMV is held latent by CD8 T cells, which still function until CD4 help is almost gone.","check":"Compare with the first paragraph of this section and the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-02-C06","obj":"IM-15-B.6","title":"Noninfectious complications of HIV and AIDS","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Noninfectious complications fall into three groups: virus-driven cancers that T cells no longer control, organ injury from HIV itself, and inflammation when immunity is restored."},{"type":"h","text":"Virus-driven cancers"},{"type":"p","text":"Kaposi sarcoma is caused by human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8, also called KSHV). Latent viral proteins drive proliferation and survival (LANA binds and inhibits p53 and Rb; vFLIP activates NF-kappaB). Infected spindle cells of endothelial origin form slit-like vascular spaces filled with extravasated red cells and hemosiderin. Lesions are red-purple macules, plaques, or nodules on the skin, oral palate, gut, and lungs. They can appear at any CD4 count. Bacillary angiomatosis (Bartonella) looks similar but shows neutrophils."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary CNS lymphoma is an EBV-positive diffuse large B cell lymphoma, usually at CD4 counts below 50. Without cytotoxic T cell surveillance, EBV-infected B cells proliferate unchecked. It typically forms a single, often periventricular, ring-enhancing lesion, and EBV DNA may be found in the cerebrospinal fluid. Systemic B cell lymphomas (including Burkitt lymphoma) and HPV-related cervical and anal carcinoma are also increased."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a brain lesion at CD4 of 40","text":"A man with untreated AIDS and a CD4 count of 40 cells per mm3 has headache and focal weakness. Imaging shows ring-enhancing lesions. At this count, the leading causes are Toxoplasma encephalitis and primary CNS lymphoma. Several lesions in the basal ganglia with positive Toxoplasma IgG favor toxoplasmosis, because reactivation needs prior infection. A single periventricular lesion with negative Toxoplasma IgG and EBV DNA in the cerebrospinal fluid favors lymphoma. Nonenhancing white matter lesions without mass effect point instead to PML."},{"type":"h","text":"Direct HIV injury"},{"type":"p","text":"HIV-associated nephropathy (HIVAN) is a collapsing form of focal segmental glomerulosclerosis. HIV infects podocytes and tubular epithelium, causing the glomerular capillary tuft to collapse beneath hypertrophied, proliferating podocytes, with microcystic tubular dilation. Patients have nephrotic-range proteinuria, large echogenic kidneys, and rapid progression to kidney failure. It occurs mostly in people of African ancestry with two APOL1 risk variants and high viral loads."},{"type":"p","text":"HIV enters the brain early inside infected monocytes and infects perivascular macrophages and microglia, but not neurons. Viral proteins (gp120, Tat), inflammatory cytokines, and excess glutamate injure neurons indirectly. HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder is a subcortical pattern of slowed thinking, apathy, and motor slowing, ranging from mild impairment to dementia, with microglial nodules and multinucleated giant cells. Distal symmetric sensory polyneuropathy is a length-dependent axonal neuropathy with burning feet and reduced ankle reflexes, caused by viral proteins and inflammation acting on dorsal root ganglia and axons."},{"type":"h","text":"Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"When antiretroviral therapy lowers the viral load, CD4 T cells recover, usually within weeks to a few months, and mount a vigorous Th1 response against antigens already present. Paradoxical IRIS is worsening of a known infection; unmasking IRIS is the first appearance of one that was subclinical. Risk is highest with a low starting CD4 count and preexisting tuberculosis, MAC, Cryptococcus, or CMV; fever, enlarging nodes, new infiltrates, or worsening meningitis follow because immunity is improving, not failing."},{"type":"table","title":"Noninfectious complications and their causes","cols":["Complication","Cause","Key finding"],"rows":[["Kaposi sarcoma","HHV-8 in spindle cells of endothelial origin","Purple plaques; slit-like vessels with red cells"],["Primary CNS lymphoma","EBV-driven B cell proliferation","Single ring-enhancing lesion; EBV DNA in CSF"],["HIV-associated nephropathy","HIV infection of podocytes; APOL1 variants","Collapsing FSGS; nephrotic proteinuria"],["Neurocognitive disorder","Infected microglia and macrophages; neurotoxins","Subcortical slowing, apathy, motor signs"],["Distal sensory polyneuropathy","Viral proteins and inflammation in sensory neurons","Burning feet, reduced or absent ankle reflexes"],["IRIS","Recovering T cells attack existing antigen","Worsening weeks after therapy begins"]],"caption":"Cancers follow loss of viral surveillance; organ injury follows direct infection; IRIS follows recovery."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Acquired immunodeficiency ties immunology to microbiology and pathology through one principle: the lost defense predicts the disease. Drugs remove a single arm, so their infections are narrow and predictable, while HIV erodes CD4 help progressively, so infections appear in order as each defense fails. Molecular virology explains the tests (p24 before antibody, RNA before both) and the drug targets, and transplacental IgG explains why infants need nucleic acid testing. Pathology adds the herpesvirus cancers and direct organ injury that follow loss of surveillance."},{"type":"p","text":"The thresholds and drug associations are probabilities, not rules. Tuberculosis and Kaposi sarcoma occur at any CD4 count, several infections share similar thresholds, and patients often take more than one immunosuppressant. Interpret each finding with the CD4 count, serology, and imaging."}],"summary":["Glucocorticoids and calcineurin inhibitors suppress T cell cytokines and IL-2, causing Pneumocystis, herpesvirus, and fungal infections.","TNF-alpha holds granulomas together, so TNF blockade reactivates latent tuberculosis and endemic fungi.","Rituximab depletes CD20 B cells, allowing hepatitis B reactivation and, rarely, JC virus PML.","Severe neutropenia (ANC below 500 per mm3) causes gram-negative bacteremia and, if prolonged, Candida and Aspergillus, often with fever as the only sign.","HIV gp120 binds CD4 and then CCR5 or CXCR4, and gp41 mediates fusion; CCR5 delta32 homozygotes resist R5 strains.","Acute HIV brings peak viremia and gut CD4 loss; clinical latency hides ongoing lymphoid replication; AIDS is CD4 below 200 per mm3 or an AIDS-defining illness.","Fourth generation screening detects p24 and antibody; a negative differentiation assay after a reactive screen calls for HIV-1 RNA testing.","CD4 count measures immune damage, viral load measures replication, and infants need nucleic acid testing because maternal IgG persists up to about 18 months.","Thrush and hairy leukoplakia appear early, Pneumocystis below 200, Toxoplasma below 100, and CMV retinitis and MAC below 50.","Kaposi sarcoma (HHV-8), CNS lymphoma (EBV), collapsing FSGS, neurocognitive disorder, neuropathy, and IRIS are the main noninfectious complications."],"sources":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Congenital and Acquired Immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System, acquired immunodeficiency syndrome"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"AIDS and Lentiviruses"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Immune System, acquired immunodeficiency syndrome"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney, focal segmental glomerulosclerosis and HIV-associated nephropathy"}]},"IM-15-C":{"unit":"IM-15-C","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03","title":"Diseases of Hypersensitivity","subject":"Immune System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"Hypersensitivity is an adaptive immune response that injures the host. This chapter uses the Gell and Coombs classification as a set of four mechanisms: IgE and mast cells (type I), antibody against fixed cell or matrix antigens (type II), deposited immune complexes (type III), and T cells (type IV). Each disease is taught from its mechanism, so that timing, tissue distribution, serology, complement levels, and immunofluorescence pattern can be predicted rather than memorized. Board questions usually give a vignette and ask for the mechanism, the expected laboratory or biopsy finding, or the type of reaction, and real diseases often combine more than one type.","prerequisites":["Antibody classes and their effector functions (complement fixation, Fc receptor binding)","Complement pathways, C3a and C5a, opsonization, and the membrane attack complex","CD4 T helper subsets (Th1, Th2, Th17), CD8 cytotoxic T cells, and their cytokines","MHC class I and II and the basis of self tolerance","Glomerular structure and the nephritic syndrome"],"connections":[{"to":"Hematology","how":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia and drug-induced hemolysis are read with the direct Coombs test, the smear, and markers of red cell destruction."},{"to":"Renal pathology","how":"Linear, granular, and pauci-immune immunofluorescence patterns separate anti-GBM disease, immune complex glomerulonephritis, and ANCA vasculitis."},{"to":"Neuromuscular physiology","how":"The safety factor of neuromuscular transmission explains why myasthenia gravis weakness is fatigable."},{"to":"Microbiology","how":"Group A Streptococcus, Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Epstein-Barr virus, hepatitis B, and Mycobacterium tuberculosis each trigger a hypersensitivity disease in this chapter."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal system","how":"Rheumatoid synovitis and lupus arthritis show how immune injury in the joint can be erosive or nonerosive."}],"terms":[{"term":"Hypersensitivity","def":"An adaptive immune response, against a foreign or self antigen, that is excessive or misdirected and damages tissue."},{"term":"Sensitization","def":"The first exposure to an antigen that produces the antibodies or memory T cells that cause injury on later exposure."},{"term":"Degranulation","def":"Exocytosis of preformed mediators such as histamine and tryptase from mast cell or basophil granules."},{"term":"Opsonization","def":"Coating of a target by IgG or C3b so that phagocytes with matching receptors bind and remove it."},{"term":"Direct Coombs test","def":"A test in which anti-human globulin added to washed patient red cells causes agglutination if the cells are coated with IgG or C3; also called the direct antiglobulin test."},{"term":"Immune complex","def":"A lattice of antigen bound to antibody that can fix complement and deposit in vessels, glomeruli, and synovium."},{"term":"Crescent","def":"A proliferation of parietal epithelial cells and macrophages with fibrin in Bowman space, marking severe glomerular injury."},{"term":"Granuloma","def":"A focal aggregate of activated (epithelioid) macrophages, often with multinucleated giant cells and a lymphocyte rim, that walls off persistent antigen."},{"term":"Caseous necrosis","def":"Cheese-like central necrosis of a granuloma that leaves amorphous granular debris, typical of tuberculosis."},{"term":"Hapten","def":"A small molecule, such as a penicillin metabolite, that becomes immunogenic only after it binds a host protein."},{"term":"Fibrinoid necrosis","def":"Vessel wall injury in which deposited immune complexes, plasma proteins, and fibrin give a bright pink smudged appearance."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C01","obj":"IM-15-C.1","title":"Type I hypersensitivity: IgE and mast cells","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Type I (immediate) hypersensitivity is a reaction of preformed IgE bound to mast cells and basophils. It needs two exposures. During sensitization, dendritic cells present the allergen to naive CD4 T cells, which in an atopic host differentiate into Th2 cells. Th2 cells secrete IL-4 and IL-13, which, together with CD40 ligand on the T cell, switch B cells to IgE production; IL-5 matures and activates eosinophils. Free IgE has a serum half-life of only a few days, but once it binds the high-affinity Fc epsilon receptor I (FcepsilonRI) on mast cells, it can arm them for weeks to months."},{"type":"p","text":"On re-exposure, a multivalent allergen bridges two or more adjacent IgE molecules. Cross-linking clusters FcepsilonRI, activates tyrosine kinases, and raises cytosolic Ca2+. Monovalent antigen cannot do this, which is why cross-linking, not binding, is the trigger. Three classes of mediator follow. Preformed granule contents (histamine, tryptase, heparin) are released within seconds to minutes. Membrane phospholipid yields arachidonic acid, which 5-lipoxygenase converts to leukotrienes C4, D4, and E4 and cyclooxygenase converts to prostaglandin D2 over minutes. Cytokines (TNF, IL-4, IL-5, IL-13) are transcribed over hours."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sensitization and re-exposure in type I hypersensitivity","steps":["Dendritic cell presents allergen to naive CD4 T cell","Th2 cell releases IL-4 and IL-13","B cell switches to IgE","IgE binds FcepsilonRI on mast cells","Re-exposure: allergen cross-links bound IgE","Degranulation: histamine and tryptase within minutes","Leukotrienes, PGD2, cytokines; eosinophils arrive hours later"],"caption":"The first exposure builds the response; only the second exposure causes symptoms."},{"type":"p","text":"The immediate phase peaks within about 5 to 30 minutes. Histamine acting on H1 receptors dilates arterioles, opens endothelial gaps in postcapillary venules, contracts bronchial and gut smooth muscle, and stimulates sensory nerves (itch). Leukotrienes are far more potent and longer-acting bronchoconstrictors and also increase permeability and mucus secretion. The late phase begins about 2 to 8 hours later and can last a day: cytokines and chemokines recruit eosinophils, Th2 cells, basophils, and neutrophils, and eosinophil granule proteins (major basic protein) damage epithelium. The late phase explains why allergic asthma persists after the initial bronchospasm and why repeated reactions cause chronic airway remodeling."},{"type":"table","title":"Mast cell mediators and their effects","cols":["Mediator","Timing","Main effects"],"rows":[["Histamine","Preformed; seconds to minutes","Vasodilation, venular leak, bronchoconstriction, itch"],["Tryptase","Preformed; seconds to minutes","Protease; marker of mast cell activation in serum"],["Leukotrienes C4, D4, E4","Synthesized; minutes","Prolonged bronchoconstriction, leak, mucus"],["Prostaglandin D2","Synthesized; minutes","Bronchoconstriction, vasodilation"],["IL-4, IL-5, IL-13, TNF","Transcribed; hours","IgE production, eosinophil recruitment, late phase"]],"caption":"Preformed mediators drive the first minutes; synthesized lipids and cytokines sustain the reaction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C02","obj":"IM-15-C.2","title":"Anaphylaxis and serum tryptase","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Anaphylaxis is a rapid, systemic, potentially fatal type I reaction in which antigen reaches mast cells in many tissues at once. The common triggers are foods (peanut, tree nuts, shellfish, milk, egg), Hymenoptera venom (bee, wasp, fire ant), drugs (penicillins and other beta-lactams), and natural rubber latex. Penicillin is too small to be immunogenic alone; its breakdown product, the benzylpenicilloyl group, binds host proteins covalently as a hapten and creates the epitope that IgE recognizes. The same picture can occur without IgE when agents such as iodinated contrast, opioids, or vancomycin activate mast cells directly; this is still anaphylaxis clinically but is not a type I reaction."},{"type":"p","text":"Each sign follows from a mediator effect. Dermal venular leak produces urticaria (raised, itchy wheals), and leak in the deeper dermis and submucosa produces angioedema of the lips, tongue, and larynx, giving hoarseness and stridor. Leukotrienes and histamine contract bronchial smooth muscle, causing wheeze and dyspnea. Generalized arteriolar dilation and capillary leak, which can shift a large fraction of plasma volume out of the circulation within minutes, cause distributive shock with tachycardia and hypotension. Gut smooth muscle contraction causes cramping, vomiting, and diarrhea. Onset is usually within minutes of injected or stung antigen and within about 2 hours of ingestion, and some patients have a second (biphasic) reaction hours later without new exposure."},{"type":"table","title":"Manifestations of anaphylaxis explained by mechanism","cols":["System","Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Skin and mucosa","Urticaria, flushing, pruritus, angioedema","Histamine on H1 receptors; venular leak"],["Upper airway","Hoarseness, stridor","Laryngeal edema"],["Lower airway","Wheeze, dyspnea","Leukotriene and histamine bronchoconstriction, mucus"],["Cardiovascular","Hypotension, tachycardia, syncope","Vasodilation and loss of plasma volume"],["Gastrointestinal","Cramps, vomiting, diarrhea","Smooth muscle contraction, mucosal edema"]],"caption":"Skin findings are the most common, but airway obstruction and shock cause death."},{"type":"p","text":"Serum tryptase confirms mast cell activation after the fact. Histamine peaks within minutes and is cleared within about an hour, so it is rarely captured. Tryptase peaks about 1 to 2 hours after onset and has a half-life of about 2 hours, so blood drawn roughly 15 minutes to 3 hours after onset is most useful, compared with a baseline drawn at least 24 hours after recovery. A commonly used rule counts a rise as significant when the acute value exceeds 120% of baseline plus 2 ng/mL. A normal tryptase does not exclude anaphylaxis, because food-induced reactions, especially in children, often involve less mast cell tryptase release. A persistently high baseline (above about 11 ng/mL) points to a mast cell disorder such as systemic mastocytosis or to hereditary alpha-tryptasemia."},{"type":"example","title":"Interpreting a tryptase rise","text":"A woman develops hives, wheeze, and a blood pressure of 78/40 mm Hg ten minutes after a wasp sting. Tryptase drawn 90 minutes after onset is 14 ng/mL; her baseline, drawn a week later, is 5 ng/mL. The threshold is 1.2 times 5 plus 2, which is 8 ng/mL. Her acute value of 14 ng/mL exceeds it, so the episode involved systemic mast cell degranulation, consistent with venom anaphylaxis. Her baseline is normal, so a mast cell disorder is not suggested. If the acute value had been 7 ng/mL, the rise would not reach the threshold, but the clinical diagnosis would still stand, because tryptase is specific for mast cell activation but not fully sensitive."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C03","obj":"IM-15-C.3","title":"Warm and cold autoimmune hemolytic anemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA) is a type II reaction in which autoantibody binds antigens on the patient's own red cells. The antibody class decides the temperature at which it binds, whether it fixes complement, and where the red cells are destroyed. Both forms share the general signs of hemolysis: anemia, reticulocytosis, raised unconjugated bilirubin and LDH, and low haptoglobin. Both are proven by a positive direct Coombs test (direct antiglobulin test), in which anti-human globulin added to washed patient red cells agglutinates them if IgG or C3 is already on their surface."},{"type":"p","text":"Warm AIHA, the more common form, is caused by IgG (usually IgG1 or IgG3) that binds best at 37 °C (98.6 °F), often to Rh-related membrane proteins. IgG fixes complement weakly, so coated cells are not lysed in the circulation. Instead, splenic macrophages bind them through Fc gamma receptors and remove pieces of membrane. A red cell that loses membrane without losing volume becomes a spherocyte, which is rigid and is trapped and destroyed in the splenic cords. This is extravascular hemolysis, and the spleen enlarges. About half of cases are idiopathic; the rest accompany SLE, chronic lymphocytic leukemia and other lymphomas, or drugs. Spherocytes with a positive Coombs test separate warm AIHA from hereditary spherocytosis, which is Coombs negative."},{"type":"p","text":"Cold agglutinin disease is caused by IgM, which binds red cells best at about 0 to 4 °C (32 to 39 °F) and still binds in the cooler blood of the fingers, toes, ears, and nose. Pentameric IgM agglutinates red cells, causing acrocyanosis in the cold, and fixes C1 strongly. When the blood returns to the warm core, IgM detaches, but C3b remains, so the Coombs test detects C3 rather than IgG. Kupffer cells in the liver remove C3b-coated cells, and some cells proceed to the membrane attack complex and lyse in the vessels, causing hemoglobinuria. Secondary cases follow Mycoplasma pneumoniae (anti-I antibody) and Epstein-Barr virus infectious mononucleosis (anti-i), are polyclonal, and resolve within weeks. Chronic primary disease reflects a clonal B cell disorder that makes monoclonal IgM. Agglutinates on the smear can also produce a falsely high MCV on automated counters."},{"type":"table","title":"Warm AIHA versus cold agglutinin disease","cols":["Feature","Warm AIHA","Cold agglutinin disease"],"rows":[["Antibody","IgG","IgM"],["Optimal binding","37 °C (98.6 °F)","0 to 4 °C (32 to 39 °F)"],["Complement","Weak fixation","Strong fixation (C3b)"],["Site of destruction","Spleen (extravascular)","Liver; some intravascular lysis"],["Smear","Spherocytes","Red cell agglutinates"],["Direct Coombs","IgG, sometimes also C3","C3 only"],["Associations","Idiopathic, SLE, CLL, drugs","Mycoplasma pneumoniae, EBV, clonal B cell disorders"],["Physical clue","Splenomegaly","Acrocyanosis in the cold"]],"caption":"The antibody class predicts temperature, complement use, the organ that destroys the cells, and the Coombs pattern."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C04","obj":"IM-15-C.4","title":"Goodpasture syndrome (anti-GBM disease)","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Anti-glomerular basement membrane (anti-GBM) disease is a type II reaction against a structural antigen. IgG autoantibodies bind the noncollagenous (NC1) domain of the alpha3 chain of type IV collagen. This chain is concentrated in the glomerular basement membrane and the alveolar basement membrane, which explains why the kidney and lung are the two targets. In the intact collagen network the epitope is partly hidden, and an injury that exposes it is thought to allow antibody access. Susceptibility is strongly linked to HLA-DR15. Goodpasture syndrome refers to the combination of glomerulonephritis and pulmonary hemorrhage; some patients have kidney disease alone."},{"type":"p","text":"Bound IgG activates complement and engages Fc receptors on neutrophils and macrophages, which release proteases and oxidants that break the basement membrane. Fibrin and plasma proteins then leak into Bowman space, and parietal epithelial cells and infiltrating macrophages proliferate into crescents that compress the glomerular tuft. When crescents involve most glomeruli, filtration falls over days to weeks, which is rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (RPGN), presenting as a nephritic syndrome with hematuria, red cell casts, proteinuria, and rising creatinine. Because the antigen is a continuous sheet, immunofluorescence shows smooth linear IgG along the capillary walls rather than the lumpy granular deposits of immune complexes."},{"type":"flow","title":"From anti-GBM antibody to crescents","steps":["IgG binds alpha3 chain of type IV collagen","Complement activation and Fc receptor binding","Neutrophils and macrophages break the basement membrane","Fibrin leaks into Bowman space","Parietal cells and macrophages form crescents","Glomerular filtration falls: rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis"],"caption":"The same antibody causes alveolar hemorrhage when the alveolar basement membrane is accessible."},{"type":"p","text":"The alveolar basement membrane is normally shielded by tight alveolar endothelium, so lung hemorrhage occurs mostly when it is injured, especially in cigarette smokers and after inhaled hydrocarbons or infection. Bleeding into the alveoli causes hemoptysis, diffuse infiltrates, hypoxemia, iron deficiency anemia, and hemosiderin-laden macrophages in the sputum or lavage. The disease has a bimodal pattern: younger men often present with the pulmonary-renal form, and older adults more often with kidney disease alone. Serum anti-GBM antibody supports the diagnosis, and a minority of patients are also positive for MPO-ANCA."},{"type":"table","title":"Immunofluorescence types of crescentic glomerulonephritis","cols":["Pattern","Mechanism","Examples"],"rows":[["Linear IgG","Antibody to a continuous basement membrane antigen","Anti-GBM disease, Goodpasture syndrome"],["Granular immune complexes","Deposited or in situ immune complexes","Lupus nephritis, PSGN, IgA nephropathy"],["Pauci-immune (little or no staining)","ANCA-activated neutrophils","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis, microscopic polyangiitis"]],"caption":"The staining pattern reflects the shape of the antigen and the mechanism of injury."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C05","obj":"IM-15-C.5","title":"Myasthenia gravis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Myasthenia gravis is a type II reaction in which antibody impairs the function of a receptor without killing the muscle fiber, which is why it is called noncytotoxic. In about 85% of generalized cases, IgG binds the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (AChR) on the postsynaptic membrane of the neuromuscular junction. These antibodies act in three ways: they fix complement and damage the tips of the junctional folds, they cross-link receptors and speed their internalization and degradation, and some block the acetylcholine binding site. Fewer receptors and simplified folds mean that each quantum of acetylcholine produces a smaller end-plate potential. A smaller group has IgG4 antibodies to muscle-specific kinase (MuSK), which does not fix complement but disrupts the clustering of AChRs."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal transmission has a safety factor: the end-plate potential is several times larger than needed to reach threshold. During sustained activity, acetylcholine release per impulse falls as the readily releasable vesicle pool is depleted. Healthy junctions tolerate this, but in myasthenia the reduced end-plate potential drops below threshold in more and more fibers, so strength fades with use and recovers with rest. Repetitive nerve stimulation at about 3 Hz shows a decremental compound muscle action potential for the same reason."},{"type":"p","text":"Extraocular muscles and the levator palpebrae are affected first in most patients, giving ptosis and diplopia that worsen late in the day; their high firing rates and low safety factor are the usual explanation. Generalized disease adds proximal limb weakness, bulbar weakness (dysarthria, dysphagia, nasal speech), and in a crisis respiratory failure. Sensation and reflexes are preserved, and the pupils are spared because the iris muscles are autonomically controlled smooth muscle, which lacks the skeletal muscle nicotinic receptor. Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase prolongs acetylcholine in the cleft and transiently improves strength, consistent with a shortage of functional postsynaptic receptors."},{"type":"p","text":"The thymus is central. Thymic myoid cells express AChR, and thymic follicular hyperplasia with germinal centers is found in most young patients with AChR antibodies. About 10 to 15% of patients have a thymoma, and a substantial minority of patients with thymoma develop myasthenia. MuSK-positive disease rarely shows thymic pathology."},{"type":"table","title":"Myasthenia gravis versus Lambert-Eaton syndrome","cols":["Feature","Myasthenia gravis","Lambert-Eaton syndrome"],"rows":[["Target","Postsynaptic AChR or MuSK","Presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channel"],["Effect of repeated use","Weakness worsens","Strength briefly improves"],["Repetitive stimulation","Decrement at low rates","Increment at high rates"],["Ocular involvement","Early and prominent","Mild or absent"],["Autonomic signs and reflexes","Absent; reflexes normal","Dry mouth; reflexes reduced"],["Association","Thymic hyperplasia, thymoma","Small cell lung carcinoma"]],"caption":"The side of the junction that is attacked decides whether activity worsens or improves strength."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has drooping eyelids that worsen through the day, normal pupils, and normal reflexes. Why are the pupils spared?","explain":"The antibodies target the nicotinic receptor of skeletal muscle. Pupil size is set by autonomically controlled smooth muscle (muscarinic sphincter, adrenergic dilator), which lacks that receptor, so pupils remain normal.","check":"The manifestations paragraph and the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C06","obj":"IM-15-C.6","title":"Systemic lupus erythematosus: pathogenesis and serology","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is a chronic autoimmune disease driven by autoantibodies to nuclear antigens. Most of its tissue injury is type III (immune complex deposition), while its cytopenias are type II (antibody against blood cells). The central defect is loss of tolerance to nuclear material that is normally hidden or quickly removed."},{"type":"p","text":"Apoptotic cells are normally cleared by phagocytes before they release their contents, with help from opsonins such as C1q. In SLE this clearance is impaired. Inherited deficiency of early classical complement components carries a high risk of lupus (over 90% with C1q deficiency, lower with C4 and C2), which shows that complement protects against autoimmunity as well as causing injury. Ultraviolet light increases keratinocyte apoptosis, explaining photosensitivity. Persisting DNA, nucleosomes, and ribonucleoproteins bind antibody, and the complexes are taken up by plasmacytoid dendritic cells and B cells through Fc receptors and B cell receptors. Inside endosomes, self RNA engages TLR7 and self DNA engages TLR9, driving type I interferon production and activating autoreactive B cells. Female sex hormones, genes such as HLA-DR2 and HLA-DR3, and some drugs add to risk. Complexes deposit in glomeruli, skin, joints, serosa, and vessel walls, activate complement, and recruit neutrophils and macrophages."},{"type":"flow","title":"How lupus loses tolerance to nuclear antigens","steps":["UV light and other stimuli cause apoptosis","Defective clearance (for example C1q deficiency)","Nuclear antigens persist","Antinuclear antibodies form immune complexes","TLR7 and TLR9 signaling raises type I interferon","More autoreactive B cell activation","Complexes deposit; complement and neutrophils injure tissue"],"caption":"A loop of antigen release, autoantibody, and interferon sustains the disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Serology rests on the difference between sensitivity and specificity. The antinuclear antibody (ANA) by immunofluorescence is positive in about 95% or more of patients, so a negative ANA makes SLE very unlikely, but it is also positive in other autoimmune diseases and in many healthy people at low titer. Anti-double-stranded DNA and anti-Smith antibodies are highly specific, so a positive result supports the diagnosis. Anti-dsDNA titers rise with disease activity and nephritis; anti-Smith does not track activity. Anti-histone antibodies are found in nearly all cases of drug-induced lupus (hydralazine, procainamide, isoniazid), which typically has arthritis and serositis but spares the kidney and brain, lacks anti-dsDNA, and resolves when the drug is stopped. Anti-histone is also common in idiopathic SLE, so it is useful only in that setting."},{"type":"p","text":"Flares consume complement through the classical pathway, so C3 and C4 fall and rise again with remission. Falling complement with rising anti-dsDNA is the classic signal of active nephritis. The ESR is often raised in flares, whereas the CRP is often only mildly raised unless serositis or infection is present."},{"type":"table","title":"Interpreting lupus serology","cols":["Test","Strength","Meaning"],"rows":[["ANA","Very sensitive, not specific","Negative result largely excludes SLE"],["Anti-dsDNA","Specific, moderately sensitive","Supports SLE; titer tracks activity and nephritis"],["Anti-Smith","Very specific, low sensitivity","Supports SLE; does not track activity"],["Anti-histone","Present in nearly all drug-induced lupus","Suggests drug-induced lupus when anti-dsDNA is absent"],["Anti-Ro/SSA","Crosses the placenta","Neonatal lupus and congenital heart block"],["C3 and C4","Activity markers","Low during flares from consumption"]],"caption":"Screen with a sensitive test, confirm with specific ones, and follow activity with anti-dsDNA and complement."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C07","obj":"IM-15-C.7","title":"Manifestations of SLE and antiphospholipid syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"SLE affects women about nine times as often as men, usually between puberty and menopause, and it is more frequent and more severe in women of African, Hispanic, and Asian ancestry. The typical vignette is a young woman with fatigue, fever, joint pain, and a rash after sun exposure."},{"type":"table","title":"Manifestations of SLE and their mechanisms","cols":["Site","Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Skin","Malar rash sparing the nasolabial folds; photosensitivity; discoid scarring lesions","UV-induced apoptosis; immune complexes at the dermal-epidermal junction"],["Mucosa","Painless oral ulcers","Immune-mediated mucosal injury"],["Joints","Symmetric, nonerosive arthritis","Synovitis without pannus; deformities are reducible"],["Serosa","Pleuritis, pericarditis","Immune complex serositis"],["Blood","Hemolytic anemia, leukopenia, lymphopenia, thrombocytopenia","Type II antibodies to blood cells"],["Kidney","Proteinuria, hematuria, red cell casts","Glomerular immune complex deposition"],["Heart valves","Libman-Sacks endocarditis","Sterile vegetations from immune injury"],["Nervous system","Seizures, psychosis, cognitive change","Antibodies, cytokines, and small vessel injury"]],"caption":"Most features are immune complex injury; cytopenias are direct antibody attack on cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Lupus nephritis is the most important predictor of outcome. Immune complexes deposit in the mesangium, beneath the endothelium (subendothelial), or beneath the podocytes (subepithelial), and the site determines the pattern. Subendothelial deposits are in contact with circulating blood, so they provoke the most inflammation and cause proliferative nephritis with a nephritic picture. Large subendothelial deposits thicken capillary walls so that they look rigid and bright on light microscopy, the wire loop lesion of diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis. Immunofluorescence is granular, often with IgG, IgA, IgM, C3, and C1q together (a full house pattern). Subepithelial deposits cause membranous lupus nephritis with nephrotic syndrome. Libman-Sacks endocarditis consists of small sterile vegetations on either surface of the leaflets, most often the mitral valve; they can scar the valve or embolize."},{"type":"p","text":"Antiphospholipid syndrome (APS) occurs alone or with SLE. Its antibodies are directed against phospholipid-binding plasma proteins, chiefly beta2-glycoprotein I, and are measured as lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, and anti-beta2-glycoprotein I. In the test tube, lupus anticoagulant interferes with the phospholipid surface of clotting assays and prolongs the aPTT, yet in the body the antibodies activate endothelium, platelets, monocytes, and complement and cause thrombosis. Clinical events are venous thrombosis (deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism), arterial thrombosis (stroke), and pregnancy morbidity from placental thrombosis and complement-mediated trophoblast injury: recurrent early losses, fetal death, or early preeclampsia. Anticardiolipin can also give a false-positive nontreponemal syphilis test (VDRL, RPR), because that test uses cardiolipin as its antigen. The antibody must be persistent, on testing at least 12 weeks apart."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman with SLE has a prolonged aPTT and a recent deep vein thrombosis. How can a test that suggests poor clotting coexist with thrombosis?","explain":"The prolonged aPTT is a laboratory artifact: the antibodies bind the phospholipid surface the assay depends on. In the body, the same antibodies activate endothelium, platelets, and complement, favoring clot formation.","check":"The antiphospholipid syndrome paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C08","obj":"IM-15-C.8","title":"Polyarteritis nodosa","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Polyarteritis nodosa (PAN) is a systemic necrotizing vasculitis of medium-sized muscular arteries, the main visceral arteries and their branches. It spares arterioles, capillaries, and venules, so it does not cause glomerulonephritis, and it characteristically spares the pulmonary arteries, so the lungs are not involved. It is not associated with ANCA, which separates it from the small vessel vasculitides such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis and microscopic polyangiitis. Middle-aged adults are affected most often."},{"type":"p","text":"A subset of cases is caused by chronic hepatitis B infection, in which complexes of hepatitis B surface antigen and antibody deposit in arterial walls, fix complement, and recruit neutrophils. This is the basis for classifying PAN as a type III disease. Hepatitis B vaccination has made this association much less common, and most current cases have no identified trigger. Hairy cell leukemia is a rare association."},{"type":"p","text":"The lesion is segmental and transmural. In the acute phase, neutrophils, eosinophils, and mononuclear cells infiltrate the whole wall, and fibrinoid necrosis replaces it. Later the wall heals with fibrosis. Because new lesions keep appearing, acute and healed lesions are seen side by side in the same patient and even in the same vessel; this mixture of stages is a distinguishing feature of PAN. Necrosis weakens the wall, producing small aneurysms, and thrombosis or fibrotic narrowing occludes the lumen, producing infarcts. Angiography of the renal and mesenteric arteries shows multiple microaneurysms and stenoses (a beaded pattern)."},{"type":"table","title":"Organ involvement in polyarteritis nodosa","cols":["Organ","Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Kidney","Hypertension, renal infarcts, no glomerulonephritis","Interlobar and arcuate arteritis; ischemia raises renin"],["Gut","Abdominal pain after meals, bleeding, bowel infarction","Mesenteric arteritis and aneurysm rupture"],["Peripheral nerves","Mononeuritis multiplex (for example foot drop)","Infarction of nerves through vasa nervorum"],["Skin","Livedo reticularis, tender nodules, ulcers","Arteritis of dermal and subcutaneous arteries"],["Testis","Testicular pain","Testicular artery involvement"],["Systemic","Fever, weight loss, myalgia","Cytokines from active inflammation"],["Lung","Spared","Pulmonary arteries not involved"]],"caption":"Ischemia in the territory of an inflamed artery explains each feature; absence of lung and glomerular disease is the key negative."},{"type":"p","text":"Diagnosis relies on biopsy of an affected organ, such as nerve, muscle, or skin, showing necrotizing arteritis, or on angiographic microaneurysms. Hepatitis B serology is part of the evaluation because it identifies the cause."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C09","obj":"IM-15-C.9","title":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN) is an immune complex nephritis that follows infection with certain nephritogenic strains of group A beta-hemolytic Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes). It occurs about 1 to 4 weeks after the infection: roughly 1 to 3 weeks after pharyngitis and closer to 3 to 6 weeks after skin infection (impetigo). This latent period is the time needed to make antibody, which separates PSGN from IgA nephropathy, in which hematuria appears within a day or two of a mucosal infection. Children of about 5 to 12 years are most often affected."},{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcal antigens, chiefly the cationic proteinase streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin B (SpeB) and the nephritis-associated plasmin receptor, lodge in the glomerulus, where antibody binds them in situ; circulating complexes also deposit. The complexes activate complement mainly through the alternative pathway, so serum C3 is low while C4 is usually normal or only mildly low. C3 returns to normal within about 6 to 8 weeks; persistently low C3 suggests another diagnosis, such as lupus nephritis or C3 glomerulopathy."},{"type":"p","text":"Complement and neutrophils make the glomeruli enlarged and hypercellular (diffuse proliferative and exudative glomerulonephritis). Electron microscopy shows large subepithelial deposits called humps, and immunofluorescence shows granular IgG and C3 along capillary walls and in the mesangium. Inflamed capillaries leak red cells and reduce filtration, producing the nephritic syndrome: smoke or cola colored urine with red cell casts, mild to moderate proteinuria, oliguria, periorbital edema, and hypertension. Sodium and water are retained because the GFR falls while tubular reabsorption continues. Recent infection is shown by antistreptolysin O (ASO), which rises after pharyngitis, or anti-DNase B, which is the better marker after skin infection because skin lipids inactivate streptolysin O and blunt the ASO response. Most children recover fully; adults recover less reliably."},{"type":"example","title":"Hematuria after a sore throat","text":"Two patients have cola colored urine and red cell casts. Patient A, aged 8, had a sore throat 2 weeks earlier, has periorbital edema and hypertension, a low C3, a normal C4, and a high ASO. Patient B, aged 22, noticed the hematuria 1 day after the onset of a sore throat and has a normal C3. Patient A fits PSGN: the 2 week delay allowed antibody production, and immune complexes consumed C3 through the alternative pathway. Patient B fits IgA nephropathy: hematuria appears almost at once, mesangial IgA deposits do not lower serum complement, and biopsy would show mesangial IgA rather than subepithelial humps. If patient A still had a low C3 at 3 months, the diagnosis of PSGN should be questioned."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C10","obj":"IM-15-C.10","title":"Rheumatoid arthritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is a chronic autoimmune synovitis that destroys cartilage and bone. It affects about 0.5 to 1% of adults, women about three times as often as men, usually starting between 30 and 50 years. Its injury is driven mainly by T cells, macrophages, and cytokines, with a contribution from immune complexes in the joint and in extra-articular lesions; this is why it appears under type III in many lists, although it combines types III and IV."},{"type":"p","text":"Susceptibility is strongest with HLA-DRB1 alleles that share a common amino acid sequence (the shared epitope), especially HLA-DR4. Peptidyl arginine deiminase enzymes convert arginine in proteins to citrulline, a process increased by smoking in the lung. HLA-DR4 binds citrullinated peptides well, presenting them to CD4 T cells, which then help B cells make anti-citrullinated protein antibodies (ACPA, measured as anti-CCP). These antibodies can appear years before arthritis. In the synovium, Th1 and Th17 cells, B cells, and plasma cells accumulate, and macrophages release TNF, IL-1, and IL-6. Synovial fibroblasts proliferate and, with new vessels and inflammatory cells, form pannus, which invades cartilage at the joint margins. RANKL on T cells and fibroblasts activates osteoclasts, producing marginal erosions and periarticular osteopenia."},{"type":"flow","title":"From citrullinated peptide to joint erosion","steps":["Smoking and inflammation increase protein citrullination","HLA-DR4 presents citrullinated peptide to CD4 T cells","B cells make anti-CCP and rheumatoid factor","Synovial macrophages release TNF, IL-1, IL-6","Fibroblasts proliferate into invasive pannus","RANKL activates osteoclasts: marginal bone erosions"],"caption":"The same cytokines that build pannus cause systemic symptoms and anemia of chronic disease."},{"type":"p","text":"The arthritis is symmetric and involves the small joints of the hands (MCP and PIP joints), the wrists, and the MTP joints, while sparing the DIP joints, which are typical targets of osteoarthritis and psoriatic arthritis. Morning stiffness lasting more than about an hour improves with use. Later deformities include ulnar deviation, swan neck, and boutonniere deformities, and cervical C1 to C2 instability. Rheumatoid factor is an antibody, usually IgM, against the Fc portion of IgG; it is positive in most patients but also in hepatitis C, Sjogren syndrome, chronic infections, and healthy older adults. Anti-CCP is slightly less sensitive but far more specific (about 95%) and predicts erosive disease. Rheumatoid nodules form over pressure points such as the extensor forearm and consist of central fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by palisading macrophages; they occur mainly in seropositive patients. Felty syndrome is seropositive, long-standing RA with splenomegaly and neutropenia, which predisposes to bacterial infection. Other extra-articular features include interstitial lung disease, pleural effusion with low glucose, scleritis, vasculitis, and secondary AA amyloidosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C11","obj":"IM-15-C.11","title":"Type IV hypersensitivity and granulomatous inflammation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity is injury caused by T cells rather than antibody. CD4 T cells act through cytokines: Th1 cells activate macrophages and Th17 cells recruit neutrophils. CD8 T cells kill antigen-bearing cells directly. Memory T cells must be recruited to the site and activated before injury appears, so the reaction peaks about 48 to 72 hours after exposure."},{"type":"p","text":"A granuloma is the Th1 response to an antigen that macrophages ingest but cannot destroy, such as Mycobacterium tuberculosis, which survives inside phagosomes. Infected macrophages and dendritic cells secrete IL-12, which drives naive CD4 T cells to become Th1 cells. Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages: phagolysosome fusion improves, reactive oxygen and nitrogen species increase, and MHC class II rises. Activated macrophages enlarge, gain abundant pink cytoplasm, and resemble epithelial cells (epithelioid cells), and some fuse into multinucleated giant cells, including Langhans giant cells with nuclei arranged in a horseshoe. TNF from macrophages and T cells induces chemokines and adhesion molecules that keep recruiting monocytes and hold the granuloma together. When TNF is blocked, granulomas break down and latent tuberculosis can reactivate, and inherited defects in IL-12 or IFN-gamma signaling cause severe mycobacterial infection."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a granuloma forms","steps":["Macrophage ingests antigen it cannot destroy","Macrophage IL-12 drives CD4 T cells to Th1","Th1 IFN-gamma activates macrophages","Activated macrophages become epithelioid cells and giant cells","TNF recruits monocytes and maintains the granuloma","Lymphocyte rim and fibrosis wall off the antigen"],"caption":"The granuloma contains the antigen at the cost of tissue destruction and scarring."},{"type":"p","text":"Granulomas are divided by whether the center is necrotic. Caseating granulomas have central caseous necrosis, an amorphous granular pink debris, and are the classic lesion of tuberculosis; endemic fungi such as Histoplasma also cause them. Noncaseating granulomas lack central necrosis and occur in sarcoidosis, Crohn disease, berylliosis, and reactions to foreign material. A caseating granuloma should always prompt a search for mycobacteria or fungi with special stains and culture."},{"type":"p","text":"The tuberculin skin test is a small, deliberate type IV reaction. Purified protein derivative (PPD) is injected into the dermis; a person with memory Th1 cells to mycobacterial antigens develops induration at 48 to 72 hours as T cells and macrophages accumulate. Only induration, not redness, is measured in millimeters. A positive test means prior sensitization (latent or active infection, or BCG vaccination); it does not distinguish latent from active disease. False negatives occur when T cell function is impaired (anergy), as in advanced HIV, sarcoidosis, high-dose glucocorticoids, overwhelming miliary tuberculosis, or very recent infection."},{"type":"table","title":"Common tuberculin skin test cutoffs","cols":["Induration","Counted as positive in"],"rows":[["5 mm or more","HIV infection, recent close contact, fibrotic changes on chest film, organ transplant or other immunosuppression"],["10 mm or more","Recent arrival from a high-prevalence country, injection drug use, residents and staff of high-risk settings, children under 4 years"],["15 mm or more","People with no risk factors"]],"caption":"The cutoff falls as the pretest probability of infection or the risk of progression rises."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C12","obj":"IM-15-C.12","title":"Sarcoidosis and Lofgren syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sarcoidosis is a multisystem disease of noncaseating granulomas of unknown cause. It is thought to reflect an exaggerated Th1 and Th17 response to an unidentified antigen in a genetically susceptible host. It usually begins between 20 and 40 years, with a second peak in older women, and in the United States it is more common and more severe in Black women. CD4 T cells concentrate in the lungs, giving a high CD4 to CD8 ratio in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, while blood lymphocytes fall. This redistribution helps explain the cutaneous anergy seen on skin testing."},{"type":"p","text":"The lungs and intrathoracic lymph nodes are involved in more than 90% of patients. Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on chest imaging is the classic finding, with or without reticular lung opacities; lung disease causes cough, dyspnea, a restrictive pattern, and reduced diffusing capacity, and it can progress to fibrosis. The granulomas are tight and well formed; giant cells may contain asteroid bodies or Schaumann bodies, but these inclusions are not specific."},{"type":"p","text":"Two laboratory findings arise from granuloma macrophages. Epithelioid cells produce angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), so serum ACE is often raised and roughly reflects granuloma burden; it is neither sensitive nor specific enough to diagnose sarcoidosis. Activated macrophages also express 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which converts 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol without control by PTH. Calcitriol increases intestinal calcium absorption, causing hypercalciuria (more common) and hypercalcemia with a suppressed PTH, which can worsen after sun exposure."},{"type":"table","title":"Extrapulmonary sarcoidosis","cols":["Site","Finding"],"rows":[["Eye","Anterior uveitis (red, painful eye, blurred vision)"],["Skin","Erythema nodosum (tender red nodules on the shins); lupus pernio (violaceous plaques on nose and cheeks)"],["Heart","Conduction block, ventricular arrhythmia, sudden death"],["Nervous system","Facial nerve palsy, other cranial neuropathies"],["Liver and spleen","Granulomas, often without symptoms"],["Calcium","Hypercalciuria, hypercalcemia, kidney stones"]],"caption":"Erythema nodosum is a nonspecific panniculitis; lupus pernio signals chronic disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Lofgren syndrome is an acute presentation with erythema nodosum, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, and arthritis, typically of the ankles, often with fever. It carries a good prognosis, and most patients remit spontaneously within about 2 years. Because noncaseating granulomas have other causes, sarcoidosis is a diagnosis of exclusion: tuberculosis and fungal infection must be excluded with stains and cultures, and beryllium exposure considered."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C13","obj":"IM-15-C.13","title":"Transplant rejection and graft-versus-host disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Rejection is an immune response against donor alloantigens, mainly the highly polymorphic MHC (HLA) molecules, and against ABO antigens on vascular endothelium. Recipient T cells recognize donor MHC in two ways. In direct recognition, donor dendritic cells carried in the graft present intact foreign MHC, which activates an unusually large fraction of recipient T cells and drives acute cellular rejection. In indirect recognition, recipient antigen-presenting cells process donor MHC into peptides, which helps B cells make donor-specific antibody and sustains chronic rejection. Timing reflects mechanism."},{"type":"table","title":"Types of rejection","cols":["Type","Timing","Mechanism","Pathology"],"rows":[["Hyperacute","Minutes to hours","Preformed antibody (ABO, anti-HLA from pregnancy, transfusion, prior graft); type II","Complement on endothelium, thrombosis, ischemic necrosis; graft turns dusky and mottled"],["Acute cellular","Days to months, mostly first months","CD8 T cells kill graft cells; CD4 cytokines; type IV","Interstitial lymphocytes, tubulitis, endothelialitis"],["Acute antibody-mediated","Days to months","New donor-specific antibody against endothelium","Capillaritis, C4d in peritubular capillaries"],["Chronic","Months to years","Repeated T cell and antibody injury to vessels","Graft arteriosclerosis, interstitial fibrosis, organ-specific scarring"]],"caption":"Preformed antibody acts at once; T cells must first expand; chronic injury accumulates in vessels."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic rejection is the main cause of late graft loss. Smooth muscle cells proliferate in the intima of graft arteries, producing concentric narrowing (graft arteriosclerosis) and ischemic fibrosis. Its form depends on the organ: interstitial fibrosis and tubular atrophy in the kidney, accelerated diffuse coronary disease in a heart graft (often silent because the heart is denervated), bronchiolitis obliterans with progressive airflow obstruction in a lung graft, and loss of bile ducts in a liver graft."},{"type":"p","text":"Graft-versus-host disease (GVHD) reverses the direction of attack. Immunocompetent donor T cells in a graft rich in lymphocytes, usually a hematopoietic stem cell transplant, recognize host tissues as foreign. The host must be unable to reject those cells, because of conditioning or immunodeficiency. Acute GVHD targets epithelia: the skin (a maculopapular rash, often starting on the palms and soles, that can progress to blistering), the liver (cholestatic jaundice from bile duct injury), and the gut (watery or bloody diarrhea from crypt cell apoptosis). Chronic GVHD resembles autoimmune disease, with sclerodermatous skin, dry eyes and mouth, and obliterative bronchiolitis. The same donor T cells can attack residual leukemia, the graft-versus-leukemia effect."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman with three prior pregnancies receives a kidney, and within minutes after the vascular clamps are released the graft becomes mottled and soft. What caused this?","explain":"Pregnancy exposed her to paternal HLA, so she had preformed anti-HLA antibodies. They bound graft endothelium at once, activated complement, and caused thrombosis: hyperacute rejection, a type II reaction.","check":"The rejection table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C14","obj":"IM-15-C.14","title":"Serum sickness and the Arthus reaction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Serum sickness is the model systemic type III reaction. It was first described after injections of horse serum antitoxin and now follows foreign proteins such as antithymocyte globulin and chimeric monoclonal antibodies. Its course follows the changing ratio of antigen to antibody. A large dose of antigen circulates, and after about 5 to 7 days antibody production begins. While antigen is still in moderate excess, small and intermediate complexes form; these fix complement but are poorly cleared by mononuclear phagocytes, so they deposit in vessel walls, glomeruli, and synovium. Complement fragments, especially C5a, attract neutrophils, whose enzymes cause vasculitis, glomerulonephritis, and arthritis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Course of serum sickness","steps":["Large dose of foreign protein","Antibody production begins after about 5 to 7 days","Small complexes form in antigen excess","Complexes deposit in vessels, glomeruli, synovium","Complement consumed; C5a recruits neutrophils","Fever, rash, arthralgia, proteinuria","Antigen cleared; symptoms resolve"],"caption":"Symptoms appear when antibody first meets abundant antigen and fade when antigen is gone."},{"type":"p","text":"Symptoms begin about 1 to 2 weeks after first exposure, or within a few days in someone already sensitized. Fever, urticarial or morbilliform rash, arthralgia or arthritis, lymphadenopathy, and proteinuria are typical, and C3 and C4 are low because complement is consumed. The illness resolves over weeks as antigen is cleared and antibody excess forms large complexes that phagocytes remove."},{"type":"p","text":"The Arthus reaction is the local form. When antigen is injected into the skin of a person who already has high levels of IgG, complexes form within the walls of local small vessels. Complement activation and neutrophil influx cause a localized necrotizing vasculitis with edema, hemorrhage, and sometimes ulceration, peaking at about 4 to 12 hours. Overly frequent tetanus or diphtheria toxoid boosters are a classic cause. The timing separates it from a type I wheal, which appears in minutes, and from a type IV reaction, which takes 48 to 72 hours."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-03-C15","obj":"IM-15-C.15","title":"Classifying drug hypersensitivity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Drugs cause all four types of hypersensitivity. Most drugs are too small to be antigens, so they act as haptens: the drug or a reactive metabolite binds a host protein covalently, and the drug-protein conjugate is recognized as foreign. Which type results depends on whether the immune response is dominated by IgE, IgG against cells, circulating complexes, or T cells, and on where the antigen sits. Penicillin is the classic example of a single drug that can cause more than one type."},{"type":"table","title":"Drug reactions by hypersensitivity type","cols":["Type","Example","Mechanism","Timing"],"rows":[["I","Penicillin anaphylaxis or urticaria","IgE to penicilloyl-protein conjugates on mast cells","Minutes to about an hour"],["II","Penicillin hemolytic anemia","High-dose drug coats red cells; IgG to drug-coated cells; splenic removal","Days to weeks of therapy"],["II","Methyldopa hemolytic anemia","Drug induces true autoantibody to red cell antigens; warm AIHA","Months of therapy"],["III","Serum sickness from chimeric monoclonal antibodies or antithymocyte globulin","Antibody to foreign protein forms immune complexes","About 1 to 2 weeks"],["IV","Contact dermatitis from topical neomycin","Hapten binds skin proteins; Langerhans cells prime T cells","48 to 72 hours after re-exposure"]],"caption":"One drug can cause different reactions depending on the immune response it provokes."},{"type":"p","text":"The two type II hemolytic patterns differ in a testable way. With penicillin, antibody recognizes the drug on the red cell membrane, so hemolysis requires high drug levels and stops when the drug is withdrawn; the direct Coombs test is positive for IgG. With methyldopa, the drug alters immune regulation so that the patient makes autoantibodies against red cell antigens themselves. A positive direct Coombs test is common in long-term users, but hemolysis develops in only a small fraction, and the antibody can persist for months after the drug is stopped."},{"type":"p","text":"In contact dermatitis, the hapten penetrates the skin and binds epidermal proteins; Langerhans cells carry it to regional lymph nodes and prime T cells during sensitization. On re-exposure, effector T cells return to the skin and cause a pruritic, eczematous, often vesicular eruption sharply limited to the area of contact. Nickel and urushiol from poison ivy act the same way. Reactions that mimic allergy without immune memory, such as direct mast cell activation by vancomycin or iodinated contrast, are not true hypersensitivity, and they can occur on the first exposure."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The four types are mechanisms, not diseases, and most diseases combine them. Lupus uses type III injury in the kidney and type II injury to blood cells; rheumatoid arthritis combines T cell synovitis with immune complexes; transplant rejection can be type II (hyperacute, antibody-mediated) or type IV (acute cellular). The useful questions are where the antigen is (fixed on a cell or matrix, circulating, or inside macrophages), which effector is active (IgE, IgG or IgM with complement, or T cells), and how long the response takes. These answers predict the biopsy: linear IgG for a matrix antigen, granular deposits for immune complexes, and granulomas or lymphocytic infiltrates for T cell injury."},{"type":"p","text":"The laboratory follows the same logic. Complement falls when immune complexes or complement-fixing antibodies consume it (SLE flares, PSGN, serum sickness, cold agglutinin disease) but not in type I or type IV reactions. Microbes link pathology to microbiology: group A Streptococcus, Mycoplasma pneumoniae, hepatitis B, and Mycobacterium tuberculosis each cause a disease in this chapter through the host response rather than direct tissue invasion. The classification has limits: some antibody-mediated diseases, such as myasthenia gravis and Graves disease, alter receptor function instead of destroying cells, and some type IV reactions are mediated by CD8 cytotoxic T cells rather than cytokine-activated macrophages."}],"summary":["Type I: Th2 IL-4 and IL-13 switch B cells to IgE, which arms mast cells; multivalent allergen cross-links IgE and releases histamine and tryptase in minutes, with leukotrienes and an eosinophil late phase hours later.","Anaphylaxis is systemic mast cell activation causing urticaria, angioedema, bronchospasm, and distributive shock; serum tryptase peaks at 1 to 2 hours and supports the diagnosis but can be normal in food reactions.","Warm AIHA is IgG at 37 °C (98.6 °F) with splenic removal and spherocytes; cold agglutinin disease is IgM fixing C3 in cool extremities after Mycoplasma or EBV; both are direct Coombs positive.","Anti-GBM disease targets the alpha3 chain of type IV collagen, giving linear IgG, crescentic RPGN, and alveolar hemorrhage; myasthenia gravis antibodies remove or block AChR, causing fatigable ocular and proximal weakness linked to the thymus.","SLE results from impaired clearance of apoptotic nuclear material; ANA is sensitive, anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith are specific, anti-histone suggests drug-induced lupus, and C3 and C4 fall in flares.","Lupus features include malar rash, nonerosive arthritis, serositis, cytopenias, wire loop nephritis, and Libman-Sacks endocarditis; antiphospholipid antibodies prolong the aPTT but cause thrombosis and pregnancy loss.","PAN is an ANCA-negative, hepatitis B-linked necrotizing arteritis of medium arteries with lesions of different ages and renal and mesenteric microaneurysms, sparing the lung and glomeruli.","PSGN follows group A strep after a 1 to 4 week delay, with low C3, high ASO or anti-DNase B, subepithelial humps, and granular immunofluorescence; RA is HLA-DR4 and anti-CCP associated pannus that erodes MCP, PIP, and wrist joints and spares the DIPs.","Th1 IFN-gamma and TNF build granulomas: caseating in tuberculosis, noncaseating in sarcoidosis, where macrophage calcitriol causes hypercalcemia; the tuberculin test is read as induration at 48 to 72 hours.","Hyperacute rejection is preformed antibody in minutes, acute rejection is T cell or antibody attack over weeks to months, chronic rejection is vascular and fibrotic, and GVHD is donor T cells attacking host skin, liver, and gut."],"sources":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity, and Immune Responses Against Tumors and Transplants"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System: hypersensitivity, autoimmune diseases, and transplant rejection"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Kidney: glomerular diseases"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Streptococci, and Mycobacteria"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the immune system and inflammatory rheumatic diseases"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Excitation of skeletal muscle: neuromuscular transmission"}]},"IM-15-D":{"unit":"IM-15-D","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-04","title":"Other Autoimmune Diseases: Systemic Sclerosis, Rheumatic Fever, and Sjogren Syndrome","subject":"Immune System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers systemic sclerosis, acute rheumatic fever, Sjogren syndrome, neonatal lupus from maternal anti-Ro antibodies, and mixed connective tissue disease. Each is taught from mechanism to finding: how small vessel injury and TGF-beta driven fibrosis stiffen skin, gut, lung, and kidney; how antibodies raised against a streptococcal protein attack the heart; and how lymphocytes destroy exocrine glands. Board questions usually give an autoantibody, a preceding infection, or an organ pattern and ask you to name the disease, predict its complication, or explain its lesion. If you can link each antibody to its subset and explain why each complication follows, you can reason through most of these questions.","prerequisites":["Hypersensitivity types I to IV and their effector mechanisms (IM-15-C)","Self-tolerance, its failure, and molecular mimicry (IM-13)","IgG structure and transplacental transfer through the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn)","Group A streptococcus and its M protein (IM-14)","The renin-angiotensin system and the cardiac conduction system (CV-7)"],"connections":[{"to":"Diseases of hypersensitivity (IM-15-C)","how":"Rheumatic fever is the classic antibody-mediated (type II) example, while lupus shares anti-Ro antibodies and neonatal disease with Sjogren syndrome."},{"to":"Valvular heart diseases (CV-18-D)","how":"Chronic rheumatic heart disease remains a leading cause of mitral stenosis worldwide."},{"to":"Conduction system and the ECG (CV-7)","how":"Maternal anti-Ro antibodies scar the fetal AV node and cause congenital complete heart block."},{"to":"Restrictive pulmonary and pulmonary vascular disease (PU-14-A, PU-14-E)","how":"Systemic sclerosis and mixed connective tissue disease cause interstitial lung disease and pulmonary arterial hypertension, their main causes of death."},{"to":"Salivary gland disease and gastrointestinal motility (GI-13-A, GI-6)","how":"Sjogren syndrome destroys salivary glands, and systemic sclerosis replaces esophageal smooth muscle with collagen."}],"terms":[{"term":"Systemic sclerosis","def":"A chronic autoimmune disease of small vessel injury and excess collagen deposition in skin and internal organs; also called scleroderma."},{"term":"Raynaud phenomenon","def":"Episodic cold- or stress-induced vasospasm of digital arteries producing white, then blue, then red fingers."},{"term":"Sclerodactyly","def":"Thickened, tight skin of the fingers that limits their movement."},{"term":"Scleroderma renal crisis","def":"Abrupt severe hypertension and acute kidney injury from renal arteriolar narrowing and renin release in systemic sclerosis."},{"term":"Molecular mimicry","def":"Cross-reaction of antibodies or T cells raised against a microbial antigen with a structurally similar self antigen."},{"term":"Aschoff body","def":"The focal inflammatory lesion of acute rheumatic carditis, with fibrinoid change, lymphocytes, and activated macrophages."},{"term":"Anitschkow cell","def":"An activated macrophage in an Aschoff body whose chromatin condenses into a slender wavy ribbon, giving the caterpillar cell appearance."},{"term":"Jones criteria","def":"Clinical criteria that diagnose acute rheumatic fever from major and minor manifestations plus evidence of recent group A streptococcal infection."},{"term":"Keratoconjunctivitis sicca","def":"Drying and injury of the cornea and conjunctiva caused by reduced tear production."},{"term":"Anti-Ro (SSA) and anti-La (SSB)","def":"Autoantibodies against ribonucleoprotein antigens, typical of Sjogren syndrome and also found in lupus."},{"term":"Neonatal lupus","def":"Disease of a fetus or newborn caused by maternal anti-Ro or anti-La IgG that crossed the placenta."},{"term":"Anti-U1 RNP","def":"Antibody against the U1 small nuclear ribonucleoprotein of the spliceosome; at high titer it marks mixed connective tissue disease."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-04-C01","obj":"IM-15-D.1","title":"Systemic sclerosis: vascular injury, autoimmunity, and fibrosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma) is a chronic autoimmune disease in which small blood vessels are injured and fibroblasts deposit excess collagen in the skin and internal organs. It affects women about three to four times as often as men and usually begins in middle age. Its cause is unknown, but three processes act together: microvascular injury, immune activation, and fibrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Endothelial injury is the earliest change. Damaged endothelium releases endothelin, makes less nitric oxide, and allows platelets to adhere, so small arteries constrict and their intima proliferates until the lumen narrows. Capillaries are lost, which is visible as dilated and dropped-out nailfold capillaries. The tissue downstream becomes chronically ischemic. At the same time, CD4 T cells, many with a Th2 profile, infiltrate the skin and release IL-4, IL-13, and TGF-beta, and B cells make the autoantibodies that define the subsets (C02)."},{"type":"p","text":"TGF-beta is the central fibrotic signal. Through SMAD signaling it converts fibroblasts into contractile myofibroblasts that produce type I and type III collagen and resist apoptosis, so collagen accumulates instead of being remodeled. Ischemia and repeated injury keep the fibroblasts activated. The result is the paired lesion of every affected organ: narrowed small arteries and dense collagen."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis","steps":["Unknown trigger in a genetically susceptible person","Endothelial injury: more endothelin, less nitric oxide","Intimal proliferation narrows arterioles; capillaries are lost","T cells release IL-4, IL-13, and TGF-beta; B cells make autoantibodies","TGF-beta turns fibroblasts into collagen-producing myofibroblasts","Ischemia plus fibrosis in skin, gut, lung, kidney, and heart"],"caption":"Vascular injury and immune activation converge on TGF-beta driven fibrosis, and ischemia keeps the cycle going."},{"type":"table","title":"Organ findings explained by mechanism","cols":["Organ","Lesion","Finding"],"rows":[["Digital arteries","Vasospasm on top of fixed intimal narrowing","Raynaud phenomenon in about 95%, often first; fingertip ulcers and pitted scars"],["Skin","Edema, then dense dermal collagen with loss of appendages","Puffy fingers, then sclerodactyly, tight face, small mouth"],["Esophagus","Smooth muscle of lower two thirds atrophies and is replaced by collagen","Dysphagia, low LES pressure, reflux, Barrett esophagus, strictures"],["Small intestine","Smooth muscle fibrosis and hypomotility","Bacterial overgrowth and malabsorption"],["Lung","Interstitial fibrosis; pulmonary arteriolar narrowing","Restrictive pattern with low DLCO; pulmonary arterial hypertension"],["Kidney","Intimal thickening of interlobular arteries","Hypertension and renal crisis (C02)"],["Heart","Patchy myocardial fibrosis","Arrhythmias, conduction block, diastolic dysfunction"]],"caption":"Each organ shows the same two lesions, narrowed small vessels and excess collagen, so its symptoms follow from lost blood flow or lost elasticity."},{"type":"p","text":"Raynaud phenomenon reflects this structure. Cold or stress constricts digital arteries whose lumens are already narrowed, so the fingers blanch white (ischemia), turn blue (deoxygenated blood pools), then flush red as flow returns (reactive hyperemia). The esophagus shows why fibrosis is so damaging: only its upper third is skeletal muscle, so the smooth muscle below fails, peristalsis disappears, and the weak lower esophageal sphincter lets acid reflux freely. Manometry therefore shows low LES pressure with absent distal peristalsis, the opposite of achalasia, where LES pressure is high and fails to relax. In the lung, interstitial collagen stiffens the parenchyma and thickens the alveolar wall, lowering lung volumes and diffusing capacity; lung disease is now the leading cause of death in systemic sclerosis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients have dysphagia for solids and liquids. One has a low lower esophageal sphincter pressure with absent distal peristalsis; the other has a high LES pressure that fails to relax. Which has systemic sclerosis?","explain":"The first. Collagen replaces the smooth muscle of the lower esophagus and sphincter, so the sphincter is weak and peristalsis is lost, causing reflux. Achalasia comes from loss of inhibitory myenteric neurons, so the sphincter stays tight.","check":"The esophagus row of the organ table and the paragraph after it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-04-C02","obj":"IM-15-D.2","title":"Diffuse versus limited systemic sclerosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Systemic sclerosis is divided by how far skin thickening spreads, and the two subsets differ in antibody, pace, and the organ most likely to kill. Diffuse cutaneous disease involves skin proximal to the elbows and knees, including the trunk, and progresses quickly, with visceral fibrosis in the first few years. Limited cutaneous disease keeps skin changes distal to the elbows and knees and on the face, follows years of Raynaud phenomenon, and progresses slowly. Each subset has a characteristic antibody, and the antibodies are nearly mutually exclusive, so the antibody predicts the course."},{"type":"table","title":"Diffuse and limited systemic sclerosis compared","cols":["Feature","Diffuse cutaneous","Limited cutaneous (CREST)"],"rows":[["Skin","Proximal limbs and trunk as well as hands and face","Fingers, hands, forearms, face"],["Course","Rapid; organs involved early","Slow; Raynaud for years first"],["Typical antibody","Anti-Scl-70 (topoisomerase I); anti-RNA polymerase III","Anticentromere"],["Lung","Interstitial lung disease, early and severe","Pulmonary arterial hypertension, late"],["Kidney","Scleroderma renal crisis","Rare"],["Other","Tendon friction rubs, myocardial fibrosis","Calcinosis, telangiectasia; association with primary biliary cholangitis"]],"caption":"Anti-Scl-70 points to diffuse skin disease and lung fibrosis; anticentromere points to CREST and late pulmonary hypertension."},{"type":"p","text":"CREST names the features of limited disease: calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, esophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, and telangiectasia. Calcinosis is dystrophic calcification, calcium salts deposited in damaged subcutaneous tissue of the fingers while serum calcium and phosphate stay normal. Telangiectasias are dilated capillaries and venules on the face, lips, hands, and gut mucosa, a sign of abnormal vascular repair, and gastrointestinal lesions can bleed. In limited disease the slow obliteration of small pulmonary arteries raises pulmonary vascular resistance, so pulmonary arterial hypertension and right heart failure appear many years after onset. Anticentromere antibodies give a distinctive discrete speckled pattern (centromere) pattern on immunofluorescence ANA testing."},{"type":"p","text":"Scleroderma renal crisis is the classic emergency of diffuse disease, especially early disease with rapidly spreading skin and anti-RNA polymerase III antibodies. Intimal proliferation narrows the interlobular arteries in an onion-skin pattern, renal perfusion falls, and the juxtaglomerular cells release renin. Angiotensin II then raises blood pressure and constricts the already narrowed arterioles further, so ischemia and renin release reinforce each other. The patient develops abrupt severe hypertension, a rising creatinine, and often microangiopathic hemolytic anemia with schistocytes as red cells shear on injured vessels."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Scleroderma renal crisis","steps":["Endothelial injury narrows interlobular arteries and afferent arterioles","Juxtaglomerular cells sense low perfusion and release renin","Angiotensin II causes severe systemic hypertension","Angiotensin II constricts renal arterioles further","Renal perfusion falls more, releasing more renin"],"caption":"A positive feedback loop: ischemia drives renin, and renin's product deepens the ischemia, causing acute kidney injury."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-04-C03","obj":"IM-15-D.3","title":"Acute rheumatic fever: molecular mimicry and pancarditis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute rheumatic fever is an immune-mediated inflammatory disease that follows pharyngitis with group A streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes) by about 2 to 4 weeks. It mainly affects children aged about 5 to 15 years and follows untreated or inadequately treated throat infection, often mild enough to go unnoticed; streptococcal skin infection can cause poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis but is not the classic trigger. The throat infection has often cleared by the time symptoms begin, because the disease is caused by the immune response, not by infection of the heart, so cultures of heart tissue and blood are sterile."},{"type":"p","text":"M protein is the main surface virulence factor of group A streptococcus; it resists phagocytosis, and antibody against it is protective. Some M protein epitopes resemble self proteins, especially cardiac myosin, tropomyosin, and laminin and other proteins of valve tissue. In a genetically susceptible host, antibodies and CD4 T cells raised against the bacterium cross-react with these self antigens, a process called molecular mimicry. Antibody binding to valve endothelium increases adhesion molecules so cross-reactive T cells enter the valve. Because antibody against a fixed tissue antigen starts the injury, rheumatic fever is classified as type II hypersensitivity, although T cells contribute heavily. The 2 to 4 week delay is the time needed to make these antibodies and expand the T cells."},{"type":"flow","title":"From pharyngitis to rheumatic carditis","steps":["Group A streptococcal pharyngitis, untreated","B and T cells respond to M protein","After 2 to 4 weeks, cross-reaction with cardiac myosin and valve proteins","Antibody activates valve endothelium; T cells enter","Pancarditis with Aschoff bodies; joints, skin, and brain also affected"],"caption":"Molecular mimicry explains both the delay and the sterile inflammation."},{"type":"p","text":"The heart lesion is a pancarditis that inflames all three layers. The characteristic focus is the Aschoff body, which contains fibrinoid change of collagen, T lymphocytes, occasional plasma cells, and plump activated macrophages called Anitschkow cells. These have abundant cytoplasm and a central nucleus whose chromatin condenses into a slender wavy ribbon (caterpillar cells); some fuse into multinucleated Aschoff giant cells. Aschoff bodies heal into fibrous scars."},{"type":"table","title":"Pancarditis by layer","cols":["Layer","Lesion","Consequence"],"rows":[["Pericardium","Fibrinous or serofibrinous pericarditis","Friction rub; usually resolves without sequelae"],["Myocardium","Aschoff bodies in interstitial connective tissue","Dilation, functional mitral regurgitation, heart failure; main cause of death in the acute phase"],["Endocardium and valves","Valvulitis with fibrinoid necrosis and small (1 to 2 mm) verrucae along the lines of closure; MacCallum plaques in the left atrium","New regurgitant murmur; leaflet scarring that leads to chronic disease"]],"caption":"Only the valve injury commonly leaves permanent damage, which is why rheumatic fever is remembered for its valve disease."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child develops tea-colored urine 10 days after impetigo. Could this child develop rheumatic carditis from the same infection, and why are the two diseases classified differently?","explain":"Classically no. Skin infection can cause poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis but is not the usual trigger for rheumatic fever, which follows pharyngitis. Glomerulonephritis is type III, from immune complexes deposited in glomeruli; rheumatic fever is type II, from antibody and T cell cross-reaction with cardiac antigens.","check":"The first two paragraphs of this section and the type II and type III comparison in IM-15-C."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-04-C04","obj":"IM-15-D.4","title":"Jones criteria and chronic rheumatic heart disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Because no single test proves acute rheumatic fever, it is diagnosed with the Jones criteria (revised by the American Heart Association in 2015). A first episode requires evidence of preceding group A streptococcal infection plus either two major manifestations or one major and two minor manifestations. Evidence of infection is a raised or rising antistreptolysin O (ASO) or anti-DNase B titer, a positive throat culture, or a positive rapid antigen test. Each major manifestation reflects mimicry in a different tissue."},{"type":"table","title":"Jones major manifestations","cols":["Major criterion","Features","Mechanism and course"],"rows":[["Carditis","New regurgitant murmur (usually mitral), rub, heart failure, or valvulitis seen only on echocardiography","Pancarditis; the only manifestation that can leave lasting damage"],["Arthritis","Migratory polyarthritis of large joints (knees, ankles, elbows, wrists), very painful","Transient synovitis; an old clinical saying holds that rheumatic fever licks the joints but bites the heart"],["Sydenham chorea","Rapid, purposeless movements, emotional lability; may begin months after infection","Antibodies cross-react with basal ganglia neurons"],["Erythema marginatum","Evanescent, nonpruritic pink rings with clear centers on trunk and proximal limbs, sparing the face","Uncommon; lesions fade within hours and reappear, so they are easily missed"],["Subcutaneous nodules","Painless firm nodules over bony prominences and extensor tendons","Central fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by macrophages; usually seen with carditis"]],"caption":"Joints, skin, and brain recover; the heart is the organ that scars."},{"type":"table","title":"Jones minor manifestations (low-risk populations)","cols":["Minor criterion","Threshold"],"rows":[["Polyarthralgia","Joint pain without objective arthritis (not counted if arthritis is a major criterion)"],["Fever","38.5 degrees C (101.3 degrees F) or higher"],["Acute phase reactants","ESR 60 mm/h or higher, or CRP 3.0 mg/dL or higher"],["Prolonged PR interval","Adjusted for age (not counted if carditis is a major criterion)"]],"caption":"In moderate- and high-risk populations the 2015 revision lowers the thresholds and also accepts monoarthritis or polyarthralgia as major arthritis."},{"type":"p","text":"A prolonged PR interval reflects inflammation near the AV node and is a minor criterion, but it is not carditis. Chorea is the exception to the timing rule: it can appear months after infection, when antibody titers have fallen, and may then support the diagnosis on its own."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic rheumatic heart disease develops after repeated or severe attacks. Valves heal by fibrosis and neovascularization: leaflets thicken, commissures fuse, and chordae tendineae shorten, thicken, and fuse. The mitral valve is affected in nearly all cases, alone or with the aortic valve, because the left-sided valves close against the highest pressures. Fused commissures produce a narrow fish-mouth orifice, so chronic rheumatic disease is the leading cause of mitral stenosis. Flow across the narrow valve needs a higher left atrial pressure, which enlarges the atrium, predisposes to atrial fibrillation and mural thrombus, and raises pulmonary venous and then arterial pressure. Scarred valves also attract infective endocarditis."},{"type":"example","title":"Applying the Jones criteria","text":"A 9-year-old has had swollen, painful joints for 10 days: first the left knee, then the right ankle, then the left wrist. Temperature is 38.9 degrees C (102.0 degrees F), ESR is 75 mm/h, and there is a new apical holosystolic murmur. Three weeks ago she had a sore throat that was never treated. ASO titer is high. Migratory polyarthritis and carditis are two major criteria, and the high ASO gives evidence of infection, so the diagnosis is made. Fever and ESR are minor criteria and are not needed. The murmur is acute mitral regurgitation from valvulitis. If attacks recur, the valve will scar and decades later stenosis, not regurgitation, will dominate."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-04-C05","obj":"IM-15-D.5","title":"Sjogren syndrome: autoimmune destruction of exocrine glands","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sjogren syndrome is a chronic autoimmune disease in which lymphocytes infiltrate and destroy the lacrimal and salivary glands, causing dry eyes and dry mouth. It affects women about nine times as often as men, usually in middle age. Primary Sjogren syndrome (sicca syndrome) occurs alone; secondary Sjogren syndrome accompanies another autoimmune disease, most often rheumatoid arthritis, and also lupus or systemic sclerosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Glandular epithelial cells appear to start and sustain the response. After an unknown trigger, possibly a viral infection, they express HLA class II and costimulatory molecules and release type I interferon and B cell activating factor (BAFF), so they present self antigen and keep lymphocytes alive. CD4 T cells gather around ducts, followed by B cells that can form germinal centers within the gland. Acinar and ductal cells are lost and replaced by lymphocytes and fibrosis, so tear and saliva secretion falls. Biopsy of minor labial salivary glands shows this focal periductal lymphocytic sialadenitis."},{"type":"table","title":"Manifestations explained by mechanism","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Keratoconjunctivitis sicca: gritty, burning eyes, corneal erosions","Lacrimal destruction lowers tear volume; low Schirmer test result"],["Xerostomia: dry mouth, trouble swallowing dry food, rampant dental caries, oral candidiasis","Less saliva to buffer acid, clear bacteria, and supply antimicrobial proteins"],["Bilateral parotid enlargement","Lymphocytic infiltration of the gland"],["Extraglandular disease: arthralgia, interstitial lung disease, distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis, peripheral neuropathy, palpable purpura","Lymphocytic infiltration of other epithelia and immune complex vasculitis"],["Anti-Ro (SSA), anti-La (SSB), ANA, rheumatoid factor, polyclonal hypergammaglobulinemia","Chronic B cell hyperactivity"]],"caption":"Gland loss explains the dryness; B cell hyperactivity explains the antibodies, the vasculitis, and the lymphoma risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Ro and La are intracellular ribonucleoproteins that become exposed on the surface of cells undergoing apoptosis, which may let them reach the immune system. Anti-Ro and anti-La are the characteristic antibodies, and rheumatoid factor is often positive even without arthritis. Anti-Ro is not specific: it also occurs in lupus, especially subacute cutaneous lupus, and it is the antibody that harms the fetus (C06)."},{"type":"p","text":"The most serious complication is B cell lymphoma, mostly extranodal marginal zone (MALT) lymphoma arising in the salivary glands. Years of antigen-driven B cell proliferation inside the gland, supported by BAFF and rheumatoid factor producing clones, allow one clone to acquire mutations and grow independently of normal controls. Persistent parotid enlargement, purpura, low complement C4, cryoglobulins, or a new monoclonal protein signal this transformation. Dryness alone is not specific for Sjogren syndrome: anticholinergic drugs, radiation, aging, and infiltrative diseases also reduce secretion, so the antibodies or biopsy confirm it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-04-C06","obj":"IM-15-D.6","title":"Neonatal lupus and congenital heart block","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neonatal lupus is disease of a fetus or newborn caused by maternal anti-Ro (SSA) or anti-La (SSB) IgG. The mother may have Sjogren syndrome or lupus, but many mothers have no symptoms and learn of their antibodies only through the affected child. The infant does not have lupus; it is passively exposed to its mother's antibodies."},{"type":"p","text":"IgG is the only class that crosses the placenta. Syncytiotrophoblast cells take it up and protect it from degradation through the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn), then release it into fetal blood. Transfer rises from the second trimester, the period when the fetal conduction system is developing. Anti-Ro antibodies bind Ro antigens exposed on fetal cardiomyocytes undergoing normal developmental apoptosis and may also interfere with L-type and T-type calcium channels, on which AV nodal conduction depends. Opsonized cells are taken up by macrophages, which release inflammatory and fibrotic signals including TGF-beta, so the AV node and surrounding tissue are inflamed, then fibrosed and calcified."},{"type":"flow","title":"Congenital heart block from maternal anti-Ro","steps":["Mother carries anti-Ro (SSA) IgG, with or without symptoms","FcRn carries IgG across the placenta in the second trimester","Antibody binds Ro exposed on fetal cardiac cells","Macrophage inflammation, then fibrosis and calcification of the AV node","Complete heart block with fetal bradycardia, usually from about 18 to 24 weeks"],"caption":"The antibody is temporary, but the scar it causes is permanent."},{"type":"p","text":"Complete (third degree) heart block is the defining lesion. Atria are driven by the sinus node at a normal fetal rate while the ventricles follow a slow junctional or ventricular escape rhythm, so the fetal heart rate is persistently low with AV dissociation. Some fetuses also develop myocarditis, endocardial fibroelastosis, or dilated cardiomyopathy, which raise mortality. Only about 1 to 2% of pregnancies in anti-Ro positive women are affected, but the risk is much higher in a later pregnancy after one affected child, which shows that antibody is necessary but not sufficient."},{"type":"table","title":"Fixed versus transient findings of neonatal lupus","cols":["Finding","Course","Reason"],"rows":[["Complete heart block","Permanent","Fibrosed conduction tissue cannot regenerate"],["Annular, photosensitive rash, often around the eyes","Fades over about 6 to 8 months","Depends on circulating maternal IgG, which is catabolized"],["Cytopenias and raised transaminases","Transient","Same: clears with maternal antibody"]],"caption":"Damage that depends on continuing antibody resolves; damage that has already become scar does not."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A newborn has a photosensitive rash around the eyes and a heart rate of 55 bpm with P waves unrelated to QRS complexes. The mother is well. Which findings will still be present at 1 year of age, and why?","explain":"Only the complete heart block. Maternal anti-Ro IgG has a half-life of about 3 weeks and is gone within months, so the rash and any cytopenias resolve. The AV node was replaced by fibrous tissue before birth, and that scar remains.","check":"The flow and the table of fixed and transient findings."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-04-C07","obj":"IM-15-D.7","title":"Mixed connective tissue disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mixed connective tissue disease (MCTD) is an autoimmune syndrome that combines features of lupus, systemic sclerosis, and polymyositis in one patient, together with a high titer of antibody to U1 ribonucleoprotein (anti-U1 RNP). U1 RNP is part of the spliceosome, the complex that removes introns from pre-mRNA. Anti-Smith antibody, which is specific for lupus, binds the core Sm proteins shared by the same small nuclear ribonucleoproteins, so low titers of anti-U1 RNP are common in lupus. What defines MCTD is a high anti-U1 RNP titer without the antibodies that define the other diseases. The ANA is strongly positive with a speckled pattern."},{"type":"table","title":"Overlap features of mixed connective tissue disease","cols":["Borrowed from","Typical features in MCTD","Usually absent in MCTD"],"rows":[["Systemic sclerosis","Raynaud phenomenon (nearly all patients), puffy swollen hands, sclerodactyly, esophageal dysmotility, pulmonary hypertension","Diffuse truncal skin thickening, renal crisis"],["Lupus","Arthritis, rash, serositis, cytopenias","Severe lupus nephritis, CNS disease, anti-dsDNA, anti-Smith"],["Polymyositis","Proximal muscle weakness with raised creatine kinase","Severe progressive myopathy"]],"caption":"MCTD usually spares the kidney and brain, the organs that make lupus most dangerous, but pulmonary arterial hypertension is its leading cause of death."},{"type":"p","text":"The mechanism follows the pattern of the parent diseases. Endothelial injury and intimal proliferation narrow digital and pulmonary arterioles, causing Raynaud phenomenon and pulmonary arterial hypertension; lymphocytic infiltration and immune complexes cause synovitis and serositis; and inflammatory infiltrates in muscle cause myositis. Puffy hands come from edema of the fingers, an early sign that may progress to sclerodactyly. Whether MCTD is a distinct disease or an early stage that later evolves into lupus or systemic sclerosis is debated, and some patients do evolve over years. On an exam, the combination of Raynaud phenomenon, swollen hands, myositis, and high-titer anti-U1 RNP without anti-dsDNA or anti-Smith points to MCTD."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading an autoantibody panel","text":"A 34-year-old woman has Raynaud phenomenon, swollen fingers, proximal weakness with high creatine kinase, and arthritis. ANA is high titer, speckled. Step 1: anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith are negative, so lupus is unlikely despite the arthritis. Step 2: anti-Scl-70 and anticentromere are negative, so diffuse or limited systemic sclerosis is unlikely despite the Raynaud phenomenon. Step 3: anti-U1 RNP is very high, which unites the three disease patterns as MCTD. Step 4: predict risk by mechanism. The kidney is usually spared, but the same arteriolar narrowing that causes her Raynaud phenomenon can obliterate pulmonary arterioles, so pulmonary arterial hypertension is the complication to anticipate."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology explain where these diseases strike. Systemic sclerosis injures the smallest arteries and the smooth muscle layers, so it damages the lower esophagus but spares its skeletal upper third, and narrowing of renal arterioles turns the juxtaglomerular renin response into a self-reinforcing crisis. Sjogren syndrome targets exocrine epithelium, so tear and saliva loss follow, while the fetal AV node is the only normal electrical path from atria to ventricles, so scarring it causes complete block. Placental physiology decides which antibodies matter: only IgG crosses, through FcRn, and it is cleared within months."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology, immunology, and pathology connect the triggers to the lesions. Rheumatic fever shows how a bacterial virulence factor can redirect immunity against the host through mimicry; systemic sclerosis shows how cytokines such as TGF-beta convert inflammation into fibrosis; and Sjogren syndrome shows how chronic antigen-driven B cell stimulation can end in lymphoma. In systemic sclerosis, Sjogren syndrome, and MCTD the autoantibodies mainly mark subset and prognosis, whereas in rheumatic fever and neonatal lupus antibody injures the target tissue directly. Their limits matter: anti-Ro and low-titer anti-U1 RNP also occur in lupus, and some patients have no subset antibody, so findings are interpreted together."}],"summary":["Systemic sclerosis combines endothelial injury, T and B cell autoimmunity, and TGF-beta driven myofibroblast collagen deposition, producing narrowed small arteries and fibrosis in every affected organ.","Raynaud phenomenon, often the first sign, is vasospasm of already narrowed digital arteries; esophageal fibrosis gives absent distal peristalsis and a weak LES with reflux.","Interstitial lung disease (restrictive, low DLCO) and pulmonary arterial hypertension are the leading causes of death in systemic sclerosis.","Diffuse disease: proximal and truncal skin, anti-Scl-70 (topoisomerase I), early lung fibrosis, and renal crisis from a renin-angiotensin positive feedback loop.","Limited disease (CREST): distal skin, anticentromere, calcinosis with normal serum calcium, telangiectasia, and late pulmonary arterial hypertension.","Acute rheumatic fever follows untreated group A streptococcal pharyngitis by 2 to 4 weeks; M protein mimicry of cardiac myosin and valve proteins (type II) causes pancarditis with Aschoff bodies and Anitschkow cells.","Jones criteria: evidence of streptococcal infection plus two major, or one major and two minor, manifestations; only carditis leaves lasting damage, and repeated attacks fuse mitral commissures into mitral stenosis.","Sjogren syndrome is lymphocytic destruction of lacrimal and salivary glands with anti-Ro and anti-La, often with RA, and a raised risk of marginal zone (MALT) lymphoma.","Maternal anti-Ro IgG crosses the placenta through FcRn and scars the fetal AV node, so congenital complete heart block is permanent while rash and cytopenias resolve.","Mixed connective tissue disease overlaps lupus, systemic sclerosis, and polymyositis with high-titer anti-U1 RNP, usually spares the kidney, and is most dangerous through pulmonary arterial hypertension."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System, autoimmune diseases: systemic sclerosis, Sjogren syndrome, and mixed connective tissue disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, rheumatic fever and rheumatic heart disease"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunologic tolerance and autoimmunity"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Streptococci, poststreptococcal diseases"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Inflammatory rheumatic diseases"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Role of the kidneys in long-term control of arterial pressure, the renin-angiotensin system"}]},"IM-15-E":{"unit":"IM-15-E","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-05","title":"Diseases of Amyloid","subject":"Immune System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"Amyloidosis is a group of diseases in which a normally soluble protein misfolds, assembles into insoluble fibrils, and is deposited outside cells, where it stiffens organs and crowds out working tissue. This chapter explains what makes a deposit amyloid, how it is recognized and confirmed on biopsy, and how the source protein decides which organs fail. It compares primary (AL) amyloidosis from a plasma cell clone with secondary (AA) amyloidosis from chronic inflammation, then matches the other important amyloid proteins to their settings. Board questions usually describe an organ pattern or an underlying disease and ask for the protein, the stain, or the expected finding, so the aim is to reason from precursor protein to clinical picture.","prerequisites":["Protein secondary structure: alpha helices and beta-pleated sheets","Immunoglobulin structure: heavy and light chains, variable and constant regions","Plasma cell differentiation and the meaning of a monoclonal protein","The acute-phase response and the cytokines IL-1, IL-6, and TNF","The glomerular filtration barrier and the definition of nephrotic syndrome"],"connections":[{"to":"Innate and adaptive immune activation (IM-13)","how":"Inflammasome signaling and cytokine-driven acute-phase protein synthesis are the upstream events of AA amyloidosis and familial Mediterranean fever."},{"to":"Blood cells and plasma (HE-2) and plasma cell neoplasms","how":"AL amyloid comes from the same kind of clonal plasma cell population seen in monoclonal gammopathy and multiple myeloma."},{"to":"Glomerular diseases (UR-12-A)","how":"Amyloid is a cause of nephrotic syndrome whose glomerular deposits are Congo red positive, unlike other causes."},{"to":"Cardiomyopathies","how":"AL and transthyretin amyloid are the classic infiltrative causes of restrictive cardiomyopathy with thick walls and low ECG voltage."},{"to":"Neurodegeneration and endocrine pathology","how":"Abeta in Alzheimer disease, calcitonin in medullary thyroid carcinoma, and amylin in type 2 diabetes are localized amyloids made by the local cells."}],"terms":[{"term":"Amyloid","def":"Extracellular deposit of insoluble protein fibrils built from cross-beta-pleated sheets, identified by apple-green birefringence after Congo red staining."},{"term":"Cross-beta structure","def":"Arrangement in which beta strands run perpendicular to the long axis of the fibril, stacking into a rigid, protease-resistant core."},{"term":"Congo red","def":"A dye that binds in an ordered way along amyloid fibrils, staining them red in ordinary light."},{"term":"Apple-green birefringence","def":"The green color of Congo red stained amyloid under polarized light, which confirms that a deposit is amyloid."},{"term":"Serum amyloid P component","def":"A plasma pentraxin that binds all amyloid fibril types and protects them from breakdown."},{"term":"Plasma cell dyscrasia","def":"A clonal expansion of plasma cells that secretes a single (monoclonal) immunoglobulin or light chain."},{"term":"Free light chain","def":"An immunoglobulin kappa or lambda light chain circulating unbound to heavy chain; in urine it is called a Bence Jones protein."},{"term":"Serum amyloid A","def":"An acute-phase apolipoprotein made by hepatocytes under IL-1, IL-6, and TNF; its N-terminal fragment forms AA amyloid."},{"term":"Transthyretin","def":"A liver-made tetrameric plasma protein that carries thyroxine and retinol-binding protein; its misfolded monomers form ATTR amyloid."},{"term":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy","def":"Heart muscle disease in which stiff ventricles fill poorly in diastole while systolic function is relatively preserved."},{"term":"Nephrotic syndrome","def":"Proteinuria above about 3.5 g per day with hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia, caused by injury to the glomerular filtration barrier."},{"term":"Autoinflammatory disease","def":"A disorder of innate immunity, such as familial Mediterranean fever, with recurrent inflammation but no autoantibodies or autoreactive T cells."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-05-C01","obj":"IM-15-E.1","title":"What amyloid is and how it is diagnosed","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Amyloid is not a single protein but a shared physical form that more than 30 unrelated proteins can adopt. A newly made protein normally folds into its native shape, and molecular chaperones, the ubiquitin-proteasome system, and autophagy refold or destroy molecules that misfold. Amyloid forms when misfolded molecules outpace these defenses: when a precursor is made in excess (light chains from a plasma cell clone, serum amyloid A in chronic inflammation), when a mutation destabilizes it (hereditary transthyretin), when its clearance fails (beta2-microglobulin in kidney failure), or with aging (wild-type transthyretin)."},{"type":"p","text":"Misfolded molecules expose hydrophobic segments that stack into beta-pleated sheets whose strands run perpendicular to the long axis of the fibril, the cross-beta structure. On electron microscopy the fibrils are nonbranching, about 7.5 to 10 nm wide, and of indefinite length. Tight, regular stacking makes them insoluble and resistant to proteolysis, so they accumulate instead of being cleared. About 95% of a deposit is fibril protein; the remainder is serum amyloid P component and glycosaminoglycans such as heparan sulfate, which bind the fibrils and help stabilize them."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a soluble protein becomes an amyloid deposit","steps":["Precursor protein is in excess, mutant, or poorly cleared","Partial unfolding exposes hydrophobic segments","Monomers assemble into soluble oligomers","Oligomers stack into cross-beta fibrils","Serum amyloid P and glycosaminoglycans coat the fibrils","Protease-resistant deposits accumulate outside cells"],"caption":"Deposits grow when production of misfolded protein exceeds clearance. Soluble precursors and oligomers can also be directly toxic, as AL light chains are to cardiac myocytes."},{"type":"p","text":"Deposits damage organs mainly by physical means. They are laid down between cells, along basement membranes, and in vessel walls, where they compress parenchymal cells into atrophy, stiffen tissue, and weaken small vessels. In the kidney they collect in the mesangium and along glomerular capillary loops; in the heart, between myocytes; in the liver, in the space of Disse; and in the spleen, in follicles or the red pulp. Affected organs are often enlarged, firm, and waxy."},{"type":"h","text":"Recognizing amyloid in tissue"},{"type":"p","text":"On hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining, amyloid is pink, amorphous, glassy extracellular material that can be confused with collagen or other hyaline deposits. Congo red dye binds along the ordered fibrils. In ordinary light the deposits look red to pink; under polarized light the aligned dye gives apple-green birefringence, and this finding defines amyloid. Because the stain does not reveal the precursor, the protein is then typed by immunohistochemistry or, most reliably, by mass spectrometry, and the type defines the disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Where to biopsy for amyloid","cols":["Site","Advantage","Limitation"],"rows":[["Abdominal fat pad aspirate","Simple and low risk; samples small vessels in systemic disease","Can miss deposits, more often in ATTR than AL; a negative result does not exclude amyloid"],["Bone marrow, minor salivary gland, or rectal biopsy","Easily reached tissue; marrow also shows the plasma cell clone in AL","Moderate sensitivity"],["Affected organ (kidney, heart, liver, nerve)","Highest yield; proves that organ is involved","Invasive; fragile vessels raise bleeding risk"]],"caption":"A screening site is tried first in suspected systemic disease; an organ biopsy is used when screening is negative but suspicion remains."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A glomerulus contains pink acellular material on H&E. It looks faintly red with Congo red but shows no green color under polarized light. Can you call it amyloid?","explain":"No. Many hyaline deposits are pink on H&E, and red color alone is not specific. Apple-green birefringence arises only when dye is aligned along ordered cross-beta fibrils, so it is the required finding.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on recognizing amyloid in tissue."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-05-C02","obj":"IM-15-E.2","title":"Primary (AL) amyloidosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Primary amyloidosis, or AL (amyloid light chain) amyloidosis, arises from a plasma cell dyscrasia: a clone of plasma cells that secretes a monoclonal immunoglobulin light chain in excess of heavy chain. Whole free light chains, or N-terminal fragments containing the variable region, misfold and form fibrils. Lambda light chains are involved about three times as often as kappa. The clone is usually small, with only modest marrow plasmacytosis, and most patients do not have multiple myeloma; conversely, about 5% to 15% of patients with myeloma develop AL amyloid. Because free light chains reach every tissue through the blood, AL is systemic and characteristically involves the heart, kidney, nerves, soft tissues, and vessel walls."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of AL amyloidosis","steps":["Clonal plasma cells expand in the bone marrow","Excess free monoclonal light chain enters plasma (usually lambda)","Variable region fragments misfold and aggregate","Fibrils deposit in organs and vessel walls throughout the body","Stiff heart, leaky glomeruli, compressed nerves, fragile vessels"],"caption":"The clone may be too small to cause myeloma, yet its light chain alone is enough to produce systemic amyloid."},{"type":"p","text":"The clone is found with serum and urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation and the serum free light chain assay; free light chains in urine are Bence Jones proteins. The normal serum kappa to lambda ratio is about 0.26 to 1.65, so a low ratio with a raised lambda level points to a lambda clone. A monoclonal protein does not prove AL, because monoclonal gammopathy is common in older adults who may have transthyretin amyloid instead, so the deposit itself must be typed."},{"type":"table","title":"AL amyloidosis: findings explained by mechanism","cols":["Site","Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Heart","Restrictive cardiomyopathy: heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, low-voltage QRS, AV block, atrial fibrillation","Interstitial deposits stiffen the ventricles and replace myocytes and conduction tissue"],["Kidney","Nephrotic syndrome: proteinuria above about 3.5 g/day, hypoalbuminemia, edema, hyperlipidemia","Mesangial and capillary loop deposits disrupt the glomerular filtration barrier"],["Tongue","Macroglossia, with tooth indentations along the lateral borders","Deposits enlarge the tongue muscle and stroma"],["Skin and small vessels","Periorbital purpura after coughing or straining; easy bruising","Deposits make vessel walls fragile; factor X binding to fibrils can add an acquired factor X deficiency"],["Wrist","Carpal tunnel syndrome","Deposits in the flexor retinaculum and tendon sheaths compress the median nerve"],["Peripheral and autonomic nerves","Symmetric painful sensory neuropathy; orthostatic hypotension, gastrointestinal dysmotility","Deposits in nerves and their vessels injure small fibers first"],["Liver","Hepatomegaly with raised alkaline phosphatase","Deposits fill the space of Disse"]],"caption":"Most findings follow from stiffness, compression, or vessel fragility; the heart and kidney determine outcome."},{"type":"p","text":"The heart decides survival in AL. Deposits between myocytes thicken the ventricular walls, but this is infiltration, not hypertrophy: working muscle is unchanged or lost, so the ECG shows low QRS voltage while the echocardiogram shows thick walls. The stiff ventricle fills poorly, so filling pressures and jugular venous pressure rise, the atria enlarge, and stroke volume falls even while ejection fraction is preserved early. Infiltration of the conduction system explains AV block and atrial arrhythmias."},{"type":"example","title":"Thick walls with low voltage","text":"A 62-year-old woman has dyspnea and edema. Echocardiography shows a 16 mm left ventricular wall (normal up to about 11 mm) with preserved ejection fraction, yet QRS voltage is low. Urine protein is 6 g/day, and she has periorbital purpura. Step 1: hypertensive hypertrophy adds muscle and raises voltage, so thick walls with low voltage mean the wall is thickened by something that is not muscle, an infiltrate. Step 2: heart, glomeruli, and small vessels together suggest a circulating precursor, the pattern of a light chain. Step 3: confirm amyloid with Congo red on a fat pad aspirate and find the clone; a low kappa to lambda ratio supports AL. Wild-type transthyretin amyloid would not explain the nephrotic syndrome."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-05-C03","obj":"IM-15-E.3","title":"Secondary (AA) amyloidosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Serum amyloid A (SAA) is an acute-phase apolipoprotein that travels with high-density lipoprotein. When IL-6, IL-1, and TNF signal inflammation, hepatocytes raise SAA synthesis, and plasma levels can climb up to about 1,000-fold within a day or two. Normally the excess is cleared once inflammation settles. When inflammation persists for years, SAA stays high, and incomplete breakdown by macrophage proteases leaves an N-terminal fragment of about 76 amino acids, the AA protein, which forms fibrils. Secondary (reactive systemic) amyloidosis is therefore a complication of another disease, and its risk tracks how high and how long SAA stays elevated."},{"type":"flow","title":"From chronic inflammation to nephrotic syndrome","steps":["Persistent inflammation: RA, IBD, chronic infection, or FMF","Macrophages release IL-1, IL-6, and TNF","Hepatocytes sustain high serum amyloid A production","Incomplete proteolysis leaves the N-terminal AA fragment","AA fibrils deposit in kidney, liver, spleen, and adrenals","Glomerular deposits cause proteinuria and nephrotic syndrome"],"caption":"The cytokines that drive the underlying disease are the same ones that drive SAA production, so uncontrolled inflammation feeds amyloid formation."},{"type":"table","title":"Settings of AA amyloidosis","cols":["Category","Examples","Why SAA stays high"],"rows":[["Chronic inflammatory arthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Continuous cytokine production in inflamed synovium"],["Inflammatory bowel disease","Crohn disease more often than ulcerative colitis","Chronic transmural inflammation"],["Chronic infection","Tuberculosis, bronchiectasis, chronic osteomyelitis, skin abscesses from injection drug use","Unresolved infection sustains the acute-phase response"],["Autoinflammatory disease","Familial Mediterranean fever and other periodic fever syndromes","Inflammasome overactivity releases IL-1 in repeated attacks"],["Some tumors","Renal cell carcinoma, Hodgkin lymphoma","Tumor-associated cytokines"]],"caption":"Any disease that sustains the acute-phase response for years can lead to AA amyloid."},{"type":"p","text":"Familial Mediterranean fever (FMF) is an autosomal recessive autoinflammatory disease most common in people of Sephardic Jewish, Armenian, Turkish, and Arab ancestry. The MEFV gene encodes pyrin, and disease-causing mutations allow inappropriate activation of the pyrin inflammasome, which activates caspase-1 and releases IL-1 beta. Patients have attacks of fever lasting about 1 to 3 days with sterile serositis (peritonitis causing severe abdominal pain, pleuritis), monoarthritis, and an erysipelas-like rash on the lower legs, separated by symptom-free intervals. SAA rises with each attack and often stays raised between attacks, so AA amyloid of the kidney is the main long-term threat. Because the inflammation comes from innate immunity, with no autoantibodies or autoreactive T cells, FMF is autoinflammatory rather than autoimmune."},{"type":"p","text":"The kidney is the dominant target of AA amyloid. Deposits in the mesangium and along glomerular capillary walls damage the filtration barrier, causing proteinuria that often reaches the nephrotic range, followed by progressive loss of filtration as glomeruli are replaced. The kidneys are often normal in size or enlarged, though long-standing disease shrinks them. The liver, spleen, adrenal glands, and gut are also involved, but clinically important heart disease is uncommon and macroglossia is characteristic of AL rather than AA, which helps separate the two."},{"type":"table","title":"AL compared with AA amyloidosis","cols":["Feature","AL (primary)","AA (secondary)"],"rows":[["Precursor","Monoclonal immunoglobulin light chain","Serum amyloid A"],["Key laboratory clue","Abnormal free light chain ratio; monoclonal band on immunofixation","Persistently raised CRP and SAA; known inflammatory disease"],["Heart","Common; main cause of death","Uncommon"],["Kidney","Nephrotic syndrome common","Main organ; nephrotic syndrome and kidney failure"],["Distinctive findings","Macroglossia, periorbital purpura, carpal tunnel syndrome, neuropathy","Findings of the underlying disease"]],"caption":"Both are systemic and cause nephrotic syndrome; precursor and organ pattern separate them."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man with 15 years of poorly controlled rheumatoid arthritis develops edema and 6 g/day of proteinuria. His serum free light chain ratio is normal and immunofixation shows no monoclonal band. What protein is in his glomeruli?","explain":"AA protein from serum amyloid A. Years of IL-1, IL-6, and TNF drive hepatic SAA, and the kidney is the main AA target. No light chain clone argues against AL.","check":"Compare with the AA pathogenesis flow and the AL versus AA table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-05-C04","obj":"IM-15-E.4","title":"Other amyloid proteins and their settings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each amyloid type is named A followed by an abbreviation of its precursor. Systemic types circulate and deposit in many organs; localized types are made and deposited in one tissue."},{"type":"table","title":"Amyloid proteins and their settings","cols":["Type","Precursor","Setting","Main deposits and findings"],"rows":[["AL","Immunoglobulin light chain","Plasma cell dyscrasia, sometimes multiple myeloma","Heart, kidney, tongue, nerves, skin vessels"],["AA","Serum amyloid A","Chronic inflammation, FMF","Kidney, liver, spleen, adrenals"],["Abeta2M","Beta2-microglobulin","Long-term dialysis","Synovium, tendons, bone"],["ATTR, wild type","Normal transthyretin","Older adults, mostly men over 70","Heart; carpal tunnel syndrome"],["ATTR, hereditary","Mutant transthyretin","Autosomal dominant mutations","Peripheral and autonomic nerves, heart"],["Abeta","Amyloid precursor protein","Alzheimer disease, Down syndrome","Neuritic plaques; cerebral vessel walls"],["ACal","Calcitonin (procalcitonin)","Medullary thyroid carcinoma","Tumor stroma"],["AIAPP","Amylin (islet amyloid polypeptide)","Type 2 diabetes","Pancreatic islets"]],"caption":"AL, AA, Abeta2M, and ATTR are systemic; Abeta, ACal, and AIAPP are localized."},{"type":"p","text":"Beta2-microglobulin is the invariant light chain of MHC class I and is shed from all nucleated cells. At about 12 kDa it is freely filtered and is normally reabsorbed and catabolized by the proximal tubule. In kidney failure it accumulates, and conventional dialysis membranes remove it poorly, so after years of dialysis it forms Abeta2M amyloid with a strong affinity for joints and tendons, causing carpal tunnel syndrome, shoulder pain, destructive arthropathy, and bone cysts."},{"type":"p","text":"Transthyretin is made by the liver and circulates as a tetramer that carries thyroxine and retinol-binding protein, which gives it its name (an older term is prealbumin). Amyloid forms when the tetramer dissociates into monomers that misfold, the rate-limiting step shown below. In wild-type ATTR amyloidosis, normal transthyretin deposits in the hearts of older adults, often after years of bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome. In hereditary ATTR amyloidosis a point mutation destabilizes the tetramer: Val30Met classically causes familial amyloid polyneuropathy with autonomic failure, while Val122Ile, carried by about 3% to 4% of Black Americans, mainly causes late-onset cardiomyopathy. ATTR has no monoclonal protein and rarely causes nephrotic syndrome."},{"type":"flow","title":"Transthyretin amyloid formation","steps":["Liver secretes transthyretin as a stable tetramer","Aging or a point mutation destabilizes the tetramer","Tetramer dissociates into monomers","Monomers partly unfold and aggregate","Fibrils deposit in heart, ligaments, and nerves"],"caption":"Tetramer dissociation is the rate-limiting step, so anything that destabilizes the tetramer speeds amyloid formation."},{"type":"p","text":"Abeta is a 40 to 42 amino acid peptide cut from amyloid precursor protein (APP), a membrane protein encoded on chromosome 21, by sequential beta-secretase and gamma-secretase cleavage; Abeta42 aggregates most readily. It forms the cores of extracellular neuritic plaques in Alzheimer disease and deposits in cortical and leptomeningeal vessels as cerebral amyloid angiopathy, a cause of lobar hemorrhage in older adults. An extra copy of APP explains early Alzheimer disease in Down syndrome. Neurofibrillary tangles are intracellular aggregates of hyperphosphorylated tau, a separate lesion. Endocrine amyloid comes from a cell's own secretory product: calcitonin from the C cells of medullary thyroid carcinoma forms amyloid in the tumor stroma, and amylin, co-secreted with insulin by beta cells, deposits in islets in type 2 diabetes, where it is associated with beta cell loss."},{"type":"example","title":"Three patients with carpal tunnel syndrome","text":"Three patients have carpal tunnel syndrome with apple-green birefringent deposits in the flexor retinaculum. Patient 1 has been on hemodialysis for 9 years: failed renal clearance of beta2-microglobulin points to Abeta2M amyloid. Patient 2 is a 78-year-old man who later develops heart failure with thick ventricular walls, low voltage, and no monoclonal protein: aging destabilizes normal transthyretin, so this is wild-type ATTR. Patient 3, a 58-year-old woman with macroglossia, nephrotic syndrome, and a high lambda free light chain, has AL amyloid. The stain is identical in all three; the setting and protein typing identify the disease."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Amyloidosis links biochemistry to pathology through one idea: any protein that is overproduced, destabilized, or poorly cleared can escape protein quality control and fold into the same cross-beta fibril. Immunology supplies the two commonest precursors. A plasma cell clone that would otherwise be a harmless monoclonal gammopathy causes AL amyloid through the physical behavior of its light chain, and the innate cytokines IL-1, IL-6, and TNF that drive rheumatoid arthritis, Crohn disease, chronic infection, and familial Mediterranean fever also drive the hepatic SAA that becomes AA amyloid. Renal physiology explains Abeta2M amyloid, because the proximal tubule normally disposes of beta2-microglobulin."},{"type":"p","text":"The stain unifies the diagnosis but does not separate the diseases. Congo red with apple-green birefringence proves amyloid; the organ pattern and the precursor decide the type. This matters because the same restrictive cardiomyopathy can come from AL or ATTR, and a monoclonal protein in an older patient can be incidental. The limit of pattern recognition is overlap, so board reasoning should move from the setting to the likely precursor and then to the test that confirms it."}],"summary":["Amyloid is an extracellular deposit of insoluble cross-beta-pleated sheet fibrils that resist proteolysis and damage organs by compression, stiffening, and vessel fragility.","Congo red stains amyloid red; apple-green birefringence under polarized light is the defining finding, and the precursor is then typed by immunohistochemistry or mass spectrometry.","Abdominal fat pad aspirate is the simple first biopsy in systemic disease, but a negative result does not exclude amyloid; organ biopsy has the highest yield.","AL amyloid comes from monoclonal free light chains (lambda more than kappa) of a plasma cell dyscrasia; most patients do not have overt multiple myeloma.","AL predicts restrictive cardiomyopathy with thick walls and low ECG voltage, nephrotic syndrome, macroglossia, periorbital purpura, carpal tunnel syndrome, and neuropathy.","AA amyloid comes from serum amyloid A, an acute-phase protein driven by IL-1, IL-6, and TNF in years of RA, IBD, chronic infection, or familial Mediterranean fever.","The kidney is the main target of AA amyloid, presenting as proteinuria and nephrotic syndrome; the heart is rarely significant.","Familial Mediterranean fever is an autosomal recessive pyrin (MEFV) inflammasome disorder with IL-1 driven fever and serositis, and AA amyloid is its main long-term risk.","Beta2-microglobulin amyloid follows long-term dialysis; transthyretin amyloid is wild type in the aging heart or hereditary in nerves and heart.","Localized amyloids come from local products: Abeta in Alzheimer plaques and vessels, calcitonin in medullary thyroid carcinoma, and amylin in type 2 diabetic islets."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System, section on amyloidosis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart, section on restrictive cardiomyopathy and cardiac amyloidosis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, section on Alzheimer disease"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Innate immunity, inflammasomes and the acute-phase response"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Structure of Proteins, protein misfolding and amyloid"}]},"IM-15-F":{"unit":"IM-15-F","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-06","title":"Systemic Infectious Diseases","subject":"Immune System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the classic systemic infections of childhood and young adulthood: the rash illnesses (measles, rubella, erythema infectiosum, roseola, scarlet fever), Haemophilus influenzae type b, influenza, mumps, Epstein-Barr virus and cytomegalovirus mononucleosis, and toxoplasmosis. Board questions give an age, a rash pattern, and a timeline, then ask for the organism and the complication most likely in a particular host. Nearly every complication here follows from three questions: which cell the organism infects, which arm of immunity controls it, and what happens when that host is pregnant, has a short red cell lifespan, or has lost T cell immunity.","prerequisites":["Structure and replication of DNA and RNA viruses, enveloped and nonenveloped","T-independent versus T-dependent antibody responses and the role of the spleen","Cell-mediated immunity: Th1 cells, IFN-gamma, macrophage activation, and CD8 cytotoxic T cells","Erythropoiesis, reticulocytes, and red cell lifespan","Hypersensitivity types II and III"],"connections":[{"to":"Hematology","how":"Parvovirus B19 halts erythropoiesis and EBV triggers cold agglutinin hemolysis, so red cell kinetics explain who becomes anemic."},{"to":"Cardiovascular and renal pathology","how":"Group A streptococcal pharyngitis leads to rheumatic heart disease and poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis through two different hypersensitivity mechanisms."},{"to":"Embryology and teratology","how":"Rubella, CMV, Toxoplasma, and parvovirus B19 cross the placenta, and the organs damaged depend on what is forming at the time of infection."},{"to":"HIV and transplant immunology","how":"Loss of T cell surveillance reactivates latent CMV, EBV, and Toxoplasma, producing retinitis, lymphoma, and ring-enhancing brain lesions."},{"to":"Neoplasia","how":"EBV latent proteins drive B cell proliferation and block apoptosis, linking a common infection to Burkitt lymphoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, and nasopharyngeal carcinoma."}],"terms":[{"term":"Exanthem","def":"A widespread skin rash that accompanies a systemic, usually viral, infection."},{"term":"Enanthem","def":"A rash on a mucous membrane, such as Koplik spots on the buccal mucosa in measles."},{"term":"Aplastic crisis","def":"A sudden fall in hemoglobin with almost no reticulocytes, caused by a temporary stop in red cell production in a patient whose red cells already survive only a short time."},{"term":"Hydrops fetalis","def":"Abnormal fluid accumulation in at least two fetal compartments (skin, pleura, pericardium, peritoneum), here caused by severe fetal anemia and heart failure."},{"term":"Polyribosylribitol phosphate (PRP)","def":"The polysaccharide capsule of H influenzae type b that resists complement and phagocytosis."},{"term":"Atypical lymphocyte","def":"A large activated T lymphocyte, mostly CD8, with abundant basophilic cytoplasm, seen in the blood in mononucleosis."},{"term":"Heterophile antibody","def":"An IgM antibody produced during EBV infection that agglutinates sheep or horse red cells even though it is not directed against EBV."},{"term":"Latency","def":"Persistence of a viral genome in host cells with little or no virus production, allowing later reactivation."},{"term":"Superantigen","def":"A toxin that links MHC class II to the T cell receptor outside the antigen groove, activating many T cells at once and causing massive cytokine release."},{"term":"Molecular mimicry","def":"Cross-reaction of antibodies or T cells raised against a microbial antigen with a similar host antigen, as in rheumatic fever."},{"term":"Ring-enhancing lesion","def":"A brain lesion whose contrast-enhancing rim marks inflammation and a broken blood-brain barrier around a necrotic center."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-06-C01","obj":"IM-15-F.1","title":"Parvovirus B19: erythema infectiosum, aplastic crisis, and hydrops fetalis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Parvovirus B19 is a small, nonenveloped, single-stranded DNA virus spread mainly by respiratory droplets, and less often by blood products and across the placenta. Its receptor is the P antigen (globoside), which is dense on erythroid progenitors, so the virus replicates in and kills pronormoblasts in the marrow. During viremia, about one week after exposure, red cell production stops for about 7 to 10 days. With a normal red cell lifespan of about 120 days, that pause lowers hemoglobin only slightly and goes unnoticed."},{"type":"p","text":"In school-age children the illness is erythema infectiosum (fifth disease). A mild, flu-like prodrome accompanies the viremia. About one to two weeks later, as IgM and IgG appear, bright red cheeks with relative sparing around the mouth (slapped cheek) are followed by a lacy, reticular rash on the extensor limbs and trunk that can fade and return for weeks with heat, sunlight, or exercise. The rash and joint symptoms are produced by immune complexes, so by the time the rash appears the child is no longer viremic or contagious. Adults, especially women, more often develop symmetric arthralgia or arthritis of the hands, wrists, and knees, frequently without the rash."},{"type":"h","text":"Why some hosts become severely anemic"},{"type":"p","text":"The same one-week pause in production becomes dangerous whenever red cells survive only briefly or demand is very high. In sickle cell disease or hereditary spherocytosis, red cells may last only 10 to 20 days, so hemoglobin falls sharply over a few days. The clue is a reticulocyte count near zero, the opposite of the high count expected in hemolysis, and the marrow shows giant pronormoblasts with intranuclear inclusions. A fetus has a rapidly expanding red cell mass and shorter red cell survival, and the virus can also infect fetal myocardium, so severe anemia leads to high-output heart failure and hydrops fetalis, with the greatest risk after maternal infection in the first half of pregnancy."},{"type":"table","title":"Outcome of parvovirus B19 infection by host","cols":["Host","Why the host is vulnerable","Result"],"rows":[["Healthy child","Normal red cell survival; prompt antibody response","Erythema infectiosum; trivial fall in hemoglobin"],["Healthy adult","Immune complexes deposit in joints","Symmetric small-joint arthropathy, often without rash"],["Chronic hemolytic anemia","Red cells survive days to weeks, so a production pause cannot be absorbed","Transient aplastic crisis with reticulocytes near zero"],["Immunocompromised (HIV, transplant, chemotherapy)","Cannot make neutralizing antibody to clear the virus","Chronic pure red cell aplasia"],["Fetus","Expanding red cell mass, short red cell survival, possible myocarditis","Severe anemia, heart failure, hydrops fetalis, fetal death"]],"caption":"The virus does the same thing in every host; the consequence depends on red cell lifespan and on the ability to make antibody."},{"type":"flow","title":"Parvovirus B19 aplastic crisis","steps":["Virus binds P antigen on erythroid progenitors","Virus replicates in and lyses pronormoblasts","Red cell production stops for about a week","Short red cell survival turns the pause into severe anemia","Reticulocytes near zero; in the fetus, hydrops fetalis"],"caption":"The production pause is harmless with a 120-day red cell lifespan and dangerous when cells survive only days."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child with hereditary spherocytosis has a hemoglobin that falls from 10 to 5 g/dL over four days after a cold. What will the reticulocyte count show, and how does that separate this event from a hemolytic crisis?","explain":"Parvovirus B19 has stopped production, so reticulocytes are near zero; in a hemolytic crisis the marrow is working and the reticulocyte count rises.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on why some hosts become severely anemic and the host table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-06-C02","obj":"IM-15-F.2","title":"Haemophilus influenzae: invasive type b disease and nontypeable mucosal infection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Haemophilus influenzae is a small gram-negative coccobacillus that colonizes the nasopharynx and spreads by respiratory droplets. It needs factor X (hemin) and factor V (NAD) to grow, which is why it grows on chocolate agar or around Staphylococcus aureus colonies on blood agar. Strains with a capsule are typed a to f, and the type b capsule, polyribosylribitol phosphate (PRP), is the key virulence factor: it blocks complement deposition and phagocytosis, letting the organism survive in blood and seed the meninges, epiglottis, joints, and lungs. An IgA protease helps all strains colonize mucosa."},{"type":"p","text":"Clearing an encapsulated organism requires anticapsular antibody that opsonizes it, followed by removal in the spleen. Maternal IgG protects for the first few months. Children younger than about 2 years respond poorly to T-independent polysaccharide antigens, so between roughly 3 months and 3 years they had little anti-PRP antibody and invasive Hib disease peaked. The conjugate vaccine links PRP to a protein carrier, which recruits T cell help, class switching, and memory even in infants. Invasive Hib disease is now seen mainly in unvaccinated or incompletely vaccinated children and in people with asplenia or antibody or complement deficiency."},{"type":"h","text":"Invasive syndromes"},{"type":"p","text":"Epiglottitis is cellulitis of the supraglottic tissues. The swollen epiglottis narrows the laryngeal inlet, an extrathoracic site, so obstruction is worst during inspiration, when airway pressure falls below atmospheric pressure, producing inspiratory stridor. The child has abrupt high fever and looks toxic, drools because swallowing is painful, has a muffled voice, and sits upright, leaning forward on the hands with the chin thrust up (tripod position), to hold the airway open. A lateral neck film shows a swollen epiglottis (thumb sign). Croup, usually caused by parainfluenza virus, differs by gradual onset, low fever, barking cough, and subglottic narrowing (steeple sign). Before vaccination Hib was also a leading cause of bacterial meningitis in young children, often leaving sensorineural hearing loss, and caused bacteremia, pneumonia, septic arthritis, and facial cellulitis."},{"type":"table","title":"Type b versus nontypeable H influenzae","cols":["Feature","Type b (Hib)","Nontypeable"],"rows":[["Capsule","PRP polysaccharide","None"],["Survival in blood","Good; resists complement and phagocytosis","Poor; rarely invades the bloodstream"],["Spread","Hematogenous to meninges, epiglottis, joints, lungs","Contiguous from nasopharynx to nearby mucosa"],["Typical disease","Epiglottitis, meningitis, bacteremia, pneumonia, septic arthritis","Otitis media, sinusitis, conjunctivitis, bronchitis and COPD exacerbations"],["Typical host","Unvaccinated child under about 5 years","Children of any vaccine status; adults with chronic lung disease"],["Prevented by Hib conjugate vaccine","Yes","No; the vaccine targets PRP"]],"caption":"The capsule decides whether the organism invades the blood or stays on mucosal surfaces."},{"type":"p","text":"Nontypeable strains lack a capsule, so complement and phagocytes clear them from blood easily. They therefore cause disease by spreading along mucosal surfaces from the nasopharynx into the middle ear, sinuses, conjunctiva, and lower airways, where obstruction of drainage or damaged mucociliary clearance lets them persist. Because the vaccine induces only anti-PRP antibody, these infections remain common in fully vaccinated children."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-06-C03","obj":"IM-15-F.3","title":"Influenza and its complications","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Influenza virus is an orthomyxovirus: enveloped, with a genome of eight segments of negative-sense RNA. Hemagglutinin binds sialic acid on respiratory epithelium and mediates fusion inside the endosome, and neuraminidase cleaves sialic acid so new virions can leave the cell and pass through mucus. Point mutations in these two genes (antigenic drift) cause yearly epidemics, while reassortment of whole segments between human and animal strains (antigenic shift), which occurs only in influenza A, produces pandemics."},{"type":"p","text":"After an incubation of about 1 to 4 days, illness begins abruptly with fever, often 38 to 40 °C (100.4 to 104 °F), chills, headache, myalgia, malaise, sore throat, and a dry cough. The virus stays in the respiratory tract; the systemic symptoms come from circulating cytokines such as type I interferons, IL-6, and TNF. Fever usually lasts 3 to 5 days, but cough and fatigue can persist for weeks while the epithelium regenerates. Complications are most likely in adults over 65, children under 2, pregnant women, and people with chronic heart or lung disease or immunosuppression."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications of influenza","cols":["Complication","Timing and clues","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Primary viral pneumonia","Within the first few days; progressive dyspnea, hypoxemia, bilateral interstitial infiltrates","Virus spreads to the alveoli and causes diffuse alveolar damage"],["Secondary bacterial pneumonia","Improvement for several days, then new fever, productive cough, lobar consolidation; S pneumoniae most often, S aureus (often necrotizing, with cavities), H influenzae","Loss of ciliated epithelium and exposed basement membrane let bacteria adhere and invade"],["Reye syndrome","Child or adolescent given aspirin during influenza or varicella; vomiting, then confusion, seizures, coma, with hepatic dysfunction but little or no jaundice","Salicylate plus viral infection injures hepatic and other mitochondria"]],"caption":"The relapse after initial improvement is the clue that separates bacterial superinfection from primary viral pneumonia."},{"type":"flow","title":"Influenza to secondary bacterial pneumonia","steps":["Hemagglutinin binds sialic acid on airway epithelium","Virus kills ciliated epithelial cells","Mucociliary clearance fails; basement membrane exposed","S aureus or S pneumoniae adhere and invade","Improvement, then relapse with bacterial pneumonia"],"caption":"Viral injury removes the barrier; bacteria cause the second illness."},{"type":"p","text":"Reye syndrome shows why mitochondrial failure affects liver and brain together. Impaired mitochondrial beta-oxidation causes microvesicular fatty change in hepatocytes and hypoglycemia, and loss of the mitochondrial urea cycle enzymes raises blood ammonia. Aminotransferases rise while bilirubin stays normal or only mildly raised. Ammonia is taken up by astrocytes and converted to glutamine, which swells them and produces cerebral edema and raised intracranial pressure without inflammation in the brain. The syndrome became rare once aspirin was no longer given to febrile children."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-06-C04","obj":"IM-15-F.4","title":"Measles: prodrome, rash, immune suppression, and complications","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Measles (rubeola) virus is a paramyxovirus: enveloped, with nonsegmented negative-sense RNA. It is among the most contagious human pathogens, spreading by airborne droplets to about 90% of susceptible contacts. Its H protein binds CD150 (SLAM) on activated lymphocytes, dendritic cells, and macrophages, and nectin-4 on airway epithelium; its F protein fuses cells into multinucleated giant cells. The virus travels from airway immune cells to lymph nodes, spreads by viremia to skin, conjunctiva, and respiratory and gut mucosa, and is shed through the epithelium."},{"type":"p","text":"After about 10 to 14 days of incubation, a 2 to 4 day prodrome brings high fever and the three Cs: cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis with photophobia. Koplik spots, small bluish-white spots on a red base on the buccal mucosa opposite the molars, appear 1 to 2 days before the rash and are pathognomonic. The maculopapular rash begins at the hairline and behind the ears, spreads down over about 3 days, becomes confluent on the face and upper body, and fades in the same order with brownish staining and fine scaling. Fever peaks with the rash and the child looks ill. The rash reflects CD8 T cells attacking infected cells in dermal capillaries, so in severe T cell deficiency it may be absent while a fatal giant cell pneumonia progresses."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the virus infects and kills memory lymphocytes, measles causes lymphopenia, loss of tuberculin skin reactivity, and a prolonged loss of existing immunity (immune amnesia). This explains why bacterial superinfections and other infections follow measles for weeks to months. Lymphoid tissue shows Warthin-Finkeldey multinucleated giant cells."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications of measles","cols":["Complication","Timing","Mechanism or clue"],"rows":[["Otitis media","During or soon after the rash","Most common complication; bacterial superinfection"],["Pneumonia","During or after the rash","Most common cause of measles death; viral giant cell pneumonia or secondary bacterial pneumonia"],["Acute encephalitis","Within about 2 weeks of the rash; about 1 per 1,000 cases","Mostly postinfectious autoimmune demyelination (acute disseminated encephalomyelitis); fever, seizures, altered consciousness; often leaves deficits"],["Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE)","Years later, often 7 to 10","Persistent defective virus (faulty M protein) spreads between neurons; cognitive decline, myoclonus, death; high anti-measles antibody in CSF; risk higher after infection before age 2"],["Keratitis and blindness","During illness","Especially with vitamin A deficiency"]],"caption":"Respiratory complications kill most patients; the neurologic complications differ by timing and mechanism."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin A, as retinoic acid, is needed to maintain differentiated respiratory, intestinal, and corneal epithelium and for normal mucosal immune responses. Measles consumes body stores of vitamin A, and children who start deficient have more severe pneumonia and diarrhea, corneal ulceration progressing to blindness, and higher mortality. This is why measles deaths are concentrated in malnourished children."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-06-C05","obj":"IM-15-F.5","title":"Infectious mononucleosis: EBV, atypical lymphocytes, and heterophile antibodies","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV, human herpesvirus 4) is an enveloped double-stranded DNA gammaherpesvirus spread in saliva. It infects oropharyngeal epithelium and B lymphocytes: its glycoprotein gp350 binds CD21 (complement receptor 2) on B cells, and MHC class II serves as a coreceptor. Infected B cells proliferate and spread through lymphoid tissue. Most infections in young children are silent, but a first infection in an adolescent or young adult (about 15 to 24 years) usually causes infectious mononucleosis after an incubation of about 4 to 6 weeks. The symptoms come mainly from the vigorous T cell response, which is why they are worse in older patients."},{"type":"p","text":"The classic patient has fever, marked fatigue, sore throat with enlarged, often exudative tonsils, and lymphadenopathy that includes the posterior cervical chain (strep pharyngitis usually involves only anterior nodes). Splenomegaly occurs in about half, from lymphoid hyperplasia, and palatal petechiae and mild hepatomegaly are common."},{"type":"p","text":"Blood shows an absolute lymphocytosis with atypical lymphocytes, usually more than 10% of lymphocytes. These are mainly activated CD8 T cells killing infected B cells: large cells with abundant pale blue cytoplasm that scallops around neighboring red cells. They are not infected B cells. Heterophile antibodies are IgM made by polyclonally activated B cells; they agglutinate sheep or horse red cells (the basis of the Monospot test) but do not recognize EBV."},{"type":"table","title":"Laboratory evidence of EBV infection","cols":["Test","What it detects","Interpretation"],"rows":[["Atypical lymphocytes","Activated CD8 T cells","Supportive; also seen in CMV, acute HIV, toxoplasmosis"],["Heterophile (Monospot)","Polyclonal IgM against sheep or horse red cells","Positive in about 85% of adolescents and adults; often negative in the first week and in children under 4"],["Anti-VCA IgM","Antibody to viral capsid antigen","Acute infection"],["Anti-EBNA IgG","Antibody to nuclear antigen","Appears weeks to months later; its presence argues against acute infection"]],"caption":"A negative heterophile test early in illness or in a young child does not exclude EBV."},{"type":"p","text":"When an adolescent with EBV pharyngitis is thought to have strep throat and receives amoxicillin or ampicillin, a generalized, itchy maculopapular rash commonly appears within several days to about a week of starting the drug. It is thought to be an immune reaction to the drug in the setting of intense B and T cell activation, and it usually does not indicate a lasting penicillin allergy."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 17-year-old with 3 days of fever, exudative pharyngitis, posterior cervical nodes, and atypical lymphocytes has a negative Monospot. Does this exclude EBV?","explain":"No. Heterophile IgM takes time to rise and is often negative in the first week; repeat testing or anti-VCA IgM would answer the question.","check":"Compare with the table on laboratory evidence of EBV infection."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-06-C06","obj":"IM-15-F.6","title":"Complications of EBV: spleen, liver, blood, and cancer","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The acute complications follow from lymphocyte infiltration of organs. In the spleen, lymphocytes expand the red pulp and infiltrate the thin capsule and trabeculae, so an enlarged spleen is also a fragile one. Rupture is rare (well under 1% of cases), occurs mostly in the first 3 weeks, and can follow minor trauma or happen spontaneously, with left upper quadrant pain, pain referred to the left shoulder from blood irritating the diaphragm, and hypotension. Mild hepatitis with raised aminotransferases is present in most patients, from portal lymphocytic infiltration rather than direct killing of hepatocytes, and jaundice is uncommon."},{"type":"p","text":"Blood complications come from immune dysregulation. Polyclonal B cell activation can produce cold agglutinins, IgM with anti-i specificity that binds red cells in cool peripheral vessels and fixes complement, causing usually mild, transient autoimmune hemolytic anemia. Thrombocytopenia and neutropenia also occur. Massive tonsillar enlargement can obstruct the airway. Boys with X-linked lymphoproliferative disease (defective SAP protein) cannot control the T cell response and may die of uncontrolled lymphoproliferation and hemophagocytosis."},{"type":"h","text":"How EBV causes cancer"},{"type":"p","text":"After primary infection EBV stays latent for life in memory B cells. Latent proteins keep infected cells alive: EBNA-1 maintains the viral episome, EBNA-2 activates host genes including MYC, and LMP-1 acts like a permanently activated CD40 receptor, driving NF-kappaB and anti-apoptotic proteins such as BCL-2. In a healthy host, CD8 T cells kill cells that express these proteins. Cancer emerges when that surveillance fails, as in transplant recipients and people with AIDS, or when an additional genetic event, such as a MYC translocation, lets a cell grow while expressing few viral proteins that T cells can see."},{"type":"table","title":"EBV-associated malignancies","cols":["Tumor","Setting","Key features","Link to EBV"],"rows":[["Burkitt lymphoma","Endemic form in children in equatorial Africa (with malaria); sporadic form elsewhere","Jaw or facial mass (endemic) or abdominal mass (sporadic); t(8;14) placing MYC next to the immunoglobulin heavy chain gene; starry-sky pattern","Nearly all endemic cases; a minority of sporadic cases"],["Hodgkin lymphoma","Young adults and older adults","Reed-Sternberg cells (CD15 and CD30 positive) in a reactive background","About 40% overall, most often mixed cellularity type"],["Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","Southern China, North Africa, Arctic populations","Undifferentiated carcinoma with lymphoid stroma; neck node mass, nasal obstruction, epistaxis","Nearly all cases"],["Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder","Solid organ or stem cell transplant recipients","B cell proliferation ranging from polyclonal to monoclonal lymphoma","Most cases, especially early after transplant"],["Primary CNS lymphoma","AIDS with very low CD4 counts","Usually a single periventricular enhancing mass; EBV DNA in CSF","Nearly all cases in AIDS"]],"caption":"B cell tumors follow failed T cell control or MYC activation; nasopharyngeal carcinoma shows that EBV also transforms epithelium."},{"type":"p","text":"EBV also causes oral hairy leukoplakia in immunosuppressed patients, a nonmalignant white, corrugated plaque on the lateral tongue from viral replication in squamous epithelium. It cannot be scraped off, which separates it from oral candidiasis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-06-C07","obj":"IM-15-F.7","title":"Mumps: parotitis and glandular and neural spread","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mumps virus is a paramyxovirus: enveloped, with nonsegmented negative-sense RNA and two surface glycoproteins, hemagglutinin-neuraminidase (HN) and fusion (F) protein. It spreads by respiratory droplets and saliva, with an incubation of about 16 to 18 days. The virus replicates in upper airway epithelium and regional lymph nodes, then a viremia seeds glandular tissue (salivary glands, testes, ovaries, pancreas, breast) and the central nervous system. This shared viremia explains why parotitis, orchitis, pancreatitis, and meningitis are parts of one infection and why any of them can occur without parotid swelling."},{"type":"p","text":"After a short prodrome of low fever and malaise, one or both parotid glands swell and become tender, often one after the other, obscuring the angle of the mandible and pushing the earlobe up and out. The parotid is enclosed in a tight fascial capsule, so swelling is painful, and chewing or sour foods, which stimulate saliva secretion, worsen the pain. The opening of the parotid duct opposite the upper second molar may be red. Patients are contagious from a few days before to about 5 days after the swelling begins."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications of mumps","cols":["Complication","Who and when","Mechanism or clue"],"rows":[["Orchitis","Postpubertal males; about a week after parotitis or without it; usually one testis","Painful, swollen testis with fever; the inelastic tunica albuginea lets edema raise pressure, causing ischemic damage and later atrophy; sterility is uncommon"],["Oophoritis and mastitis","Postpubertal females","Lower abdominal pain or breast tenderness"],["Aseptic meningitis","Common, often mild","Headache, neck stiffness; CSF lymphocytic pleocytosis with negative Gram stain"],["Encephalitis","Rare","Altered consciousness, seizures"],["Pancreatitis","Uncommon","Epigastric pain and vomiting with raised lipase"],["Sensorineural hearing loss","Uncommon","Usually one ear; may be permanent"]],"caption":"Viremia carries the virus to every gland and to the meninges, so complications can appear without parotitis."},{"type":"h","text":"Amylase and lipase"},{"type":"p","text":"Serum amylase comes from two sources: salivary (S-type) amylase from the salivary glands and pancreatic (P-type) amylase from the pancreas. An inflamed parotid leaks S-type amylase into the blood, so total serum amylase rises with parotitis alone. Lipase is made almost entirely by the pancreas, so a raised lipase, or a rise in the pancreatic amylase fraction, is what indicates mumps pancreatitis. A high amylase in a child with parotid swelling is therefore expected and does not by itself prove pancreatic involvement."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-06-C08","obj":"IM-15-F.8","title":"Roseola infantum: fever first, rash after","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Roseola infantum (exanthem subitum, sixth disease) is the primary infection with human herpesvirus 6, mainly the HHV-6B variant, and less often HHV-7. Both are enveloped double-stranded DNA betaherpesviruses, the same subfamily as CMV. They infect CD4 T lymphocytes (HHV-7 uses CD4 itself as its receptor), establish lifelong latency in monocytes and macrophages, and are shed in the saliva of healthy older children and adults. Maternal IgG protects for the first months, so infection occurs mostly between about 6 months and 2 years, and nearly all children are infected by age 3."},{"type":"p","text":"The illness begins with an abrupt high fever, often 39 to 40 °C (102.2 to 104 °F), that lasts 3 to 5 days in a child who usually looks surprisingly well, sometimes with mild upper respiratory symptoms, cervical lymphadenopathy, or a bulging fontanelle. The fever then ends abruptly. Within hours to a day, small rose-pink, blanching macules and papules appear on the trunk and spread to the neck, face, and limbs. The rash does not itch and fades in 1 to 2 days. The rash is thought to reflect the immune response as viremia is cleared, which is why it follows the fever instead of accompanying it."},{"type":"p","text":"Febrile seizures are the most important complication. Primary HHV-6 infection is one of the most common identified causes of febrile seizures in children under 2. Seizures occur during the febrile phase, before any rash, because high fever lowers the seizure threshold of the immature brain, and HHV-6 itself is neurotropic and is often found in the CSF. These seizures are usually generalized and brief. Encephalitis is rare in children; in stem cell transplant recipients, reactivated HHV-6 can cause encephalitis and marrow suppression."},{"type":"table","title":"Roseola at a glance","cols":["Feature","Finding","Reason"],"rows":[["Age","About 6 months to 2 years","Maternal antibody has waned; virus is shed by nearly all adults"],["Fever","3 to 5 days, high, child appears well","Viremia in CD4 T cells"],["Rash","Appears as the fever breaks; trunk first, then neck, face, limbs","Immune clearance of viremia"],["Main risk","Febrile seizure during the fever","High fever in an immature brain; neurotropic virus"]],"caption":"Rash after defervescence separates roseola from measles and scarlet fever, in which the child is still febrile when the rash appears."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-06-C09","obj":"IM-15-F.9","title":"Rubella and congenital rubella syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Rubella virus is an enveloped, positive-sense, single-stranded RNA virus, classically grouped with the togaviruses. It spreads by respiratory droplets, has an incubation of about 14 to 21 days, replicates in the nasopharynx and regional lymph nodes, and then spreads by viremia. Patients shed virus from about a week before to a week after the rash, and up to half of infections cause no symptoms."},{"type":"p","text":"Tender postauricular, suboccipital, and posterior cervical lymphadenopathy often appears before the rash, reflecting viral replication in those nodes. A low fever and mild malaise accompany a pink, discrete maculopapular rash that starts on the face, spreads to the trunk and limbs within a day, and fades in about 3 days. Unlike the measles rash, it does not become confluent or leave brown staining, and the child looks well. Adults, especially women, often develop arthralgia or arthritis of the fingers, wrists, and knees, attributed to immune complexes, which can last for weeks."},{"type":"h","text":"Congenital rubella"},{"type":"p","text":"When a nonimmune woman acquires rubella in pregnancy, the virus crosses the placenta and causes a chronic infection of fetal tissues. It slows cell division, triggers apoptosis, and damages vascular endothelium during organogenesis, so the organs forming at the time of infection are affected and the infant is small for gestational age. The risk of defects is highest with infection in the first 12 weeks and becomes low after about 16 to 20 weeks. Affected infants shed virus in urine and nasopharyngeal secretions for months, and rubella-specific IgM in the newborn proves fetal infection, because maternal IgM does not cross the placenta."},{"type":"table","title":"Congenital rubella syndrome","cols":["Organ","Finding","Basis"],"rows":[["Ear","Sensorineural deafness, the most common single defect","Damage to the developing cochlea"],["Eye","Cataracts, microphthalmia, glaucoma, salt-and-pepper retinopathy","Persistent infection of the developing lens and retina"],["Heart","Patent ductus arteriosus, pulmonary artery stenosis","Injury to developing vascular tissue"],["Skin and blood","Blueberry muffin rash, thrombocytopenic purpura","Dermal extramedullary hematopoiesis; low platelets"],["Brain and growth","Microcephaly, intellectual disability, growth restriction, hepatosplenomegaly","Reduced cell division and chronic infection"]],"caption":"The classic triad is cataracts, sensorineural deafness, and patent ductus arteriosus after first-trimester infection."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-06-C10","obj":"IM-15-F.10","title":"Scarlet fever and the nonsuppurative sequelae of group A streptococcus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes (group A streptococcus) is a gram-positive coccus in chains that is beta-hemolytic, catalase negative, and bacitracin sensitive. Scarlet fever is streptococcal pharyngitis, or less often a skin or wound infection, caused by a strain that makes streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins (classically SpeA and SpeC, also called erythrogenic toxins), whose genes are carried by a bacteriophage. These toxins act as superantigens and cause diffuse capillary dilation in the skin. It is most common between about 5 and 15 years of age."},{"type":"p","text":"Fever and sore throat with exudative tonsillitis, tender anterior cervical nodes, and palatal petechiae come first, and the rash appears within 12 to 48 hours. It is diffuse, blanching erythema with fine papules that give a sandpaper feel, starting on the neck, axillae, and groin and generalizing within a day while sparing the palms and soles. The cheeks are flushed with circumoral pallor, and Pastia lines are linear petechial streaks in skin creases such as the antecubital fossa, where fragile capillaries leak. The tongue first has a white coat with red papillae, then the coat peels to leave a red strawberry tongue. About a week after the rash fades, the skin desquamates, most visibly on the fingertips, palms, and soles."},{"type":"h","text":"Rheumatic fever and glomerulonephritis"},{"type":"p","text":"These sequelae are not caused by bacteria in the heart or kidney but by the immune response to the preceding infection, so they appear after a latent period. Rheumatic fever follows only pharyngitis: antibodies and T cells against the streptococcal M protein cross-react with cardiac myosin, valve tissue, joints, and basal ganglia neurons. Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis follows either throat or skin infection with nephritogenic strains, whose antigens form immune complexes in glomeruli that activate complement and lower serum C3."},{"type":"table","title":"Rheumatic fever versus poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","cols":["Feature","Acute rheumatic fever","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis"],"rows":[["Preceding infection","Pharyngitis only","Pharyngitis or skin infection (impetigo)"],["Latent period","About 2 to 4 weeks","About 1 to 2 weeks after pharyngitis; 3 to 6 weeks after skin infection"],["Mechanism","Type II hypersensitivity; molecular mimicry of M protein","Type III hypersensitivity; immune complexes with complement activation"],["Main findings","Carditis (mitral valve most often), migratory polyarthritis, Sydenham chorea, erythema marginatum, subcutaneous nodules","Cola-colored urine, periorbital edema, hypertension, oliguria (nephritic syndrome)"],["Pathology and labs","Aschoff bodies in the heart; raised ASO titer","Subepithelial humps on electron microscopy; low C3; raised ASO or anti-DNase B"],["Long-term course","Repeated attacks scar valves (chronic rheumatic heart disease)","Most children recover fully"]],"caption":"Skin infection can lead to glomerulonephritis but not rheumatic fever."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 7-year-old had impetigo 4 weeks ago and now has cola-colored urine and puffy eyes. Could the same infection have caused rheumatic fever, and what will serum C3 show?","explain":"Rheumatic fever follows only pharyngitis, so it is not expected after impetigo. Immune complexes activate complement, so C3 is low.","check":"Compare with the rheumatic fever versus glomerulonephritis table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-06-C11","obj":"IM-15-F.11","title":"Toxoplasmosis in healthy, fetal, and immunosuppressed hosts","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Toxoplasma gondii is an obligate intracellular protozoan. Cats are the definitive host: the sexual cycle in the cat intestine releases oocysts in feces, which become infectious after 1 to 5 days in the environment. Humans are infected by swallowing these oocysts from litter, soil, or unwashed produce, by eating tissue cysts in undercooked meat such as pork or lamb, across the placenta, or through a transplanted organ."},{"type":"p","text":"Rapidly dividing tachyzoites invade almost any nucleated cell and spread through blood and lymph. Control depends on cell-mediated immunity: dendritic cell IL-12 drives NK cells and Th1 cells to produce IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages, and CD8 T cells kill infected cells. Under this pressure the parasite converts to slowly dividing bradyzoites inside tissue cysts in brain, retina, and skeletal and cardiac muscle, where they persist for life. Antibody limits spread but does not eliminate the cysts."},{"type":"p","text":"In a healthy host, about 80 to 90% of infections are silent. Symptomatic infection is a self-limited, mononucleosis-like illness with fever, fatigue, and painless cervical lymphadenopathy, often posterior cervical, with a negative heterophile test."},{"type":"p","text":"Congenital toxoplasmosis usually follows a primary infection during pregnancy; a woman infected before pregnancy has immunity that keeps cysts dormant and rarely transmits. Transmission becomes more likely as pregnancy advances and the placenta grows, but fetal damage is most severe with early infection. Tachyzoites cause necrotizing inflammation in the fetal brain and retina. Necrosis around the cerebral aqueduct obstructs CSF flow (hydrocephalus), necrotic foci throughout the brain calcify (diffuse intracranial calcifications), and retinal infection causes chorioretinitis, which may first appear years after birth."},{"type":"p","text":"In AIDS, when the CD4 count falls below about 100 cells/mm3, the IFN-gamma response fails and brain cysts reactivate. Toxoplasmic encephalitis causes headache, fever, confusion, seizures, and focal deficits. Imaging shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions with surrounding edema, typically in the basal ganglia and at the gray-white junction; the enhancing rim is inflammation and a broken blood-brain barrier around a necrotic center. Because this is reactivation, Toxoplasma IgG is almost always positive."},{"type":"flow","title":"Toxoplasma reactivation in AIDS","steps":["Oocysts or tissue cysts are ingested","Tachyzoites spread; IFN-gamma drives them into tissue cysts","Bradyzoite cysts persist in brain for life","CD4 count falls below about 100 cells/mm3","Cysts rupture and tachyzoites destroy brain tissue","Multiple ring-enhancing lesions with focal deficits"],"caption":"Latent infection becomes disease when T cell immunity can no longer hold cysts dormant."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: ring-enhancing lesions in AIDS","text":"A man with AIDS and a CD4 count of 40 cells/mm3 has headache and right arm weakness. MRI shows three ring-enhancing lesions in the basal ganglia and at the gray-white junction, and Toxoplasma IgG is positive. Step 1: CD4 below 100 places him at risk for both toxoplasmosis and primary CNS lymphoma. Step 2: multiple lesions in these locations favor Toxoplasma, while lymphoma is more often a single periventricular mass. Step 3: positive IgG fits reactivation of latent cysts; a negative IgG would make toxoplasmosis much less likely. Step 4: EBV DNA in the CSF would point instead to lymphoma. The best answer is toxoplasmic encephalitis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-06-C12","obj":"IM-15-F.12","title":"Telling the childhood exanthems apart","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Five questions separate these rashes: when the rash appears relative to the fever, where it starts and how it spreads, what it looks and feels like, how ill the child looks, and what the accompanying clue is. Timing reflects pathogenesis. The measles rash comes at the peak of the T cell attack on infected cells, so the child is still febrile and sick. Scarlet fever reflects toxin acting during an ongoing bacterial infection, so fever and rash also coincide. The rashes of erythema infectiosum and roseola appear as antibody clears the viremia, so the fever is over and the child is well."},{"type":"table","title":"Childhood exanthems by timing and pattern","cols":["Disease","Cause","Rash relative to fever","Where it starts and how it spreads","Distinguishing clue"],"rows":[["Measles","Measles virus (paramyxovirus)","After 2 to 4 days of prodrome, while fever peaks; child ill","Hairline and behind ears, downward over 3 days; becomes confluent","Cough, coryza, conjunctivitis; Koplik spots"],["Rubella","Rubella virus","With mild or no fever; child well","Face to trunk within a day; discrete pink spots, fades in about 3 days","Postauricular and suboccipital nodes; adult arthralgia"],["Erythema infectiosum","Parvovirus B19","After a mild prodrome has ended; child well and no longer contagious","Slapped cheeks, then lacy reticular rash on limbs","Rash recurs with heat or sun; adult arthropathy"],["Roseola","HHV-6 (HHV-7)","After 3 to 5 days of high fever, as the fever breaks","Trunk first, then neck, face, and limbs","Age under 2; febrile seizures"],["Scarlet fever","Group A streptococcus toxin","Within 12 to 48 hours of fever and sore throat","Neck, axillae, and groin, then generalized; spares palms and soles","Sandpaper texture, circumoral pallor, strawberry tongue, Pastia lines, later desquamation"]],"caption":"Fever still present when the rash appears points to measles or scarlet fever; rash after the fever points to roseola or erythema infectiosum."},{"type":"p","text":"Three pairs cause most errors. Measles and rubella both spread from head to trunk, but measles follows a severe prodrome with the three Cs and a confluent rash in a sick child, while rubella is mild with prominent posterior nodes. Roseola and measles both begin with high fever, but in roseola the rash follows defervescence. Scarlet fever differs from the viral rashes in its rough texture, the exudative pharyngitis, and the tongue and skin-fold changes. A diffuse rash that appears days after amoxicillin in a teenager with pharyngitis points instead to EBV."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: fever first, then rash","text":"A 14-month-old has had a temperature of 40 °C (104 °F) for 4 days but has stayed alert and playful. On day 5 the fever is gone, and that evening pink blanching spots appear on his trunk and spread to his neck. Step 1: the rash appeared after the fever ended, which rules against measles and scarlet fever, in which the child is febrile when the rash appears. Step 2: there was no cough, coryza, or conjunctivitis, and no sore throat. Step 3: his age is typical for primary HHV-6 infection. The diagnosis is roseola infantum, and the complication to anticipate during the febrile phase was a febrile seizure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-15-06-C13","obj":"IM-15-F.13","title":"Cytomegalovirus: mononucleosis, congenital infection, and disease in immunosuppression","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV, human herpesvirus 5) is an enveloped double-stranded DNA betaherpesvirus. It spreads through saliva, urine, genital secretions, breast milk, leukocyte-containing blood, transplanted organs, and across the placenta. It infects epithelial and endothelial cells, fibroblasts, smooth muscle, and monocytes, which is why it can damage almost any organ. It stays latent in monocytes and myeloid progenitor cells and reactivates when T cell immunity falls. Infected cells enlarge (cytomegaly) and contain a large basophilic intranuclear inclusion surrounded by a clear halo, the owl's eye appearance, often with smaller cytoplasmic inclusions."},{"type":"p","text":"In healthy young adults, primary CMV infection can cause a mononucleosis syndrome with prolonged fever, fatigue, atypical lymphocytes, and mildly raised aminotransferases, but with less pharyngitis and lymphadenopathy than EBV. The heterophile test is negative, because heterophile antibodies arise from polyclonal activation of EBV-infected B cells, which CMV does not cause. CMV is the most common cause of heterophile-negative mononucleosis. A similar syndrome can follow transfusion of blood from a seropositive donor, because the virus travels in donor leukocytes."},{"type":"p","text":"CMV is the most common congenital infection. Transmission and severe disease are most likely after a primary maternal infection, although reactivation or reinfection can also infect the fetus. About 90% of infected newborns look normal at birth, but some of them develop hearing loss later. The virus targets the rapidly dividing cells lining the fetal ventricles, so necrosis there leaves periventricular calcifications, along with microcephaly, chorioretinitis, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, petechiae or blueberry muffin spots, and growth restriction. Congenital CMV is the leading nongenetic cause of sensorineural hearing loss in children. In transplant recipients and people with AIDS, disease follows reactivation or new infection once T cells can no longer hold the virus latent, and in transplant recipients it is most common in the first months after transplant, when immunosuppression is most intense."},{"type":"table","title":"CMV in immunosuppressed hosts","cols":["Setting","Disease","Clues"],"rows":[["AIDS, CD4 below about 50 cells/mm3","Retinitis","Painless, progressive vision loss and floaters; hemorrhagic necrosis along retinal vessels"],["AIDS or transplant","Colitis and esophagitis","Diarrhea, bleeding, odynophagia; shallow ulcers; owl's eye inclusions in endothelial and stromal cells on biopsy"],["Stem cell or lung transplant","Pneumonitis","Fever, hypoxemia, bilateral interstitial infiltrates"],["Solid organ transplant","Hepatitis, graft dysfunction, fever with low white count","Highest risk when a seronegative recipient receives an organ from a seropositive donor"]],"caption":"Disease in immunosuppression comes from primary infection or reactivation that T cells can no longer control."},{"type":"table","title":"Congenital infections in this chapter","cols":["Infection","Brain","Eye and ear","Other"],"rows":[["CMV","Microcephaly; periventricular calcifications","Sensorineural hearing loss; chorioretinitis","Hepatosplenomegaly, petechiae"],["Toxoplasma","Hydrocephalus; diffuse calcifications","Chorioretinitis","Often normal at birth"],["Rubella","Microcephaly","Cataracts; sensorineural deafness","Patent ductus arteriosus; blueberry muffin rash"],["Parvovirus B19","Not typical","Not typical","Severe anemia, hydrops fetalis"]],"caption":"Periventricular calcification with hearing loss points to CMV; diffuse calcification with hydrocephalus points to Toxoplasma."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each infection in this chapter is defined by the cell it infects and by the arm of immunity that controls it. Parvovirus B19 targets erythroid progenitors through P antigen, so its effects are read through reticulocyte counts and red cell lifespan. Hib is controlled by anticapsular antibody and the spleen, so it strikes infants who cannot yet respond to polysaccharide, and asplenic patients. EBV, CMV, HHV-6, and Toxoplasma are held latent by T cells, so the same organisms that cause a mild illness in a healthy teenager cause lymphoma, retinitis, encephalitis, or ring-enhancing brain lesions when CD4 counts fall or transplant drugs suppress T cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Pregnancy and immune-mediated injury tie the chapter to other disciplines. Rubella, CMV, Toxoplasma, and parvovirus B19 cross the placenta, and the damage depends on timing and target cell: organogenesis for rubella, the periventricular germinal tissue for CMV, necrotizing inflammation for Toxoplasma, and fetal erythropoiesis for parvovirus. IgM in the newborn proves fetal infection because maternal IgM does not cross the placenta. Many findings here are produced by the host response rather than the microbe itself: the rashes of measles and fifth disease, the atypical lymphocytes of mononucleosis, and the rheumatic fever and glomerulonephritis that follow streptococcal infection. These ideas are mechanisms to reason from, and real presentations overlap, so the age, timeline, and host usually decide the answer."}],"summary":["Parvovirus B19 kills erythroid progenitors through P antigen; the one-week production pause causes aplastic crisis with reticulocytes near zero in hemolytic anemia and hydrops fetalis in the fetus, while the slapped cheek rash appears after contagiousness ends.","The PRP capsule lets Hib invade blood; children under about 2 years cannot make antibody to polysaccharide, so unvaccinated toddlers get epiglottitis and meningitis, while unencapsulated nontypeable strains cause otitis media and sinusitis.","Influenza causes abrupt systemic symptoms from cytokines; relapse after improvement means secondary S pneumoniae or S aureus pneumonia, and aspirin in a child with influenza or varicella can cause Reye syndrome (microvesicular steatosis, hyperammonemia, cerebral edema).","Measles brings the three Cs and Koplik spots before a head-to-toe confluent rash; it destroys immune memory, kills mainly through pneumonia, causes SSPE years later, and is worse with vitamin A deficiency.","EBV enters B cells through CD21; atypical lymphocytes are CD8 T cells, heterophile antibodies are polyclonal IgM that may be negative early, and amoxicillin triggers a rash.","EBV complications include splenic rupture, hepatitis, cold agglutinin hemolysis, and B cell or epithelial cancers when T cell surveillance fails or MYC is translocated.","Mumps viremia seeds glands and meninges, causing parotitis, orchitis in postpubertal males, aseptic meningitis, and pancreatitis; amylase rises with parotitis alone, while lipase marks pancreatitis.","Roseola (HHV-6) gives several days of high fever, then a truncal rash as the fever breaks, with febrile seizures during the fever.","Rubella is mild with posterior nodes, but first-trimester infection causes cataracts, deafness, PDA, and blueberry muffin rash; scarlet fever is a toxin rash with pharyngitis that can be followed by rheumatic fever (throat only) or glomerulonephritis (throat or skin).","Toxoplasma and CMV are held latent by T cells: congenital Toxoplasma gives hydrocephalus and diffuse calcifications, congenital CMV gives periventricular calcifications and hearing loss, and in AIDS they cause ring-enhancing brain lesions (CD4 below 100) and retinitis (CD4 below 50)."],"sources":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Herpesviruses; Paramyxoviruses and Rubella Virus; Orthomyxoviruses (Influenza Viruses); Parvoviruses"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Streptococci; Haemophilus; Medical Parasitology (Toxoplasma gondii)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Heart (rheumatic fever) and The Kidney (poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis)"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to Microbes"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases"}]},"IM-2":{"unit":"IM-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-02-01","title":"Histology of the Lymphoid Organs","subject":"Immune System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the lymph node, spleen, thymus, and mucosa associated lymphoid tissue are built, and why each layout suits its job. Secondary lymphoid organs place antigen, antigen-presenting cells, T cells, and B cells in separate zones that are wired together, so rare lymphocytes can find their antigen and their helpers. The thymus is built for selection rather than filtering. Board questions show a section or describe a patient and ask which zone holds which cell, which zone is empty in an immunodeficiency, which zone enlarges in a reactive node, how blood moves through the spleen, or how mucosal IgA is made.","prerequisites":["Lymphocyte lineages and markers: B cells (CD19, CD20), helper T cells (CD4), cytotoxic T cells (CD8)","MHC class I and class II and how dendritic cells present antigen","Antibody isotypes, class switching, and plasma cell differentiation","Basic connective tissue: reticular fibers (type III collagen) and endothelium","Pharyngeal pouch derivatives and the anatomy of the ileum and pharynx"],"connections":[{"to":"Cells and development of the immune system (IM-1)","how":"The lineages made in bone marrow and thymus are the cells that fill each zone described here."},{"to":"Primary immunodeficiencies","how":"Which zone is empty on a node or spleen biopsy tells you whether the T cell or B cell lineage has failed."},{"to":"Hematology","how":"The red pulp removes old, rigid, and inclusion-bearing red cells, which explains extravascular hemolysis, splenomegaly, and postsplenectomy blood smear changes."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal system","how":"Peyer patches and M cells are the entry point for Salmonella, Shigella, and Yersinia and the source of intestinal secretory IgA."},{"to":"Pathology of lymphoid neoplasms","how":"Reactive follicles must be told apart from follicular lymphoma, and chronic mucosal inflammation can give rise to MALT lymphoma."}],"terms":[{"term":"Secondary lymphoid organ","def":"An organ (lymph node, spleen, MALT) where mature naive lymphocytes meet antigen and start adaptive responses, as opposed to the primary organs (bone marrow, thymus) where lymphocytes develop."},{"term":"Primary follicle","def":"A round cluster of resting naive B cells on a mesh of follicular dendritic cells, without a germinal center."},{"term":"Germinal center","def":"The pale center of a secondary follicle where antigen-activated B cells proliferate, undergo somatic hypermutation and class switching, and are selected for high affinity."},{"term":"Follicular dendritic cell","def":"A stromal (non-bone marrow) cell of follicles that holds intact antigen as immune complexes on complement and Fc receptors for B cells to sample."},{"term":"High endothelial venule (HEV)","def":"A postcapillary venule with plump cuboidal endothelium, found in the T cell zones of nodes and MALT, through which naive lymphocytes leave the blood."},{"term":"Periarteriolar lymphoid sheath (PALS)","def":"The cuff of T cells that surrounds the central arteriole in splenic white pulp."},{"term":"Marginal zone","def":"The region surrounding splenic white pulp where incoming blood meets macrophages and marginal zone B cells that respond rapidly to blood-borne antigens."},{"term":"Cords of Billroth","def":"The splenic cords of the red pulp: a spongy mesh of reticular cells and macrophages lying between venous sinusoids."},{"term":"Epithelial reticular cell","def":"A thymic epithelial cell that forms the meshwork supporting thymocytes, contributes to the blood thymus barrier, and presents self MHC during selection."},{"term":"Hassall corpuscle","def":"A concentric whorl of flattened, partly keratinized epithelial reticular cells found only in the thymic medulla."},{"term":"M cell","def":"A specialized cell of the follicle-associated epithelium over MALT that transports luminal antigen by transcytosis to immune cells beneath it."},{"term":"Secretory IgA","def":"Dimeric IgA joined by a J chain and carrying secretory component, the main antibody released onto mucosal surfaces."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-02-01-C01","obj":"IM-2.1","title":"Lymph node microanatomy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A lymph node is a bean-shaped filter placed along lymphatic vessels. Its job is to bring antigen arriving in lymph together with the few naive lymphocytes that can recognize it. A dense connective tissue capsule surrounds the node and sends trabeculae inward, and a meshwork of reticular fibers (type III collagen) made by fibroblastic reticular cells supports the rest. Several afferent lymphatics, which carry valves, pierce the convex surface; one or two efferent lymphatics leave at the hilum, where the artery enters and the vein exits."},{"type":"flow","title":"Path of lymph through a node","steps":["Afferent lymphatics pierce the capsule","Subcapsular (marginal) sinus under the capsule","Trabecular (cortical) sinuses along trabeculae","Medullary sinuses between medullary cords","Efferent lymphatic leaves at the hilum"],"caption":"Sinuses are lined by endothelium and crossed by reticular fibers, which slow lymph and expose it to macrophages and dendritic cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Antigen is captured along this path. Macrophages lining the subcapsular sinus trap particles and pass intact antigen to B cells in the follicles below, while small soluble antigens travel through narrow reticular fiber conduits into the cortex. Dendritic cells that picked up antigen in the tissue arrive in the same lymph already carrying processed peptide on MHC."},{"type":"h","text":"Cortex, paracortex, and medulla"},{"type":"p","text":"The outer cortex holds the B cell follicles. A primary follicle is a dense, uniform cluster of resting naive B cells on a network of follicular dendritic cells. After antigen stimulation it becomes a secondary follicle, with a pale germinal center surrounded by a darker mantle zone of naive IgM and IgD positive B cells. The germinal center has a dark zone, where proliferating centroblasts undergo somatic hypermutation, and a light zone, where centrocytes compete for antigen held on follicular dendritic cells and for help from follicular helper T cells. Cells that fail selection die by apoptosis and are eaten by tingible body macrophages."},{"type":"p","text":"The paracortex (deep cortex) is the T cell zone, rich in CD4 and CD8 T cells and interdigitating dendritic cells. It contains the high endothelial venules, the gateway by which most naive lymphocytes enter the node. The medulla consists of medullary cords, which hold plasma cells, macrophages, and lymphocytes, alternating with medullary sinuses. Plasma cells formed in the cortex settle in the cords and release antibody into the efferent lymph."},{"type":"flow","title":"Naive lymphocyte entry through a high endothelial venule","steps":["L-selectin on lymphocyte binds PNAd on HEV","Lymphocyte rolls along the HEV","CCL19 and CCL21 activate CCR7 signaling","LFA-1 integrin binds ICAM-1 firmly","Lymphocyte crosses the HEV wall","T cells stay in paracortex; CXCL13 draws B cells to follicles"],"caption":"PNAd is peripheral node addressin. Lymphocytes that do not find antigen leave in efferent lymph, guided by sphingosine 1-phosphate."},{"type":"table","title":"Lymph node zones","cols":["Zone","Main cells","Function"],"rows":[["Subcapsular sinus","Sinus macrophages, lymph","First capture of lymph-borne antigen"],["Cortical follicles","B cells, follicular dendritic cells, follicular helper T cells","B cell activation; germinal center selection"],["Paracortex","T cells, interdigitating dendritic cells, HEVs","Lymphocyte entry; antigen presentation to T cells"],["Medullary cords","Plasma cells, macrophages, lymphocytes","Antibody secretion into efferent lymph"],["Medullary sinuses","Macrophages, lymph","Final filtering before the efferent vessel"]],"caption":"B cells occupy the outer cortex, T cells the deep cortex, and plasma cells the medulla."},{"type":"p","text":"The layout explains the timing of a response. Helper T cells activated in the paracortex move to the edge of the follicle, where B cells that have taken up the same antigen present it to them. This T cell and B cell meeting at the follicle border launches the germinal center reaction that produces high-affinity, class-switched antibody."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-02-01-C02","obj":"IM-2.2","title":"Depleted and expanded zones in disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each zone of the node is filled by the lymphocytes that home to it, so a node biopsy works as a map of which lineage is present. If a lineage never develops, its zone stays empty while the other zones still form. If a lineage is stimulated strongly, its zone enlarges."},{"type":"h","text":"Depleted zones"},{"type":"p","text":"DiGeorge syndrome is most often a 22q11.2 deletion that disrupts development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches. The thymus (third pouch) is small or absent, so few mature T cells reach the periphery, and the paracortex and the splenic PALS are thin or empty. B cell follicles still form, but germinal centers may be small because B cells need T cell help to build them."},{"type":"p","text":"X-linked agammaglobulinemia is caused by a defect in Bruton tyrosine kinase (BTK), a kinase needed for pre-B cell receptor signaling. B cell development stops at the pre-B stage in the marrow, so lymph nodes and spleen lack follicles, germinal centers, and plasma cells, and the tonsils are tiny or absent. The paracortex is normal because T cells are unaffected. Symptoms usually begin after about 6 months of age, once maternal IgG has been catabolized."},{"type":"h","text":"Expanded zones"},{"type":"p","text":"Reactive follicular hyperplasia follows strong B cell stimulation. Follicles are numerous and large, with polarized germinal centers of varied size, many tingible body macrophages, and an intact mantle. Typical causes are rheumatoid arthritis, toxoplasmosis, syphilis, and early HIV infection. Reactive paracortical hyperplasia follows T cell stimulation, which expands the paracortex with activated T cells and large immunoblasts that can push follicles aside. Typical causes are viral infections such as infectious mononucleosis (EBV), recent vaccination, and drugs such as phenytoin."},{"type":"table","title":"Zone changes in lymph nodes","cols":["Condition","Mechanism","Paracortex","Follicles and germinal centers"],"rows":[["DiGeorge syndrome","Thymic hypoplasia; few T cells","Depleted","Present; germinal centers may be small"],["X-linked agammaglobulinemia","BTK defect; arrest at pre-B stage","Normal","Absent; no plasma cells"],["Severe combined immunodeficiency","Neither functional T nor B cells","Depleted","Absent"],["Reactive follicular hyperplasia","B cell stimulation","Normal or compressed","Enlarged, many germinal centers"],["Reactive paracortical hyperplasia","T cell stimulation","Expanded, with immunoblasts","Normal or pushed aside"]],"caption":"Absence of a lineage empties its zone; stimulation of a lineage expands its zone."},{"type":"p","text":"Reactive follicles must be distinguished from follicular lymphoma, a neoplasm most often driven by t(14;18), which places BCL2 next to the immunoglobulin heavy chain locus. Neoplastic follicles are crowded back to back, uniform in size, lack polarity and tingible body macrophages (because BCL2 blocks apoptosis), and their germinal center cells stain for BCL2, whereas reactive germinal center cells do not."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading an empty zone","text":"A 9-month-old boy has recurrent otitis media and pneumonia with Streptococcus pneumoniae. Serum IgG, IgA, and IgM are all very low, and the tonsils are barely visible. Which lymph node zone is depleted? Recurrent infection with encapsulated bacteria points to failure of antibody, not of T cells. Onset after 6 months fits loss of maternal IgG. Tiny tonsils mean lymphoid follicles are missing. Therefore the B cell zones (cortical follicles and germinal centers) and the medullary plasma cells are absent, while the paracortex is normal. This is the pattern of X-linked agammaglobulinemia. With hypocalcemia, a conotruncal heart defect, and viral or fungal infections instead, the paracortex would be empty (DiGeorge syndrome)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A node from a child with complete DiGeorge syndrome has primary follicles but almost no germinal centers. Why are the follicles present but not active?","explain":"B cells develop normally in the marrow and home to follicles using CXCL13, so primary follicles form. Germinal centers require CD40 ligand and cytokines from follicular helper T cells, which are missing, so B cells cannot be driven into the germinal center reaction.","check":"The DiGeorge paragraph and the T cell and B cell meeting at the follicle border in C01."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-02-01-C03","obj":"IM-2.3","title":"Splenic histology and blood flow","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The spleen is the largest secondary lymphoid organ and the only one placed in the bloodstream rather than in lymph. It has no afferent lymphatics, so its lymphoid tissue is arranged around arterioles instead of sinuses. A dense connective tissue capsule sends trabeculae inward carrying arteries and veins. The cut surface shows small gray-white nodules (white pulp) scattered in a dark red background (red pulp)."},{"type":"h","text":"White pulp and marginal zone"},{"type":"p","text":"White pulp forms around central arterioles. T cells form the periarteriolar lymphoid sheath (PALS) that cuffs each arteriole, and B cell follicles, primary or with germinal centers, sit beside the sheath and push the arteriole to an eccentric position. Surrounding the white pulp is the marginal zone, where many arteriolar branches open. Blood here percolates past marginal zone macrophages and marginal zone B cells. These B cells respond quickly, often without T cell help, to polysaccharide capsules, producing IgM that opsonizes and activates complement."},{"type":"h","text":"Red pulp"},{"type":"p","text":"Red pulp consists of splenic cords (cords of Billroth) and venous sinusoids. The cords are a spongy reticular mesh full of macrophages and blood cells. The sinusoids are lined by long, rod-shaped endothelial cells held by a basal lamina arranged in rings, like barrel staves and hoops, leaving narrow slits between the cells. To enter a sinusoid from a cord, a red cell about 7 to 8 micrometers across must fold and squeeze through a slit only a few micrometers wide, or less."},{"type":"flow","title":"Blood flow through the spleen","steps":["Splenic artery enters at the hilum","Trabecular arteries in connective tissue trabeculae","Central arteriole surrounded by PALS","Branches empty into the marginal zone","Penicillar arterioles, then sheathed capillaries","Open: into cords; closed: directly into sinusoids","Red cells squeeze through slits into sinusoids","Pulp veins, trabecular veins, splenic vein"],"caption":"In humans most blood takes the open route through the cords, which is the route that tests red cell deformability. The splenic vein joins the superior mesenteric vein to form the portal vein."},{"type":"p","text":"This design explains splenic function. Normal red cells, which live about 120 days, deform easily and pass. Aged cells, spherocytes, sickled cells, and antibody-coated cells are held in the cords and eaten by macrophages (culling), which is extravascular hemolysis. Rigid inclusions such as Howell-Jolly bodies (nuclear remnants) and Heinz bodies (denatured hemoglobin) are pinched out as the cell squeezes through (pitting). The spleen also holds about one third of circulating platelets."},{"type":"table","title":"Splenic compartments","cols":["Compartment","Contents","Main role"],"rows":[["PALS","T cells around central arteriole","T cell activation by blood-borne antigen"],["Follicles","B cells; germinal centers when active","Antibody responses with T cell help"],["Marginal zone","Macrophages, marginal zone B cells","Capture of blood-borne antigen; rapid IgM to encapsulated bacteria"],["Cords of Billroth","Reticular mesh, macrophages, blood cells","Culling of old or rigid red cells; phagocytosis"],["Venous sinusoids","Rod-shaped endothelium with slits","Deformability test; return of blood to veins"]],"caption":"White pulp is immune; red pulp is a filter for blood cells."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: after splenectomy","text":"A man has his spleen removed after trauma. Predict his smear and his infection risk. Without cords and slits, inclusions are no longer pitted, so Howell-Jolly bodies appear in red cells. Target cells and a rise in platelet count follow because cells are no longer remodeled or pooled in the spleen. Without the marginal zone, blood-borne encapsulated bacteria are neither trapped by macrophages nor met by rapid IgM, and opsonization is impaired. He is therefore at lifelong risk of overwhelming sepsis from Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, and Neisseria meningitidis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-02-01-C04","obj":"IM-2.4","title":"Thymic cortex and medulla","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thymus is a primary lymphoid organ in the anterior superior mediastinum where T cells mature. Its framework is epithelial, derived from third pharyngeal pouch endoderm, and is colonized by T cell precursors from the bone marrow. Septa from a thin capsule divide each lobe into incomplete lobules. Each lobule has a dark outer cortex and a pale inner medulla, and the medulla is continuous from lobule to lobule."},{"type":"p","text":"The supporting cells are epithelial reticular cells (thymic epithelial cells), not fibroblasts and reticular fibers as in a node. Joined by desmosomes into a meshwork, they express MHC class I and II and secrete IL-7 and thymic hormones."},{"type":"h","text":"Cortex"},{"type":"p","text":"The cortex stains dark because it is packed with small immature thymocytes, mostly CD4 and CD8 double positive cells. Cortical epithelial cells carry self MHC and test each new T cell receptor. Thymocytes that bind self MHC weakly receive a survival signal (positive selection), and the class they bind decides their fate: class II produces CD4 cells and class I produces CD8 cells. Most thymocytes fail and die by apoptosis, so macrophages with ingested debris are scattered through the cortex."},{"type":"p","text":"The blood thymus barrier protects cortical thymocytes from circulating antigen, so that selection is shaped by self antigens presented by thymic cells. It is present in the cortex only and has three layers: continuous capillary endothelium with tight junctions and a thick basal lamina, a perivascular space containing macrophages that remove escaping antigen, and a sheath of epithelial reticular cells with their own basal lamina."},{"type":"h","text":"Medulla"},{"type":"p","text":"The medulla stains paler because its T cells are fewer, larger, and mostly mature single positive CD4 or CD8 cells. Here negative selection removes cells that bind self peptide on MHC strongly. Medullary epithelial cells use the transcription factor AIRE to express proteins normally made only in other organs, such as insulin, and dendritic cells present them as well; some self-reactive CD4 cells become regulatory T cells instead of dying. Hassall corpuscles, whorls of flattened, partly keratinized epithelial cells, are the medulla's signature and may support regulatory T cell development."},{"type":"flow","title":"Thymocyte journey through the lobule","steps":["Marrow precursors enter near the corticomedullary junction","Double negative cells move to the outer cortex","Double positive cells undergo positive selection in cortex","Survivors commit to CD4 or CD8","Negative selection in medulla using AIRE and dendritic cells","Mature naive T cells exit to the circulation"],"caption":"Position in the lobule tracks maturity: immature in the outer cortex, mature in the medulla."},{"type":"table","title":"Telling cortex from medulla, and thymus from lymph node","cols":["Feature","Thymic cortex","Thymic medulla","Lymph node"],"rows":[["Staining","Dark, crowded","Pale, less crowded","Follicles at periphery; pale germinal centers"],["Lymphocytes","Immature double positive thymocytes","Mature single positive T cells","Mature B and T cells"],["Stroma","Epithelial reticular cells","Epithelial reticular cells, Hassall corpuscles","Fibroblastic reticular cells, reticular fibers"],["Blood barrier","Blood thymus barrier present","Absent","Absent"],["Selection","Positive","Negative","No T cell selection; B cell selection in germinal centers"],["Follicles and sinuses","None","None","Follicles and lymph sinuses present"]],"caption":"A lobulated organ with dark cortex, pale medulla, Hassall corpuscles, and no follicles or sinuses is thymus."},{"type":"p","text":"The thymus involutes after puberty and is progressively replaced by adipose tissue, though adults still export some T cells. Lymphoid follicles with germinal centers in the thymic medulla are abnormal; this thymic follicular hyperplasia is classically associated with myasthenia gravis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If AIRE were absent but cortical selection were normal, would T cell numbers fall, and what disease pattern would follow?","explain":"T cell numbers would be roughly normal because positive selection works. Cells reactive to tissue-restricted self proteins would escape negative selection, causing autoimmunity against several endocrine organs, the pattern of autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1.","check":"The cortex and medulla paragraphs on positive and negative selection."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-02-01-C05","obj":"IM-2.5","title":"Mucosa associated lymphoid tissue and secretory IgA","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mucosa associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) is lymphoid tissue in the lamina propria and submucosa of the gastrointestinal, respiratory, and urogenital tracts. It ranges from scattered lymphocytes and plasma cells, through solitary follicles, to organized aggregates such as tonsils, Peyer patches, and the appendix. Gut-associated tissue is called GALT and bronchus-associated tissue BALT. MALT has no capsule and no afferent lymphatics. Instead of waiting for antigen to arrive in lymph, it samples antigen directly from the lumen through the overlying epithelium."},{"type":"table","title":"Tonsils and Peyer patches","cols":["Structure","Surface epithelium","Distinguishing features"],"rows":[["Palatine tonsil","Stratified squamous nonkeratinized","Deep branching crypts; partial capsule on the deep surface"],["Pharyngeal tonsil (adenoid)","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","Folds rather than deep crypts; on posterior nasopharynx wall"],["Lingual tonsil","Stratified squamous nonkeratinized","Posterior tongue; usually a single shallow crypt per unit"],["Peyer patch","Simple columnar with M cells over domes","Ileum, antimesenteric wall; follicles extend into submucosa"]],"caption":"Each tonsil is identified by its covering epithelium and crypt pattern; Peyer patches are identified by ileal location and dome epithelium."},{"type":"h","text":"Peyer patches and M cells"},{"type":"p","text":"Peyer patches are aggregates of lymphoid follicles with germinal centers in the ileal lamina propria and submucosa. Each follicle bulges toward the lumen as a dome covered by follicle-associated epithelium, which has few goblet cells (and so less mucus) and contains M cells (microfold cells). An M cell has short, irregular microfolds instead of a brush border, a thin glycocalyx, and a basolateral pocket holding lymphocytes and dendritic cells. It takes up luminal antigens and microbes by endocytosis and releases them intact into the pocket, a process called transcytosis. Dendritic cells in the dome then carry antigen to T cells in the interfollicular areas, which contain HEVs, and to B cells in the follicles. Some pathogens exploit this route, including Salmonella, Shigella, Yersinia, and poliovirus."},{"type":"h","text":"From M cell to secretory IgA"},{"type":"p","text":"B cells activated in Peyer patches switch preferentially to IgA. TGF-beta from local cells is the main switch signal, supported by helper T cells and by T cell independent signals. Gut dendritic cells make retinoic acid, which gives the activated cells the gut-homing integrin alpha4beta7 and the chemokine receptor CCR9. The cells leave through lymph to the mesenteric nodes, enter the blood, and return by binding MAdCAM-1 on mucosal venules, settling as IgA plasma cells in the lamina propria. Some primed cells reach other mucosae and lactating breast, so mucosal immunity is partly shared."},{"type":"flow","title":"Generation of secretory IgA","steps":["M cell transcytoses luminal antigen","Dome dendritic cells present to T and B cells","TGF-beta drives class switch to IgA","Retinoic acid induces alpha4beta7 and CCR9","Cells return to lamina propria via MAdCAM-1","Plasma cell secretes dimeric IgA with J chain","Polymeric Ig receptor carries IgA across epithelium","Cleavage leaves secretory component on luminal IgA"],"caption":"Secretory component shields IgA from proteases, so it remains intact in the gut lumen."},{"type":"p","text":"The plasma cell makes IgA as a dimer joined by a J chain. The polymeric Ig receptor on the basolateral surface of the epithelial cell binds the J chain-containing dimer, carries it across the cell in vesicles, and is cleaved at the apical surface. The fragment that stays bound is secretory component. Secretory IgA blocks microbial attachment and neutralizes toxins without activating the classical complement pathway, so it protects without inflaming the mucosa. More IgA is produced each day than any other isotype, mostly at mucosal surfaces. Chronic antigen stimulation can induce MALT in sites that normally lack it, such as the stomach in Helicobacter pylori gastritis, which is the setting for gastric MALT lymphoma."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The four organs follow one principle: structure places antigen and the right lymphocytes in the same space. Nodes filter lymph through sinuses, the spleen filters blood through the marginal zone and red pulp, MALT samples the lumen through M cells, and the thymus excludes blood antigen behind a barrier so selection is made on self. Embryology explains DiGeorge syndrome (third and fourth pouches), and molecular immunology explains where cells settle: CCR7 ligands hold T cells in the paracortex, CXCL13 holds B cells in follicles, and alpha4beta7 with MAdCAM-1 returns IgA plasma cells to the gut."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology reads these zones backward. An empty paracortex or PALS means T cell failure, absent follicles and tonsils mean B cell failure, and enlarged zones show which lineage is responding. Histologic patterns are a guide rather than proof: lineage defects are confirmed by flow cytometry, immunoglobulin levels, and genetic testing, and reactive hyperplasia is separated from lymphoma with immunostains such as BCL2."}],"summary":["Lymph flows from afferent lymphatics through subcapsular, trabecular, and medullary sinuses to the efferent vessel at the hilum.","In a lymph node, B cells occupy outer cortical follicles, T cells the paracortex with its HEVs, and plasma cells the medullary cords.","Naive lymphocytes enter through HEVs using L-selectin, CCR7, and LFA-1; CXCL13 then draws B cells into follicles.","DiGeorge syndrome empties the paracortex and PALS; X-linked agammaglobulinemia removes follicles, germinal centers, plasma cells, and tonsils.","Follicular hyperplasia reflects B cell stimulation; paracortical hyperplasia reflects T cell stimulation such as EBV, vaccination, or phenytoin.","Splenic white pulp has T cells in the PALS, B cells in follicles, and a marginal zone that defends against encapsulated bacteria.","Splenic blood passes central arterioles, the marginal zone, and penicillar arterioles into cords, then squeezes through sinusoid slits into veins.","The red pulp culls rigid red cells and pits inclusions, so asplenia shows Howell-Jolly bodies and risk of sepsis from encapsulated organisms.","Thymic cortex is dark with immature thymocytes, positive selection, and the blood thymus barrier; medulla is pale with mature T cells, AIRE, and Hassall corpuscles.","M cells in Peyer patch domes transcytose antigen; TGF-beta drives IgA switching, and the polymeric Ig receptor delivers dimeric IgA with secretory component."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Immune System and Lymphoid Organs"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Cells and Tissues of the Immune System; Specialized Immunity at Epithelial Barriers and in Immune Privileged Tissues"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System (immunodeficiency); Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Red Blood Cells, Anemia, and Polycythemia (destruction of red cells in the spleen)"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck, pharyngeal pouch derivatives"}]},"IM-3":{"unit":"IM-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-03-01","title":"Histocompatibility Antigens and Disease","subject":"Immune System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains the major histocompatibility complex (MHC), called human leukocyte antigen (HLA) in humans: the two classes of molecule, how each is loaded with peptide, how the genes are inherited, and why particular alleles raise the risk of particular autoimmune diseases. It then applies MHC biology to natural killer (NK) cell recognition and to transplant rejection. Board questions often describe a defect (absent TAP, a sensitized recipient, a virus that hides class I) and ask which T cell or NK response fails or which reaction follows. If you can say where a peptide came from, which molecule carries it, and which cell reads it, you can reason through most of them.","prerequisites":["Structure of the T cell receptor and the CD4 and CD8 coreceptors","Protein trafficking through the endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi, and endosomes","Ubiquitin and proteasomal protein degradation","Mendelian inheritance, linkage, and codominance","Complement activation and type II hypersensitivity"],"connections":[{"to":"Humoral and cell-mediated immunity (IM-5)","how":"Thymic positive and negative selection on self MHC decides which T cells survive, and MHC restriction follows from that selection."},{"to":"Diseases of hypersensitivity (IM-15-C)","how":"Hyperacute rejection is a type II reaction in which preformed antibody and complement destroy graft endothelium."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal pathology (seronegative spondyloarthropathies)","how":"HLA-B27 links ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis, and psoriatic arthritis through a shared genetic risk."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal and endocrine pathology","how":"HLA-DQ2 and DQ8 present gluten peptides in celiac disease, and DR3, DR4, and DQ8 shape risk for type 1 diabetes."},{"to":"Virology and tumor immunology","how":"Viruses and tumors that lower class I escape CD8 T cells but expose themselves to NK cells."}],"terms":[{"term":"Major histocompatibility complex (MHC)","def":"The gene cluster on chromosome 6 that encodes the peptide-presenting molecules recognized by T cells; in humans it is called HLA."},{"term":"MHC restriction","def":"The rule that a T cell recognizes a peptide only when it is bound to a self MHC molecule, with CD8 cells reading class I and CD4 cells reading class II."},{"term":"Beta2-microglobulin","def":"The small, nonpolymorphic light chain of class I, encoded on chromosome 15, needed for class I to fold and reach the surface."},{"term":"TAP","def":"Transporter associated with antigen processing, an ATP-driven pump that moves cytosolic peptides into the endoplasmic reticulum for class I loading."},{"term":"Invariant chain","def":"A protein that occupies the class II groove in the endoplasmic reticulum and steers class II to endosomes."},{"term":"CLIP","def":"Class II-associated invariant chain peptide, the fragment of invariant chain left in the class II groove until HLA-DM removes it."},{"term":"Haplotype","def":"The set of linked HLA alleles on one copy of chromosome 6, usually inherited as a single block."},{"term":"Codominance","def":"Expression of the alleles from both parents at the same time, so each cell displays both sets of HLA molecules."},{"term":"Missing self","def":"NK cell killing of a cell that has lost the self class I molecules that normally switch the NK cell off."},{"term":"Sensitization","def":"Formation of antibodies against foreign HLA after exposure through transfusion, pregnancy, or a previous transplant."},{"term":"Crossmatch","def":"A test of recipient serum against donor lymphocytes that detects preformed antibodies to donor HLA."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-03-01-C01","obj":"IM-3.1","title":"Structure, distribution, and T cell partners of MHC class I and class II","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A T cell receptor cannot bind a free protein. It recognizes a short peptide held in the groove of an MHC molecule on another cell's surface, and it recognizes that peptide only on self MHC (MHC restriction). The two classes of MHC sample different compartments and are read by different T cells, so the immune system can decide whether a cell should be killed or whether a response should be helped."},{"type":"p","text":"Class I molecules are encoded by HLA-A, -B, and -C. Each is one polymorphic heavy (alpha) chain with three extracellular domains, noncovalently paired with beta2-microglobulin. The alpha1 and alpha2 domains form the peptide groove, which is closed at both ends, so it holds peptides of about 8 to 10 amino acids. The alpha3 domain is conserved and is the binding site for CD8. Class I is on all nucleated cells and on platelets, but not on mature red cells, which have no nucleus or protein synthesis."},{"type":"p","text":"Class II molecules are encoded by HLA-DP, -DQ, and -DR. Each is an alpha chain paired with a beta chain, both spanning the membrane. The alpha1 and beta1 domains form a groove open at both ends, so peptides of about 13 to 25 amino acids can overhang it. CD4 binds the conserved beta2 domain. Class II is expressed constitutively on professional antigen-presenting cells (dendritic cells, macrophages, and B cells) and on thymic epithelium, and can be induced on other cells such as endothelium by interferon gamma, which also raises class I."},{"type":"table","title":"MHC class I compared with class II","cols":["Feature","Class I","Class II"],"rows":[["HLA loci","HLA-A, -B, -C","HLA-DP, -DQ, -DR"],["Chains","Heavy chain (alpha1 to alpha3) plus beta2-microglobulin","Alpha chain plus beta chain"],["Groove","Alpha1 and alpha2; closed ends; about 8 to 10 amino acids","Alpha1 and beta1; open ends; about 13 to 25 amino acids"],["Coreceptor binding site","Alpha3 binds CD8","Beta2 binds CD4"],["Cells expressing it","All nucleated cells and platelets","Dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells, thymic epithelium; inducible by interferon gamma"],["Peptide source","Cytosolic (endogenous) proteins","Endocytosed (exogenous) proteins"],["Responding T cell","CD8 cytotoxic T cell, which kills the presenting cell","CD4 helper T cell, which directs macrophages and B cells"]],"caption":"Class I reports on what a cell is making, and class II reports on what an antigen-presenting cell has taken up."},{"type":"p","text":"The logic of the pairing explains the distribution. Any cell can be infected by a virus or become malignant, so every nucleated cell must be able to show its internal proteins to CD8 cells, which kill it. Help from CD4 cells is needed only where antigen is captured and an immune response is organized, so class II is restricted to antigen-presenting cells. This is why loss of class I (TAP deficiency, C02) leaves CD8 T cells low, and loss of class II (bare lymphocyte syndrome, from defects in the transcription factors that drive class II genes, such as CIITA and RFX subunits) leaves CD4 T cells low with poor antibody responses: thymocytes need the matching MHC for positive selection."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-03-01-C02","obj":"IM-3.2","title":"Antigen processing for class I and class II","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Antigen processing is the conversion of a protein into peptides and their loading onto MHC. The two pathways are kept separate by where loading happens and by what blocks the groove until then, which keeps a cytosolic viral peptide on class I and an engulfed bacterial peptide on class II."},{"type":"flow","title":"Class I pathway","steps":["Cytosolic proteins, including viral proteins, are tagged with ubiquitin","Proteasome cleaves them into short peptides","TAP pumps peptides into the endoplasmic reticulum","Peptide-loading complex (tapasin, calreticulin) loads class I","Stable class I and peptide travel through Golgi to surface","CD8 T cell recognizes peptide on class I"],"caption":"Peptides from proteins synthesized in the cytosol reach class I inside the endoplasmic reticulum."},{"type":"p","text":"Class I loading takes place in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER). The proteasome degrades old, misfolded, and ubiquitinated cytosolic proteins, and interferon gamma induces immunoproteasome subunits that favor peptides suited to class I. TAP, a heterodimer of TAP1 and TAP2 in the ER membrane, uses ATP to move the peptides into the ER, where ER aminopeptidases may trim them. Newly made heavy chain and beta2-microglobulin wait in the peptide-loading complex, which includes tapasin bridging class I to TAP. Class I without peptide is unstable and is degraded, so in TAP deficiency little class I reaches the surface, CD8 T cells are few, and patients have chronic bacterial respiratory infections and often granulomatous skin lesions."},{"type":"flow","title":"Class II pathway","steps":["Class II alpha and beta assemble in ER with invariant chain","Invariant chain blocks groove and sorts class II to endosomes","Endocytosed protein is degraded in acidic endosomes","Proteases trim invariant chain to CLIP in the groove","HLA-DM exchanges CLIP for an endosomal peptide","Loaded class II goes to the surface for CD4 T cells"],"caption":"The invariant chain keeps class II empty until it reaches the endosome where exogenous peptides are made."},{"type":"p","text":"Class II is also built in the ER, which is full of TAP-delivered peptides, but the invariant chain (CD74) occupies its groove there and carries a sorting signal that sends the complex to late endosomes. Endocytosed or phagocytosed proteins are degraded there by acid-dependent proteases such as cathepsins. The same proteases cut the invariant chain down to CLIP, which still blocks the groove. HLA-DM, a nonpolymorphic class II-like molecule that does not itself present peptide, catalyzes release of CLIP and favors stable binding of higher-affinity peptides. Agents that raise endosomal pH, such as chloroquine, impair this step, which shows that class II loading depends on acidity."},{"type":"p","text":"One exception matters. Dendritic cells can deliver engulfed antigen to the class I pathway (cross-presentation), so CD8 T cells can be primed against viruses and tumors that do not infect dendritic cells themselves."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A virus encodes a protein that blocks TAP. What happens to surface class I and class II on an infected macrophage, and which T cell response fails?","explain":"Class I falls because empty class I is unstable and degraded, so CD8 recognition fails. Class II is unaffected because its peptides come from endosomes and its loading does not need TAP.","check":"Compare with the two flow figures and the paragraph on TAP and the peptide-loading complex."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-03-01-C03","obj":"IM-3.3","title":"HLA genetics, haplotype inheritance, and sibling matching","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The HLA genes lie together on the short arm of chromosome 6 (6p21.3). The class I region contains HLA-A, -B, and -C; the class II region contains HLA-DP, -DQ, and -DR along with genes for antigen processing, including TAP and HLA-DM; and between them the class III region encodes non-presenting proteins such as complement C2, C4, and factor B, and TNF. Beta2-microglobulin is encoded on chromosome 15, so it is not inherited with the HLA haplotype."},{"type":"p","text":"HLA genes are the most polymorphic in the human genome, with thousands of alleles at the classical loci. Most of the variation sits in the residues lining the peptide groove, so different alleles bind different peptides. Expression is codominant: each cell displays the molecules from both parental chromosomes, giving up to six different classical class I molecules. Because each molecule binds a range of peptides, an individual can present peptides from almost any pathogen, and a population with many alleles is unlikely to contain a pathogen that escapes everyone. The cost is that two unrelated people rarely match."},{"type":"p","text":"The loci are close enough that recombination between them is uncommon, so the alleles on one chromosome travel together as a haplotype. Each parent carries two haplotypes and passes one, at random, to each child. If the father is a/b and the mother is c/d, every child is ac, ad, bc, or bd, each with probability one in four."},{"type":"table","title":"HLA sharing expected between relatives","cols":["Relationship","Full match (2 haplotypes)","Half match (1 haplotype)","No shared haplotype"],"rows":[["Sibling","25%","50%","25%"],["Parent and child","0% (unless parents share a haplotype)","100%","0%"],["Identical twin","100%","Not applicable","Not applicable"],["Unrelated person","Rare, because of polymorphism","Variable","Most likely"]],"caption":"Values assume no recombination within the HLA region and four distinct parental haplotypes."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: sibling donors","text":"A patient needs a stem cell donor and has three siblings with the same parents. For any one sibling, the chance of receiving the same paternal haplotype as the patient is 1/2, and the same maternal haplotype is also 1/2. Because the two events are independent, a full match is 1/2 x 1/2 = 1/4, or 25%. Each sibling is an independent draw, so the chance that none of the three matches is (3/4) cubed, about 0.42, and the chance that at least one matches is about 58%. A parent is always a half match because the child carries one parental haplotype, and an identical twin is the only certain full match."},{"type":"p","text":"Linkage also explains linkage disequilibrium, the finding that some alleles on neighboring loci occur together more often than chance predicts, such as the ancestral haplotype carrying HLA-A1, B8, and DR3. This is why a disease association found with one allele may actually reflect a nearby gene on the same haplotype (C04)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-03-01-C04","obj":"IM-3.4","title":"HLA alleles and disease susceptibility","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Because each HLA allele binds its own set of peptides, the alleles a person inherits influence which self or foreign peptides are shown to T cells, both in the thymus during selection and in the periphery. Some alleles present a self peptide, or a modified self peptide, in a way that favors an autoreactive response. An HLA association raises risk but does not cause disease by itself: most carriers of a risk allele never develop the disease, and disease also requires other genes and environmental triggers such as infection or diet."},{"type":"table","title":"Classic HLA and disease associations","cols":["HLA type","Disease","Proposed link"],"rows":[["HLA-B27 (class I)","Ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, arthritis of inflammatory bowel disease","Seronegative spondyloarthropathies with enthesitis and sacroiliitis; mechanism unproven"],["HLA-DQ2 and DQ8","Celiac disease","Bind deamidated gluten peptides and present them to CD4 T cells"],["HLA-DR3 and DR4 (with DQ8)","Type 1 diabetes","Present beta cell antigens; DR3/DR4 heterozygotes have the highest risk"],["HLA-DR4","Rheumatoid arthritis","Shared epitope in the DR beta chain binds citrullinated peptides"],["HLA-DR2 (DR15)","Multiple sclerosis, Goodpasture syndrome","Present myelin antigens and type IV collagen alpha3 chain peptides"]],"caption":"Most associations are with class II, which presents to CD4 helper cells that drive autoantibody and inflammatory responses; B27 is the major class I exception."},{"type":"p","text":"Celiac disease shows the mechanism most clearly. Gluten proteins (gliadins) are rich in proline and glutamine and resist digestion, leaving long peptides in the small intestine. Tissue transglutaminase deamidates some glutamine residues to glutamate, adding negative charges that fit pockets in the DQ2 and DQ8 grooves, so these molecules bind the modified peptides tightly and present them to gluten-specific CD4 T cells. The resulting inflammation causes villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis. Nearly all patients carry DQ2 or DQ8, but these alleles are common in the general population, so their absence makes celiac disease very unlikely while their presence proves little."},{"type":"p","text":"In rheumatoid arthritis, several DR4 alleles (and some others) share a sequence in the DR beta chain, the shared epitope, that favors binding of citrullinated peptides, which matches the anti-citrullinated protein antibodies typical of the disease. In type 1 diabetes, risk comes mainly from DR3 and DR4 haplotypes and the DQ8 allele carried with DR4, and the DR3/DR4 heterozygote carries the highest risk. HLA-B27 is present in about 90% of patients with ankylosing spondylitis in populations of European descent, yet only a small fraction of B27 carriers develop it. Its mechanism is debated: hypotheses include presentation of arthritogenic bacterial or self peptides to CD8 cells, misfolding of B27 heavy chains in the ER that triggers an inflammatory stress response, and B27 heavy chain dimers that engage receptors on NK and other cells. Reactive arthritis after enteric or genitourinary infection fits the idea of an infectious trigger in a susceptible host."},{"type":"p","text":"Some associations reflect linkage rather than antigen presentation. Hereditary hemochromatosis, for example, is caused by the HFE gene, which lies near HLA-A on chromosome 6 and is often inherited with HLA-A3."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-03-01-C05","obj":"IM-3.5","title":"NK cells and missing self","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"NK cells are innate lymphocytes that kill infected and malignant cells without prior sensitization and without antigen-specific receptors. They express no T cell receptor. Instead, each NK cell carries several germline-encoded inhibitory and activating receptors, and whether it kills depends on the balance of signals at the moment it contacts a target."},{"type":"table","title":"Main NK cell receptors","cols":["Receptor","Ligand","Effect"],"rows":[["KIRs (killer cell immunoglobulin-like receptors)","HLA-A, -B, -C","Inhibitory (most); ITIMs recruit phosphatases such as SHP-1"],["CD94/NKG2A","HLA-E, which displays leader peptides from other class I molecules","Inhibitory; reports overall class I synthesis"],["NKG2D","Stress-induced ligands MICA, MICB, and ULBPs","Activating"],["CD16 (Fc gamma RIIIa)","Fc region of IgG on antibody-coated cells","Activating; antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity"]],"caption":"Inhibition by self class I normally dominates over activating signals."},{"type":"p","text":"Healthy cells display self class I, which engages inhibitory receptors whose cytoplasmic immunoreceptor tyrosine-based inhibitory motifs (ITIMs) recruit phosphatases. These remove the phosphates that activating receptors add to signaling kinases, so the NK cell stays quiet. When a cell is infected or transformed, two things tend to happen together: stress pathways induce activating ligands such as MICA and MICB, and class I expression falls."},{"type":"flow","title":"Missing-self recognition","steps":["Virus or tumor lowers class I to escape CD8 T cells","Inhibitory KIR and CD94/NKG2A receptors are no longer engaged","Stress ligands engage activating receptors such as NKG2D","Activating signals are no longer opposed by phosphatases","NK cell releases perforin and granzymes","Target cell undergoes apoptosis"],"caption":"Loss of class I removes the brake, and stress ligands supply the accelerator."},{"type":"p","text":"Class I loss is common because it protects a virus or tumor from CD8 T cells. Herpes simplex virus blocks TAP, adenovirus retains class I in the ER, and cytomegalovirus proteins send class I for degradation; many tumors lose class I or beta2-microglobulin. Each strategy that hides a cell from CD8 cells exposes it to NK cells, so the two systems are complementary. Some viruses answer this too: cytomegalovirus makes a class I-like decoy and a peptide that keeps HLA-E on the surface to engage inhibitory receptors. NK cells kill through perforin and granzymes, which trigger apoptosis, and through Fas ligand, and they release interferon gamma that activates macrophages. IL-12, IL-15, and type I interferons enhance their activity."},{"type":"p","text":"Missing self alone is not enough. Mature red cells lack class I but are not killed, because they lack activating ligands. NK cells act within the first days of a viral infection, before CD8 T cells expand, and people with NK cell deficiencies suffer severe, recurrent herpesvirus infections."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A tumor loses beta2-microglobulin. Which effector cell loses the ability to recognize it, and which gains it?","explain":"Without beta2-microglobulin class I cannot reach the surface, so CD8 T cells lose their target, while NK inhibitory receptors are no longer engaged, so NK cells can kill if activating ligands are present.","check":"Compare with the missing-self flow and the receptor table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-03-01-C06","obj":"IM-3.6","title":"Sensitization, hyperacute rejection, crossmatching, and HLA matching","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Foreign HLA molecules are the main antigens that trigger graft rejection, because an unusually large fraction of a person's T cells (on the order of 1 to 2%) react directly to allogeneic MHC carrying peptide. Antibodies matter too. A person who has been exposed to foreign HLA through blood transfusion, pregnancy (fetal HLA from the father), or a previous transplant can make long-lived antibodies against those alleles; this is sensitization. ABO blood group antibodies are naturally present and act the same way against ABO-incompatible tissue, because ABO antigens are expressed on endothelium."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hyperacute rejection","steps":["Transfusion, pregnancy, or prior graft exposes recipient to foreign HLA","Plasma cells make anti-HLA antibodies that persist","Graft is perfused; antibodies bind donor endothelium","Complement activation injures and activates endothelium","Platelets aggregate and vessels thrombose","Graft becomes ischemic, mottled, and cyanotic within minutes to hours"],"caption":"Preformed antibody, not T cells, causes hyperacute rejection; it is a type II hypersensitivity reaction."},{"type":"p","text":"Hyperacute rejection begins as soon as the vascular anastomosis is opened. Antibody bound to endothelium activates complement through the classical pathway, causing endothelial injury, loss of the anticoagulant endothelial surface, and exposure of subendothelial matrix. Platelets adhere and aggregate, and thrombi occlude small vessels. Microscopically the graft shows thrombi, neutrophils in vessel walls, and fibrinoid necrosis, followed by infarction. Because the antibody is already present, no delay for T cell priming is needed, which is why the reaction begins within minutes."},{"type":"table","title":"Tests that prevent hyperacute rejection","cols":["Test","What it does","What a positive result means"],"rows":[["ABO typing","Matches donor and recipient blood groups","Natural anti-A or anti-B antibodies would attack the graft"],["Antibody screening (panel reactive antibody)","Tests recipient serum against HLA from a panel or on synthetic beads","Recipient is sensitized; percentage estimates how many donors are incompatible"],["Crossmatch","Mixes recipient serum with donor lymphocytes, read by complement-mediated killing or flow cytometry","Recipient has antibody against this donor's HLA; high risk of hyperacute or antibody-mediated rejection"]],"caption":"Lymphocytes are used in the crossmatch because they express donor class I, and B cells also express class II."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a sensitized recipient","text":"A woman with kidney failure has had three pregnancies with the same partner and several transfusions. Her husband offers a kidney. Her serum lyses his lymphocytes in a crossmatch. Each pregnancy exposed her to his paternal HLA through fetal cells, and transfusions added other HLA, so she produced anti-HLA antibodies. A positive crossmatch means those antibodies recognize his class I or class II, so if his kidney were perfused they would bind its endothelium at once and fix complement, causing hyperacute rejection. A donor who lacks the HLA her antibodies recognize would give a negative crossmatch."},{"type":"p","text":"Crossmatching and HLA matching act on different arms of rejection. The crossmatch excludes preformed antibody and so prevents hyperacute rejection. HLA matching lowers the number of mismatched alleles that recipient T cells can recognize, which reduces both T cell mediated acute rejection and the formation of new donor-specific antibody, and so improves long-term graft survival. For kidney transplantation, matching typically focuses on HLA-A, -B, and -DR."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"MHC ties the chapter's disciplines together. Cell biology explains the two loading routes, which the invariant chain keeps apart. Genetics explains why polymorphic, codominant, tightly linked HLA genes protect populations yet make unrelated donors hard to find and siblings match one time in four. Immunology follows from which cell reads each class: CD8 cells kill cells that show foreign peptide on class I, CD4 cells direct responses to peptide on class II, and NK cells cover cells that drop class I."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology applies the same rules. Autoimmune disease associations are mostly class II because CD4 helper cells drive autoantibody and inflammatory responses, and celiac disease shows how a modified dietary peptide can fit a risk allele. In transplantation, preformed antibody causes hyperacute (type II) rejection, while T cell recognition of mismatched HLA drives acute rejection, which is why crossmatching and HLA matching are separate safeguards. The limits matter: HLA risk alleles are neither necessary nor sufficient for disease, and several proposed mechanisms, especially for HLA-B27, remain unproven."}],"summary":["Class I (HLA-A, -B, -C) is a heavy chain with beta2-microglobulin on all nucleated cells and platelets; it presents cytosolic peptides of about 8 to 10 amino acids to CD8 T cells through the alpha3 domain.","Class II (HLA-DP, -DQ, -DR) is an alpha and beta chain on antigen-presenting cells and thymic epithelium; it presents endosomal peptides of about 13 to 25 amino acids to CD4 T cells through the beta2 domain.","Class I loading: ubiquitin, proteasome, TAP into the ER, peptide-loading complex; TAP loss lowers surface class I and CD8 T cells.","Class II loading: invariant chain blocks the groove and sorts class II to endosomes, it is trimmed to CLIP, and HLA-DM exchanges CLIP for peptide.","HLA genes on chromosome 6p are polymorphic, codominant, and inherited as haplotypes; a sibling is a full match 25%, a half match 50%, and no match 25% of the time.","Risk alleles: B27 with spondyloarthropathies, DQ2 and DQ8 with celiac disease, DR3 and DR4 with type 1 diabetes, DR4 with rheumatoid arthritis, DR2 with multiple sclerosis and Goodpasture syndrome; association raises risk but does not cause disease alone.","NK cells are inhibited by self class I through KIRs and CD94/NKG2A and kill cells that lose class I and express stress ligands (missing self).","Escape from CD8 T cells by lowering class I exposes infected and tumor cells to NK cells.","Preformed anti-HLA or ABO antibodies from transfusion, pregnancy, or prior grafts cause complement-mediated hyperacute rejection within minutes to hours.","The crossmatch detects preformed antibody against donor HLA; HLA matching reduces T cell mediated rejection and improves long-term graft survival."],"sources":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Antigen Capture and Presentation to Lymphocytes"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Innate Immunity (natural killer cells)"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Immune Responses Against Tumors and Transplants"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System (HLA and disease associations; rejection of transplants)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract (celiac disease)"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Immunology (major histocompatibility complex and transplantation)"}]},"IM-4":{"unit":"IM-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-04-01","title":"Lymphatic Drainage","subject":"Immune and Lymphatic System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows lymph from the tissues to the veins of the neck. It traces the cisterna chyli, thoracic duct, and right lymphatic duct, maps the territory of each palpable node group, and applies that map to infections, first nodal spread of tumors, the Virchow node, and chylothorax. Board questions usually describe an enlarged node or a tumor site and ask where the problem started or where it will spread first. Two rules carry most of these questions: skin and body wall drain to the nearest superficial node group, while an organ drains along its arteries back to the place where it developed.","prerequisites":["Starling forces and formation of interstitial fluid at the capillary","Structure of a lymph node: capsule, subcapsular sinus, cortex, paracortex, medulla (IM-2)","Major veins of the root of the neck: internal jugular, subclavian, brachiocephalic","Absorption of long-chain fat as chylomicrons in the small intestine","Descent of the gonads and development of the anal canal"],"connections":[{"to":"Lymph composition and transport (IM-9)","how":"The pumping, valves, and flow rates covered there move lymph along the routes mapped in this chapter."},{"to":"Lymphatic organ function (IM-8)","how":"Regional nodes are where antigen and tumor cells carried in afferent lymph are first filtered, so their enlargement follows the drainage map."},{"to":"Conditions of the breast (RE-13-E)","how":"Axillary and parasternal drainage determines where breast carcinoma is found first and why the sentinel node concept works."},{"to":"Reproductive neoplasms (RE-13-J)","how":"Testicular tumors spread to para-aortic nodes and scrotal or vulvar tumors to inguinal nodes because of their embryologic origins."},{"to":"Blood and lymph neoplasms (HE-9-D)","how":"Lymphoma enlarges nodes and can obstruct the thoracic duct, causing chylous effusions."}],"terms":[{"term":"Lymph","def":"Interstitial fluid that has entered lymphatic capillaries, carrying protein, cells, and in the gut absorbed fat back to the venous blood."},{"term":"Cisterna chyli","def":"A thin-walled sac in front of the L1 to L2 vertebral bodies, formed by the lumbar and intestinal lymph trunks, where the thoracic duct begins."},{"term":"Thoracic duct","def":"The largest lymph vessel, running from the cisterna chyli through the posterior mediastinum to the left venous angle."},{"term":"Right lymphatic duct","def":"A short vessel, often present only as separate trunks, that returns lymph from the right side of the head and neck, right upper limb, and right thorax to the right venous angle."},{"term":"Venous angle","def":"The junction of the internal jugular and subclavian veins on each side, where the main lymph ducts empty."},{"term":"Chyle","def":"Milky intestinal lymph containing chylomicrons from absorbed long-chain fat."},{"term":"Regional (first-echelon) node","def":"The first node group to receive lymph from a given site and therefore the first to enlarge with infection or metastasis there."},{"term":"Sentinel node","def":"The specific first node that receives lymph from a tumor site; if it is free of tumor, downstream nodes usually are too."},{"term":"Pectinate line","def":"The line in the anal canal marking the junction of endoderm-derived hindgut above and ectoderm-derived proctodeum below."},{"term":"Virchow node","def":"An enlarged left supraclavicular node, classically from abdominal malignancy carried up the thoracic duct; finding it on examination is the Troisier sign."},{"term":"Chylothorax","def":"Accumulation of chyle in the pleural space, usually after injury to or obstruction of the thoracic duct."},{"term":"Pseudochylothorax","def":"A milky, cholesterol-rich effusion from a long-standing pleural process that contains no chylomicrons."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-04-01-C01","obj":"IM-4.1","title":"The two main ducts and their territories","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lymphatic capillaries collect the fluid and plasma protein that capillaries filter but do not reabsorb. Valved collecting vessels carry this lymph through chains of nodes to a few large trunks and then to two ducts that empty into the great veins of the neck, returning about 2 to 3 L to the blood each day (the forces that move it are covered in IM-9)."},{"type":"h","text":"Lower body and abdomen: the cisterna chyli and thoracic duct"},{"type":"p","text":"Lymph from the lower limbs and pelvis passes through iliac nodes to the lumbar (lateral aortic) nodes and forms the right and left lumbar trunks. Lymph from the gut passes through preaortic nodes (celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric) into the intestinal trunk. These trunks join at the cisterna chyli, which lies in front of the L1 to L2 vertebral bodies, between the aorta and the right crus of the diaphragm."},{"type":"p","text":"The thoracic duct leaves the cisterna chyli and enters the thorax through the aortic hiatus at about T12. In the lower posterior mediastinum it lies to the right of the midline, behind the esophagus, between the thoracic aorta on its left and the azygos vein on its right. At about T4 to T6 it crosses to the left, ascends along the left side of the esophagus into the root of the neck, arches laterally at about C7, and empties into the left venous angle, the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins. Near its end it usually receives the left jugular, subclavian, and bronchomediastinal trunks, so it drains the left side of the head, neck, upper limb, and thorax as well as everything below the diaphragm."},{"type":"flow","title":"Path of the thoracic duct","steps":["Lumbar and intestinal trunks join at the cisterna chyli (L1 to L2)","Duct passes through the aortic hiatus at about T12","Ascends right of midline between aorta and azygos vein","Crosses to the left at about T4 to T6","Ascends left of the esophagus and arches at about C7","Empties into the left venous angle"],"caption":"The crossing level explains the side of a chylothorax (C05), and the termination explains the Virchow node (C04)."},{"type":"h","text":"The right upper quarter: the right lymphatic duct"},{"type":"p","text":"The right jugular, right subclavian, and right bronchomediastinal trunks drain the right side of the head and neck, the right upper limb, and the right side of the thorax. They may join as a short right lymphatic duct, about 1 cm long, or open separately into the right venous angle."},{"type":"table","title":"Territories of the two ducts","cols":["Feature","Thoracic duct","Right lymphatic duct"],"rows":[["Territory","Both lower limbs, pelvis, abdomen, left thorax, left upper limb, left head and neck","Right head and neck, right upper limb, right thorax"],["Origin","Cisterna chyli at L1 to L2","Union of right jugular, subclavian, and bronchomediastinal trunks"],["Termination","Left venous angle","Right venous angle (trunks may enter separately)"],["Carries chyle","Yes, all intestinal lymph","No"]],"caption":"Exception: much of the lower lobe of the left lung drains across to right-sided mediastinal nodes."},{"type":"p","text":"The thoracic duct also carries chyle. Chylomicrons formed from long-chain fat in enterocytes are too large to enter villous blood capillaries, so they enter the central lacteal of each villus and reach the blood through the thoracic duct rather than the portal vein. Thoracic duct lymph is therefore milky after a fatty meal and clear during fasting."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A tumor encases the cisterna chyli. Which body regions would develop lymphedema, and would fat absorption change?","explain":"Both lower limbs, the pelvis, and the genitalia lose their outflow, and chylomicrons cannot leave the gut, so chylous ascites and fat malabsorption follow. The upper limbs and head drain normally because their trunks join the ducts in the neck.","check":"The thoracic duct flow figure and the territory table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-04-01-C02","obj":"IM-4.2","title":"Palpable node groups and their territories","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Afferent lymph carries microbes, antigen, and tumor cells to the subcapsular sinus of the first node it reaches. When a node meets antigen it enlarges: lymphocytes proliferate in follicles (B cells) or the paracortex (T cells), macrophages fill the sinuses, and inflammation adds edema. Rapid swelling stretches the capsule, so infected nodes are usually tender, whereas tumor infiltration enlarges a node slowly and with fibrosis, making it hard, nontender, and fixed to surrounding tissue. Lymphoma typically produces firm, rubbery nodes. Localized enlargement points to the drainage territory; enlargement in two or more noncontiguous regions (generalized lymphadenopathy) points to a systemic process such as infectious mononucleosis, HIV infection, secondary syphilis, systemic lupus erythematosus, lymphoma, or leukemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Superficial lymph vessels tend to run with superficial veins, which explains the less obvious territories. The epitrochlear (cubital) nodes lie above the medial epicondyle beside the basilic vein and receive lymph from the ulnar side of the hand and forearm, while radial-side vessels follow the cephalic vein directly to axillary nodes. Skin and superficial tissue below the umbilicus drain to the superficial inguinal nodes, and above the umbilicus to the axillary nodes. The vertical group of superficial inguinal nodes follows the great saphenous vein and drains most of the lower limb skin, but the lateral foot and back of the calf follow the small saphenous vein to the popliteal nodes."},{"type":"table","title":"Palpable node groups","cols":["Node group","Main territory","Typical cause of enlargement"],"rows":[["Preauricular and parotid","Eyelids, conjunctiva, anterior scalp, temple","Viral or chlamydial conjunctivitis"],["Occipital and postauricular","Posterior scalp, skin behind the ear","Scalp infection or lice; rubella"],["Submental and submandibular","Lips, chin, floor of mouth, anterior tongue, teeth, gums","Dental abscess, lip or mouth infection"],["Anterior (deep) cervical, including jugulodigastric","Tonsils, pharynx, posterior tongue; final path for all head and neck lymph","Streptococcal pharyngitis, tonsillitis"],["Posterior cervical","Scalp and posterior neck","Infectious mononucleosis (EBV)"],["Right supraclavicular","Mediastinum, lungs, esophagus","Lung or esophageal cancer, lymphoma"],["Left supraclavicular (Virchow)","Abdomen and thorax via the thoracic duct","Gastric or other abdominal or thoracic cancer"],["Axillary","Upper limb, most of the breast, trunk wall above the umbilicus","Hand or arm infection, cat-scratch disease, breast cancer"],["Epitrochlear","Ulnar side of the hand and forearm, medial fingers","Infection of the ring or little finger; secondary syphilis"],["Superficial inguinal","Lower limb skin, trunk wall below the umbilicus, buttock, perineum, scrotum, vulva, lower vagina, anal canal below the pectinate line","Leg cellulitis, genital ulcers (syphilis, chancroid, herpes, lymphogranuloma venereum)"],["Popliteal","Lateral foot, heel, back of the leg","Infected heel or lateral foot"]],"caption":"Each group drains next into deeper nodes: head and neck groups to deep cervical nodes, popliteal and superficial inguinal nodes to deep inguinal and external iliac nodes."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal nodes are usually under about 1 cm, though inguinal nodes up to about 1.5 to 2 cm are common in adults because the legs and feet are exposed to constant minor injury. Any palpable supraclavicular node is abnormal and carries a high likelihood of malignancy, because the supraclavicular nodes drain the chest and abdomen rather than exposed skin. Some exceptions in the inguinal territory are worth knowing: the glans penis and glans clitoris drain to deep inguinal and external iliac nodes, and lymph from the uterine fundus near the attachment of the round ligament can follow the ligament to the superficial inguinal nodes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Reasoning from an infection to the enlarged node","steps":["Identify the exact site: skin, body wall, or organ","Skin or body wall: nearest superficial group","Check the superficial vein: basilic, small saphenous, or great saphenous","Organ: follow its arteries to deep nodes","Tender, warm node suggests infection; hard, fixed suggests tumor"],"caption":"An infected little finger enlarges epitrochlear nodes; an infected thumb enlarges axillary nodes; an infected heel enlarges popliteal nodes."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-04-01-C03","obj":"IM-4.3","title":"First nodal spread of tumors","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Carcinomas typically spread first through lymphatics, so the first nodes involved can be predicted from the drainage map. Two rules decide nearly every question. Skin, and any surface lined by skin, drains to the nearest superficial node group. An organ drains along its arteries back toward the aorta, to nodes at the level where its arteries arise, which reflects where the organ developed rather than where it ends up."},{"type":"h","text":"Breast"},{"type":"p","text":"About three quarters of breast lymph, including nearly all from the lateral quadrants, drains to the axillary nodes, first to the pectoral (anterior) group and then through central and apical groups to the subclavian trunk. Most of the rest, mainly from the medial quadrants, follows perforating branches of the internal thoracic vessels to the parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes beside the sternum. Some lymph from the inferior quadrants reaches abdominal nodes, and some crosses to the opposite breast. Carcinoma is most common in the upper outer quadrant, which contains the most glandular tissue, so axillary nodes are the usual first site of spread; a medial tumor can involve parasternal nodes that are not palpable."},{"type":"h","text":"Testis and scrotum"},{"type":"p","text":"The testis develops on the posterior abdominal wall near the kidney and descends through the inguinal canal, bringing its testicular artery from the aorta at about L2 and its lymphatics with it. Testicular lymph therefore runs in the spermatic cord to the lumbar (para-aortic and paracaval) nodes near the renal vessels, so testicular cancer spreads first to the retroperitoneum, not the groin. The scrotum is skin of the body wall and drains to the superficial inguinal nodes. The ovary follows the same rule as the testis and drains to para-aortic nodes."},{"type":"h","text":"Anal canal and the pectinate line"},{"type":"p","text":"The pectinate line marks where the endoderm-derived hindgut meets the ectoderm-derived proctodeum, and arteries, veins, nerves, and lymph all change there. Above the line the canal is supplied by the superior rectal artery and lined by columnar epithelium, and its lymph drains to the internal iliac nodes. Below the line it is supplied by the inferior rectal artery, lined by stratified squamous epithelium, and drains with the perineal skin to the superficial inguinal nodes."},{"type":"table","title":"First nodes for tumor sites","cols":["Site","First nodes","Reason"],"rows":[["Lateral breast","Axillary (pectoral group first)","Lateral vessels run to the axilla"],["Medial breast","Parasternal (internal thoracic)","Lymph follows internal thoracic perforators"],["Testis","Para-aortic and paracaval (lumbar), near L1 to L2","Lymph follows the testicular vessels back to their origin"],["Scrotum","Superficial inguinal","Scrotal skin is body wall"],["Ovary","Para-aortic (lumbar)","Same developmental logic as the testis"],["Anal canal above the pectinate line","Internal iliac","Hindgut; follows the rectal vessels"],["Anal canal below the pectinate line","Superficial inguinal","Proctodeum; drains with perineal skin"],["Vulva and lower vagina","Superficial inguinal","Skin-lined perineal surfaces"]],"caption":"Organs drain to deep nodes along their arteries; skin drains to the nearest superficial group."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: testis versus scrotum","text":"A 28-year-old man has a firm, painless testicular mass, and a 70-year-old former chimney sweep has an ulcerated scrotal lesion. Which nodes are checked first in each? Step 1: the testis is an organ that formed near the kidney, and its artery arises from the aorta at about L2. Step 2: its lymph follows that artery, so the first nodes are para-aortic, imaged in the retroperitoneum, and groin nodes are normally spared. Step 3: the scrotal lesion is squamous carcinoma of skin, and scrotal skin is body wall below the umbilicus. Step 4: its first nodes are superficial inguinal, which can be palpated. An enlarged inguinal node in a man with testicular cancer suggests scrotal involvement or altered drainage, not the usual route."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-04-01-C04","obj":"IM-4.4","title":"The Virchow node","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The left supraclavicular nodes lie beside the termination of the thoracic duct at the left venous angle. They are the last nodes on the path of lymph from the abdomen, pelvis, lower limbs, and left thorax before it enters the blood. An enlarged, hard, nontender left supraclavicular node is called the Virchow node, and finding it on examination is the Troisier sign. It is classically associated with gastric adenocarcinoma but occurs with many abdominal, pelvic, and thoracic cancers and with lymphoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Two mechanisms carry tumor cells there. Tumor cells that enter the abdominal lymphatics pass through, or bypass, regional preaortic and para-aortic nodes, travel up the thoracic duct, and lodge in nodes at its terminal portion. In addition, when tumor obstructs the end of the duct, lymph backs up and flows retrogradely into the neighboring supraclavicular nodes, carrying tumor cells with it. Either way, a node in the neck can be the first palpable sign of a cancer that is hidden in the abdomen and has already spread beyond its regional nodes, which is why the finding implies advanced disease."},{"type":"flow","title":"How gastric cancer reaches the left neck","steps":["Gastric adenocarcinoma invades submucosal lymphatics","Cells spread through perigastric and celiac nodes","Cells enter the intestinal trunk and cisterna chyli","Cells ascend the thoracic duct","Cells lodge in nodes at the left venous angle","Hard, fixed left supraclavicular node appears"],"caption":"Abdominal lymph reaches only the left venous angle, so abdominal cancer produces a left, not a right, supraclavicular node."},{"type":"p","text":"The side matters. The right supraclavicular nodes drain the right upper quarter, including the mediastinum, lungs, and esophagus, but receive no abdominal lymph, so a hard right supraclavicular node points to lung or esophageal cancer or lymphoma rather than an abdominal primary. The left supraclavicular nodes can also enlarge from left lung or mediastinal disease, because the left bronchomediastinal trunk joins the end of the thoracic duct. A Virchow node is distinguished from a reactive node by its features: reactive nodes from upper limb or neck infection are tender and mobile, whereas metastatic nodes are hard, nontender, and often matted or fixed because tumor provokes a fibrous (desmoplastic) reaction."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 66-year-old with weight loss and early satiety has a hard 2 cm node above the right clavicle and none on the left. Is gastric cancer the most likely source?","explain":"No. The stomach drains through the thoracic duct to the left venous angle. A right supraclavicular node reflects the right lymphatic territory, so the mediastinum, lung, or esophagus is the likelier source, and weight loss with early satiety can accompany an esophageal or mediastinal cancer.","check":"The side paragraph in C04 and the territory table in C01."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-04-01-C05","obj":"IM-4.5","title":"Thoracic duct injury and chylothorax","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A chylothorax is chyle in the pleural space. It follows disruption or obstruction of the thoracic duct or its tributaries. The leading causes are surgical injury (esophagectomy, lung resection, and other cardiothoracic or neck operations) and malignancy, of which lymphoma is the most frequent nontraumatic cause; chest trauma and venous thrombosis that raises pressure at the venous angle also cause it. Because a resting adult sends about 1.5 to 2.5 L of lymph through the duct each day, and more after fatty meals, a torn duct can fill a hemithorax quickly."},{"type":"h","text":"Which side?"},{"type":"p","text":"The side follows the course of the duct. Below about T5 the duct lies right of midline in the posterior mediastinum, so injury there usually produces a right chylothorax. Above the crossing it lies on the left, so injury there, as in surgery at the left root of the neck or upper mediastinum, usually produces a left chylothorax. Injury near the crossing or mediastinal leakage can produce bilateral effusions."},{"type":"flow","title":"From duct injury to chylothorax","steps":["Surgery, trauma, or tumor disrupts the thoracic duct","Chyle collects in the mediastinum","Mediastinal pleura ruptures; chyle enters the pleural space","Below about T5: right effusion; above: left","Milky, triglyceride-rich, lymphocyte-predominant fluid accumulates","Continued loss of fat, protein, vitamins, and lymphocytes"],"caption":"Volume rises when enteral fat is given, because fat increases intestinal lymph flow."},{"type":"h","text":"The fluid explained"},{"type":"p","text":"Each finding in the fluid follows from what the duct carries. Chylomicrons scatter light, so the fluid is milky white and stays milky after centrifugation, unlike an empyema, whose turbidity comes from cells and clears when they are spun down. Chylomicrons are triglyceride-rich, so pleural triglyceride above about 110 mg/dL (1.24 mmol/L) strongly supports chylothorax, a value below about 50 mg/dL (0.56 mmol/L) makes it unlikely, and lipoprotein analysis showing chylomicrons confirms it. The duct carries recirculating lymphocytes, mainly T cells, so the cells are predominantly lymphocytes. Chyle has little ability to irritate the pleura, so the fluid is usually odorless and the effusion can be large without fever or much pain."},{"type":"table","title":"Milky pleural fluid: chylothorax versus pseudochylothorax","cols":["Feature","Chylothorax","Pseudochylothorax"],"rows":[["Cause","Thoracic duct disruption or obstruction","Long-standing effusion with thickened pleura (tuberculosis, rheumatoid pleurisy)"],["Onset","Days after injury or with a new tumor","Months to years"],["Triglyceride","Above about 110 mg/dL","Usually low"],["Cholesterol","Usually below about 200 mg/dL","Above about 200 mg/dL, cholesterol crystals"],["Chylomicrons","Present","Absent"],["Predominant cells","Lymphocytes","Variable"]],"caption":"Milkiness alone does not identify chyle; chylomicrons and the triglyceride level do."},{"type":"p","text":"A fasting patient is an important exception. When no fat is being absorbed, chyle contains few chylomicrons and can look clear or serous, so a postoperative patient kept without food may show a nonmilky effusion that turns milky once enteral feeding resumes. Medium-chain fatty acids are absorbed directly into portal blood bound to albumin and are not packaged into chylomicrons, which is why they add little to chyle flow. Prolonged leakage depletes what the duct carries: fat and fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K), protein (hypoalbuminemia and low immunoglobulins), and lymphocytes (lymphopenia), producing malnutrition and impaired immunity. Leakage into the peritoneum causes chylous ascites, and in lymphatic filariasis abnormal lymph-urinary connections cause chyluria."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: predicting the side and the fluid","text":"Four days after an esophagectomy with an incision through the right chest, a patient who has just started enteral feeding develops a large right pleural effusion. Step 1: the lower thoracic duct lies right of midline beside the esophagus, below its crossing at about T5, so injury during the dissection gives a right effusion. Step 2: the delay fits feeding, which raised chyle flow and made the fluid milky. Step 3: expect milky fluid that stays milky after centrifugation, triglyceride above about 110 mg/dL, chylomicrons, and lymphocyte predominance. Step 4: if drainage continues, expect falling albumin, falling lymphocyte count, and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology explains both the drainage map and its variants. The thoracic duct forms from paired embryonic lymph channels that anastomose, with the lower part of the right channel, the anastomosis, and the upper part of the left persisting, which is why the adult duct rises on the right and ends on the left, and why duplicated or right-sided terminations occur. The gonads and their vessels descend from the posterior abdominal wall, and the pectinate line marks the meeting of endoderm and ectoderm, so the same embryology that sets blood and nerve supply also sets lymph drainage."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology and pathology explain what the map predicts. Regional nodes filter afferent lymph, so an enlarged node is a reactive or infiltrated node in the territory of an infection or tumor; carcinomas characteristically spread through lymphatics, whereas sarcomas more often spread through the blood, so the map applies best to carcinomas. Its limits are real: drainage varies between people, tumors can skip the first node, and obstructed lymphatics reroute flow, so a predicted first node is a probability, not a certainty. The thoracic duct also links nutrition, fluid balance, and immunity, which is why its injury affects all three."}],"summary":["Lymph from the lower limbs, pelvis, and gut collects in the cisterna chyli at L1 to L2 and ascends the thoracic duct through the aortic hiatus.","The thoracic duct runs right of midline in the lower posterior mediastinum, crosses to the left at about T4 to T6, and empties at the left venous angle.","The right lymphatic duct, or its separate trunks, drains only the right head and neck, right upper limb, and right thorax.","Skin and body wall drain to the nearest superficial node group; below the umbilicus that is the superficial inguinal group.","Epitrochlear nodes drain the ulnar side of the hand and forearm; popliteal nodes drain the lateral foot and back of the leg.","Organs drain along their arteries to where they developed: testis and ovary to para-aortic nodes, scrotum to superficial inguinal nodes.","Lateral breast drains to axillary nodes and medial breast to parasternal nodes; the anal canal drains to internal iliac nodes above the pectinate line and superficial inguinal nodes below it.","A hard left supraclavicular (Virchow) node signals abdominal or thoracic cancer carried up the thoracic duct, classically gastric adenocarcinoma; a right one points to the chest.","Thoracic duct injury below about T5 causes a right chylothorax and above it a left one; the fluid is milky, triglyceride above about 110 mg/dL, with chylomicrons and lymphocytes.","Pseudochylothorax is cholesterol-rich and lacks chylomicrons; prolonged chyle loss causes malnutrition, fat-soluble vitamin loss, and lymphopenia."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, posterior mediastinum and the thoracic duct"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum, lymphatic drainage of the pelvic organs and perineum"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb and Lower Limb, lymphatic drainage; Thorax, lymphatic drainage of the breast"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular System, development of the lymphatic system; Urogenital System, descent of the testis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, routes of metastasis; The Lung, pleural effusions"}]},"IM-5":{"unit":"IM-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-05-01","title":"Humoral and Cell-Mediated Immunity","subject":"Immunological System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains the two effector arms of adaptive immunity. Humoral immunity is carried by antibodies: how they are built, what each isotype does, and how helper T cells turn a low-affinity IgM response into high-affinity, class-switched antibody and memory. Cell-mediated immunity is carried by T cells: how helper subsets direct macrophages, eosinophils, and neutrophils, and how cytotoxic T cells and NK cells kill infected or abnormal cells. Board questions present a serology pattern, an antibody curve, or a pattern of infections and ask you to name the missing molecule, cell, or pathway. Each topic here is taught by mechanism so those patterns can be reasoned out rather than memorized.","prerequisites":["Innate recognition, antigen-presenting cells, and costimulation (IM-13)","MHC class I and class II structure and antigen processing pathways (IM-3)","Lymph node and spleen architecture: follicles, germinal centers, marginal zone (IM-2, IM-8)","V(D)J recombination and generation of receptor diversity (IM-12)","Intrinsic and extrinsic pathways of apoptosis and the caspase cascade"],"connections":[{"to":"Complement (IM-7)","how":"IgM and IgG bound to antigen recruit C1q through their Fc regions and start the classical pathway."},{"to":"Cell and cytokine signaling (IM-6)","how":"The cytokines that define helper subsets also drive inflammation, fever, and the acute phase response."},{"to":"Congenital and acquired immunodeficiency (IM-15-A, IM-15-B)","how":"Defects in CD40L, FOXP3, STAT3, perforin, or CD4 cells produce infection patterns predicted by the functions taught here."},{"to":"Hypersensitivity diseases (IM-15-C)","how":"IgE and Th2 cells drive type I reactions, IgG and IgM drive types II and III, and Th1 and CD8 cells drive type IV."},{"to":"Reproductive physiology and the newborn","how":"Placental transfer of maternal IgG and secretory IgA in breast milk give the infant passive protection, and maternal IgG explains hemolytic disease of the newborn."}],"terms":[{"term":"Immunoglobulin isotype","def":"The antibody class (IgM, IgG, IgA, IgE, IgD) set by the constant region of the heavy chain."},{"term":"Complementarity-determining region (CDR)","def":"One of three hypervariable loops in each variable domain that together form the antigen-binding site."},{"term":"Opsonization","def":"Coating of a particle by antibody or complement so that phagocytes bind it through Fc or complement receptors and ingest it."},{"term":"Class switch recombination","def":"DNA recombination that joins the same VDJ exon to a different heavy chain constant gene, changing isotype but not specificity."},{"term":"Somatic hypermutation","def":"Point mutation of rearranged variable region genes in germinal center B cells, the raw material for affinity maturation."},{"term":"Affinity maturation","def":"The rise in average antibody affinity over a response as B cells with better-binding receptors are selected in germinal centers."},{"term":"T follicular helper (Tfh) cell","def":"A CD4 T cell that migrates into B cell follicles and supplies CD40L and cytokines such as IL-21 to germinal center B cells."},{"term":"Avidity","def":"The total binding strength of a multivalent antibody, which for pentameric IgM greatly exceeds the affinity of a single binding site."},{"term":"Perforin","def":"A pore-forming protein released from cytotoxic granules that lets granzymes enter the target cell."},{"term":"Antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity (ADCC)","def":"Killing of an IgG-coated target by NK cells that bind the antibody through the Fc receptor CD16."},{"term":"Titer","def":"The highest dilution of a serum that still gives a positive antibody reaction, a semiquantitative measure of antibody amount."},{"term":"Passive immunity","def":"Protection from preformed antibody made by another person or animal, immediate in onset but without memory."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-05-01-C01","obj":"IM-5.1","title":"Immunoglobulin structure: Fab binds, Fc acts","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An immunoglobulin monomer (IgG, about 150 kDa) is built from two identical heavy chains and two identical light chains joined by disulfide bonds into a Y shape. Each light chain is either kappa or lambda, never both in one molecule, because each B cell expresses a single productively rearranged light chain gene (allelic and light chain isotype exclusion). Each chain is folded into repeating immunoglobulin domains of about 110 amino acids: a light chain has one variable (VL) and one constant (CL) domain, and a heavy chain has one variable (VH) and three constant domains (IgG, IgA, IgD) or four (IgM, IgE)."},{"type":"p","text":"Within each variable domain, three hypervariable loops called complementarity-determining regions (CDR1 to CDR3) sit between more conserved framework regions. The six CDRs from VH and VL come together at the tip of each arm to form the antigen-binding site. CDR3 is the most variable because it is encoded at the joins created by V(D)J recombination, where nucleotides are added and removed. Because each antibody has two identical arms, an IgG monomer is bivalent."},{"type":"table","title":"Regions of the immunoglobulin molecule","cols":["Region","Composition","Function"],"rows":[["Fab (fragment antigen binding)","Whole light chain plus VH and CH1 of the heavy chain","Binds antigen; Fab alone can neutralize but cannot recruit effector functions"],["Hypervariable regions (CDRs)","Three loops in each VH and VL domain","Contact the epitope and set specificity"],["Hinge","Proline-rich segment between CH1 and CH2 (IgG, IgA, IgD)","Flexibility, so both arms can reach epitopes at different spacings"],["Fc (fragment crystallizable)","Paired heavy chain constant domains below the hinge","Defines isotype; binds C1q, Fc receptors, and FcRn"]],"caption":"Papain cleaves above the hinge disulfides and releases two Fab fragments and one Fc; pepsin cleaves below them and leaves one bivalent F(ab')2."},{"type":"p","text":"The Fc region is what makes binding useful. Antibody bound to a surface brings several Fc regions close together, and that clustering is the signal. C1q, the first component of the classical complement pathway, must bind at least two Fc regions at once (in IgG the CH2 domain; in IgM the CH3 domain), which is why a single soluble IgG does not activate complement but antigen-bound IgG does. Fc gamma receptors on macrophages and neutrophils bind IgG-coated particles and trigger phagocytosis (opsonization), CD16 on NK cells triggers ADCC, and FcεRI on mast cells holds IgE. The neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn) binds the IgG Fc and carries IgG across the placenta and rescues IgG taken up by endothelial cells, returning it to the blood instead of lysosomes, which gives IgG its long half-life."},{"type":"p","text":"Each B cell first makes a membrane form of its antibody (the B cell receptor, with a transmembrane tail) and, after differentiating into a plasma cell, the secreted form; the switch is made by alternative RNA processing at the 3' end of the heavy chain transcript. Antigenic variants of antibodies are classified as isotypes (heavy chain class), allotypes (inherited allelic differences in constant regions), and idiotypes (the unique shape of one antibody's binding site). A plasma cell clone that overproduces one antibody yields a monoclonal protein, and excess free light chains filtered into urine are Bence Jones proteins."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient's antibody against a toxin is digested with papain. Will the Fab fragments still neutralize the toxin, and will they fix complement?","explain":"Fab keeps the complete antigen-binding site, so it still binds and blocks the toxin. It has lost the Fc, so it cannot bind C1q or Fc receptors, and complement fixation and opsonization are lost.","check":"Compare with the table of regions and the paragraph on Fc clustering."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-05-01-C02","obj":"IM-5.2","title":"The five isotypes and what each does","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"All five isotypes can carry the same variable region; they differ in the heavy chain constant region (mu, gamma, alpha, epsilon, delta), and that difference decides size, location, half-life, and which effector systems the antibody can engage. A naive mature B cell coexpresses membrane IgM and IgD from one transcript by alternative splicing, because the C-mu and C-delta genes lie next to each other. All other isotypes require class switch recombination (C03)."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparison of the immunoglobulin isotypes","cols":["Isotype","Form in body","Approximate adult serum level","Distinctive functions"],"rows":[["IgM","Pentamer with J chain (10 binding sites); monomer on naive B cells","About 0.4 to 2.3 g/L","First antibody of a primary response; strongest classical complement activator; best agglutinator; stays in blood"],["IgG","Monomer; subclasses IgG1 to IgG4","About 7 to 16 g/L (about 75% of serum Ig)","Crosses placenta via FcRn; opsonizes, neutralizes, fixes complement (IgG3 and IgG1 best); ADCC; half-life about 3 weeks"],["IgA","Monomer in serum; dimer with J chain and secretory component in secretions","About 0.7 to 4 g/L","Mucosal neutralization in gut, airway, tears, saliva, breast milk; does not activate the classical pathway; largest daily production"],["IgE","Monomer, mostly bound to FcεRI","Trace (nanogram per mL range)","Arms mast cells and basophils; immediate hypersensitivity; helminth defense with eosinophils"],["IgD","Monomer on naive B cells","Trace","Antigen receptor with IgM on naive B cells; serum role unclear"]],"caption":"Serum reference ranges vary by laboratory and age; values are approximate adult ranges."},{"type":"p","text":"IgM's ten binding sites give it high avidity, which compensates for the low affinity typical of an early, unmutated response. One pentamer bound to a surface presents several Fc regions together, so a single IgM molecule can activate C1q, whereas IgG needs two molecules side by side. Its size (about 900 kDa) keeps IgM largely intravascular and prevents placental transfer, so IgM in cord blood was made by the fetus."},{"type":"p","text":"IgG is the dominant antibody of secondary responses and tissue fluids. FcRn transfers it across the syncytiotrophoblast, mainly in the third trimester, so preterm infants receive less. The same transport explains how maternal IgG anti-RhD causes hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn, while naturally occurring anti-A and anti-B, mostly IgM, usually do not. IgG2 is the main subclass made against polysaccharide capsules, and IgG4 fixes complement poorly."},{"type":"flow","title":"How secretory IgA reaches the mucosal lumen","steps":["Lamina propria plasma cell secretes dimeric IgA with J chain","Polymeric Ig receptor binds dimer on basolateral epithelial surface","Vesicle transcytoses the complex across the epithelial cell","Receptor is cleaved at the apical surface","Secretory component stays attached and resists luminal proteases","Secretory IgA neutralizes microbes and toxins without causing inflammation"],"caption":"The J chain is required for binding to the polymeric Ig receptor; the retained secretory component is a fragment of that receptor."},{"type":"p","text":"Some pathogens (Neisseria, Haemophilus influenzae, Streptococcus pneumoniae) secrete IgA1 proteases that cleave its hinge. Selective IgA deficiency is the most common primary immunodeficiency; most patients are well, some have sinopulmonary and gastrointestinal infections, and those who form anti-IgA antibodies can react to IgA in transfused blood products. IgE circulates at very low levels but is held on mast cells for weeks by the high-affinity receptor FcεRI; antigen crosslinking two bound IgE molecules triggers degranulation (C04, IM-15-C)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-05-01-C03","obj":"IM-5.3","title":"T dependent and T independent B cell activation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A naive B cell recognizes intact antigen through its B cell receptor (BCR), which signals through Ig-alpha and Ig-beta; C3d on the antigen binds the coreceptor CR2 (CD21) and lowers the activation threshold. For a protein antigen this is not enough to produce class-switched, high-affinity antibody without help from a CD4 T cell that recognizes the same antigen."},{"type":"p","text":"The link is antigen presentation by the B cell itself. The B cell internalizes the antigen bound to its BCR, degrades it in endosomes, and displays peptides on MHC class II. A CD4 T cell primed by a dendritic cell against one of those peptides meets the B cell at the follicle edge (linked recognition: the B cell and T cell epitopes differ but lie on the same antigen). The T cell expresses CD40 ligand (CD40L, CD154), which binds CD40 on the B cell and activates NF-kappa-B, inducing proliferation and activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID). Some activated T cells become T follicular helper (Tfh) cells, which enter the follicle (CXCR5) and secrete IL-21."},{"type":"flow","title":"T dependent B cell response","steps":["BCR binds and internalizes protein antigen","B cell presents antigen peptide on MHC class II","Primed CD4 T cell recognizes peptide at follicle edge","CD40L binds CD40; cytokines direct the switch","Early IgM plasma cells form; others seed a germinal center","Dark zone: proliferation and somatic hypermutation via AID","Light zone: selection on follicular dendritic cells and Tfh help","Output: long-lived plasma cells and memory B cells"],"caption":"Selected B cells may return to the dark zone for further rounds of mutation."},{"type":"p","text":"AID deaminates cytidine to uridine in DNA. In switch regions upstream of each constant gene this leads to double-strand breaks and class switch recombination: the intervening DNA is deleted, the VDJ exon is joined to a downstream constant gene, and the change is irreversible. Cytokines decide which switch region is opened: in humans IL-4 (with IL-13) directs IgE and IgG4, TGF-beta directs IgA, and IFN-gamma favors opsonizing, complement-fixing IgG subclasses. In variable genes the same enzyme causes somatic hypermutation. Follicular dendritic cells display antigen as immune complexes; B cells whose mutated receptors bind best capture the most antigen, present the most peptide to Tfh cells, and survive, while the rest die by apoptosis (affinity maturation)."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading an immunoglobulin profile","text":"A 2-year-old boy has recurrent otitis media and Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia. IgM is high; IgG, IgA, and IgE are very low; B cell numbers are normal. B cells develop and make IgM, so what fails is switching, which needs CD40 signaling to induce AID. Pneumocystis also points to a T cell side defect, because CD40L on T cells also activates macrophages and dendritic cells through their CD40. A boy with both features most likely has X-linked CD40L deficiency (hyper-IgM syndrome). AID deficiency gives the same immunoglobulin pattern with enlarged lymph nodes but no opportunistic infections, because T cell to macrophage help is intact."},{"type":"table","title":"T dependent versus T independent responses","cols":["Feature","T dependent (protein)","T independent (polysaccharide, TI-2)"],"rows":[["Antigen","Proteins, peptides","Repeating polysaccharides (bacterial capsules), some lipids and nucleic acids"],["Main B cells","Follicular B cells","Marginal zone B cells (spleen) and B-1 cells"],["T cell help","Required (CD40L, cytokines)","Not used; extensive BCR crosslinking substitutes"],["Isotypes","Switched (IgG, IgA, IgE)","Mostly IgM, limited switching (IgG2 in humans)"],["Affinity maturation and memory","Yes","Little or none"],["Infants under about 2 years","Respond","Respond poorly (immature marginal zone)"]],"caption":"At high doses, TI-1 antigens such as LPS activate B cells polyclonally."},{"type":"p","text":"Polysaccharides yield no peptides for MHC, so they recruit no T cell help. The splenic marginal zone is the main site of the response, which is why asplenia predisposes to overwhelming infection by encapsulated organisms. A conjugate vaccine couples the polysaccharide to a protein carrier: a polysaccharide-specific B cell internalizes the conjugate and presents carrier peptides to T cells, producing switched IgG and memory even in infants."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-05-01-C04","obj":"IM-5.4","title":"Helper T cell subsets and regulatory T cells","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A naive CD4 T cell activated by a dendritic cell (peptide on MHC class II plus B7 to CD28 costimulation) can become one of several effector subsets. Which one it becomes depends mainly on the cytokines present at activation, and those depend on the microbe: the pattern recognition receptors a dendritic cell engages decide whether it secretes IL-12, IL-6 and IL-1, or neither. Each polarizing cytokine signals through a STAT protein that induces a master transcription factor, and that factor turns on the subset's cytokine genes and its own positive feedback while repressing the other programs."},{"type":"table","title":"CD4 T cell subsets","cols":["Subset","Induced by (STAT; master factor)","Signature cytokines","Main effects and targets"],"rows":[["Th1","IL-12 and IFN-gamma (STAT4, STAT1; T-bet)","IFN-gamma (also IL-2, TNF)","Activates macrophages to kill ingested microbes; favors opsonizing IgG; intracellular bacteria such as mycobacteria; granulomas and delayed-type hypersensitivity"],["Th2","IL-4 (STAT6; GATA-3)","IL-4, IL-5, IL-13","IL-4 and IL-13 switch to IgE; IL-5 makes and activates eosinophils; IL-13 increases mucus and gut motility; helminths; allergy and asthma"],["Th17","TGF-beta with IL-6 or IL-1, sustained by IL-23 (STAT3; ROR-gamma-t)","IL-17A, IL-17F, IL-22","Epithelial chemokines and G-CSF recruit neutrophils; IL-22 boosts antimicrobial peptides and barrier; extracellular bacteria and fungi such as Staphylococcus aureus and Candida"],["Regulatory T cell (Treg)","Thymic selection, or TGF-beta with IL-2 in the periphery (STAT5; FOXP3)","IL-10, TGF-beta","Suppresses effector T cells and antigen-presenting cells; maintains self tolerance"]],"caption":"Tfh cells (BCL-6, IL-21) are a further subset that helps B cells (C03)."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The Th1 and macrophage amplification loop","steps":["Macrophage or dendritic cell ingests intracellular microbe","Phagocyte secretes IL-12","Naive CD4 T cell becomes Th1 (T-bet)","Th1 releases IFN-gamma and expresses CD40L","Macrophage increases reactive oxygen species, nitric oxide, lysosomal killing, and IL-12"],"caption":"The loop repeats until the microbe is cleared; when it cannot be cleared, chronic activation forms a granuloma."},{"type":"p","text":"Cross-regulation makes responses polarized. IFN-gamma inhibits Th2 development, and IL-4 inhibits Th1 development, so a response tends to lock into one pattern. Leprosy illustrates the stakes: a strong Th1 response gives tuberculoid leprosy with few bacilli and well-formed granulomas, while a Th2-dominated response gives lepromatous leprosy with abundant bacilli in poorly activated macrophages. Inherited defects in the IL-12 receptor or IFN-gamma receptor (Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease) cause disseminated infection with weakly virulent mycobacteria and Salmonella, which shows that the IL-12 to IFN-gamma loop is the essential defense against intracellular bacteria."},{"type":"p","text":"Th1 and Th17 both fight bacteria but by different effector cells. Th1 arms macrophages against microbes already inside them; Th17 brings neutrophils to skin and mucosal surfaces for extracellular bacteria and fungi. Loss of Th17 function produces chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis and staphylococcal abscesses, as in autosomal dominant hyper-IgE syndrome from STAT3 loss-of-function mutations. TGF-beta participates in both Treg and Th17 induction; IL-6 from an inflamed site tips naive cells toward Th17 rather than Treg."},{"type":"p","text":"Regulatory T cells are CD4 and CD25 (the high-affinity IL-2 receptor alpha chain) positive and depend on FOXP3. They suppress through IL-10 and TGF-beta, by consuming IL-2, and through CTLA-4, which removes B7 from antigen-presenting cells and so deprives effector cells of costimulation. Loss of FOXP3 causes IPEX (immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X-linked): infant boys develop autoimmune enteropathy with diarrhea, early type 1 diabetes, and eczematous dermatitis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has disseminated infection after BCG vaccination but handles Candida and Staphylococcus normally. Which signaling axis is defective, and which subset is spared?","explain":"The IL-12 to IFN-gamma axis is defective, so Th1-driven macrophage activation fails and mycobacteria survive inside macrophages. Th17 function, which recruits neutrophils against Candida and Staphylococcus, is intact.","check":"Compare with the subset table and the paragraph contrasting Th1 and Th17."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-05-01-C05","obj":"IM-5.5","title":"How cytotoxic T cells and NK cells kill","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cytotoxic CD8 T cells (CTLs) recognize foreign peptides on MHC class I, which almost every nucleated cell expresses, so they can find virus-infected and malignant cells anywhere in the body. Naive CD8 cells are activated by dendritic cells that present the antigen on class I, including antigen acquired from other cells (cross-presentation), and CD4 help through CD40 on the dendritic cell strengthens activation and memory. Natural killer (NK) cells are innate lymphocytes (CD56 positive, CD3 negative) that kill without prior sensitization."},{"type":"p","text":"Both cell types kill by making the target undergo apoptosis. When a CTL recognizes its target, LFA-1 binds ICAM-1 to form a tight immunological synapse, and the microtubule organizing center turns toward the target so that lytic granules are released only into that narrow cleft. This directed release spares neighboring healthy cells, and one CTL can detach and kill several targets in sequence."},{"type":"flow","title":"Perforin and granzyme killing","steps":["T cell receptor recognizes peptide on MHC class I","Synapse forms; granules move to the contact zone","Perforin polymerizes into pores in target membrane","Granzymes enter the target cytoplasm","Granzyme B activates caspase-3 and cleaves BID","Truncated BID opens mitochondria; cytochrome c activates caspase-9","Executioner caspases dismantle the cell; DNA fragments"],"caption":"Granzyme B engages both the executioner caspases directly and the mitochondrial (intrinsic) pathway."},{"type":"flow","title":"Fas ligand killing","steps":["Fas ligand on killer cell binds Fas (CD95) on target","Clustered Fas recruits the adaptor FADD","FADD recruits procaspase-8 into the death-inducing signaling complex","Active caspase-8 activates caspase-3 and caspase-7","Target cell undergoes apoptosis"],"caption":"This is the extrinsic pathway; caspase-8 can also cleave BID to amplify the signal through mitochondria."},{"type":"p","text":"Apoptosis matters because the target membrane stays intact long enough for the cell to shrink, fragment into membrane-bound bodies, and expose phosphatidylserine, so macrophages remove it without releasing its contents. Intracellular viruses are destroyed with little inflammation, in contrast to necrosis. The Fas pathway also limits immune responses by deleting repeatedly activated lymphocytes; mutations in Fas cause autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome, with lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, autoimmune cytopenias, and increased double-negative (CD4 negative, CD8 negative) T cells. Loss of perforin, or of granule trafficking (Chediak-Higashi syndrome), causes hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis because infected cells and activated antigen-presenting cells are not eliminated and macrophages remain continuously activated."},{"type":"table","title":"CD8 cytotoxic T cells versus NK cells","cols":["Feature","CD8 cytotoxic T cell","NK cell"],"rows":[["Immunity","Adaptive; clonal, needs priming (days)","Innate; acts within hours"],["Recognition","Specific peptide on MHC class I","Balance of activating (NKG2D binds stress ligands) and inhibitory (KIR, CD94-NKG2A bind class I) receptors"],["Response to lost MHC class I","Cannot see the target","Inhibition removed; target is killed (missing self)"],["Antibody-guided killing","No","Yes, ADCC through CD16 binding IgG"],["Killing mechanisms","Perforin and granzymes; Fas ligand","Perforin and granzymes; Fas ligand and TRAIL"],["Activated by","Antigen, costimulation, IL-2, CD4 help","IL-12, IL-15, type I interferons"]],"caption":"Both cells also secrete IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages and increases MHC class I expression."},{"type":"p","text":"The two systems cover each other's gaps. Many viruses (for example cytomegalovirus) and tumors reduce MHC class I to escape CTLs, but losing class I removes the inhibitory signal that NK cells read, so the cell becomes an NK target. This is why NK cell deficiency presents with severe herpesvirus infections."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A tumor cell stops expressing MHC class I. How does that change its susceptibility to CD8 T cells and to NK cells?","explain":"CD8 T cells can no longer recognize it because they require peptide on class I. NK cells lose inhibition through KIR and CD94-NKG2A, and if activating ligands such as stress-induced NKG2D ligands are present, they kill it.","check":"Compare with the CD8 versus NK table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-05-01-C06","obj":"IM-5.6","title":"Primary and secondary antibody responses; active and passive immunity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Antibody response curves plot serum titer, usually on a logarithmic scale, against time after exposure. In a primary response the lag phase lasts about a week, because the few naive B and T cells specific for the antigen must be found, activated, and expanded. IgM appears first, from early plasma cells formed before germinal centers mature, and IgG follows as class switching proceeds, typically peaking around 2 weeks. Peak titer is modest, affinity is low at first, and titers decline within weeks as short-lived plasma cells die."},{"type":"p","text":"The primary response leaves memory B cells that are more numerous than the original naive clone, mostly class-switched, and of higher affinity, together with long-lived plasma cells in bone marrow that keep secreting antibody. On re-exposure the lag is only a few days, the response is dominated by IgG (or IgA or IgE, depending on earlier switching), peak titer is much higher, often by an order of magnitude or more, affinity is higher still, and antibody persists longer. This requires T dependent antigens; plain polysaccharides give similar IgM responses each time."},{"type":"table","title":"Primary versus secondary antibody response","cols":["Feature","Primary","Secondary"],"rows":[["Responding cells","Naive B cells (few)","Memory B cells (many)"],["Lag phase","About 5 to 10 days","About 1 to 3 days"],["Main isotype","IgM first, then IgG","Mostly IgG"],["Peak titer","Lower","Much higher"],["Average affinity","Lower","Higher (affinity maturation)"],["Duration","Shorter","Longer"]],"caption":"Lag times are approximate and vary with antigen, dose, adjuvant, and route."},{"type":"p","text":"These kinetics are the basis of serologic diagnosis. Pathogen-specific IgM indicates current or recent infection; IgG alone indicates past infection or immunization. Because IgG may already be present, recent infection is shown by a fourfold or greater rise in IgG titer between acute and convalescent samples taken about 2 to 4 weeks apart."},{"type":"table","title":"Active versus passive immunity","cols":["Type","Natural example","Artificial example","Onset and duration","Memory"],"rows":[["Active","Infection","Vaccination (toxoid, inactivated, live attenuated, conjugate)","Slow onset (days to weeks); long-lasting","Yes"],["Passive","Maternal IgG across placenta; secretory IgA in breast milk","Pooled immune globulin, antitoxin, monoclonal antibody","Immediate; lasts weeks to months, set by antibody half-life","No"]],"caption":"Passive antibody from animal sources can provoke antibodies against the foreign protein and immune complex serum sickness (type III hypersensitivity)."},{"type":"p","text":"Passive antibody protects without activating the recipient's lymphocytes, so it decays with its half-life and leaves no memory. Maternal IgG is the clearest example. A term newborn has IgG levels close to the mother's, but this IgG declines with a half-life of about 3 weeks while the infant's own production rises slowly, so total IgG reaches a physiologic low at about 3 to 6 months. Infants with X-linked agammaglobulinemia, who had normal maternal IgG at birth, therefore begin to have recurrent infections with encapsulated bacteria after about 6 months of age."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: newborn serology","text":"A newborn with microcephaly and periventricular calcifications has positive cytomegalovirus IgG and IgM in serum. The IgG may be the mother's, transferred by FcRn, and proves only that the mother was infected at some time. IgM cannot cross the placenta, so the IgM was made by the fetus and points to intrauterine infection. Had the infant been IgG positive and IgM negative, the result would not distinguish maternal antibody from fetal infection; a falling IgG titer over the first months would indicate maternal antibody decaying, whereas persistent or rising IgG would indicate the infant's own response. Direct detection of the virus is used to confirm, because IgM tests can give false negative or false positive results."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Structure, cells, and genes form one chain of reasoning. V(D)J recombination in the bone marrow builds the CDRs; lymphoid architecture places follicular B cells next to T cell zones and marginal zone B cells in the splenic filter; and AID, CD40L, cytokine receptors, and FOXP3 decide what each response produces. Microbiology supplies the selective pressure: capsules defeat T dependent help, IgA proteases attack mucosal antibody, and herpesviruses remove MHC class I, while pathology shows each defect as a predictable infection pattern or autoimmune disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Most effector functions require complement, phagocytes, or mast cells, so antibody alone neutralizes but rarely kills and should be read together with IM-7 and IM-6. The subset scheme is a model: helper cells show plasticity, mixed responses are common, and human cytokine-to-isotype rules differ from the mouse data in which many were first described. Serum immunoglobulin ranges vary with age and laboratory, so pediatric values must be judged against age-specific references."}],"summary":["Two heavy and two light chains form each monomer; six CDRs at the Fab tip bind antigen, and the Fc sets the isotype and binds C1q, Fc receptors, and FcRn.","IgM is a pentamer, appears first, and activates complement most strongly; IgG is the main serum and secondary antibody, opsonizes, and crosses the placenta via FcRn.","Secretory IgA is a J chain dimer carried across epithelium by the polymeric Ig receptor; IgE arms mast cells through FcεRI; IgD is a naive B cell receptor.","T dependent responses need linked recognition, CD40L to CD40 signaling, and cytokines that induce AID for class switching and somatic hypermutation in germinal centers.","Polysaccharides give T independent, mostly IgM responses with little memory and are weak before about age 2; protein conjugation adds T cell help.","IL-12 drives Th1 and IFN-gamma activates macrophages; IL-4 drives Th2, and IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 promote IgE, eosinophils, and mucus.","Th17 cells use IL-17 and IL-22 to recruit neutrophils and protect barriers against extracellular bacteria and fungi; FOXP3 Tregs suppress through IL-10, TGF-beta, IL-2 consumption, and CTLA-4.","CTLs and NK cells kill by perforin and granzymes and by Fas ligand to Fas, both producing caspase-driven apoptosis without inflammation; NK cells kill cells lacking MHC class I.","A secondary response has a shorter lag, more IgG, higher titer and affinity, and longer persistence than a primary response.","Active immunity is slow and leaves memory; passive immunity such as maternal IgG is immediate, wanes over about 6 months, and leaves no memory."],"sources":[{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Antibodies and Antigens; B Cell Activation and Antibody Production; Effector Mechanisms of Humoral Immunity"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Differentiation and Functions of CD4+ Effector T Cells; Differentiation and Functions of CD8+ Effector T Cells"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Immunology section: antibodies, cell-mediated immunity, and serologic diagnosis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System; Cell Injury, Cell Death, and Adaptations"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Resistance of the Body to Infection: II. Immunity and Allergy"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Immune System"}]},"IM-6":{"unit":"IM-6","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-06","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-06-01","title":"Cell and Cytokine Signaling in Injury and Infection","subject":"Immunology (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the innate response from first contact to systemic effect. Sentinel cells recognize microbial and damage patterns, cytokines recruit leukocytes out of venules, the liver and hypothalamus mount the acute phase response, and phagocytes ingest and kill what they find. When the stimulus cannot be cleared, macrophages wall it off in a granuloma; when cytokine release escapes local control, the result is septic or toxic shock. Board questions usually give a finding, such as a delayed umbilical cord separation, giant neutrophil granules, or a high ferritin with low serum iron, and ask for the receptor, cytokine, or defective step that explains it.","prerequisites":["Cells of innate immunity: neutrophils, monocytes and macrophages, dendritic cells, mast cells","Structure of the gram-negative and gram-positive cell envelope, including lipid A","MHC class II structure and antigen presentation to CD4 T cells (IM-4, IM-5)","Basic G protein-coupled receptor and NF-kappaB transcription factor signaling","Complement activation and the generation of C3b and C5a (IM-7)"],"connections":[{"to":"Complement (IM-7)","how":"C3b opsonizes microbes for phagocytosis and C5a is one of the strongest neutrophil chemoattractants."},{"to":"Hematology and iron metabolism","how":"Hepcidin made in the acute phase response traps iron in macrophages and produces anemia of chronic disease."},{"to":"Microbiology of gram-negative and gram-positive bacteria","how":"Lipid A drives septic shock through TLR4, while staphylococcal and streptococcal superantigens drive toxic shock through T cells."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal pathology (gout)","how":"Monosodium urate crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome, so gout flares are IL-1beta driven."},{"to":"Respiratory pathology (tuberculosis, sarcoidosis)","how":"Th1 cells and IFN-gamma build the immune granulomas that define these diseases."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pattern recognition receptor (PRR)","def":"A germline-encoded receptor of innate cells that binds a molecular pattern shared by a class of microbes or released by damaged cells."},{"term":"PAMP","def":"Pathogen-associated molecular pattern: a conserved microbial structure, such as lipid A or flagellin, that host cells do not make."},{"term":"DAMP","def":"Damage-associated molecular pattern: a host molecule, such as extracellular ATP or urate crystals, that signals cell injury when it appears outside its normal place."},{"term":"Inflammasome","def":"A cytosolic protein complex that activates caspase-1, which cleaves pro-IL-1beta and pro-IL-18 into active cytokines."},{"term":"Extravasation","def":"The ordered movement of leukocytes from the lumen of a venule into tissue through rolling, adhesion, diapedesis, and chemotaxis."},{"term":"Chemotaxis","def":"Directed movement of a cell along a concentration gradient of a chemical attractant."},{"term":"Acute phase reactant","def":"A plasma protein whose hepatic synthesis rises (positive) or falls (negative) by at least about 25% during inflammation, mainly under IL-6 control."},{"term":"Opsonin","def":"A molecule, chiefly IgG or C3b, that coats a particle and links it to receptors on phagocytes so it is ingested efficiently."},{"term":"Phagolysosome","def":"The vesicle formed when a phagosome containing an ingested particle fuses with lysosomes and granules."},{"term":"Granuloma","def":"A focal collection of activated (epithelioid) macrophages, often with multinucleated giant cells, that walls off material the body cannot eliminate."},{"term":"SIRS","def":"Systemic inflammatory response syndrome: two or more abnormalities of temperature, heart rate, respiration, or white cell count reflecting systemic cytokine action."},{"term":"Superantigen","def":"A microbial protein that cross-links MHC class II to the V-beta region of the T cell receptor outside the peptide groove, activating many T cells at once."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-06-01-C01","obj":"IM-6.1","title":"Pattern recognition: Toll-like receptors and the NLRP3 inflammasome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Innate cells cannot recognize every microbe individually, so they use pattern recognition receptors (PRRs) that bind structures shared by whole classes of microbes. These pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) are essential to the microbe and absent or hidden in the host, so a single receptor detects many organisms and rarely reacts to self. PRRs also detect damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs), host molecules that signal injury when they escape cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Toll-like receptors (TLRs) are the best known PRRs. Surface TLRs bind cell wall and surface components. Lipopolysaccharide (LPS) from gram-negative bacteria is carried in plasma by LPS-binding protein to CD14, which hands the lipid A portion to the TLR4 and MD-2 complex. TLR5 binds flagellin. The nucleic acid sensors TLR3, 7, 8, and 9 sit in endosomal membranes, where ingested microbes are broken down and their genomes exposed. Host DNA and RNA normally stay in the nucleus and cytosol and do not reach endosomes, which protects against self-reaction; when self nucleic acids do reach them, as in systemic lupus erythematosus, TLR7 and TLR9 drive autoimmunity."},{"type":"table","title":"Toll-like receptors tested most often","cols":["Receptor","Location","Ligand","Typical source"],"rows":[["TLR4 with CD14 and MD-2","Cell surface","LPS (lipid A)","Gram-negative bacteria"],["TLR2 (with TLR1 or TLR6)","Cell surface","Lipoproteins, lipoteichoic acid, zymosan","Gram-positive bacteria, mycobacteria, fungi"],["TLR5","Cell surface","Flagellin","Motile bacteria"],["TLR3","Endosome","Double-stranded RNA","Many viruses"],["TLR7 and TLR8","Endosome","Single-stranded RNA","RNA viruses"],["TLR9","Endosome","Unmethylated CpG DNA","Bacteria and DNA viruses"]],"caption":"Surface TLRs see microbial walls and surface proteins; endosomal TLRs see nucleic acids released after uptake."},{"type":"p","text":"Most TLRs signal through the adaptor MyD88 to activate NF-kappaB, which switches on genes for TNF-alpha, IL-6, chemokines such as IL-8, adhesion molecules, and the inactive precursor pro-IL-1beta. TLR3 signals through a different adaptor, TRIF, and TLR4 uses both MyD88 and TRIF. TRIF activates interferon regulatory factors, so these pathways induce type I interferons (IFN-alpha and IFN-beta), which suit an antiviral response."},{"type":"h","text":"The NLRP3 inflammasome"},{"type":"p","text":"IL-1beta is a powerful pyrogen and is not secreted in active form simply because its gene is transcribed. Release needs two signals. Signal 1, usually TLR to NF-kappaB, makes pro-IL-1beta and NLRP3 protein. Signal 2 is a DAMP or cell stress that triggers potassium efflux or lysosomal damage: extracellular ATP from dying cells, monosodium urate crystals, cholesterol crystals, silica, and some microbial toxins. NLRP3 then assembles with the adaptor ASC and pro-caspase-1, which activates itself. Caspase-1 cleaves pro-IL-1beta and pro-IL-18 into active cytokines and cleaves gasdermin D, which forms membrane pores that release IL-1beta and can kill the cell by an inflammatory lysis called pyroptosis. This explains the intense neutrophilic inflammation of gout and pseudogout, and gain-of-function NLRP3 mutations cause the cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes of recurrent fever."},{"type":"flow","title":"Two signals for active IL-1beta","steps":["Signal 1: PAMP binds TLR, MyD88 activates NF-kappaB","Transcription of pro-IL-1beta and NLRP3","Signal 2: DAMP (ATP, urate crystals) causes K+ efflux","NLRP3, ASC, and pro-caspase-1 assemble","Caspase-1 cleaves pro-IL-1beta, pro-IL-18, gasdermin D","IL-1beta released; pyroptosis possible"],"caption":"Transcription alone produces only an inactive precursor; the inflammasome supplies the second, activating step."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-06-01-C02","obj":"IM-6.2","title":"Leukocyte extravasation and leukocyte adhesion deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Leukocytes leave the blood mainly in postcapillary venules, where shear stress is low and inflammation makes the endothelium sticky. Each step uses a different pair of molecules, and local mediators switch each one on, so leukocytes exit only where injury or infection is present. In inflamed vessels, increased permeability and slower flow push leukocytes toward the vessel wall (margination)."},{"type":"p","text":"Rolling comes first. Endothelial P-selectin is stored in Weibel-Palade bodies and moves to the surface within minutes of exposure to histamine or thrombin; E-selectin is synthesized over hours after TNF-alpha and IL-1 stimulate the endothelium. Both bind sialyl Lewis X carbohydrate on leukocyte glycoproteins. Selectin bonds form and break quickly, so the cell tumbles slowly along the wall. While rolling, the leukocyte meets chemokines displayed on endothelial proteoglycans, and chemokine receptor signaling shifts its integrins from a low affinity to a high affinity conformation. The beta-2 integrins LFA-1 (CD11a/CD18) and Mac-1 (CD11b/CD18) then bind ICAM-1, which TNF-alpha and IL-1 have induced on the endothelium, and the cell stops firmly."},{"type":"p","text":"In diapedesis the cell squeezes between endothelial cells, using PECAM-1 (CD31) homophilic binding at intercellular junctions, and secretes enzymes to cross the basement membrane. In tissue it follows chemotactic gradients of bacterial N-formyl peptides, complement C5a, leukotriene B4 (LTB4), and IL-8 (CXCL8). These act on G protein-coupled receptors that polymerize actin at the leading edge. Neutrophils dominate the first 6 to 24 hours; monocytes follow over 24 to 48 hours."},{"type":"table","title":"Steps of extravasation","cols":["Step","Endothelial molecule","Leukocyte molecule","Inducer"],"rows":[["Rolling","P-selectin, E-selectin","Sialyl Lewis X on glycoproteins","Histamine, thrombin (P); TNF, IL-1 (E)"],["Firm adhesion","ICAM-1 (VCAM-1)","LFA-1, Mac-1 (CD18 integrins); VLA-4 on monocytes and lymphocytes","TNF, IL-1 on endothelium; chemokines activate integrins"],["Diapedesis","PECAM-1 (CD31)","PECAM-1 (CD31)","Junctional, constitutive"],["Chemotaxis","Chemoattractant gradient","Receptors for fMLP, C5a, LTB4, IL-8","Bacteria, complement, mast cells, macrophages"]],"caption":"A different molecular pair controls each step, so a single missing molecule halts the sequence at a predictable point."},{"type":"p","text":"Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1 (LAD-1) is an autosomal recessive defect of the ITGB2 gene encoding CD18, the shared beta-2 chain. Neutrophils roll normally but cannot adhere firmly, so they remain in the circulation. The findings follow directly: marked neutrophilia even between infections, recurrent bacterial skin and mucosal infections without pus, periodontitis, poor wound healing, and delayed separation of the umbilical cord, which needs neutrophil entry to separate. LAD-2 is a defect in fucose metabolism that removes sialyl Lewis X and blocks rolling, with similar but milder infections plus the Bombay blood phenotype and developmental delay."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A newborn's umbilical cord is still attached at 4 weeks, the WBC is 40,000/mm3 with mostly neutrophils, and an infected skin wound contains no pus. Which step of extravasation fails?","explain":"Firm adhesion fails because CD18 is missing, so LFA-1 and Mac-1 cannot bind ICAM-1. Neutrophils roll but stay in the blood, raising the count and leaving tissue without pus.","check":"Compare with the extravasation table and the LAD-1 paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-06-01-C03","obj":"IM-6.3","title":"Fever and the acute phase response","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha are the endogenous pyrogens. They act on endothelial cells of the circumventricular region near the preoptic hypothalamus (the organum vasculosum of the lamina terminalis), where the blood-brain barrier is weak, and induce cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2). The prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) produced raises the hypothalamic temperature set point. Core temperature is now below the new set point, so the body responds as if cold: cutaneous vasoconstriction, shivering, and heat-seeking behavior raise temperature until it matches the set point. When the stimulus falls, sweating and vasodilation return temperature to normal. This differs from hyperthermia, in which the set point is normal and heat gain exceeds heat loss. Blocking COX-2 removes the PGE2 signal, which is the mechanism by which aspirin and NSAIDs lower fever."},{"type":"flow","title":"How cytokines cause fever","steps":["Macrophages release IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha","Endothelium near preoptic hypothalamus induces COX-2","PGE2 raises the temperature set point","Vasoconstriction, shivering, heat conservation","Core temperature rises to the new set point"],"caption":"Fever is a regulated rise in the set point, not a failure of heat loss."},{"type":"p","text":"The acute phase response is a change in hepatic protein synthesis driven mainly by IL-6, with help from IL-1 and TNF-alpha. The liver shifts output toward proteins that opsonize, clot, and withhold iron, and away from transport proteins. Positive reactants rise; negative reactants fall, partly because amino acids and synthetic capacity are diverted and partly from increased capillary leak and catabolism."},{"type":"table","title":"Acute phase reactants","cols":["Protein","Change","Function and consequence"],"rows":[["C-reactive protein (CRP)","Up, fast and large","Pentraxin that binds phosphocholine on microbes and damaged cells, activates C1q, opsonizes"],["Fibrinogen","Up","Coagulation; coats red cells, promotes rouleaux, raises ESR"],["Serum amyloid A","Up","HDL apolipoprotein; sustained elevation can deposit as AA amyloid"],["Ferritin","Up","Stores iron inside cells, away from microbes"],["Hepcidin","Up","Degrades ferroportin; low serum iron, anemia of chronic disease"],["Albumin","Down","Lower oncotic pressure; low albumin marks chronic inflammation"],["Transferrin","Down","Low TIBC, so iron studies separate this anemia from iron deficiency"]],"caption":"IL-6 is the main hepatic signal; haptoglobin, complement components, and ceruloplasmin also rise, and transthyretin also falls."},{"type":"p","text":"CRP is the most responsive marker. It begins to rise within about 6 hours, peaks at about 48 hours, and falls quickly when inflammation resolves because its half-life is about 19 hours. The erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) changes more slowly because it depends on fibrinogen, which neutralizes the negative surface charge that normally keeps red cells apart. Hepcidin binds ferroportin, the only cellular iron exporter, causing its internalization and degradation. Enterocytes stop releasing absorbed iron and macrophages keep recycled iron, so serum iron falls while ferritin is normal or high. This withholds iron from bacteria but limits erythropoiesis."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading iron studies in inflammation","text":"A patient with active rheumatoid arthritis has a mild normocytic anemia. Serum iron is low, TIBC is low, ferritin is high, and CRP is high. Start with the cytokine: IL-6 raises hepcidin, which degrades ferroportin, so iron is trapped in macrophages (high ferritin) and cannot reach plasma (low serum iron). IL-6 also lowers transferrin, so TIBC is low. In iron deficiency, by contrast, stores are empty (low ferritin) and the liver makes more transferrin (high TIBC). The direction of TIBC and ferritin, not the serum iron, separates the two."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a fever of 39.5 °C (103.1 °F) is given a COX inhibitor. Which molecule is no longer made, and does the patient sweat or shiver as the temperature falls?","explain":"PGE2 synthesis in the hypothalamus stops, so the set point returns to normal. Core temperature is now above the set point, so the patient vasodilates and sweats until temperature falls.","check":"Compare with the fever flow figure and the first paragraph of this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-06-01-C04","obj":"IM-6.4","title":"Opsonization, phagolysosome fusion, and Chediak-Higashi syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Phagocytes bind many microbes poorly on their own, especially bacteria with polysaccharide capsules, which are hydrophilic and negatively charged and hide surface ligands. Opsonins solve this by coating the microbe with molecules that phagocyte receptors recognize. IgG, mainly the IgG1 and IgG3 subclasses, binds by its Fab ends to the microbe and presents its Fc region to Fc gamma receptors. C3b, deposited covalently on microbial surfaces by any complement pathway, binds complement receptor 1 (CR1, CD35), and its breakdown product iC3b binds CR3, which is Mac-1 (CD11b/CD18). CRP and mannose-binding lectin also act as opsonins. IgM is a poor direct opsonin because phagocytes lack an Fc receptor that mediates ingestion of IgM-coated particles, but it activates complement efficiently and so deposits C3b."},{"type":"p","text":"Engulfment forms a phagosome, which must fuse with lysosomes and neutrophil granules to become a phagolysosome. Inside it, NADPH oxidase converts oxygen to superoxide (the respiratory burst), superoxide becomes hydrogen peroxide, and myeloperoxidase from azurophilic granules converts hydrogen peroxide and chloride to hypochlorous acid. Lysosomal hydrolases, defensins, and lactoferrin add oxygen-independent killing. A defect anywhere in this sequence allows organisms to survive inside phagocytes."},{"type":"h","text":"Chediak-Higashi syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"Chediak-Higashi syndrome is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutation of LYST, the lysosomal trafficking regulator gene. LYST controls the size and movement of lysosomes and lysosome-related organelles. Without it, these organelles fuse into giant granules that move poorly and fail to empty into phagosomes, so killing is impaired. Neutrophils also show reduced chemotaxis, and many die in the marrow, causing neutropenia. Because the same trafficking defect affects every lysosome-related organelle, one mutation explains findings in several tissues."},{"type":"table","title":"Chediak-Higashi syndrome: one organelle defect, many findings","cols":["Affected organelle or cell","Finding"],"rows":[["Neutrophil granules","Giant peroxidase-positive granules on smear; recurrent pyogenic infections, mainly S aureus and streptococci"],["Melanosomes","Partial oculocutaneous albinism; silvery hair with clumped pigment"],["Platelet dense granules","Mild bleeding tendency"],["Neurons","Progressive peripheral neuropathy and later neurodegeneration"],["NK and cytotoxic T cell granules","Poor cytotoxicity; risk of a lymphohistiocytic accelerated phase"]],"caption":"The giant inclusions on a blood smear are the diagnostic clue."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-06-01-C05","obj":"IM-6.5","title":"Foreign body granulomas and immune granulomas","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A granuloma is a pattern of chronic inflammation in which activated macrophages wall off something the body cannot eliminate. Its defining cell is the epithelioid macrophage, an activated macrophage with abundant pink cytoplasm and indistinct borders that resembles squamous epithelium. Epithelioid cells may fuse into multinucleated giant cells. The stimulus decides which of two mechanisms builds the granuloma."},{"type":"p","text":"A foreign body granuloma forms around inert material too large for a single macrophage to engulf, such as suture, talc injected with crushed tablets, wood splinters, or keratin from a ruptured cyst. Macrophages gather around the material and fuse into foreign body giant cells, whose many nuclei are scattered haphazardly. No antigen-specific T cell response is required, so lymphocytes are few. The material is usually visible within or beside the giant cells, and talc and many sutures are birefringent under polarized light."},{"type":"p","text":"An immune granuloma forms around a persistent antigen that provokes a T cell response, most often an intracellular organism that survives in macrophages, such as Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Macrophages and dendritic cells present antigen on MHC class II and secrete IL-12, which drives CD4 T cells to the Th1 subset. Th1 cells secrete IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages to kill better and to become epithelioid cells and Langhans giant cells, whose nuclei form a horseshoe at the periphery. Macrophage TNF-alpha recruits cells and maintains the structure, so blocking TNF can dissolve granulomas and reactivate latent tuberculosis. A cuff of lymphocytes surrounds the core, and fibroblasts lay down a fibrous rim. This is the effector arm of type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity. Defects in the IL-12 and IFN-gamma axis prevent proper granuloma formation and cause disseminated mycobacterial infection."},{"type":"flow","title":"Building an immune granuloma","steps":["Macrophage harbors persistent antigen (M tuberculosis)","Antigen on MHC class II; IL-12 secreted","CD4 T cells become Th1 cells","Th1 IFN-gamma activates macrophages","Epithelioid cells and Langhans giant cells form","TNF-alpha maintains the wall; fibrosis surrounds it"],"caption":"Th1 cytokines, not the stimulus alone, organize the immune granuloma."},{"type":"table","title":"Foreign body versus immune granuloma","cols":["Feature","Foreign body granuloma","Immune granuloma"],"rows":[["Stimulus","Inert, nonantigenic material","Persistent antigen, often intracellular microbe"],["Driving signal","Frustrated phagocytosis; no specific T cells","Th1 IFN-gamma, maintained by TNF-alpha"],["Giant cells","Foreign body type, nuclei scattered","Langhans type, nuclei in a peripheral horseshoe"],["Lymphocyte cuff","Sparse","Prominent"],["Necrosis","Usually absent","Caseous in tuberculosis; absent in sarcoidosis"],["Clue on histology","Visible, often polarizable material","Organisms on special stains or culture in infectious types"],["Examples","Suture, talc, splinter, keratin","Tuberculosis, leprosy, histoplasmosis, sarcoidosis, Crohn disease"]],"caption":"Giant cell nuclear pattern is a supporting clue, not a rule; both giant cell types can appear in either lesion."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-06-01-C06","obj":"IM-6.6","title":"LPS, cytokines, and the mechanisms of sepsis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sepsis is a local defense turned systemic. In gram-negative bacteremia, lipid A reaches TLR4 on monocytes, macrophages, and endothelium through LPS-binding protein and CD14, and NF-kappaB signaling releases large amounts of TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6 into the blood. Gram-positive wall components (through TLR2) and fungal products act similarly. Effects that help at a single site, such as vasodilation, leaky vessels, and local clotting, become damaging when they occur in every vascular bed at once."},{"type":"flow","title":"From LPS to organ dysfunction","steps":["Lipid A binds LBP, CD14, then TLR4 and MD-2","NF-kappaB releases TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6 systemically","iNOS nitric oxide dilates arterioles: hypotension","Endothelial activation: capillary leak and edema","Tissue factor expression: DIC and microthrombi","Hypoperfusion: lactic acidosis, multiorgan dysfunction"],"caption":"Vasodilation, leak, and clotting act together to cut tissue oxygen delivery."},{"type":"p","text":"Three mechanisms explain the clinical picture. First, cytokines induce inducible nitric oxide synthase (iNOS) in vascular smooth muscle and endothelium, and the large amounts of nitric oxide relax arterioles. Systemic vascular resistance falls, causing hypotension with warm, flushed skin in early septic shock, and cardiac output rises at first. Second, activated endothelium loosens its junctions and loses its glycocalyx, so protein-rich fluid leaks into tissues, lowering intravascular volume and producing edema, including pulmonary edema of acute respiratory distress syndrome. Third, monocytes and endothelium express tissue factor, while thrombomodulin, protein C activation, and fibrinolysis are suppressed. Widespread microthrombi consume platelets and clotting factors, which is disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC): low platelets and fibrinogen, prolonged PT and PTT, raised D-dimer, and schistocytes, with bleeding and ischemia at the same time."},{"type":"p","text":"Low pressure, leak, and microthrombi reduce oxygen delivery, while cytokines also impair mitochondrial oxygen use. Cells shift to anaerobic glycolysis and lactate accumulates, causing an anion gap metabolic acidosis. Organ dysfunction follows: acute kidney injury, acute respiratory distress syndrome, hepatic dysfunction with cholestasis, myocardial depression, and confusion."},{"type":"table","title":"SIRS criteria (two or more)","cols":["Variable","Abnormal value"],"rows":[["Temperature","Above 38 °C (100.4 °F) or below 36 °C (96.8 °F)"],["Heart rate","Above 90 beats/min"],["Respiration","Above 20 breaths/min, or PaCO2 below 32 mm Hg"],["White cell count","Above 12,000/mm3, below 4,000/mm3, or more than 10% bands"]],"caption":"SIRS measures systemic cytokine effect; it is not specific for infection and also occurs after trauma, burns, and pancreatitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Each criterion reflects a cytokine effect: fever (or, in severe cases, hypothermia) from the hypothalamus, tachycardia from fever and falling resistance, tachypnea from lactic acidosis and lung injury, and leukocytosis from IL-1 and G-CSF releasing marrow neutrophils, including immature bands. Leukopenia signals neutrophil consumption and worse prognosis. Sepsis was classically defined as SIRS with suspected infection; current consensus (Sepsis-3) instead defines it as infection with organ dysfunction, but SIRS criteria remain widely taught and tested."},{"type":"example","title":"Applying SIRS criteria","text":"A patient with pyelonephritis has temperature 38.6 °C (101.5 °F), pulse 112/min, respirations 18/min, PaCO2 38 mm Hg, and WBC 9,800/mm3 with 14% bands. Check each criterion. Temperature is above 38 °C: met. Pulse is above 90: met. Respirations are 20 or below and PaCO2 is not below 32 mm Hg: not met. WBC is within 4,000 to 12,000, but bands exceed 10%: met. Three of four criteria are present, so SIRS is present. The bands matter: a normal total count does not exclude the criterion."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-06-01-C07","obj":"IM-6.7","title":"Superantigens and toxic shock syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normally a T cell is activated only when its T cell receptor (TCR) recognizes a specific peptide held in the groove of an MHC molecule. Because each peptide is recognized by very few clones, a conventional antigen activates on the order of 1 in 10,000 to 1 in 100,000 T cells. Superantigens bypass this specificity. They are not processed; instead the intact toxin binds the outside of MHC class II on antigen-presenting cells, away from the peptide groove, and simultaneously binds the variable region of the TCR beta chain (V-beta). Any T cell whose TCR uses a compatible V-beta family is activated regardless of its peptide specificity, so up to about 20% of all T cells respond at once."},{"type":"flow","title":"Superantigen cytokine storm","steps":["Toxin binds MHC class II outside the peptide groove","Same toxin binds TCR V-beta region","Polyclonal activation of up to about 20% of T cells","Massive IL-2, IFN-gamma, TNF release","Macrophages add IL-1 and TNF-alpha","Fever, rash, capillary leak, shock"],"caption":"Activation depends on V-beta family, not antigen specificity, so the response is polyclonal."},{"type":"p","text":"Activated T cells release IL-2 and IFN-gamma, and IFN-gamma activates macrophages to release TNF-alpha and IL-1. The result is a cytokine storm with the same downstream effects as gram-negative sepsis (vasodilation, capillary leak, DIC, and multiorgan dysfunction), but the trigger is T cell activation rather than TLR4. Afterward, many activated clones are deleted or become anergic, leaving a temporary state of immune hyporesponsiveness."},{"type":"p","text":"Toxic shock syndrome (TSS) is the clinical form. Staphylococcal TSS is caused mainly by toxic shock syndrome toxin-1 (TSST-1) produced by S aureus colonizing a tampon, nasal packing, or a surgical wound; staphylococcal enterotoxins are also superantigens. It presents with high fever, hypotension, a diffuse macular erythroderma like sunburn, involvement of three or more organ systems, and desquamation of the palms and soles about 1 to 2 weeks later. Blood cultures are usually negative because the toxin, not invasion, causes disease. Streptococcal TSS is caused by S pyogenes pyrogenic exotoxins (SpeA and SpeC), usually from an invasive soft tissue infection such as necrotizing fasciitis; blood cultures are more often positive and mortality is higher."},{"type":"table","title":"Two routes to cytokine shock","cols":["Feature","Gram-negative septic shock","Superantigen toxic shock"],"rows":[["Trigger","LPS (lipid A)","TSST-1, enterotoxins, SpeA, SpeC"],["Receptor","TLR4 with CD14 and MD-2 on innate cells","MHC class II plus TCR V-beta"],["First responding cell","Monocytes and macrophages","CD4 and CD8 T cells, polyclonal"],["Key early cytokines","TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6","IL-2, IFN-gamma, then TNF-alpha and IL-1"],["Distinctive feature","Gram-negative focus or bacteremia; no rash","Erythroderma, later desquamation"]],"caption":"Both converge on TNF-alpha and IL-1, so the shock physiology is similar even though the receptors differ."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a superantigen activate thousands of times more T cells than a conventional peptide antigen?","explain":"It binds MHC class II and TCR V-beta outside the peptide groove, so every T cell using a compatible V-beta family responds, regardless of which peptide its TCR recognizes.","check":"Compare with the first paragraph and the flow figure of this section."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology and immunology meet at the receptor. Lipid A, flagellin, and CpG DNA are recognized because they are conserved and essential to the microbe, while staphylococcal and streptococcal toxins exploit the structure of MHC class II and the TCR itself. The same three cytokines, IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha, link the local response to the whole body: they induce endothelial adhesion molecules, raise the hypothalamic set point through PGE2, reprogram hepatic protein synthesis, and at high concentration cause shock and DIC. Biochemistry explains the killing (NADPH oxidase and myeloperoxidase) and the iron withholding (hepcidin and ferroportin)."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology shows each step failing in a recognizable way. Loss of CD18 stops adhesion (LAD-1), loss of LYST disrupts granule trafficking (Chediak-Higashi syndrome), excess NLRP3 activity drives gout and periodic fever syndromes, and a defective IL-12 and IFN-gamma axis prevents granuloma formation. The limits matter: SIRS is nonspecific, acute phase reactants show that inflammation exists but not where or why, and giant cell morphology supports rather than proves the type of granuloma."}],"summary":["Surface TLRs detect wall components (TLR4 with CD14 and MD-2 for LPS, TLR5 for flagellin); endosomal TLR3, 7, 8, and 9 detect nucleic acids.","TLR signaling through MyD88 activates NF-kappaB for TNF-alpha, IL-6, and pro-IL-1beta; the NLRP3 inflammasome, triggered by DAMPs such as ATP and urate, activates caspase-1 to release IL-1beta.","Extravasation proceeds by selectin rolling, integrin (CD18) adhesion to ICAM-1 induced by TNF and IL-1, PECAM-1 diapedesis, and chemotaxis to C5a, LTB4, IL-8, and formyl peptides.","LAD-1 (absent CD18) causes neutrophilia, infections without pus, and delayed umbilical cord separation.","IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha cause fever by inducing hypothalamic COX-2 and PGE2, which raises the set point.","IL-6 raises CRP, fibrinogen, serum amyloid A, ferritin, and hepcidin and lowers albumin and transferrin; hepcidin causes anemia of chronic disease.","IgG (via Fc gamma receptors) and C3b (via CR1) opsonize microbes; killing requires phagolysosome fusion and the oxidative burst.","LYST mutation in Chediak-Higashi syndrome causes giant granules, partial albinism, neuropathy, bleeding, and pyogenic infections.","Foreign body granulomas surround inert material without specific T cells; immune granulomas are built by Th1 IFN-gamma and maintained by TNF-alpha.","LPS-driven cytokines cause vasodilation, capillary leak, and DIC in sepsis; superantigens cross-link MHC class II and TCR V-beta to cause polyclonal T cell activation in toxic shock syndrome."],"sources":[{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Innate immunity; Effector mechanisms of cell-mediated immunity"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Inflammation and repair; Hemodynamic disorders, thromboembolism, and shock"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Body temperature regulation and fever"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Pathogenesis of bacterial infection; Staphylococci; Streptococci"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the immune system and infectious diseases"}]},"IM-7":{"unit":"IM-7","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-07","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-07-01","title":"Complement","subject":"Immunological System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"Complement is a set of plasma and membrane proteins that marks microbes for phagocytes, recruits inflammatory cells, lyses some bacteria, and clears immune complexes and dying cells. This chapter explains how three different recognition systems all place a C3 convertase on a target surface, what each cleavage fragment does, and how host cells keep this always-active system off their own membranes. It then shows how each missing component or regulator produces a predictable disease and how serum C3 and C4 reveal which pathway is being consumed. Board questions usually give an infection pattern, an autoimmune picture, or a pair of complement levels and ask you to name the missing protein or the active pathway.","prerequisites":["Structure of IgG and IgM, including Fc regions and antibody isotypes (IM-5)","Innate recognition of microbial patterns and the acute-phase response (IM-6, IM-13)","Serine proteases, zymogen activation, and proteolytic cascades","Type III (immune complex) hypersensitivity (IM-15-C)","Red cell destruction and markers of hemolysis (HE-3)"],"connections":[{"to":"Humoral immunity (IM-5)","how":"Bound IgM and IgG start the classical pathway, and C3d on antigen lowers the threshold for B cell activation."},{"to":"Glomerular diseases (UR-12-A)","how":"Immune complex and alternative pathway glomerulonephritis are read largely through serum C3 and C4."},{"to":"Diseases of blood cell lysis (HE-9-B)","how":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria is complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis caused by missing GPI-anchored regulators."},{"to":"Infections of the CNS (NE-20-G)","how":"Terminal complement and properdin deficiencies predispose to recurrent or fulminant meningococcal disease."},{"to":"Hemostasis (HE-4)","how":"C1 esterase inhibitor also controls factor XIIa and kallikrein, linking complement to the contact system and bradykinin."}],"terms":[{"term":"Complement","def":"A group of more than 30 plasma and cell-surface proteins, mostly made by hepatocytes, that act as a proteolytic cascade to tag, attract cells to, and sometimes lyse targets."},{"term":"C3 convertase","def":"A two-part enzyme bound to a surface that cleaves C3 into C3a and C3b: C4b2a in the classical and lectin pathways, C3bBb in the alternative pathway."},{"term":"C5 convertase","def":"A C3 convertase that has bound an additional C3b and now cleaves C5, beginning assembly of the membrane attack complex."},{"term":"Tickover","def":"Slow spontaneous hydrolysis of the internal thioester bond of C3 in plasma, which keeps the alternative pathway running at a low level at all times."},{"term":"Thioester bond","def":"A reactive internal bond in C3 and C4 that, once exposed by cleavage, lets the b fragment attach covalently to a nearby surface."},{"term":"Opsonization","def":"Coating of a particle with molecules, such as C3b or IgG, that phagocyte receptors recognize, which greatly increases ingestion."},{"term":"Anaphylatoxin","def":"A small complement fragment (C5a, C3a, and weakly C4a) that triggers mast cell degranulation and increases vascular permeability."},{"term":"Membrane attack complex (MAC)","def":"The pore formed by C5b, C6, C7, C8, and polymerized C9 in a target membrane, causing osmotic lysis."},{"term":"GPI anchor","def":"A glycosylphosphatidylinositol lipid that tethers certain proteins, including CD55 and CD59, to the outer leaflet of the plasma membrane."},{"term":"Bradykinin","def":"A peptide cleaved from high molecular weight kininogen by kallikrein that increases vascular permeability and is degraded by angiotensin-converting enzyme."},{"term":"CH50","def":"A functional assay of the classical and terminal pathways that measures the serum dilution lysing 50% of antibody-coated sheep red cells."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-07-01-C01","obj":"IM-7.1","title":"Three activation pathways converging on C3 convertase","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Complement proteins circulate as inactive precursors. Activation is a cascade in which each enzyme cleaves the next component, so a small trigger produces a large, surface-focused response. The central event in every pathway is the same: a C3 convertase forms on a target surface and cleaves many C3 molecules. The three pathways differ only in how they recognize the target."},{"type":"h","text":"Classical pathway"},{"type":"p","text":"C1 is a complex of one C1q with two C1r and two C1s proteases. C1q has six globular heads, and it activates only when at least two heads bind Fc regions at the same time. Free antibody in plasma therefore does not trigger it. One IgM pentamer bound to a surface changes shape and exposes several Fc sites, so a single bound IgM is enough. IgG has one Fc per molecule, so several IgG molecules must bind close together, which is why IgM is the more efficient activator. IgG3 and IgG1 activate well, IgG2 weakly, and IgG4, IgA, IgE, and IgD do not activate the classical pathway. C1q can also bind C-reactive protein and apoptotic cells. Once bound, C1r activates C1s, which cleaves C4 and then C2. C4b attaches to the surface through its thioester, C2a joins it, and the result is the C3 convertase C4b2a."},{"type":"h","text":"Lectin pathway"},{"type":"p","text":"Mannose-binding lectin (MBL) and the related ficolins recognize repeating mannose and N-acetylglucosamine residues in the spacing found on bacterial, yeast, and some viral surfaces. Host glycoproteins usually end in sialic acid and are not bound. MBL-associated serine protease 2 (MASP-2) cleaves C4 and C2 just as C1s does, so the lectin pathway builds the same C4b2a without any antibody."},{"type":"h","text":"Alternative pathway"},{"type":"p","text":"The thioester inside plasma C3 hydrolyzes slowly and spontaneously (tickover). This altered C3 binds factor B, factor D cleaves the bound B, and a small fluid-phase convertase releases a steady trickle of C3b. Newly formed C3b bonds covalently to whatever surface is nearest or is inactivated within moments. On host cells, regulators destroy it at once. On a microbial surface that lacks these regulators, C3b binds factor B, factor D cleaves it, and C3bBb forms; properdin stabilizes it and extends its short half-life. Because every C3b deposited by any pathway can seed a new C3bBb, the alternative pathway also serves as the amplification loop for the other two."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The alternative pathway amplification loop","steps":["C3b deposits on a microbial surface","Factor B binds the C3b","Factor D cleaves bound factor B","C3bBb forms; properdin stabilizes it","C3bBb cleaves more C3","More C3b deposits nearby"],"caption":"Each turn multiplies surface C3b, so any pathway that deposits a little C3b is rapidly amplified."},{"type":"p","text":"When a C3 convertase binds one more C3b, it becomes a C5 convertase (C4b2a3b or C3bBb3b), which cleaves C5 and starts the shared terminal sequence."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparison of the three activation pathways","cols":["Feature","Classical","Lectin","Alternative"],"rows":[["Trigger","IgM or clustered IgG bound to antigen; also CRP and apoptotic cells","Microbial mannose and N-acetylglucosamine patterns","Spontaneous C3 hydrolysis; C3b survives on surfaces lacking regulators"],["Recognition molecule","C1q","MBL or ficolins","None specific; C3b itself"],["Protease that starts it","C1r, then C1s","MASP-2","Factor D (cleaves factor B)"],["Components used before C3","C1, C4, C2","MBL, MASPs, C4, C2","Factor B, factor D, properdin"],["C3 convertase","C4b2a","C4b2a","C3bBb"],["C5 convertase","C4b2a3b","C4b2a3b","C3bBb3b"]],"caption":"All three pathways converge on C3 cleavage; only the classical and lectin pathways consume C4 and C2."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does circulating IgG at a normal concentration of about 1,000 mg/dL not activate complement continuously in plasma?","explain":"C1q must bind at least two Fc regions at once. Soluble IgG monomers rarely sit that close; their Fc regions cluster only when several bind neighboring epitopes on a surface or form large immune complexes.","check":"The C1q binding requirement in the classical pathway paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-07-01-C02","obj":"IM-7.2","title":"Effector functions of complement","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cleavage of C3 and C5 splits each into a small soluble a fragment and a larger b fragment. The b fragment exposes a binding site and stays on the target, where it labels the surface. The a fragment diffuses away and signals to cells nearby. This split explains the division of labor: C3b and C5b act where complement was activated, while C3a and C5a spread inflammation into the surrounding tissue."},{"type":"h","text":"Opsonization"},{"type":"p","text":"C3b is the main complement opsonin. Neutrophils and macrophages bind it through complement receptor 1 (CR1, CD35). Factor I then cleaves surface C3b to iC3b, which binds CR3 (CD11b/CD18) and CR4 and is an even stronger signal for ingestion. C3b and IgG together are more effective than either alone. Opsonization is the main defense against encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae, whose capsules resist phagocytosis until they are coated. A further breakdown product, C3d, binds CR2 (CD21) on B cells, which signals with CD19 and lowers the antigen dose needed to activate the B cell."},{"type":"h","text":"Anaphylatoxins and chemotaxis"},{"type":"p","text":"C5a and C3a bind G protein-coupled receptors on mast cells and basophils and cause histamine release, which produces vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, and smooth muscle contraction. C5a is the most potent, and C4a is at most a weak anaphylatoxin. C5a is also the main complement chemoattractant: neutrophils and monocytes migrate up its concentration gradient, and C5a increases their integrin adhesion and oxidative burst."},{"type":"h","text":"Membrane attack complex"},{"type":"p","text":"C5b binds C6 and C7, and the C5b-7 complex becomes hydrophobic and inserts into the nearest lipid membrane. C8 then penetrates the bilayer, and C9 polymerizes into a ring that forms a pore about 10 nm across. Water and ions rush in and the cell lyses osmotically. The thin outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria is vulnerable, and Neisseria depend most on this route for their clearance; the thick peptidoglycan wall of gram-positive bacteria resists MAC insertion."},{"type":"flow","title":"Assembly of the membrane attack complex","steps":["C5 convertase cleaves C5 into C5a and C5b","C5b binds C6, then C7","C5b-7 inserts into the target membrane","C8 binds and penetrates the bilayer","Multiple C9 molecules polymerize into a pore","Water and ions enter; the cell lyses"],"caption":"CD59 on host cells blocks the C8 and C9 steps, so the pore cannot form on self membranes."},{"type":"h","text":"Clearance of immune complexes"},{"type":"p","text":"C1q, C4b, and C3b bind antigen-antibody complexes and keep them small and soluble so they do not grow into lattices that deposit in vessel walls and glomeruli. C3b-coated complexes then bind CR1 on red cells, which carry them to the liver and spleen. There, macrophages strip the complexes off and the red cells return to circulation. The same early components tag apoptotic cells for quiet removal."},{"type":"table","title":"Complement fragments, receptors, and effects","cols":["Fragment","Receptor or partner","Main effect"],"rows":[["C3b","CR1 (CD35) on phagocytes and red cells","Opsonization; immune complex transport"],["iC3b","CR3 (CD11b/CD18), CR4","Strong opsonization"],["C3d","CR2 (CD21) on B cells","Lowers B cell activation threshold"],["C3a","C3a receptor on mast cells and basophils","Anaphylatoxin, weaker than C5a"],["C5a","C5a receptor (CD88) on mast cells, neutrophils, monocytes","Strongest anaphylatoxin; chemotaxis and neutrophil activation"],["C5b to C9","Target membrane","Pore formation and osmotic lysis"]],"caption":"Bound b fragments act on the target; soluble a fragments act on nearby cells."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-07-01-C03","obj":"IM-7.3","title":"Regulators of complement, hereditary angioedema, and PNH","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Because tickover never stops and nascent C3b bonds to any nearby surface, host cells are under constant low-level attack. Regulators protect them at three checkpoints: the start of the classical and lectin pathways, the convertases, and MAC assembly. Microbes lack these host proteins, which lets complement tell self from nonself without antibody."},{"type":"table","title":"Major complement regulators","cols":["Regulator","Location","Target and action","Disease when lost"],"rows":[["C1 esterase inhibitor (C1-INH)","Plasma (serine protease inhibitor)","Binds and inactivates C1r, C1s, MASPs, kallikrein, and factor XIIa","Hereditary angioedema"],["DAF (CD55)","GPI-anchored on blood and other cells","Speeds dissociation of C3 and C5 convertases","PNH (with CD59)"],["CD59 (protectin)","GPI-anchored on blood and other cells","Binds C8 and C9; blocks C9 polymerization","PNH (with CD55)"],["Factor H","Plasma; binds sialic acid on host surfaces","Displaces Bb from C3b; factor I cofactor","Atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome"],["Factor I","Plasma protease","Cleaves C3b and C4b with a cofactor","Secondary C3 depletion; infections"]],"caption":"C1-INH acts at the start, DAF at the convertases, CD59 at the pore."},{"type":"h","text":"Hereditary angioedema"},{"type":"p","text":"Hereditary angioedema (HAE) is autosomal dominant. One defective SERPING1 allele leaves too little functional C1-INH, either because the protein is low (type I, about 85% of cases) or present but nonfunctional (type II). The swelling is not caused by complement: C1-INH is also the main inhibitor of the contact system, and when it is deficient, factor XIIa and kallikrein cleave high molecular weight kininogen and release excess bradykinin. Bradykinin acts on endothelial B2 receptors and loosens endothelial junctions, producing nonpitting edema of the deep dermis and submucosa. Mast cells are not involved, so there is no urticaria or itching. Attacks last 2 to 5 days, often after trauma or dental work; laryngeal swelling can obstruct the airway, and bowel wall edema causes colicky abdominal pain. The complement signature is separate: unopposed C1s keeps cleaving C4 and C2, so C4 is low even between attacks, while C3 stays normal."},{"type":"flow","title":"From C1-INH deficiency to angioedema","steps":["SERPING1 mutation lowers functional C1-INH","Factor XIIa and kallikrein act unchecked","Kallikrein cleaves high molecular weight kininogen","Bradykinin binds endothelial B2 receptors","Permeability rises without mast cell activation","Nonpitting swelling of face, larynx, or bowel; no hives"],"caption":"Unopposed C1s also consumes C4."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Lip and tongue swelling without hives begins after an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor is started. C4 and C1-INH are normal. What is the mediator?","explain":"Bradykinin. ACE (kininase II) normally degrades bradykinin, so blocking it raises bradykinin with C1-INH intact. The mediator matches HAE, explaining the absent urticaria, but complement is not consumed, so C4 is normal.","check":"The HAE mechanism paragraph."},{"type":"h","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria"},{"type":"p","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH) is acquired. A somatic mutation in PIGA, an X-linked gene needed for the first step of GPI anchor synthesis, arises in a hematopoietic stem cell. Each cell has only one active copy, so one mutation is enough. All descendants of that clone (red cells, granulocytes, and platelets) lack GPI-anchored proteins, including DAF and CD59. Tickover then deposits C3b on these red cells, convertases persist, and MACs lyse them inside vessels."},{"type":"flow","title":"From PIGA mutation to intravascular hemolysis","steps":["Somatic PIGA mutation in a stem cell","No GPI anchors are made","Clone's cells lack CD55 and CD59","C3 convertases persist on red cells","MAC forms unopposed","Intravascular hemolysis and hemoglobinuria"],"caption":"Missing CD59 is the main cause of lysis; missing CD55 adds C3b deposition."},{"type":"p","text":"The mechanism explains the findings. Intravascular hemolysis releases hemoglobin, producing hemoglobinuria (dark urine, classically most noticeable in the morning), high LDH, low haptoglobin, and hemosiderinuria. The direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is negative because no antibody is involved. Free hemoglobin scavenges nitric oxide, causing smooth muscle spasm (dysphagia, abdominal pain). Thrombosis in unusual sites such as the hepatic veins (Budd-Chiari syndrome) is the leading cause of death. Clones often arise in aplastic anemia, so cytopenias are common. Flow cytometry confirms red cells and granulocytes lacking CD55 and CD59."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-07-01-C04","obj":"IM-7.4","title":"Predicting disease from complement deficiencies","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Inherited deficiency of a complement protein is rare, apart from MBL and C2, and is usually autosomal recessive; properdin deficiency is X-linked. The clinical picture follows directly from which function is lost, so the rule is to ask where the missing protein sits in the cascade and what it normally does there."},{"type":"h","text":"Early classical components: C1q, C1r, C1s, C4, C2"},{"type":"p","text":"These proteins tag immune complexes and apoptotic cells for quiet removal. When they are missing, apoptotic debris carrying nuclear antigens persists, autoantibodies form against it, and immune complexes deposit in skin, joints, and kidney, producing an SLE-like disease. Risk follows the position in the cascade: about 90% of people with complete C1q deficiency develop lupus, about 75% with complete C4 deficiency, and about 10% to 30% with C2 deficiency. C2 deficiency is the most common inherited complement deficiency in people of European ancestry and also raises the risk of infection with encapsulated bacteria."},{"type":"h","text":"C3"},{"type":"p","text":"C3 is the point where all pathways meet, so its loss removes opsonization by C3b and iC3b, most C5a generation, and the MAC. From infancy, once maternal IgG wanes, children have severe recurrent pyogenic infections with encapsulated organisms such as S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, and Neisseria meningitidis. Immune complexes are also poorly solubilized, causing membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis. Deficiency of factor H or factor I produces a secondary version of the same picture, because uncontrolled alternative pathway activity uses up C3."},{"type":"h","text":"Terminal components: C5 to C9"},{"type":"p","text":"Without a working MAC, opsonization still clears most bacteria, but serum killing of Neisseria fails. Patients have recurrent meningococcal infection, often first in adolescence, often with less common serogroups, and also disseminated gonococcal infection. C9 deficiency is milder because C5b-8 alone forms small leaky channels. Properdin deficiency destabilizes C3bBb and causes fulminant meningococcal disease, mainly in males. Blocking C5 with an antibody such as eculizumab creates the same Neisseria risk, which confirms the mechanism."},{"type":"h","text":"MBL"},{"type":"p","text":"Low MBL levels from common MBL2 variants occur in about 5% to 10% of people and are usually silent, because the classical and alternative pathways compensate. Infections cluster between about 6 and 18 months of age, after maternal antibody fades and before the child's own antibody repertoire matures."},{"type":"table","title":"Complement deficiencies and screening results","cols":["Missing protein","Function lost","Main clinical result","CH50","AH50"],"rows":[["C1q, C1r, C1s, C4, C2","Clearance of immune complexes and apoptotic cells","SLE-like disease; some encapsulated infections","Absent or very low","Normal"],["C3","Opsonization, C5a, MAC","Severe pyogenic infections; immune complex glomerulonephritis","Absent","Absent"],["C5, C6, C7, C8","MAC","Recurrent Neisseria infections","Absent","Absent"],["C9","Full MAC pore","Mild or no increase in Neisseria risk","Reduced","Reduced"],["Properdin","Stable C3bBb","Fulminant meningococcal disease","Normal","Low"],["MBL","Lectin pathway","Infections in infancy and early childhood","Normal","Normal"]],"caption":"CH50 tests the classical and terminal pathways; AH50 tests the alternative and terminal pathways; MBL deficiency needs a lectin pathway assay."},{"type":"example","title":"Locating a defect with CH50 and AH50","text":"A 17-year-old has a second episode of meningococcal meningitis. CH50 and AH50 are both undetectable; C3 and C4 levels are normal. CH50 needs C1, C4, C2, C3, and C5 to C9; AH50 needs factor B, factor D, properdin, C3, and C5 to C9. Both failing points to a component they share, which is C3 or C5 to C9. A normal C3 excludes C3, leaving a terminal component, which also fits recurrent Neisseria. If only CH50 were absent, the defect would be early classical (C1, C4, or C2), and the expected picture would be lupus-like disease. If only AH50 were low, properdin, factor B, or factor D would be the suspects."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-07-01-C05","obj":"IM-7.5","title":"Interpreting serum C3 and C4","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Serum C3 and C4 are measured as protein concentrations, with typical adult reference ranges of about 90 to 180 mg/dL for C3 and about 10 to 40 mg/dL for C4; laboratories vary. A level reflects hepatic synthesis minus consumption. Both proteins are acute-phase reactants, so inflammation raises synthesis and can hide modest consumption. A low value means consumption is outpacing production."},{"type":"p","text":"Which protein falls depends on where activation enters the cascade. Classical activation by immune complexes cleaves C4 and C2 before C3, so both C4 and C3 fall. Alternative pathway activation begins at C3 and never uses C4, so C3 falls while C4 stays normal. Loss of C1-INH lets C1s cleave C4 and C2 in plasma, but the fluid-phase C4b2a that results is unstable and quickly regulated, so little C3 is cleaved and C3 stays normal."},{"type":"p","text":"In active SLE, DNA and anti-dsDNA immune complexes activate the classical pathway in the kidney and other tissues, so C3 and C4 are both low. Falling C3 and C4 with a rising anti-dsDNA titer signal a flare, especially lupus nephritis, and levels rise toward normal with remission. In poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN), which follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis by about 1 to 3 weeks or skin infection by about 3 to 6 weeks, streptococcal antigens in the glomerulus activate mainly the alternative pathway; C3 is low, C4 usually normal, and C3 returns to normal within about 6 to 8 weeks. A C3 that stays low beyond that window suggests persistent alternative pathway activation, such as C3 glomerulopathy driven by C3 nephritic factor, an autoantibody that stabilizes C3bBb. In HAE, C4 is low with normal C3, and a low C1q in addition points to the acquired form of C1-INH deficiency rather than the hereditary one."},{"type":"table","title":"Serum complement patterns","cols":["Pattern","Pathway consumed","Examples"],"rows":[["Low C3, low C4","Classical (immune complexes)","Active SLE; serum sickness; cryoglobulinemia (C4 often very low)"],["Low C3, normal C4","Alternative","PSGN; C3 glomerulopathy with C3 nephritic factor; factor H or factor I deficiency"],["Normal C3, low C4","Early classical only, without C3 cleavage","Hereditary angioedema; acquired C1-INH deficiency"],["Normal C3 and C4, absent CH50","None consumed; a component is missing","Inherited deficiency of C1q, C2, or a terminal component (C5 to C8)"]],"caption":"Classical activation consumes C4 before C3, alternative activation starts at C3, and C1-INH loss consumes C4 without reaching C3."},{"type":"example","title":"Following C3 after a sore throat","text":"A 7-year-old has cola-colored urine, periorbital edema, and blood pressure of 128/84 mm Hg two weeks after pharyngitis. C3 is 40 mg/dL and C4 is 25 mg/dL. Low C3 with normal C4 means alternative pathway consumption, which fits PSGN, and the anti-streptolysin O titer supports recent infection. Active SLE would lower both. At 12 weeks the urine has cleared but C3 is still 45 mg/dL. In PSGN the streptococcal antigen is cleared and C3 should be normal by about 8 weeks, so persistent consumption implies a continuing driver, such as C3 nephritic factor or a factor H defect, and the diagnosis needs to be reconsidered."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In HAE, C1s cleaves C4 and C2 continuously. Why does C3 not fall as well?","explain":"The C4b2a formed in plasma is not anchored to a protected surface; C2a dissociates quickly, and factor I with a plasma cofactor inactivates the C4b. Little C3 is cleaved, so C3 stays normal while C4 is used up.","check":"The second paragraph of this section and the regulator table in C03."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry and microbiology explain how complement works and how pathogens escape it. The cascade runs on serine proteases held as zymogens, the reactive thioester of C3 and C4 lets fragments bond covalently to targets, and GPI anchor synthesis explains why one PIGA mutation removes two regulators at once. Successful pathogens exploit the same self-recognition rules: polysaccharide capsules hinder C3b deposition and access to phagocyte receptors, and Neisseria meningitidis and Streptococcus pyogenes bind factor H to their own surfaces. This is why encapsulated organisms dominate the infections of C3 and antibody deficiency and Neisseria dominates those of terminal deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and anatomy explain where complement causes harm. Immune complex disease (type III hypersensitivity) damages glomeruli, joints, and skin through local C5a recruitment of neutrophils, while uncontrolled alternative pathway activity on endothelium underlies atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome. The liver makes most complement proteins, and the spleen and liver macrophages remove opsonized bacteria and immune complexes, so severe liver disease and asplenia weaken the same defenses. Serum levels are a blunt tool: they reflect the balance of synthesis and consumption, rise in acute inflammation, and miss local activation, so they are read alongside the clinical picture and repeated over time."}],"summary":["All three pathways build a surface C3 convertase: C4b2a in the classical (C1q binding one IgM or clustered IgG) and lectin (MBL with MASP-2) pathways, and C3bBb, stabilized by properdin, in the alternative pathway.","Alternative pathway tickover is constant and nonspecific; host regulators, absent from microbes, provide self versus nonself discrimination and the C3bBb loop amplifies every pathway.","Bound b fragments act on the target (C3b and iC3b opsonize, C5b starts the MAC); soluble a fragments act on cells nearby (C3a and C5a are anaphylatoxins, and C5a recruits neutrophils).","C3b keeps immune complexes soluble and lets red cell CR1 carry them to the liver and spleen; early classical components also clear apoptotic cells.","C1-INH deficiency (autosomal dominant HAE) raises bradykinin through unchecked kallikrein, giving nonpitting swelling without hives, low C4, and normal C3.","PNH is an acquired PIGA mutation that removes GPI-anchored CD55 and CD59, causing Coombs-negative intravascular hemolysis, thrombosis, and cytopenias.","Early classical deficiency (C1q, C4, C2) causes SLE-like disease; C3 deficiency causes severe pyogenic infections and immune complex glomerulonephritis; C5 to C9 or properdin deficiency causes Neisseria infection; MBL deficiency causes infections in early childhood.","CH50 and AH50 localize a missing component: both absent means C3 or a terminal component, CH50 alone means early classical, AH50 alone means alternative pathway factors.","Low C3 and C4 indicate classical consumption (active SLE); low C3 with normal C4 indicates alternative consumption (PSGN, normal again by about 8 weeks); low C4 with normal C3 indicates C1-INH deficiency."],"sources":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Effector Mechanisms of Humoral Immunity"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Innate Immunity, section on the complement system"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Inflammation and Repair, section on the complement system"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders, section on paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria; The Kidney, section on poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Immunology section on complement"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Immune System"}]},"IM-8":{"unit":"IM-8","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-08","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-08-01","title":"Lymphatic Organ Function","subject":"Immune System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains what the thymus, spleen, and lymph nodes do. The thymus builds a T cell repertoire that recognizes self MHC but tolerates self antigen. The spleen filters blood, removing worn red cells and encapsulated bacteria. Lymph nodes bring rare naive lymphocytes together with antigen carried in from the tissues. Board questions rarely ask for these functions directly; instead they describe a patient whose organ is missing, failing, or suppressed by cortisol and ask you to predict the smear, the blood count, or the infection. Each prediction follows from a lost normal function.","prerequisites":["MHC class I and class II structure and antigen presentation to CD8 and CD4 T cells","T cell receptor gene rearrangement by RAG1 and RAG2 (V(D)J recombination)","Histology of the thymus, spleen, and lymph node (IM-2)","Complement activation and opsonization by C3b (IM-7)","Hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis and cortisol secretion"],"connections":[{"to":"Thymus embryology (IM-1)","how":"Thymic epithelium comes from the third pharyngeal pouch, so its failure in 22q11.2 deletion removes the site where T cells mature."},{"to":"Congenital immunodeficiency diseases (IM-15-A)","how":"Defects in RAG, MHC expression, or AIRE each stop thymic maturation or selection at a predictable step."},{"to":"Complement (IM-7)","how":"Splenic clearance of encapsulated bacteria depends on C3b and IgM opsonization, so complement defects and asplenia produce the same infections."},{"to":"Hematology and sickle cell disease","how":"Red pulp filtering explains why splenectomy and sickle cell autosplenectomy change the blood smear."},{"to":"Diseases of endocrine hyperfunction (EN-12-A)","how":"Cushing syndrome changes the white cell differential through glucocorticoid effects on leukocyte adhesion, survival, and distribution."}],"terms":[{"term":"Thymocyte","def":"A developing T cell inside the thymus, named by its CD4 and CD8 expression as double negative, double positive, or single positive."},{"term":"Positive selection","def":"Survival of thymocytes whose T cell receptor binds self MHC on cortical epithelial cells with low to moderate affinity."},{"term":"Negative selection","def":"Deletion, or diversion to regulatory T cells, of thymocytes whose receptor binds self peptide and MHC with high affinity."},{"term":"AIRE","def":"Autoimmune regulator, a transcriptional regulator in medullary thymic epithelial cells that switches on genes for tissue-restricted self proteins."},{"term":"Red pulp","def":"The splenic compartment of cords and venous sinuses where blood is filtered and old or damaged red cells are removed."},{"term":"Marginal zone","def":"The border between splenic white pulp and red pulp where blood-borne antigen is first sampled by specialized macrophages and B cells."},{"term":"Pitting","def":"Removal of a rigid inclusion from a red cell by splenic macrophages, returning the red cell to the circulation."},{"term":"Functional asplenia","def":"Loss of splenic filtering and immune function while splenic tissue is still present, as in early sickle cell disease."},{"term":"High endothelial venule (HEV)","def":"A postcapillary venule in lymphoid tissue lined by cuboidal endothelium that allows naive lymphocytes to enter the organ from blood."},{"term":"Homing","def":"The directed migration of lymphocytes to a particular tissue, set by the adhesion molecules and chemokine receptors they express."},{"term":"Margination","def":"The pool of neutrophils loosely attached to vessel walls, roughly equal in size to the freely circulating pool and not counted on a blood draw."},{"term":"Demargination","def":"Release of marginated neutrophils into the circulating pool, which raises the measured neutrophil count without new production."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-08-01-C01","obj":"IM-8.1","title":"Thymic T cell maturation and selection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thymus solves two problems created by random T cell receptor (TCR) gene rearrangement. Most new receptors cannot recognize peptide on self MHC at all, and some recognize self peptide strongly enough to cause autoimmunity. The thymus tests every thymocyte for both and keeps only cells that pass, so only a few percent of thymocytes leave as mature T cells."},{"type":"p","text":"The scaffold is epithelial. Cortical thymic epithelial cells (cTECs) and medullary thymic epithelial cells (mTECs) derive from third pharyngeal pouch endoderm, while thymocytes come from bone marrow precursors that enter through venules near the corticomedullary junction. The cortex is dense with immature thymocytes; the paler medulla holds mature thymocytes, dendritic cells, and Hassall corpuscles."},{"type":"flow","title":"A thymocyte's route from entry to exit","steps":["Marrow precursor enters at the corticomedullary junction","Double negative (CD4 and CD8 absent) in outer cortex","TCR beta rearranges; pre-TCR signals survival and proliferation","Cell becomes double positive; TCR alpha rearranges","Positive selection on cTEC self MHC in the cortex","Single positive cell migrates to the medulla (CCR7)","Negative selection on mTECs and dendritic cells","Naive CD4 or CD8 T cell exits to blood"],"caption":"Location tracks maturity: cortex for receptor assembly and positive selection, medulla for tolerance testing."},{"type":"h","text":"Positive selection: can the receptor see self MHC?"},{"type":"p","text":"Double positive thymocytes sample peptide and MHC presented by cTECs. A receptor that binds with low to moderate affinity delivers a survival signal; the large majority bind too weakly and die by neglect, a quiet apoptosis from absent signaling. This step creates MHC restriction, meaning mature T cells recognize antigen only on self MHC. It also fixes lineage: a cell whose receptor engages MHC class I keeps CD8, which binds class I, and one engaging class II keeps CD4."},{"type":"h","text":"Negative selection: does the receptor attack self?"},{"type":"p","text":"Single positive thymocytes move into the medulla, where mTECs and dendritic cells present self peptides. High-affinity binding triggers apoptosis (clonal deletion), and some cells with intermediate to high affinity become FOXP3 positive regulatory T cells instead. Ubiquitous proteins are easy to display, but insulin, thyroglobulin, and other organ-restricted proteins would never be seen without AIRE, which drives mTECs to transcribe thousands of tissue-restricted genes. Negative selection is incomplete, so peripheral tolerance mechanisms remain necessary."},{"type":"table","title":"Where thymic development fails","cols":["Defect","Step lost","Result"],"rows":[["Thymic aplasia (22q11.2 deletion)","No epithelial scaffold","Few or no T cells; B cell numbers preserved"],["RAG1 or RAG2 loss","No TCR (or BCR) rearrangement","Arrest at double negative stage; T and B cells absent"],["MHC class II deficiency","No class II for positive selection","Few CD4 T cells; CD8 present"],["TAP deficiency (MHC class I low)","Little class I for positive selection","Few CD8 T cells; CD4 present"],["AIRE mutation","Incomplete negative selection","Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1"]],"caption":"Each defect removes one requirement of the pathway in the flow above, so the missing T cell subset predicts the lesion."},{"type":"p","text":"AIRE failure shows how deletion protects specific organs. Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1 classically combines hypoparathyroidism, primary adrenal insufficiency, and chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, the last linked to neutralizing autoantibodies against IL-17 family cytokines and IL-22 that weaken mucosal antifungal defense. The thymus is largest relative to body size in infancy and involutes after puberty as fat replaces cortex, though output continues at a low level into adulthood."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child lacks MHC class II on all antigen-presenting cells. Will the child have CD4 T cells, CD8 T cells, or both?","explain":"CD8 T cells only. Positive selection to the CD4 lineage requires the receptor to engage class II on cTECs, so CD4 cells die by neglect, while class I selection proceeds normally.","check":"Positive selection and the failure table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-08-01-C02","obj":"IM-8.2","title":"Splenic functions: filtering blood and defending against encapsulated bacteria","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The spleen is the only lymphoid organ placed in the bloodstream rather than the lymph stream. It receives about 5% of cardiac output through the splenic artery, which branches into central arterioles inside white pulp and then empties into red pulp. Its two compartments do different jobs: red pulp filters red cells, and white pulp with its marginal zone mounts immune responses to blood-borne antigen."},{"type":"h","text":"Red pulp: quality control of red cells"},{"type":"p","text":"Much of the arterial blood enters an open circulation, pouring into the splenic cords (cords of Billroth), a meshwork of reticular fibers and macrophages. To return to the venous side, each red cell must squeeze through slits a few micrometers wide between the elongated endothelial cells of the venous sinuses. A young red cell, about 7.5 to 8 micrometers across, deforms and passes. A cell that has lost membrane and flexibility near the end of its roughly 120 day life, or that carries a rigid inclusion, is held in the cords, where flow is slow and the setting is hypoxic and acidic."},{"type":"flow","title":"Red cell passage through the red pulp","steps":["Central arteriole empties into the splenic cords","Red cell moves slowly past cord macrophages","Cell must deform through narrow sinus wall slits","Flexible cell passes into the venous sinus","Rigid or aged cell held back and phagocytosed","Cell with inclusion is pitted and returned"],"caption":"Deformability is the test. Hemoglobin iron from destroyed cells is recycled; heme becomes bilirubin."},{"type":"p","text":"Pitting removes Howell-Jolly bodies (nuclear DNA remnants), Heinz bodies (denatured hemoglobin), and Pappenheimer bodies (iron granules) without destroying the cell. Splenic macrophages also trim excess membrane from reticulocytes as they mature."},{"type":"h","text":"White pulp and marginal zone: immune clearance"},{"type":"p","text":"White pulp contains the periarteriolar lymphoid sheath (PALS) of T cells around central arterioles and B cell follicles beside it. The surrounding marginal zone receives blood first. Polysaccharide capsules (on S pneumoniae, H influenzae type b, and N meningitidis) block complement deposition and phagocyte binding. Marginal zone B cells answer them quickly with T cell-independent IgM, because polysaccharides cannot be presented to helper T cells. IgM activates complement, coating the organism with C3b, and splenic macrophages, which bind C3b and the Fc portion of IgG, remove the opsonized bacteria from slow-moving blood. The liver clears well opsonized organisms efficiently, but the spleen is the main site for poorly opsonized ones and for the first IgM response."},{"type":"table","title":"Splenic jobs by compartment","cols":["Function","Compartment and cells","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Remove aged and rigid red cells","Red pulp cords, macrophages","Failure to deform through sinus slits, then phagocytosis"],["Pit inclusions and remodel membrane","Red pulp macrophages","Inclusion or excess membrane removed; cell returned"],["Clear opsonized bacteria","Marginal zone and red pulp macrophages","Receptors for C3b and the Fc of IgG"],["Rapid IgM to polysaccharide","Marginal zone B cells","T cell-independent activation"],["Adaptive responses to blood antigen","PALS (T cells), follicles (B cells)","Germinal centers, IgG class switching"],["Platelet pool","Red pulp","About one third of total platelets held in exchange with blood"]],"caption":"Most rows reappear in C03 as a finding when the function is lost."},{"type":"p","text":"The platelet pool is exchangeable, not a store of inactive cells. When the spleen enlarges, it can trap a much larger fraction, which explains thrombocytopenia in hypersplenism. The fetal spleen also makes blood cells, and that extramedullary hematopoiesis can return in adults with marrow failure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-08-01-C03","obj":"IM-8.3","title":"Findings after splenectomy and in functional asplenia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Asplenia means absent splenic function, whether the organ was removed (after trauma, for example), never formed (congenital asplenia), or is present but not working (functional asplenia). The findings are the same because each is a lost job from C02, so they can be predicted rather than memorized."},{"type":"table","title":"Each finding traced to a lost splenic function","cols":["Finding","Lost function","Explanation"],"rows":[["Howell-Jolly bodies","Pitting","Nuclear remnants stay in circulating red cells; the most reliable smear sign of asplenia"],["Pappenheimer and Heinz bodies","Pitting","Iron granules and denatured hemoglobin are no longer removed"],["Target cells (codocytes)","Membrane remodeling","Excess membrane relative to cell volume bulges centrally"],["Acanthocytes and other irregular forms","Removal of misshapen cells","Abnormal cells that would be culled keep circulating"],["Thrombocytosis","Platelet pool","Platelets formerly pooled in the spleen stay in the blood"],["Mild leukocytosis, often lymphocytosis","Sequestration and clearance","Cells normally held or cleared in the spleen remain in blood"],["Sepsis from encapsulated bacteria","Marginal zone IgM and macrophage clearance","Unopsonized organisms multiply in blood unchecked"]],"caption":"Smear changes appear after any cause of asplenia and persist; they confirm loss of function even when a spleen is visible on imaging."},{"type":"p","text":"Platelets rise within days of splenectomy and commonly peak over the next few weeks, often above the upper reference limit of about 400 x 10^9/L (reference about 150 to 400 x 10^9/L), then settle partly. Bone marrow production is not the main change; the circulating pool simply gains the cells the spleen used to hold."},{"type":"h","text":"Overwhelming infection"},{"type":"p","text":"The serious consequence is overwhelming post-splenectomy infection: bacteremia that can progress to septic shock and disseminated intravascular coagulation within hours. S pneumoniae causes most cases, followed by H influenzae type b and N meningitidis. Without the spleen, no organ supplies rapid T cell-independent IgM against capsules or efficiently clears organisms that carry little antibody or C3b, so bacteria multiply in blood before an adaptive response develops. Risk is lifelong and highest in young children, whose marginal zone B cells respond poorly to polysaccharides before about age 2. Asplenia also worsens infection with intraerythrocytic parasites (Babesia, Plasmodium), which the spleen normally pits or removes, and with Capnocytophaga canimorsus after dog bites."},{"type":"h","text":"Sickle cell disease: functional asplenia, then autosplenectomy"},{"type":"flow","title":"From sickling to autosplenectomy","steps":["Slow, hypoxic, acidic flow in splenic cords","Deoxygenated HbS polymerizes; red cells sickle","Sickled cells block cords and sinuses","Filtering fails: functional asplenia in infancy","Repeated infarcts cause fibrosis and shrinkage","Small fibrotic remnant: autosplenectomy in early childhood"],"caption":"The spleen fails before it disappears, so Howell-Jolly bodies and infection risk appear while it is still enlarged."},{"type":"p","text":"The red pulp is the most favorable place in the body for HbS polymerization because flow is slow and oxygen tension and pH are low. Children with sickle cell anemia lose splenic function in the first years of life, and the organ becomes a small fibrotic, calcified remnant, usually by about age 5. Before fibrosis, the enlarged spleen can suddenly trap a large volume of blood (splenic sequestration crisis). Hemoglobin SC disease and sickle beta+ thalassemia often keep splenomegaly into later life."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a smear after splenic injury","text":"A 24-year-old had a splenectomy after a motorcycle crash 3 weeks ago. Platelets are 720 x 10^9/L and the smear shows Howell-Jolly bodies and target cells. Start with the platelets: above 400 x 10^9/L, but expected, because the splenic pool now circulates; this is not a marrow disorder. Howell-Jolly bodies mean no pitting, and target cells mean no membrane trimming, so splenic function is absent. The same smear in a 10-year-old with sickle cell anemia means lost splenic function from autosplenectomy. In both, the important prediction is risk of rapid sepsis from S pneumoniae, Hib, or N meningitidis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-08-01-C04","obj":"IM-8.4","title":"Lymphocyte homing and antigen encounter in lymph nodes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A naive T cell specific for one epitope may be one in 10^5 to 10^6 cells, while antigen may enter through any patch of skin or mucosa. Lymph nodes solve this search problem by collecting antigen from a tissue region and drawing naive lymphocytes from the blood into the same small space. Naive cells recirculate, spending hours to about a day in each node before moving on."},{"type":"h","text":"Entry from blood through high endothelial venules"},{"type":"p","text":"High endothelial venules (HEVs) sit in the paracortex, the T cell zone. Their tall endothelium displays peripheral node addressin (PNAd), a group of sulfated sialomucins, and presents the chemokines CCL19 and CCL21. Naive lymphocytes enter through a multistep adhesion cascade."},{"type":"flow","title":"Naive lymphocyte entry at the HEV","steps":["L-selectin (CD62L) on lymphocyte binds PNAd; cell rolls","CCL19 and CCL21 on HEV engage CCR7","Gi signaling switches LFA-1 to high affinity","LFA-1 binds ICAM-1; cell arrests firmly","Cell crosses the HEV into the paracortex","T cells stay in paracortex; B cells follow CXCL13 to follicles"],"caption":"Rolling, chemokine activation, firm arrest, and transmigration: the same logic as neutrophil recruitment, with different molecules."},{"type":"h","text":"Antigen arrives through afferent lymph"},{"type":"p","text":"Afferent lymphatics enter the convex surface and empty into the subcapsular sinus, then lymph passes through cortical and medullary sinuses to the efferent lymphatic at the hilum. Tissue dendritic cells that capture antigen and sense microbial products mature: they raise MHC and costimulatory molecules (B7) and switch on CCR7, which carries them along CCL21 on lymphatic endothelium into the paracortex. CCR7 thus brings both antigen-bearing dendritic cells and naive T cells to the same zone. Small soluble antigens travel through reticular conduits, while subcapsular sinus macrophages and follicular dendritic cells present intact antigen to B cells."},{"type":"table","title":"Molecules that direct lymphocyte traffic","cols":["Molecule","Cell","Partner and role"],"rows":[["L-selectin (CD62L)","Naive and central memory lymphocytes","PNAd on HEV; rolling"],["CCR7","Naive T and B cells, mature dendritic cells","CCL19 and CCL21; entry and positioning in T zone"],["LFA-1 (integrin)","Lymphocytes","ICAM-1; firm arrest"],["CXCR5","B cells, follicular helper T cells","CXCL13 from follicular stromal cells; follicle entry"],["Alpha4 beta7 integrin","Gut-homing lymphocytes","MAdCAM-1 on Peyer patch and gut venules"],["S1P receptor 1 (S1PR1)","Naive lymphocytes","S1P in lymph and blood; exit"],["CD69","Recently activated lymphocytes","Internalizes S1PR1; retention"]],"caption":"The receptors a lymphocyte expresses decide which tissue it can enter and when it leaves."},{"type":"h","text":"Exit and what changes after activation"},{"type":"p","text":"Sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) is high in lymph and blood and low within the node. Naive lymphocytes that meet no antigen regain S1PR1 and follow the gradient into the medullary sinuses and efferent lymph, which returns them to blood through the thoracic duct or right lymphatic duct. A T cell that recognizes antigen expresses CD69, which removes S1PR1 from the surface, so it stays for several days to proliferate. The resulting effector T cells lose L-selectin and CCR7 and gain receptors for inflamed endothelium, so they home to tissue rather than nodes. Drugs that internalize S1PR1 trap lymphocytes in nodes, producing lymphopenia without destroying cells."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A mouse lacks CCR7. What happens to naive T cells and to skin dendritic cells after a skin infection?","explain":"Naive T cells cannot arrest at HEVs or settle in the paracortex, and activated dendritic cells cannot travel through afferent lymphatics, so the two rarely meet and priming is weak and delayed.","check":"The HEV flow and the afferent lymph paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-08-01-C05","obj":"IM-8.5","title":"Cortisol effects on the blood count and lymphoid organs","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cortisol and synthetic glucocorticoids diffuse into leukocytes and bind the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor, which moves to the nucleus and changes transcription. Two mechanisms matter: it represses inflammatory gene programs, partly by interfering with NF-kappa B and AP-1, and it changes adhesion, survival, and distribution of each white cell type. The blood count shows the distribution changes within hours of a cortisol surge or a large glucocorticoid dose."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a cortisol surge reshapes the differential","steps":["Stress, Cushing syndrome, or glucocorticoid dose raises cortisol","Glucocorticoid receptor alters leukocyte gene transcription","Neutrophil adhesion falls; marginated cells enter circulation","Marrow releases mature neutrophils; tissue emigration slows","Lymphocytes redistribute to marrow and lymphoid tissue","Eosinophils, lymphocytes, and thymocytes undergo apoptosis","Neutrophilia with lymphopenia, eosinopenia, and monocytopenia"],"caption":"Single doses produce the largest shift at about 4 to 6 hours, with return toward baseline by about 24 hours."},{"type":"h","text":"Why neutrophils rise"},{"type":"p","text":"Roughly half the neutrophils within vessels are marginated, loosely rolling along venule walls, and are not sampled by venipuncture. Glucocorticoids reduce L-selectin on neutrophils and adhesion molecules on endothelium, so these cells join the circulating pool and the measured count rises without new production. Release of mature neutrophils from the marrow reserve, slower emigration into tissue, and delayed neutrophil apoptosis add to the rise. Because the extra cells are mature, there is no significant left shift (bands) or toxic granulation, which distinguishes this from bacterial infection. Epinephrine also demarginates neutrophils, but within minutes, so acute stress raises the count before cortisol acts."},{"type":"h","text":"Why lymphocytes and eosinophils fall"},{"type":"p","text":"Lymphocytes, especially T cells, leave the blood for bone marrow and lymphoid tissue, and many undergo glucocorticoid-induced apoptosis. Eosinophils are redistributed and die by apoptosis, so eosinopenia is one of the most consistent findings. Monocytes and basophils also fall. Double positive cortical thymocytes are exquisitely sensitive to glucocorticoid-induced apoptosis, so sustained cortisol excess causes rapid thymic involution, most visible in the cortex, along with shrinkage of lymph nodes and splenic white pulp. Thymic and lymphoid atrophy, adrenal enlargement, and gastric ulcers form the classic stress triad described in experimental animals. These changes, together with suppressed cytokine production and neutrophil emigration, explain the increased infection risk in Cushing syndrome."},{"type":"table","title":"Cortisol excess compared with cortisol deficiency","cols":["Cell or organ","Cortisol excess","Cortisol deficiency (primary adrenal insufficiency)"],"rows":[["Neutrophils","Increased (demargination, marrow release)","Normal or mildly decreased"],["Lymphocytes","Decreased (redistribution, apoptosis)","Relative lymphocytosis"],["Eosinophils","Decreased, often near zero","Eosinophilia"],["Monocytes and basophils","Decreased","Little change"],["Thymus and lymphoid tissue","Atrophy","May enlarge"]],"caption":"Eosinophilia in a hypotensive, stressed patient suggests cortisol is not responding as it should."},{"type":"example","title":"Absolute counts in cortisol excess","text":"A woman with Cushing syndrome has WBC 13.5 x 10^9/L: neutrophils 89%, lymphocytes 7%, monocytes 4%, eosinophils 0%. Convert to absolute counts by multiplying WBC by each fraction. Neutrophils are 12.0 x 10^9/L (reference about 1.8 to 7.7), lymphocytes 0.95 x 10^9/L (reference about 1.0 to 4.8), monocytes 0.54 x 10^9/L, and eosinophils zero. The WBC is high entirely from neutrophils, while lymphocytes and eosinophils are low. With no bands or toxic granulation, the pattern fits demargination and redistribution by cortisol rather than bacterial infection."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does the neutrophil count rise within hours of a glucocorticoid dose when marrow production takes days to increase?","explain":"Most of the early rise comes from cells already formed: marginated neutrophils released into the circulating pool and mature cells released from the marrow reserve, with fewer leaving the blood for tissue.","check":"Why neutrophils rise."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and histology set the stage: thymic epithelium from the third pharyngeal pouch provides the MHC-displaying scaffold for selection, the spleen's open red pulp circulation makes it a filter, and HEVs and lymphatic sinuses give the lymph node its two inputs. Immunology runs on that architecture, and pathology follows from removing a piece. Loss of thymic epithelium, MHC, RAG, or AIRE stops maturation or tolerance at a predictable step; loss of the spleen, by surgery or sickle cell infarction, removes pitting, the platelet pool, and the marginal zone defense against capsules."},{"type":"p","text":"Endocrinology links back through cortisol, which shifts leukocytes between pools and kills susceptible lymphocytes and thymocytes, so a blood count can reflect adrenal status. The limits are important: a smear shows loss of filtering but not its cause, neutrophilia does not distinguish stress from early infection without the rest of the picture, and the reference ranges and timing used here vary between laboratories and sources."}],"summary":["Thymocytes rearrange TCR beta at the double negative stage, then TCR alpha as double positive cells in the cortex.","Positive selection on cortical epithelial MHC keeps receptors that bind self MHC with low to moderate affinity and sets CD4 (class II) or CD8 (class I) lineage; most thymocytes die by neglect.","Negative selection in the medulla deletes strongly self-reactive cells or diverts them to FOXP3 regulatory T cells; AIRE supplies tissue-restricted antigens, and its loss causes autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1.","Splenic red pulp removes red cells that cannot deform through sinus slits and pits inclusions; the marginal zone makes T cell-independent IgM, and macrophages clear C3b and IgG coated encapsulated bacteria.","The spleen holds about one third of platelets in exchange with blood.","Asplenia shows Howell-Jolly bodies, target cells, and thrombocytosis, and risks overwhelming sepsis from S pneumoniae, H influenzae type b, and N meningitidis.","Sickle cell disease causes functional asplenia in infancy and autosplenectomy by early childhood through sickling and infarction in the slow, hypoxic red pulp.","Naive lymphocytes enter nodes through paracortical HEVs by L-selectin rolling, CCR7 activation, and LFA-1 arrest; CCR7 also brings antigen-bearing dendritic cells in through afferent lymph.","Lymphocytes leave through efferent lymph by following S1P; CD69 retains activated cells, and effector cells lose L-selectin and CCR7.","Cortisol causes neutrophilia from demargination and marrow release without left shift, plus lymphopenia, eosinopenia, monocytopenia, and thymic atrophy."],"sources":[{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Lymphocyte development and antigen receptor gene rearrangement; Cells and tissues of the immune system"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Leukocyte circulation and migration into tissues; Immunologic tolerance and autoimmunity"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Immune System and Lymphoid Organs"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Adrenocortical Hormones, section on anti-inflammatory and immune effects of cortisol"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck, pharyngeal pouch derivatives"}]},"IM-9":{"unit":"IM-9","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-09-01","title":"Lymph Composition and Transport","subject":"Immunology and Lymphatic System (IMMU)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains where lymph comes from, what it carries, how it is pushed back to the veins, and what happens when that drainage fails. Lymph begins as the small excess of fluid that Starling forces leave in the interstitium, and it is the only route by which filtered plasma protein returns to the blood. Board questions usually describe a swollen limb, a milky effusion, or a fetus with a neck mass and ask you to identify the failed step. If you can reason from Starling forces to interstitial protein to lymph flow, you can separate lymphedema from other edemas and explain why it scars.","prerequisites":["Capillary structure: continuous, fenestrated, and sinusoidal endothelium","Body fluid compartments and the composition of plasma and interstitial fluid","Hydrostatic and oncotic (colloid osmotic) pressure","Lymph node structure and lymphocyte recirculation (IM-2, IM-8)","Fat digestion and chylomicron assembly in enterocytes"],"connections":[{"to":"Capillary fluid exchange (CV-11)","how":"The Starling forces that set capillary filtration determine how much fluid the lymphatics must remove."},{"to":"Edema and heart failure (CV-18-B, CV-18-H)","how":"Comparing the protein content of edema fluid separates lymphatic failure from high venous pressure or low albumin."},{"to":"Lymphatic drainage and lymph nodes (IM-4, IM-8)","how":"Afferent lymph delivers antigen and dendritic cells to nodes, and efferent lymph returns recirculating lymphocytes to the blood."},{"to":"Lymph biochemistry (IM-11)","how":"Lacteals carry chylomicrons, so absorbed long-chain fat reaches the blood through the thoracic duct rather than the portal vein."},{"to":"Medical genetics and parasitology","how":"Milroy disease, Turner syndrome, and Wuchereria bancrofti each cause lymphedema by a different lesion in the same drainage pathway."}],"terms":[{"term":"Starling forces","def":"The hydrostatic and oncotic pressures inside and outside a capillary whose balance determines the direction and rate of fluid movement across its wall."},{"term":"Filtration coefficient (Kf)","def":"The product of capillary wall hydraulic conductance and surface area, which converts net filtration pressure into a flow of fluid."},{"term":"Reflection coefficient (sigma)","def":"A number from 0 to 1 describing how well the capillary wall holds back protein; 1 means protein cannot cross and its full oncotic pressure is effective."},{"term":"Interstitial fluid pressure","def":"The hydrostatic pressure of free fluid in the tissue spaces, normally slightly negative (about -3 mmHg) in loose subcutaneous tissue."},{"term":"Lymph","def":"Interstitial fluid that has entered lymphatic vessels, carrying filtered protein, cells, and in the intestine absorbed fat."},{"term":"Chyle","def":"Milky intestinal lymph rich in chylomicrons, formed in the lacteals after a fatty meal."},{"term":"Anchoring filaments","def":"Fine fibers that tether lymphatic capillary endothelium to the surrounding matrix and pull the vessel open as tissue swells."},{"term":"Lymphangion","def":"The segment of a collecting lymphatic between two one-way valves, which acts as a small contractile pump."},{"term":"Safety factor against edema","def":"A mechanism that opposes interstitial fluid accumulation, such as rising lymph flow or washout of interstitial protein."},{"term":"Lymphedema","def":"Protein-rich interstitial swelling caused by failure of lymphatic drainage rather than by excess filtration."},{"term":"Cystic hygroma","def":"A fluid-filled lymphatic malformation, classically in the posterior neck of a fetus, caused by failed connection of lymph sacs to the veins."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-09-01-C01","obj":"IM-9.1","title":"Lymph formation from net capillary filtration","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fluid crosses a capillary wall by bulk flow, driven by two pairs of opposing pressures called Starling forces. Capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc) pushes fluid out, and interstitial oncotic pressure (pi i), created by protein in the tissue fluid, pulls fluid out. Plasma oncotic pressure (pi c), created mainly by albumin, pulls fluid back in. Interstitial hydrostatic pressure (Pi) opposes filtration only when it is positive; in loose tissue it is normally slightly negative, so it adds a small outward pull."},{"type":"p","text":"The Starling equation combines these forces: filtration rate equals Kf multiplied by [(Pc minus Pi) minus sigma (pi c minus pi i)]. Kf, the filtration coefficient, reflects how leaky and how large the capillary bed is. Sigma, the reflection coefficient, describes how well the wall holds back protein; in most tissues it is close to 1, so plasma protein exerts nearly its full oncotic effect."},{"type":"table","title":"Starling forces in a typical systemic capillary (Guyton and Hall values)","cols":["Force","Direction","About (mmHg)","Change that raises net filtration"],"rows":[["Capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc)","Out","30 arterial end, 10 venous end, 17 functional mean","Venous obstruction, heart failure, volume overload"],["Interstitial hydrostatic pressure (Pi)","Out while negative, in when positive","-3 in subcutaneous tissue","Becomes positive as edema develops"],["Interstitial oncotic pressure (pi i)","Out","8","Protein leak (inflammation) or protein not removed (lymphatic block)"],["Plasma oncotic pressure (pi c)","In","28","Low albumin (nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, malnutrition)"]],"caption":"Values are averages for a typical systemic capillary bed; individual organs differ, and the glomerulus and lung are special cases."},{"type":"p","text":"Averaged over the whole capillary, the outward forces exceed the inward force by a fraction of a millimeter of mercury. That small imbalance, multiplied across the enormous capillary surface of the body, leaves fluid in the interstitium every minute. Lymphatic capillaries collect this excess. Guyton and Hall estimate about 100 mL per hour of flow in the thoracic duct and about 20 mL per hour in other channels, giving about 2 to 3 L of lymph returned to the blood each day."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: net filtration along a capillary","text":"At the arterial end, outward forces are Pc 30, plus 3 from the negative Pi, plus pi i 8, totaling 41 mmHg. The inward force is pi c 28 mmHg, so net pressure is 13 mmHg outward. At the venous end Pc falls to 10, so outward forces total 21 against 28 inward, a net of 7 mmHg inward. Using the functional mean Pc of 17.3, outward forces are 28.3 against 28, a net of about 0.3 mmHg outward. That small residual drives the steady net filtration that becomes lymph. If heart failure raises venous pressure and mean Pc by 10 mmHg, net outward pressure rises far above the 0.3 mmHg baseline, and lymph flow must rise to match or edema forms."},{"type":"p","text":"The lymphatics therefore act as an overflow drain matched to filtration. Edema appears when filtration rises beyond the maximum lymph flow or when lymph flow itself fails. The first situation describes heart failure or hypoalbuminemia, and the second describes lymphedema; later sections explain why the two produce different fluids."},{"type":"flow","title":"From capillary filtration to venous return","steps":["Net filtration leaves fluid and some protein in the interstitium","Interstitial volume and pressure rise slightly","Anchoring filaments open lymphatic capillary junctions","Fluid and protein enter as lymph","Lymph passes through regional lymph nodes","Thoracic or right lymphatic duct empties into a venous angle"],"caption":"Lymph returns about 2 to 3 L per day of filtered fluid, with its protein, to the subclavian and internal jugular junctions."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-09-01-C02","obj":"IM-9.2","title":"Lymph composition and the return of filtered protein","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lymph is interstitial fluid that has entered a lymphatic vessel, so its small solutes match those of tissue fluid. Electrolytes and glucose are at about plasma concentrations because they cross capillary walls freely. What distinguishes lymph is its protein, its cells, and in the intestine its fat."},{"type":"table","title":"Components of lymph and their source","cols":["Component","Source","Note"],"rows":[["Water, electrolytes, glucose","Capillary filtrate","About plasma concentrations"],["Plasma protein, mainly albumin","Leak through endothelial junctions and transcytosis","About 2 g/dL in limb lymph, 3 to 4 g/dL intestinal, up to about 6 g/dL hepatic"],["Lymphocytes","Recirculation through lymph nodes","Sparse in afferent lymph, abundant in efferent lymph"],["Dendritic cells and antigen","Peripheral tissues","Carried in afferent lymph to the node"],["Chylomicrons","Intestinal enterocytes via lacteals","Make intestinal lymph milky (chyle)"]],"caption":"Liver and intestine produce about two thirds of all lymph, which is why thoracic duct lymph is relatively protein rich, about 3 to 5 g/dL."},{"type":"p","text":"Capillary walls are not perfectly impermeable. A small fraction of plasma albumin escapes every minute through intercellular clefts and by vesicular transport, and the liver sinusoids, with their discontinuous endothelium, leak protein freely. Over a day, a large fraction of the circulating plasma protein passes into the interstitium this way."},{"type":"p","text":"Once in the interstitium, that protein cannot return to the blood by the route it came. Plasma protein concentration is higher than tissue protein concentration, so diffusion opposes its return, and capillary endothelium restricts protein movement in both directions. Lymphatic capillaries differ: they are blind-ended tubes with overlapping, loosely joined endothelial cells, little or no basement membrane, and no pericytes. Fluid flows through these openings in bulk, carrying protein with it rather than sieving it out. Lymph is therefore the only route returning filtered protein to the circulation."},{"type":"p","text":"The importance of this return is quantitative. If lymphatic drainage stopped, interstitial protein would accumulate, interstitial oncotic pressure would rise toward plasma oncotic pressure, and the main force holding fluid in capillaries would be lost. Fluid would shift into the tissues and plasma volume would fall. Guyton and Hall estimate that total failure of lymph flow would be fatal within about a day."},{"type":"p","text":"Chylomicrons explain why intestinal lymph is special. Enterocytes package absorbed long-chain fatty acids, cholesterol, and fat-soluble vitamins into chylomicrons, which are far too large to cross the basement membrane and endothelium of blood capillaries. They enter the central lacteal of each villus instead and travel through the cisterna chyli and thoracic duct, bypassing the liver on first pass. A torn or obstructed thoracic duct (trauma, thoracic surgery, lymphoma) can therefore leak chyle into the pleural space as a milky chylothorax with high triglyceride content."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Lymphatic drainage of a limb is blocked while capillary filtration stays normal. What happens to interstitial protein concentration and to filtration over the following days?","explain":"Protein keeps leaking in but cannot leave, so interstitial protein and oncotic pressure rise. That raises the outward Starling force, so filtration increases and edema becomes self-sustaining.","check":"The paragraph on why lymph is the only protein return route and the Starling table in C01."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-09-01-C03","obj":"IM-9.3","title":"Propulsion of lymph and the response to rising interstitial pressure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lymph moves from tissues at slightly negative pressure into veins at a higher pressure, so it needs a pump. Initial (capillary) lymphatics collect fluid, and they drain into collecting lymphatics whose walls contain smooth muscle. Paired one-way valves divide each collecting vessel into segments called lymphangions, and the thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct carry the final output to the venous angles where the internal jugular and subclavian veins join."},{"type":"table","title":"Forces that move lymph","cols":["Mechanism","How it works","Relative role"],"rows":[["Intrinsic lymphangion pump","Stretch of a filled segment triggers smooth muscle contraction","Main driving force"],["One-way valves","Open toward the veins and close against backflow","Make every compression move lymph forward"],["Skeletal muscle pump","Contracting muscle squeezes lymphatics between valves","Large during exercise, minimal at rest"],["Arterial pulsation and body movement","Intermittent external compression","Minor, continuous"],["Respiratory pressure changes","Inspiration lowers thoracic and raises abdominal pressure","Draws lymph from cisterna chyli into the thoracic duct"]],"caption":"External compression can only move lymph forward because valves prevent retrograde flow."},{"type":"p","text":"The intrinsic pump works like the heart's length dependence. As a lymphangion fills, its smooth muscle is stretched and contracts more often and more forcefully, ejecting lymph through the downstream valve while the upstream valve closes. The next segment then fills and contracts, so a sequence of small pumps moves lymph centrally. Sympathetic stimulation and local mediators can modify this rhythm, but stretch is the main trigger."},{"type":"p","text":"Entry into the initial lymphatics depends on interstitial pressure. When tissue fluid volume rises, the matrix swells and anchoring filaments pull on the overlapping endothelial cells, opening their junctions like flap valves. Fluid enters, and when local pressure inside the vessel exceeds tissue pressure the flaps close, preventing backflow. More entry stretches the downstream lymphangions, which pump harder."},{"type":"p","text":"Lymph flow therefore rises steeply as interstitial pressure rises from its normal negative value toward zero. Guyton and Hall describe about a 20 fold increase by the time interstitial pressure reaches about 0 to +2 mmHg. Above this, flow plateaus: the rising tissue pressure compresses the larger lymphatics from outside as much as it fills them, so further increases do not raise flow."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Lymph flow as negative feedback on interstitial volume","steps":["Filtration rises and interstitial fluid accumulates","Interstitial pressure rises toward zero","Anchoring filaments open initial lymphatics","Stretched lymphangions pump faster and harder","Lymph flow rises and removes fluid and protein","Interstitial volume and pressure fall back"],"caption":"The loop limits edema until lymph flow reaches its plateau near 0 to +2 mmHg interstitial pressure."},{"type":"p","text":"This response is one of three safety factors that keep edema from appearing until capillary pressure rises substantially. Because more fluid is carried off, more protein is also removed, so interstitial oncotic pressure falls, a process called protein washout. Together with the low compliance of the interstitium while its pressure is negative, these allow capillary pressure to rise by about 17 mmHg before clinically obvious edema develops."},{"type":"table","title":"Safety factors against edema (Guyton and Hall estimates)","cols":["Safety factor","Mechanism","About (mmHg)"],"rows":[["Low interstitial compliance in the negative pressure range","Small volume gain raises tissue pressure enough to oppose filtration","3"],["Increased lymph flow","Rising interstitial pressure raises lymph flow up to about 20 fold","7"],["Protein washout","Faster lymph flow removes interstitial protein, lowering pi i","7"]],"caption":"Sum about 17 mmHg; lymphedema removes the last two at once."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A healthy traveler sits still for a 12 hour flight and develops ankle swelling. Which lymph propulsion mechanism has been lost?","explain":"The skeletal muscle pump. Without leg muscle contraction, external compression of lymphatics and veins falls, so lymph and venous return slow while capillary pressure in the dependent legs stays high.","check":"The table of forces that move lymph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-IMMU-09-01-C04","obj":"IM-9.4","title":"Lymphedema: mechanism and classic causes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lymphedema is edema caused by failure of lymphatic drainage while filtration remains normal. Because protein that leaks from capillaries cannot leave, edema fluid is protein rich, unlike the low-protein transudate of heart failure or nephrotic syndrome. The protein raises interstitial oncotic pressure, which draws out more fluid, and two of the three safety factors (lymph flow and protein washout) are lost at once."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing edema by mechanism","cols":["Mechanism","Example","Edema fluid protein","Pitting"],"rows":[["High capillary hydrostatic pressure","Heart failure, venous obstruction","Low (transudate)","Yes"],["Low plasma oncotic pressure","Nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis","Low (transudate)","Yes"],["Increased permeability","Inflammation, burns","High (exudate)","Variable"],["Lymphatic obstruction or absence","Axillary dissection, filariasis, Milroy disease","High","Early yes, late no"]],"caption":"Protein content of edema fluid points to the failed step."},{"type":"p","text":"Early lymphedema is soft and may pit. Over months, stagnant protein-rich fluid promotes chronic inflammation, fibroblast activation with collagen deposition, and growth of adipose tissue, so the limb becomes firm and nonpitting. The skin thickens and becomes warty, and when dermal lymphatics of the breast are blocked, the swollen skin dimples where hair follicles and sweat ducts tether it, giving peau d'orange. Stagnant lymph also impairs local immune surveillance, so recurrent cellulitis is common, and long-standing lymphedema rarely gives rise to lymphangiosarcoma."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of lymphedema","steps":["Lymphatics removed, irradiated, blocked, or malformed","Filtered fluid and protein cannot leave the interstitium","Interstitial oncotic pressure rises and draws out more fluid","Protein-rich pitting edema","Chronic stasis drives inflammation, fibrosis, and fat deposition","Firm nonpitting swelling, thick skin, recurrent cellulitis"],"caption":"Secondary and primary causes share the same downstream sequence."},{"type":"p","text":"Secondary lymphedema follows damage to normal vessels. Axillary lymph node dissection for breast cancer removes the nodes and channels that drain the arm, and radiation adds fibrosis of the remaining vessels, so swelling appears in the arm on the treated side. Wuchereria bancrofti is a mosquito-borne filarial nematode. Infective larvae enter through the bite and mature in lymphatics, where adult worms and the host inflammatory response to them, especially as they die, dilate and scar the vessels. Chronic infection produces elephantiasis of the legs and, in men, hydrocele and scrotal swelling."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary lymphedema reflects abnormal lymphatic development. Lymphatic endothelium buds from embryonic veins and grows under the signal of VEGF-C acting on VEGFR3, the product of the FLT4 gene. In Milroy disease, an autosomal dominant FLT4 mutation impairs this signaling, so the legs are swollen from birth with a normal karyotype. In Turner syndrome (45,X), the jugular lymph sacs fail to connect with the internal jugular veins, so lymph collects in the posterior neck as a cystic hygroma. Later the distended skin leaves a webbed neck, and the infant may have lymphedema of the hands and feet."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: one finding, four lesions","text":"Four patients have a swollen limb with protein-rich fluid. A woman who had axillary node dissection and radiation has an arm swollen on that side: damaged vessels. A man from a filariasis-endemic region has massive leg swelling and a hydrocele: adult worms obstructing lymphatics. A newborn with bilateral leg swelling, a parent with the same history, and a normal karyotype suggests Milroy disease from an FLT4 mutation. A fetus with a posterior neck mass who is later a short girl with a webbed neck points to Turner syndrome. Each lesion differs, but each blocks the same drainage step, so all four produce protein-rich edema that tends toward fibrosis."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology supply the mechanism. Lymphatic capillaries are built to take up fluid and protein in bulk, collecting vessels are built to pump it, and the thoracic duct delivers it to the left venous angle, so the system both balances Starling filtration and returns albumin. The same channels carry dendritic cells and antigen to lymph nodes and lymphocytes back to the blood, which is why lymph node dissection affects both fluid balance and regional immunity, and why chylomicrons travel this route rather than the portal vein."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology, microbiology, and genetics supply the lesions. Surgery and radiation destroy vessels, filarial worms and the inflammation they provoke obstruct them, and FLT4 mutations or the aneuploidy of Turner syndrome prevent normal development. All converge on retained interstitial protein, which explains why lymphedema differs from transudative edema and why it becomes fibrotic. The Starling values here are averages, and newer work on the endothelial glycocalyx suggests that sustained venous-end reabsorption is smaller than the classic model implies, but the conclusion that lymph must remove the net filtrate and its protein is unchanged."}],"summary":["Capillary hydrostatic pressure, interstitial oncotic pressure, and negative interstitial pressure favor filtration; plasma oncotic pressure opposes it.","Net filtration slightly exceeds reabsorption, and lymphatics return the excess, about 2 to 3 L per day.","Lymph is the only route back to the blood for filtered plasma protein, because lymphatic capillaries take up fluid and protein in bulk.","Lymph protein is highest from the liver (about 6 g/dL) and lowest from the limbs (about 2 g/dL); efferent lymph is rich in lymphocytes and intestinal lymph carries chylomicrons as chyle.","Lymph is propelled mainly by stretch-activated lymphangion contraction, with valves, skeletal muscle, arterial pulsation, and inspiration adding to it.","Rising interstitial pressure opens initial lymphatics and raises lymph flow about 20 fold until a plateau near 0 to +2 mmHg.","Lymph flow and protein washout are safety factors against edema; lymphedema removes both.","Lymphedema is protein rich and becomes nonpitting as chronic inflammation causes fibrosis and fat deposition.","Secondary causes include axillary dissection or radiation and Wuchereria bancrofti; primary causes include Milroy disease (FLT4, VEGFR3) and Turner syndrome (cystic hygroma)."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Body Fluid Compartments, section on edema and safety factors"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation, section on lymphatics"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, section on edema; Blood Vessels, section on lymphatics"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical Parasitology, filarial nematodes"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Cardiovascular System, development of the lymphatic system"}]}});
